const SEED_NOTES = [{"id": "note-solas-1974", "v": 4, "title": "SOLAS 1974", "category": "Maritime › IMO › Conventions", "tags": ["solas", "imo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>SOLAS 1974 — International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea</b></p><p><b>What is SOLAS</b></p><ul><li>Main IMO convention for merchant ship safety</li><li>Minimum standards: construction, equipment, operation</li><li>Flag State certifies · Port State Control (PSC) inspects</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1912 Titanic → SOLAS 1914 (never in force, WWI)</li><li>1929 · 1948 · 1960 (first under IMO, then IMCO)</li><li>1974: adopted 1 Nov 1974 · in force 25 May 1980 · tacit acceptance</li><li>1978 Protocol: tanker safety · in force 1 May 1981</li><li>1988 Protocol: HSSC (harmonized surveys) · in force 3 Feb 2000</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>International voyages</li><li>Passenger ships: &gt;12 passengers</li><li>Cargo ships: ≥500 GT (Ch IV and parts of Ch V: ≥300 GT)</li><li>Not covered (I/3): warships, troopships · cargo &lt;500 GT · non-mechanically propelled · wooden primitive · pleasure yachts not in trade · fishing vessels</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch I — General Provisions</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all SOLAS ships</li></ul><p><i>Part A — Application, definitions</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application: international voyages, flag State applies the rules</li><li>Reg 2 Definitions: passenger ship = &gt;12 passengers; international voyage; new/existing ship</li><li>Reg 3 Exceptions: warships, cargo &lt;500 GT, non-mechanically propelled, primitive wooden, yachts not in trade, fishing vessels</li><li>Reg 4 Exemptions: single voyage or novel ships — Exemption Certificate issued</li><li>Reg 5 Equivalents: alternative fitting allowed if at least as effective — reported to IMO</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Surveys and certificates</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Inspection and survey: by flag State officers or RO; Administration guarantees completeness</li><li>Reg 7 Passenger ships: initial · renewal every 12 months · additional after repairs</li><li>Reg 8 Cargo ship LSA &amp; equipment: initial · annual · periodical (within 3 months of 2nd or 3rd anniversary) · renewal</li><li>Reg 9 Cargo ship radio: initial · periodical · renewal</li><li>Reg 10 Cargo ship structure &amp; machinery: initial · intermediate · annual · renewal · 2 bottom inspections in 5 years (max 36 months apart)</li><li>Reg 11 Maintenance after survey: no changes without approval · report accidents/defects to Administration</li><li>Reg 12 Issue of certificates: Passenger Ship Safety · Cargo Ship Safety Construction / Equipment / Radio (or one Cargo Ship Safety Certificate)</li><li>Reg 13 Issue by another Government: on request of flag State</li><li>Reg 14 Duration and validity: passenger 12 months · cargo 5 years · extension max 3 months (to reach survey port) or 1 month (short voyages) · renewal within 3 months before expiry keeps expiry date</li><li>Reg 15 Forms of certificates &amp; Records of Equipment (Forms P, E, C, R)</li><li>Reg 16 Availability: certificates kept on board, readily available</li><li>Reg 17 Acceptance: certificates of other Contracting Governments accepted</li><li>Reg 18 Qualification: certificate may note conditions or limits</li><li>Reg 19 Control (PSC): check valid certificates · clear grounds → detailed inspection · may detain</li><li>Reg 20 Privileges: only ships with valid certificates</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Casualties</i></p><ul><li>Reg 21 Casualties: flag State investigates · findings to IMO</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch II-1 — Construction: Structure, Subdivision, Stability, Machinery, Electrical</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships and cargo ≥500 GT (mostly new ships)</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Definitions · Reg 3 Definitions for machinery/electrical parts</li></ul><p><i>Part A-1 — Structure</i></p><ul><li>3-1 Structure: class society rules or equivalent</li><li>3-2 Protective coatings (PSPC): ballast tanks ≥500 GT, double-side skin bulk carriers ≥150 m — MSC.215(82)</li><li>3-3 Safe access to tanker bows</li><li>3-4 Emergency towing: tankers ≥20,000 dwt both ends · towing procedure for all ships — MSC.256(84)</li><li>3-5 Asbestos: new installation banned (1 Jan 2011)</li><li>3-6 Means of access to cargo area spaces: oil tankers ≥500 GT, bulk carriers ≥20,000 GT — MSC.133(76) · Ship Structure Access Manual</li><li>3-7 Construction drawings kept on board and ashore</li><li>3-8 Towing and mooring equipment: design, inspection, maintenance (updated 1 Jan 2024)</li><li>3-9 Means of embarkation/disembarkation (gangways, accommodation ladders)</li><li>3-10 Goal-Based Standards: bulk carriers &amp; oil tankers ≥150 m · Ship Construction File — MSC.287(87)</li><li>3-11 Corrosion protection of cargo oil tanks: crude oil tankers ≥5,000 dwt</li><li>3-12 Noise: ≥1,600 GT, contracted ≥1 Jul 2014 — Noise Code MSC.337(91)</li><li>3-13 Lifting appliances &amp; anchor handling winches: design, test, inspect, maintain — MSC.532(107) · 1 Jan 2026</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Subdivision and stability</i></p><ul><li>Reg 4 General: probabilistic damage stability (SOLAS 2009)</li><li>Reg 5 Intact stability: inclining test on completion · lightship check every 5 years (passenger)</li><li>5-1 Stability information for the master: approved stability booklet, limiting KG/GM curves</li><li>Reg 6 Required subdivision index R · Reg 7 Attained index A (A ≥ R) · 7-1 factor p · 7-2 factor s · 7-3 permeability</li><li>Reg 8 Passenger ship special stability · 8-1 Flooding casualty: safe return to port / onboard stability computer or shore support</li><li>Reg 9 Double bottoms: passenger ships and cargo ships (not tankers)</li><li>Reg 10–12 Watertight bulkheads: construction, testing · collision bulkhead · peak &amp; machinery bulkheads</li><li>Reg 13 / 13-1 Openings in watertight bulkheads: passenger / cargo ships</li><li>Reg 15 / 15-1 Openings in shell plating · external openings (cargo)</li><li>Reg 16 / 16-1 Watertight doors, sidescuttles, decks, trunks: construction and initial tests</li><li>Reg 17 / 17-1 Internal watertight integrity above bulkhead deck · ro-ro passenger hull integrity</li><li>Reg 18 Subdivision load lines (passenger ships)</li><li>Reg 19 Damage control information: plans and booklet on the bridge</li><li>Reg 20 Loading of passenger ships: stability check before departure</li><li>Reg 21 Watertight doors (passenger): operated daily · drills weekly · inspected at least weekly</li><li>Reg 22 Prevention and control of water ingress: watertight doors closed at sea except when necessary · logbook entries</li><li>22-1 Flooding detection: passenger ships carrying ≥36 persons</li><li>Reg 23 Ro-ro passenger ships: bow doors, inner doors, indicators, TV monitoring</li><li>Reg 24 Water ingress control on cargo ships</li><li>Reg 25 Water level detectors: single-hold cargo ships (not bulk carriers)</li><li>25-1 Water level detectors: multiple-hold cargo ships (not bulk carriers/tankers) — MSC.482(103) · 1 Jan 2024</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Machinery</i></p><ul><li>Reg 26 General · Reg 27 Machinery: overspeed and safety devices</li><li>Reg 28 Means of going astern: enough astern power to stop</li><li>Reg 29 Steering gear: main and auxiliary · rudder hard over 35° to 30° other side in ≤28 s at max draught &amp; speed · auxiliary 15° to 15° in ≤60 s at half speed</li><li>Reg 30 Electric / electrohydraulic steering gear: alarms, indicators</li><li>Reg 31 Machinery controls · Reg 32 Steam boilers · Reg 33 Steam pipes · Reg 34 Air pressure systems</li><li>Reg 35 Machinery space ventilation · 35-1 Bilge pumping arrangements</li><li>Reg 37 Bridge–engine room communication (two independent means)</li><li>Reg 38 Engineer&#39;s alarm (audible in engineers&#39; accommodation)</li><li>Reg 39 Emergency installations (passenger ships): above bulkhead deck</li></ul><p><i>Part D — Electrical</i></p><ul><li>Reg 40 General · Reg 41 Main source of power and lighting</li><li>Reg 42 Emergency source — passenger ships: 36 h</li><li>42-1 Supplementary emergency lighting (ro-ro passenger ships)</li><li>Reg 43 Emergency source — cargo ships: 18 h · emergency lighting, VHF, fire pumps, steering</li><li>Reg 44 Starting of emergency generator: stored energy for ≥3 consecutive starts + second source for 3 more starts within 30 min (unless manual start)</li><li>Reg 45 Protection against shock, fire, electrical hazards</li></ul><p><i>Part E — Periodically unattended machinery spaces (UMS)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 46–54: fire detection, bilge/flooding alarms, bridge control of propulsion, alarms, safety systems, extra rules for passenger ships</li></ul><p><i>Part F — Alternative design</i></p><ul><li>Reg 55: alternative design and arrangements (engineering analysis approved by Administration)</li></ul><p><i>Part G — Low-flashpoint fuels</i></p><ul><li>Reg 56–57: ships using LNG and other low-flashpoint fuels → IGF Code MSC.391(95)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch II-2 — Fire Protection, Detection, Extinction</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all SOLAS ships (stricter for passenger ships and tankers)</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Fire safety objectives &amp; functional requirements · Reg 3 Definitions (A/B/C-class divisions)</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Prevention of fire and explosion</i></p><ul><li>Reg 4 Probability of ignition: oil fuel flashpoint ≥60°C · signed supplier declaration before bunkering (MSC.520(106), 1 Jan 2026) · tanker inert gas (≥8,000 dwt) · cargo tank venting</li><li>Reg 5 Fire growth potential: ventilation &amp; fuel shut-offs · fire-restricting materials</li><li>Reg 6 Smoke and toxicity: low-smoke paints, deck coverings</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Suppression of fire</i></p><ul><li>Reg 7 Detection and alarm: fixed detection in machinery &amp; accommodation · manual call points</li><li>Reg 8 Control of smoke spread</li><li>Reg 9 Containment of fire: A/B-class divisions · fire doors · main vertical zones (passenger)</li><li>Reg 10 Fire fighting: fire pumps · fire main · hydrants &amp; hoses · portable extinguishers · fixed systems · fire-fighter&#39;s outfits (cargo ships: at least 2) · EEBDs · two-way radios</li><li>Reg 11 Structural integrity</li></ul><p><i>Part D — Escape</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Notification of crew and passengers: general emergency alarm, public address</li><li>Reg 13 Means of escape: two escape routes · EEBDs · low-location lighting (passenger)</li></ul><p><i>Part E — Operational requirements</i></p><ul><li>Reg 14 Operational readiness &amp; maintenance: maintenance plan for fire equipment</li><li>Reg 15 Instructions, training and drills: fire drills · training manual · fire control plan on board</li><li>Reg 16 Operations: fire safety operational booklet</li></ul><p><i>Part F — Alternative design</i></p><ul><li>Reg 17</li></ul><p><i>Part G — Special requirements</i></p><ul><li>Reg 18 Helicopter facilities</li><li>Reg 19 Carriage of dangerous goods: Document of Compliance for DG</li><li>Reg 20 Vehicle, special category &amp; ro-ro spaces: fixed fire detection, fire patrol, video monitoring (MSC.555(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>20-1 Vehicle carriers carrying hydrogen/CNG vehicles</li><li>Reg 21 Casualty threshold, safe return to port (passenger) · Reg 22 Systems to stay operational after fire · Reg 23 Safety centre (passenger)</li></ul><p><i>Codes and other changes</i></p><ul><li>FSS Code MSC.98(73) · FTP Code 2010 MSC.307(88)</li><li>PFOS foam banned: new ships from 1 Jan 2026, existing ships by first survey after</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch III — Life-Saving Appliances and Arrangements</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships and cargo ≥500 GT</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Exemptions · Reg 3 Definitions</li><li>Reg 4 Evaluation, testing and approval of LSA · Reg 5 Production tests</li></ul><p><i>Part B, Section I — All ships</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Communications: portable VHF · SARTs/AIS-SART · rocket parachute flares (12) · on-board communications · general alarm</li><li>Reg 7 Personal LSA: lifebuoys (with lights, smoke signals, line) · lifejacket for every person (+ children) · immersion suits</li><li>Reg 8 Muster list &amp; emergency instructions: posted in conspicuous places</li><li>Reg 9 Operating instructions: posters at survival craft</li><li>Reg 10 Manning of survival craft: officer or certificated person in charge</li><li>Reg 11 Muster and embarkation arrangements: lighting, embarkation ladders</li><li>Reg 12 Launching stations · Reg 13 Stowage of survival craft · Reg 14 Stowage of rescue boats · Reg 15 Stowage of MES</li><li>Reg 16 Survival craft launching &amp; recovery · Reg 17 Rescue boat launching &amp; recovery</li><li>17-1 Recovery of persons from the water: plans and procedures</li><li>Reg 18 Line-throwing appliances</li><li>Reg 19 Emergency training and drills:<ul><li>Abandon ship + fire drill: every crew member monthly · within 24 h of leaving port if &gt;25% crew new</li><li>Lifeboat launch with crew: every 3 months · free-fall: every 6 months (12 with simulated launch every 6)</li><li>Rescue boat launch: monthly if possible, at least every 3 months</li><li>Enclosed space entry &amp; rescue: every 2 months — MSC.350(92)</li><li>On-board training: within 2 weeks of joining</li></ul></li><li>Reg 20 Operational readiness, maintenance &amp; inspections:<ul><li>Weekly: visual check of survival craft, rescue boats &amp; launching appliances · engines run · general alarm tested</li><li>Monthly: checklist inspection · recorded in log</li><li>Liferafts, inflatable lifejackets, MES: serviced every 12 months (max 17)</li><li>Hydrostatic release units: serviced every 12 months (disposable: replaced at expiry)</li><li>Falls: turned end for end every ≤30 months · renewed every ≤5 years</li><li>Release gear: annual thorough exam · 5-yearly overhaul — MSC.402(96) · 1 Jan 2020</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Section II — Passenger ships (additional)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 21 Survival craft: lifeboats ≥50% each side + liferafts · rescue boat</li><li>Reg 22 Personal LSA: lifejackets +5% · children&#39;s and infant lifejackets</li><li>Reg 23 Embarkation · Reg 24 Stowage · Reg 25 Muster stations</li><li>Reg 26 Ro-ro passenger ships: MES / fast rescue boats · Reg 27 Information on passengers (count before departure)</li><li>Reg 28 Helicopter landing &amp; pick-up areas · Reg 29 Decision support system for master</li><li>Reg 30 Drills: abandon ship &amp; fire drill weekly · passenger muster before or on departure</li></ul><p><i>Section III — Cargo ships (additional)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 31 Survival craft: lifeboats each side 100% (or free-fall 100%) + liferafts 100% (each side if not easily moved)</li><li>Reg 32 Personal LSA: immersion suit for every person</li><li>Reg 33 Embarkation &amp; launching: free-fall launch test (MSC.485(103), 1 Jan 2024)</li></ul><p><i>Sections IV–V</i></p><ul><li>Reg 34 All LSA must meet the LSA Code — MSC.48(66)</li><li>Reg 35 Training manual &amp; on-board training aids</li><li>Reg 36 Instructions for on-board maintenance</li><li>Reg 37 Muster list &amp; emergency instructions: duties, signals, substitutes</li></ul><p><i>LSA Code change ahead</i></p><ul><li>New ventilation rules for lifeboats installed ≥1 Jan 2029 — MSC.535(107)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch IV — Radiocommunications (GMDSS)</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships and cargo ≥300 GT</li><li>Modernized GMDSS — MSC.496(105) · 1 Jan 2024</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Terms (sea areas A1–A4) · Reg 3 Exemptions</li><li>Reg 4 Functional requirements:<ul><li>Distress alerting ship-to-shore by 2 independent means</li><li>Receiving shore-to-ship alerts</li><li>Ship-to-ship alerting</li><li>SAR coordination and on-scene communications</li><li>Locating signals</li><li>Maritime safety information (MSI)</li><li>General radiocommunications</li><li>Bridge-to-bridge communications</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Part B — Undertakings by Contracting Governments</i></p><ul><li>Reg 5 Radio services ashore · 5-1 GMDSS identities (MMSI)</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Ship requirements</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Radio installations: protected, at conning position, with operating instructions</li><li>Reg 7 Equipment, all ships: VHF DSC (ch 70) &amp; voice (ch 16, 13) · SART/AIS-SART · NAVTEX · EGC (if needed) · EPIRB (406 MHz)</li><li>Reg 8 Sea area A1 · Reg 9 Sea areas A1+A2 (MF DSC) · Reg 10 Sea areas A1–A3 (recognized mobile satellite service or HF) · Reg 11 Sea areas A1–A4 (HF)</li><li>Reg 12 Watches: continuous DSC watch · VHF ch 16 (where practicable)</li><li>Reg 13 Sources of energy: reserve source (batteries) for all GMDSS equipment</li><li>Reg 14 Performance standards (type approved)</li><li>Reg 15 Maintenance: A1/A2 one method · A3/A4 two methods (duplication, shore-based, at-sea)</li><li>Reg 16 Radio personnel: GMDSS certificate holders (GOC / ROC)</li><li>Reg 17 Radio records: GMDSS logbook</li><li>Reg 18 Position updating: automatic position to DSC equipment</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch V — Safety of Navigation</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships, all voyages (some regs by size; warships excepted)</li></ul><p><i>Government services (Regs 1–13)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Definitions · Reg 3 Exemptions and equivalents</li><li>Reg 4 Navigational warnings · Reg 5 Meteorological services · Reg 6 Ice patrol</li><li>Reg 7 Search and rescue services · Reg 8 Life-saving signals · Reg 9 Hydrographic services</li><li>Reg 10 Ships&#39; routeing (TSS) · Reg 11 Ship reporting systems · Reg 12 VTS · Reg 13 Aids to navigation</li></ul><p><i>Ship requirements (Regs 14–35)</i></p><ul><li>Reg 14 Manning: Minimum Safe Manning Document · working language on board</li><li>Reg 15 Bridge design &amp; procedures: BRM principles, one-person operation support</li><li>Reg 16 Maintenance of equipment · Reg 17 Electromagnetic compatibility</li><li>Reg 18 Approval &amp; performance standards · annual VDR performance test</li><li>Reg 19 Carriage requirements:<ul><li>All ships: magnetic compass · charts / ECDIS · GNSS · BNWAS (≥150 GT, all passenger ships)</li><li>≥300 GT: echo sounder · 9 GHz radar · SDME · AIS (international)</li><li>≥500 GT: gyro compass · rudder angle &amp; RPM indicators · ATA</li><li>≥3,000 GT: second radar (3 GHz) · second ATA</li><li>≥10,000 GT: ARPA · heading/track control</li><li>≥50,000 GT: rate-of-turn indicator · SDME over ground</li><li>ECDIS: passenger ≥500 GT · tankers ≥3,000 GT · other cargo ≥10,000 GT (new ≥3,000 GT) · phased 2012–2018</li><li>Electronic inclinometer: containerships &amp; bulk carriers ≥3,000 GT built ≥1 Jan 2026</li><li>ECDIS S-100 standard — MSC.530(106)/Rev.1 · new installations mandatory 1 Jan 2029</li></ul></li><li>19-1 LRIT: passenger, cargo ≥300 GT, MODUs · 4 position reports a day — MSC.202(81)</li><li>Reg 20 VDR: passenger ships · cargo ≥3,000 GT (built ≥1 Jul 2002)</li><li>Reg 21 International Code of Signals &amp; IAMSAR Manual Vol III on board</li><li>Reg 22 Bridge visibility: sea view ≤2 ship lengths or 500 m (whichever less) obscured ahead · blind sectors ≤10° each, ≤20° total</li><li>Reg 23 Pilot transfer arrangements: pilot ladder, combination arrangements, officer supervising — MSC.572(110) + MSC.576(110) · new installations ≥1 Jan 2028 · existing at first survey ≥1 Jan 2029 · non-SOLAS/domestic ships by 1 Jan 2030</li><li>Reg 24 Heading/track control: manual steering available at once · test manual steering after long autopilot use and before special-caution areas</li><li>Reg 25 Steering gear: more than one power unit running in special-caution areas</li><li>Reg 26 Steering gear testing &amp; drills: tested within 12 h before departure · emergency steering drill at least every 3 months · logged</li><li>Reg 27 Nautical charts &amp; publications: adequate and up to date</li><li>Reg 28 Records of navigational activities · daily reporting to company</li><li>Reg 29 Life-saving signals table on board</li><li>Reg 30 Operational limitations (passenger ships)</li><li>Reg 31 Danger messages: dangerous ice, derelicts, tropical storms, wind force 10+ without warning, ice accretion · lost containers (2026)</li><li>Reg 32 Information required in danger messages · lost containers: position and number — MSC.550(108) · 1 Jan 2026</li><li>Reg 33 Distress: master must proceed with all speed to assist · log reason if unable</li><li>Reg 34 Safe navigation: voyage plan before sailing (appraisal, planning, execution, monitoring) — A.893(21)</li><li>34-1 Master&#39;s discretion: company cannot constrain safety decisions</li><li>Reg 35 Misuse of distress signals prohibited</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VI — Carriage of Cargoes and Oil Fuels</b></p><ul><li>Applies: cargo ships carrying solid cargoes (not liquids/gases in bulk)</li></ul><p><i>Part A — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · 1-1 Definitions · 1-2 Solid bulk cargoes other than grain → IMSBC Code MSC.268(85)</li><li>Reg 2 Cargo information: shipper gives cargo details before loading (bulk: density, moisture, hazards)</li><li>Reg 3 Oxygen analysis &amp; gas detection instruments (+ training)</li><li>Reg 4 Use of pesticides (fumigation)</li><li>Reg 5 Stowage &amp; securing: Cargo Securing Manual (CSS Code A.714(17))</li><li>5-1 Safety data sheets: MARPOL Annex I cargoes and fuel oil</li><li>5-2 No blending of bulk liquid cargoes / production processes at sea</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Solid bulk cargoes</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Acceptability for shipment: moisture below TML (Group A)</li><li>Reg 7 Loading, unloading, stowage: loading plan agreed with terminal (BLU Code A.862(20)) · trimming</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Grain</i></p><ul><li>Reg 8 Definitions · Reg 9 Grain Code MSC.23(59): Document of Authorization · heel from grain shift ≤12° · GM ≥0.30 m (amended MSC.552(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VII — Carriage of Dangerous Goods</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships carrying DG</li></ul><p><i>Part A — Packaged form</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application → IMDG Code MSC.122(75) · Amdt 42-24 (MSC.556(108)) mandatory 1 Jan 2026</li><li>Reg 3 Carriage only per IMDG Code</li><li>Reg 4 Documents: DG declaration, container packing certificate, special list/manifest or stowage plan</li><li>Reg 5 Cargo Securing Manual</li><li>Reg 6 Reporting DG incidents (loss overboard) to nearest coastal State</li></ul><p><i>Part A-1 — Solid DG in bulk</i></p><ul><li>Reg 7 Definitions · 7-1 Application · 7-2 Documents · 7-3 Stowage &amp; segregation · 7-4 Reporting incidents</li></ul><p><i>Part B — Chemical tankers</i></p><ul><li>Reg 8–10: IBC Code MSC.4(48) · ship types 1, 2, 3 · Certificate of Fitness</li></ul><p><i>Part C — Gas carriers</i></p><ul><li>Reg 11–13: IGC Code MSC.5(48) · revised MSC.370(93) · ship types 1G–3G · Certificate of Fitness</li></ul><p><i>Part D — INF cargo</i></p><ul><li>Reg 14–16: INF Code MSC.88(71) · INF Class 1, 2, 3 ships</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VIII — Nuclear Ships</b></p><ul><li>Applies: nuclear-powered ships · Code A.491(XII)</li><li>Reg 1–12: reactor approval · radiation safety · safety assessment · operating manual · surveys · Nuclear Ship Safety Certificate · special control in ports · casualty reporting</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch IX — Management for the Safe Operation of Ships (ISM)</b></p><ul><li>Applies: 1 Jul 1998 — passenger ships, tankers, gas carriers, bulk carriers, cargo HSC ≥500 GT · 1 Jul 2002 — other cargo &amp; MODU ≥500 GT</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions (Company, DOC, SMC)</li><li>Reg 2 Application</li><li>Reg 3 Safety management: Company and ship comply with ISM Code A.741(18)</li><li>Reg 4 Certification: DOC (Company) · SMC (ship) · both 5 years</li><li>Reg 5 Maintenance of conditions</li><li>Reg 6 Verification and control: annual DOC verification · SMC intermediate between 2nd–3rd anniversary · Interim DOC 12 months · Interim SMC 6 months</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch X — High-Speed Craft</b></p><ul><li>Applies: HSC built ≥1 Jan 1996</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Requirements → HSC Code 1994 MSC.36(63) / 2000 MSC.97(73) · HSC Safety Certificate &amp; Permit to Operate</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XI-1 — Special Measures to Enhance Maritime Safety</b></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Recognized organizations: RO Code MSC.349(92)</li><li>Reg 2 Enhanced surveys: ESP Code 2011 A.1049(27) · bulk carriers &amp; oil tankers · close-up surveys, thickness measurement (firms audited, MSC.553(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>2-1 Harmonized survey periods for cargo ships outside ESP</li><li>Reg 3 IMO number: passenger ≥100 GT, cargo ≥300 GT · permanently marked on hull / superstructure and inside</li><li>Reg 4 PSC on operational requirements: crew familiar with essential shipboard procedures</li><li>Reg 5 Continuous Synopsis Record (CSR): history of flag, owner, class, DOC/SMC/ISSC issuers</li><li>Reg 6 Marine casualty investigation: Casualty Investigation Code MSC.255(84)</li><li>Reg 7 Atmosphere testing instrument: O₂, flammable gases, H₂S, CO — 1 Jul 2016</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XI-2 — Special Measures to Enhance Maritime Security (ISPS)</b></p><ul><li>Applies: passenger ships, cargo ≥500 GT, MODUs (international) + port facilities</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application</li><li>Reg 3 Government obligations: set security levels</li><li>Reg 4 Company &amp; ship requirements: comply with ISPS Code (SOLAS/CONF.5/34) · Part A mandatory, Part B guidance</li><li>Reg 5 Company: master has info on who appoints crew and who employs the ship</li><li>Reg 6 Ship Security Alert System (SSAS): covert · from bridge + one other place · alerts company/authority only · no alarm on board</li><li>Reg 7 Threats to ships · Reg 8 Master&#39;s discretion for safety and security</li><li>Reg 9 Control &amp; compliance: ISSC check · info before entry · records of last 10 port calls</li><li>Reg 10 Port facilities: PFSA, PFSP, PFSO</li><li>Reg 11 Alternative security agreements · Reg 12 Equivalent arrangements · Reg 13 Communication of information to IMO</li></ul><p><i>ISPS Code key points</i></p><ul><li>Security levels 1, 2, 3</li><li>SSP, SSO, CSO, PFSO</li><li>ISSC valid 5 years</li><li>Drills every 3 months · exercises once per calendar year (≤18 months apart)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XII — Additional Safety Measures for Bulk Carriers</b></p><ul><li>Applies: bulk carriers</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Implementation schedule</li><li>Reg 4 Damage stability: ≥150 m, cargo density ≥1,000 kg/m³ — survive flooding of any one hold</li><li>Reg 5 Structural strength: withstand one-hold flooding</li><li>Reg 6 Structural &amp; other requirements · Reg 7 Survey &amp; maintenance</li><li>Reg 8 Compliance information: solid triangle mark on hull (≥150 m single-side skin, cargo ≥1,780 kg/m³)</li><li>Reg 9 Ships unable to meet Reg 4.3</li><li>Reg 10 Cargo density declaration by shipper (verified if 1,250–1,780 kg/m³)</li><li>Reg 11 Loading instrument: ≥150 m · also &lt;150 m built ≥1 Jul 2006</li><li>Reg 12 Water ingress alarms: holds, ballast &amp; dry spaces forward of collision bulkhead · pre-alarm and main alarm on bridge</li><li>Reg 13 Pumping systems: drain/pump forward ballast tanks &amp; dry spaces</li><li>Reg 14 Restrictions from sailing with any hold empty: single-side skin ≥150 m, cargo ≥1,780 kg/m³, after 10 years old · no hold &lt;10% of max allowable cargo (full load)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XIII — Verification of Compliance</b></p><ul><li>Applies: Contracting Governments</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Verification: III Code A.1070(28) · IMSAS audits since 1 Jan 2016</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XIV — Safety Measures for Ships Operating in Polar Waters</b></p><ul><li>Applies: SOLAS ships in Arctic / Antarctic waters</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Requirements: Polar Code Part I-A — MSC.385(94) · 1 Jan 2017 · Reg 4 Alternative design</li><li>Polar Ship Certificate · Categories A / B / C · Polar Water Operational Manual (PWOM)</li><li>From 1 Jan 2026: navigation &amp; voyage planning extended to fishing vessels ≥24 m, pleasure yachts ≥300 GT, cargo 300–499 GT — MSC.538(107)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch XV — Safety Measures for Ships Carrying Industrial Personnel</b></p><ul><li>Applies: cargo ships &amp; HSC cargo craft ≥500 GT carrying &gt;12 industrial personnel · new ships ≥1 Jul 2024</li><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 General (counting IP) · Reg 3 Application (existing ships by first intermediate/renewal survey) · Reg 4 Other chapters · Reg 5 Requirements: IP Code MSC.527(106), certificate, surveys</li><li>Chapter adopted by MSC.521(106)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Who is Party (status)</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS 1974: 168 Contracting States · 98.91% of world tonnage</li><li>1978 Protocol: 123 States · 1988 Protocol (HSSC): 128 States</li><li>Almost every flag is Party; the few non-Parties are mostly landlocked or small States</li><li>Non-Party ships get <b>no more favourable treatment</b> (1988 Protocol Art I(3)): PSC inspects them to the same SOLAS standard and can restrict or detain them</li></ul><hr><p><b>Special Areas &amp; Routeing (SOLAS V/10–V/12)</b></p><ul><li>V/10 Ships&#39; routeing: Traffic Separation Schemes (TSS), Areas To Be Avoided (ATBA), deep-water routes — follow the ones IMO made mandatory</li><li>V/11 Mandatory ship reporting systems, e.g.:<ul><li>REEFREP — Torres Strait &amp; Great Barrier Reef inner route (MSC.52(66))</li><li>STRAITREP — Straits of Malacca &amp; Singapore (MSC.73(69), 1 Dec 1998): ≥300 GT, ≥50 m, any ship with hazardous cargo, all passenger ships</li><li>OUESSREP (Ushant) · Finisterre · WETREP (Western Europe PSSA) · CANREP (Canaries) · GALREP (Galápagos)</li></ul></li><li>V/12 Vessel Traffic Services (VTS): follow VTS where a coastal State sets them up</li><li>Polar waters (Ch XIV + Polar Code): an extra rulebook for Arctic &amp; Antarctic</li></ul><hr><p><b>USA — local rules on top of SOLAS (USCG)</b></p><ul><li>US is a SOLAS Party · USCG does PSC and enforces SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW for foreign ships</li><li>Notice of Arrival (33 CFR 160): ≥96 h before arrival (voyages &lt;96 h: before departure, ≥24 h before arrival) via eNOAD</li><li>33 CFR 164 Navigation safety (self-propelled ≥1,600 GT):<ul><li>Tests within 12 h before entering US waters: steering gear, internal comms &amp; alarms, standby generator, emergency batteries, main propulsion ahead/astern</li><li>Emergency steering drill logged within 48 h before entry (or every 3 months)</li><li>Corrected charts + US Coast Pilot, Light List, tide &amp; current tables (foreign equivalents allowed)</li><li>2nd independent radar ≥10,000 GT · gyro repeater, rudder angle indicator, echo sounder with recorder</li><li>AIS on at all times underway &amp; at anchor (§164.46)</li><li>Report failure of radar, AIS, gyro, echo sounder, steering gear to the COTP at once (§164.53)</li></ul></li><li>Security: MTSA rules (33 CFR 104) alongside ISPS · visitors&#39; ID checked and logged</li><li>Certificate of Compliance (COC) exam: foreign tank, gas &amp; passenger ships · full exam every 2 years<ul><li>Tankers: from May 2026 the annual mid-period exam can be credited by letter for good performers (no detention, violation or casualty in 3 years)</li></ul></li><li>Marine casualty: report immediately · CG-2692 within 5 days (46 CFR 4.05)</li><li>Crew: passport or ILO 185 seafarer ID; cash &gt;US$10,000 declared</li></ul><hr><p><b>Other regional rules</b></p><ul><li>EU: Paris MoU PSC (EU Directive 2009/16) · EU domestic passenger ships (Directive 2009/45)</li><li>PSC regimes: Paris MoU (Europe &amp; N. Atlantic) · Tokyo MoU (Asia-Pacific, incl. Philippines) · USCG · Indian Ocean, Black Sea, Mediterranean, Caribbean, Viña del Mar, Abuja, Riyadh MoUs</li><li>CIC 2026 (Paris + Tokyo MoU): <b>Cargo Securing</b> (SOLAS loading, stowage &amp; securing of cargo units) · 1 Sep–30 Nov 2026</li></ul><hr><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Chapter = the rule · Code = the detailed standard</li><li>PSC &amp; MARINA focus: certificates (Ch I), drills &amp; LSA (Ch III), GMDSS (Ch IV), navigation equipment &amp; voyage planning (Ch V), ISM (Ch IX), ISPS (Ch XI-2)</li><li>Watch: 2026 changes (lost containers, flashpoint, inclinometer, ro-ro fire, lifting appliances) · 2028–2029 (pilot ladders, S-100 ECDIS, lifeboat ventilation)</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Amendments to IMO instruments: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Amendments-to-IMO-instruments.aspx</li><li>IMO — Rules in force from 1 January 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>ABS — SOLAS V/23 pilot transfer: https://ww2.eagle.org/en/rules-and-resources/regulatory-updates/SOLAS-Pilot-Transfer-Arrangements.html</li><li>Hong Kong Marine Dept — SOLAS Ch XV text: https://www.mardep.gov.hk/en/msnote/pdf/msin2312anx1.pdf</li><li>AMSA — IP Code MSC.527(106): https://www.amsa.gov.au/sites/default/files/2024-03/msc.527106.pdf</li><li>UK SI 2022/1218 Part 3 — UK law applying SOLAS XII bulk carrier rules: https://legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2022/1218/part/3/made/data.pdf</li><li>Bureau Veritas — Modernized GMDSS: https://marine-offshore.bureauveritas.com/newsroom/modernization-gmdss-entry-force-1-january-2024-amended-solas-chapter-iv</li><li>IRClass — MSC.402(96) lifeboat maintenance: https://www.irclass.org/technical-circulars/imo-resolution-msc-402-96-reg-maintenance-thorough-examination-operational-testing-overhaul-and-repair-of-lifeboats-including-free-fall-lifeboats/</li><li>West P&amp;I — SOLAS ECDIS &amp; BNWAS carriage schedule: https://www.westpandi.com/publications/news/solas-mandatory-requirements-for-ecdis-and-bnwas/</li><li>IMO — ESP Code A.1049(27): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/Documents/A%20-%20Assembly/1049(27).pdf</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (Parties &amp; tonnage): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status%20of%20IMO%20Treaties.pdf</li><li>RISK4SEA PSC wiki — ships of non-Parties, no more favourable treatment: https://risk4sea.com/psc-wiki-risk4sea/general-for-port-state-control/1-5-ships-of-non-parties/</li><li>Swedish Transport Agency — IMO mandatory ship reporting systems: https://transportstyrelsen.se/globalassets/global/sjofart/dokument/sjotrafik_dok/imo-documents-srs.pdf</li><li>MPA Singapore — STRAITREP (MSC.73(69)): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/docs/mpalibraries/circulars-and-notices/port-marine-circulars/-pc98-65</li><li>eCFR — 33 CFR Part 164 Navigation safety: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-33/chapter-I/subchapter-P/part-164</li><li>JD Supra — US arrival guide (NOA, casualty reports, crew ID): https://www.jdsupra.com/legalnews/navigating-u-s-arrival-a-guide-for-7727596/</li><li>USCG — performance-driven COC exam (May 2026): https://www.news.uscg.mil/maritime-commons/Article/4497186/coast-guard-announces-performance-driven-certificate-of-compliance-examination/</li><li>Paris MoU — CIC 2026 Cargo Securing: https://parismou.org/2026/08/concentrated-inspection-campaign-cargo-securing</li></ul>"}, {"id": "note-marpol-7378", "v": 3, "title": "MARPOL 73/78", "category": "Maritime › IMO › Conventions", "tags": ["marpol", "imo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>MARPOL 73/78 — International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships</b></p><p><b>What is MARPOL</b></p><ul><li>Main IMO convention against pollution from ships, operational and accidental</li><li>6 Annexes: oil, noxious liquids, packaged harmful substances, sewage, garbage, air</li><li>Flag State certifies (IOPP, NLS, ISPP, IAPP, IEE) · Port State Control inspects</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1954 OILPOL (oil only) · 1967 Torrey Canyon → new convention</li><li>1973: MARPOL adopted 2 Nov 1973</li><li>1978 Protocol: after 1976–77 tanker accidents · MARPOL 73/78 in force 2 Oct 1983</li><li>1997 Protocol: added Annex VI (air) · in force 19 May 2005</li><li>Annexes in force: I 2 Oct 1983 · II 6 Apr 1987 · III 1 Jul 1992 · IV 27 Sep 2003 · V 31 Dec 1988 · VI 19 May 2005</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships of Party flags (incl. hydrofoils, air-cushion, submersibles, floating craft, fixed/floating platforms)</li><li>Not covered: warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships on non-commercial service</li><li>Annex I &amp; II compulsory · Annexes III–VI optional for each Party</li></ul><p><b>Articles (main)</b></p><ul><li>Art 4 Violation: flag State sanctions; violation anywhere is prohibited</li><li>Art 5 Certificates &amp; inspection by Port States</li><li>Art 6 Detection of violations · Art 7 Undue delay to ships (compensation)</li><li>Art 8 Reports on incidents (Protocol I: report without delay)</li><li>Art 16 Amendments (tacit acceptance)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex I — Prevention of Pollution by Oil</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships · IOPP Certificate: oil tankers ≥150 GT, other ships ≥400 GT · 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1 — General</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions (oil, oily mixture, special area, en route)</li><li>Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Exemptions &amp; waivers · Reg 4 Exceptions (safety, damage) · Reg 5 Equivalents</li></ul><p><i>Ch 2 — Surveys and certification</i></p><ul><li>Reg 6 Surveys: initial, renewal, intermediate, annual, additional</li><li>Reg 7–9 IOPP Certificate &amp; Supplement (Form A other ships, Form B tankers)</li><li>Reg 10 Duration: 5 years</li><li>Reg 11 PSC on operational requirements</li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Machinery spaces of all ships</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Sludge tank: adequate capacity · no overboard connection</li><li>12A Oil fuel tank protection: aggregate fuel ≥600 m³, delivered ≥1 Aug 2010 — tanks away from shell</li><li>Reg 13 Standard discharge connection</li><li>Reg 14 Oil filtering equipment:<ul><li>≥400 GT: 15 ppm equipment</li><li>≥10,000 GT: 15 ppm alarm + automatic stop</li></ul></li><li>Reg 15 Discharge from machinery spaces:<ul><li>Outside special areas: en route · ≤15 ppm · OFE working · not from cargo pump-room bilges</li><li>Inside special areas: same (≥400 GT) · Antarctic: prohibited</li></ul></li><li>Reg 16 No oil in forepeak or collision-bulkhead tanks · ballast not in fuel tanks (≥4,000 GT)</li><li>Reg 17 Oil Record Book Part I (machinery operations) · kept 3 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 4 — Cargo area of oil tankers</i></p><ul><li>Reg 18 Segregated ballast tanks (SBT): crude ≥20,000 dwt, product ≥30,000 dwt</li><li>Reg 19 Double hull &amp; double bottom: tankers ≥5,000 dwt delivered ≥6 Jul 1996</li><li>Reg 20 Phase-out of single-hull tankers · Reg 21 Heavy grade oil in single-hull tankers</li><li>Reg 22 Pump-room bottom protection</li><li>Reg 23 Accidental oil outflow performance · Reg 24–26 Damage assumptions, outflow, tank size limits</li><li>Reg 27 Intact stability · Reg 28 Subdivision &amp; damage stability</li><li>Reg 29 Slop tanks · Reg 30 Pumping, piping, discharge arrangements</li><li>Reg 31 ODME (oil discharge monitoring) · Reg 32 Oil/water interface detector</li><li>Reg 33 Crude oil washing (COW): new crude tankers ≥20,000 dwt</li><li>Reg 34 Discharge from cargo area:<ul><li>Outside special areas: &gt;50 nm from land · en route · ≤30 L per nm · total ≤1/30,000 of last cargo · ODME &amp; slop tank working</li><li>Inside special areas: prohibited</li></ul></li><li>Reg 35 COW operations manual · Reg 36 Oil Record Book Part II (cargo/ballast operations)</li></ul><p><i>Ch 5–11</i></p><ul><li>Reg 37 SOPEP: tankers ≥150 GT, other ships ≥400 GT · tankers ≥5,000 dwt: shore-based damage stability programme</li><li>Reg 38 Reception facilities</li><li>Reg 39 Fixed/floating platforms</li><li>Reg 40–42 Ship-to-ship (STS) oil transfer: tankers ≥150 GT · STS Operations Plan · notify coastal State ≥48 h before</li><li>Reg 43 Antarctic: no heavy fuel oil carriage or use</li><li>Reg 43A Arctic HFO ban: from 1 Jul 2024 (waivers possible until 1 Jul 2029) — MEPC.329(76)</li><li>Reg 44 Verification of compliance (III Code)</li><li>Reg 46–47 Polar Code (Part II-A)</li></ul><p><i>Special areas</i></p><ul><li>Mediterranean · Baltic · Black Sea · Red Sea · Gulfs · Gulf of Aden · Antarctic · NW European waters · Oman area of Arabian Sea · Southern South African waters</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex II — Noxious Liquid Substances (NLS) in Bulk</b></p><ul><li>Applies: chemical tankers / ships carrying NLS in bulk · NLS Certificate 5 years (or IBC Certificate of Fitness)</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1–2 — General &amp; categorization</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Exceptions · Reg 4 Exemptions · Reg 5 Equivalents</li><li>Reg 6 Categories:<ul><li>X: major hazard → discharge prohibited</li><li>Y: hazard → limited discharge</li><li>Z: minor hazard → less strict limits</li><li>OS (other substances): not regulated</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Surveys and certification</i></p><ul><li>Reg 7 Chemical tankers: IBC/BCH certificate accepted</li><li>Reg 8 Surveys · Reg 9–10 NLS Certificate · 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 4 — Design and equipment</i></p><ul><li>Reg 11 Construction (IBC Code) · Reg 12 Pumping &amp; stripping:<ul><li>Residue ≤75 L per tank for X/Y/Z (ships after 2007)</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Ch 5 — Operational discharges</i></p><ul><li>Reg 13 Discharge conditions: en route ≥7 knots · below waterline · ≥12 nm from land · depth ≥25 m</li><li>Prewash before leaving unloading port: Category X · high-viscosity/solidifying Y (stricter since 2021 in NW European waters, Baltic, Norwegian &amp; North Seas)</li><li>Reg 14 Procedures &amp; Arrangements (P&amp;A) Manual</li><li>Reg 15 Cargo Record Book (kept 3 years)</li></ul><p><i>Ch 6–10</i></p><ul><li>Reg 16 PSC (surveyor endorses prewash in Cargo Record Book)</li><li>Reg 17 SMPEP (Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plan) for NLS</li><li>Reg 18 Reception facilities</li><li>Antarctic: no discharge · Polar Code Part II-A</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex III — Harmful Substances in Packaged Form</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships carrying marine pollutants (identified in IMDG Code)</li><li>Reg 1 Application (IMDG Code marine pollutants)</li><li>Reg 2 Packing: minimize hazard</li><li>Reg 3 Marking &amp; labelling: &quot;MARINE POLLUTANT&quot; mark, durable for 3 months in seawater</li><li>Reg 4 Documentation: shipping documents state &quot;marine pollutant&quot; · special list/manifest/stowage plan</li><li>Reg 5 Stowage: properly secured</li><li>Reg 6 Quantity limitations</li><li>Reg 7 Exceptions (jettison for safety)</li><li>Reg 8 PSC on operational requirements · Reg 9 Verification</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex IV — Sewage</b></p><ul><li>Applies: ships ≥400 GT, or &lt;400 GT certified for &gt;15 persons (international) · ISPP Certificate 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1–2 — General, surveys, certificate</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Exceptions</li><li>Reg 4 Surveys · Reg 5–8 ISPP Certificate · 5 years</li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Equipment &amp; discharge</i></p><ul><li>Reg 9 Sewage systems: approved sewage treatment plant, OR comminuting &amp; disinfecting system, OR holding tank</li><li>Reg 10 Standard discharge connection</li><li>Reg 11 Discharge:<ul><li>Comminuted &amp; disinfected: &gt;3 nm from land</li><li>Untreated (from holding tank): &gt;12 nm · en route ≥4 knots · moderate rate</li><li>Approved treatment plant: anywhere (not visible solids, no discoloration)</li></ul></li><li>Baltic special area (passenger ships): sewage discharge prohibited unless special-area treatment plant · new ships 1 Jun 2019 · existing 1 Jun 2021 · route-exempt ships 1 Jun 2023</li></ul><p><i>Ch 4–6</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Reception facilities · 12bis Special area reception for passenger ships</li><li>Reg 13 PSC · Reg 14 Verification</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex V — Garbage</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships (incl. fixed/floating platforms)</li><li>Revised Annex V in force 1 Jan 2013: general prohibition, exceptions listed</li></ul><p><i>Regulations</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions (garbage, food wastes, cargo residues, operational wastes, e-waste)</li><li>Reg 2 Application</li><li>Reg 3 General prohibition: all discharge prohibited unless allowed · plastics never (incl. synthetic ropes, nets, plastic bags, incinerator ash from plastics)</li><li>Reg 4 Outside special areas:<ul><li>Food waste: &gt;3 nm if comminuted (≤25 mm) · &gt;12 nm if not comminuted</li><li>Cargo residues not harmful to the marine environment (HME): &gt;12 nm</li><li>Cleaning agents in wash water: if not HME</li><li>Animal carcasses: as far as possible from land</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5 Fixed/floating platforms: comminuted food waste &gt;12 nm only</li><li>Reg 6 Inside special areas: comminuted food waste &gt;12 nm · limited cargo-residue wash water · Antarctic: no poultry unless sterilized<ul><li>Special areas: Mediterranean, Baltic, Black Sea, Red Sea, Gulfs, North Sea, Antarctic, Wider Caribbean</li></ul></li><li>Reg 7 Exceptions: safety of ship/life · accidental loss (reasonable precautions) · loss of fishing gear reported</li><li>Reg 8 Reception facilities</li><li>Reg 9 PSC on operational requirements</li><li>Reg 10 Placards, Garbage Management Plan, records:<ul><li>Placards: ships ≥12 m</li><li>Garbage Management Plan: ≥100 GT or ≥15 persons</li><li>Garbage Record Book: ≥400 GT or ≥15 persons · and ships 100–399 GT from 1 May 2024 — MEPC.360(79) · kept 2 years</li><li>Part I (all ships): A plastics · B food waste · C domestic · D cooking oil · E incinerator ash · F operational waste · G animal carcasses · H fishing gear · I e-waste</li><li>Part II (solid bulk cargo ships): J cargo residues (non-HME) · K cargo residues (HME)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 11 Verification of compliance</li></ul><hr><p><b>Annex VI — Prevention of Air Pollution from Ships</b></p><ul><li>Applies: all ships · IAPP Certificate: ≥400 GT · 5 years · IEE Certificate: ≥400 GT</li></ul><p><i>Ch 1–2 — General, surveys, certification</i></p><ul><li>Reg 1 Application · Reg 2 Definitions · Reg 3 Exceptions &amp; exemptions · Reg 4 Equivalents (e.g. scrubbers)</li><li>Reg 5 Surveys · Reg 6–9 IAPP / IEE Certificates · Reg 10 PSC · Reg 11 Detection of violations</li></ul><p><i>Ch 3 — Control of emissions</i></p><ul><li>Reg 12 Ozone-depleting substances: no new installations (HCFCs banned from 1 Jan 2020) · ODS Record Book</li><li>Reg 13 NOx (diesel engines &gt;130 kW, EIAPP Certificate, NOx Technical Code 2008):<ul><li>Tier I: ships built ≥1 Jan 2000</li><li>Tier II: built ≥1 Jan 2011</li><li>Tier III: in NOx ECAs — North American &amp; US Caribbean (built ≥1 Jan 2016) · Baltic &amp; North Sea (built ≥1 Jan 2021) · Canadian Arctic (from 1 Mar 2026, keel laid ≥1 Jan 2025) · Norwegian Sea (contract ≥1 Mar 2026, or keel ≥1 Sep 2026, or delivery ≥1 Mar 2030)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 14 SOx &amp; PM:<ul><li>Global fuel sulphur ≤0.50% since 1 Jan 2020 · carriage ban of non-compliant fuel since 1 Mar 2020 (unless scrubber)</li><li>In SOx ECAs: ≤0.10% since 1 Jan 2015</li><li>SOx ECAs: Baltic · North Sea · North American · US Caribbean · Mediterranean (1 May 2025) · Canadian Arctic &amp; Norwegian Sea (1 Mar 2027) — MEPC.392(82)</li><li>Fuel changeover procedure &amp; log entries entering/leaving ECA</li></ul></li><li>Reg 15 VOC: tankers — VOC Management Plan</li><li>Reg 16 Shipboard incineration: type-approved incinerator (built ≥2000) · no PVC, no cargo residues of Annex I/II/III · not in port</li><li>Reg 17 Reception facilities</li><li>Reg 18 Fuel oil quality:<ul><li>Bunker Delivery Note (BDN): kept 3 years</li><li>Sealed MARPOL sample: kept ≥12 months</li><li>Non-availability → FONAR to flag &amp; port State</li><li>Fuel flashpoint and sulphur verified by sampling (in-use &amp; on-board samples)</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Ch 4 — Energy efficiency &amp; carbon intensity (revised MEPC.328(76), in force 1 Nov 2022)</i></p><ul><li>EEDI: new ships ≥400 GT (required index tightened in phases)</li><li>EEXI: existing ships ≥400 GT, from 1 Jan 2023</li><li>Reg 26 SEEMP: all ships ≥400 GT · now 3 parts — efficiency measures, fuel data collection, CII improvement plan</li><li>Reg 27 Fuel Oil Data Collection System (DCS): ≥5,000 GT · report yearly · Statement of Compliance · more detail from 1 Jan 2026 (MEPC.385(81))</li><li>Reg 28 CII rating: ≥5,000 GT · A–E yearly · D for 3 years or E once → corrective action plan<ul><li>Reduction vs 2019: 2027 13.626% · 2028 16.250% · 2029 18.875% · 2030 21.5% — MEPC.400(83)</li></ul></li></ul><p><i>Pending</i></p><ul><li>IMO Net-Zero Framework (fuel standard + GHG pricing): approved MEPC 83 (Apr 2025) · not adopted at MEPC/ES.2 Oct 2025 (57–49 vote to adjourn 1 year) · session reconvenes 16–27 Nov 2026 · earliest entry into force 1 Mar 2028</li><li>North-East Atlantic ECA (SOx, NOx, PM; 0.10% S): also deferred in Oct 2025 · decision at Nov 2026 session</li><li>NOx Technical Code amendments: 1 Sep 2026 (MEPC.398(83)) · 1 Mar 2027 (MEPC.397(83))</li></ul><hr><p><b>Who is Party (status)</b></p><ul><li>Annexes I/II: 161 States (98.89% tonnage) · III: 151 · IV: 147 · V: 155 · VI: 105 (96.70%)</li><li>Annexes I &amp; II compulsory · III–VI optional, so a State can be Party to some Annexes only</li><li>Non-Party ships get <b>no more favourable treatment</b> (Art 5(4)): PSC applies MARPOL to them anyway</li><li>Related separate conventions: BWM 2004 (95 States, 92.41%) · AFS 2001 anti-fouling (96 States) · Hong Kong ship recycling (in force 26 Jun 2025)</li></ul><hr><p><b>USA — Party to Annexes I, II, III, V, VI · NOT Annex IV</b></p><ul><li>Law: Act to Prevent Pollution from Ships (APPS, 33 USC 1901) — false Oil Record Book or &quot;magic pipe&quot; = criminal case<ul><li>Annex I/II/V → 33 CFR 151, 155, 156, 157 · Annex III → hazmat rules (46 CFR 148, 49 CFR 171–176) · Annex VI → APPS + EPA engine rules (40 CFR)</li></ul></li><li>Sewage (no Annex IV): Clean Water Act — US-approved Marine Sanitation Device (33 CFR 159) · No-Discharge Zones</li><li>North American ECA (with Canada): 200 nm from coast · 0.10% S since 1 Jan 2015 · NOx Tier III for ships built ≥2016 · US Caribbean ECA (Puerto Rico, US Virgin Islands)</li><li>California (CARB): distillate fuel (MGO/MDO) within 24 nm of the California baseline · At-Berth rule: shore power or approved control at berth</li><li>Ballast water: US is <b>not</b> Party to BWM 2004 · own rules 33 CFR 151 Subparts C–D (up to 12 nm): USCG type-approved BWMS (D-2-like standard), US public water, shore discharge, or no discharge · BWM Plan, records &amp; reports</li><li>Vessel discharges: EPA Vessel General Permit (VGP) still applies until USCG finishes VIDA rules (EPA VIDA standards final Oct 2024; ships ≥79 ft; ballast, bilge, graywater, scrubber washwater, hull, coatings…)</li><li>OPA 90 oil spill rules:<ul><li>Vessel Response Plan (VRP): tankers, and non-tank ships ≥400 GT carrying oil fuel · Qualified Individual, OSRO, salvage &amp; firefighting contracts · drills</li><li>COFR (financial responsibility certificate) needed to enter US waters</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>EU — regional rules</b></p><ul><li>EU Sulphur Directive: 0.10% S at berth in EU ports (since 1 Jan 2010) · exempt &lt;2 h on timetable or on shore power · log changeover times</li><li>EU ETS (emissions trading) — ships ≥5,000 GT: surrender allowances for 40% of 2024 emissions, 70% for 2025, 100% from 2026 · 100% intra-EU voyages</li><li>FuelEU Maritime (from 1 Jan 2025, ≥5,000 GT): GHG intensity of energy −2% in 2025, rising to −80% by 2050<ul><li>Shore power: container &amp; passenger ships at TEN-T ports from 1 Jan 2030, all EEA ports with OPS from 2035 (stays &lt;2 h exempt)</li></ul></li><li>EU MRV: monitor, report, verify CO₂ (and other GHG) per voyage</li><li>Mediterranean ECA (MARPOL): 0.10% S from 1 May 2025</li></ul><hr><p><b>China — Domestic Emission Control Areas (DECA)</b></p><ul><li>Coastal DECA: 0.50% S since 1 Jan 2019 · ships without scrubber may not carry &gt;0.50% fuel since 1 Mar 2020</li><li>Inland rivers (Yangtze, Xijiang): 0.10% S since 1 Jan 2020</li><li>Hainan waters: 0.10% S since 1 Jan 2022</li><li>Scrubber washwater discharge banned in coastal DECA port areas, inland rivers and Bohai</li><li>Possible 0.10% for more coastal waters under review</li></ul><hr><p><b>Certificates &amp; Records (quick list)</b></p><ul><li>IOPP + Oil Record Book I/II · SOPEP</li><li>NLS Certificate + Cargo Record Book · P&amp;A Manual · SMPEP</li><li>ISPP · Garbage Management Plan + Garbage Record Book · placards</li><li>IAPP · EIAPP · IEE · SEEMP · DCS Statement of Compliance · ODS Record Book · BDNs + samples</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>MARPOL = what you may discharge, where, and how it is recorded</li><li>PSC focus: Oil Record Book entries vs. sounding/OWS, 15 ppm alarm, garbage records, fuel sulphur &amp; BDNs, ECA changeover logs, SEEMP/CII</li><li>&quot;Magic pipe&quot; or false ORB entries = criminal cases (heavy fines, jail) in many ports</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>Clyde &amp; Co — MARPOL Annex VI key developments 2026: https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2026/april/marpol-annex-vi-key-developments-for-2026</li><li>IMO — Amendments to IMO instruments (Mediterranean ECA, Arctic HFO ban): https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Amendments-to-IMO-instruments.aspx</li><li>RS Class — new ECAs Norwegian Sea &amp; Canadian Arctic: https://rs-class.org/en/news/general/marpol-amendments-entering-into-force-new-ecas-for-the-norwegian-sea-and-canadian-arctic-/</li><li>LISCR — MEPC 83 summary: https://www.liscr.com/marketing/liscr/media/liscr/online%20library/maritime/mepc-83-meeting-summary.pdf</li><li>IMO — EEXI and CII FAQ: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Pages/EEXI-CII-FAQ.aspx</li><li>DNV — Net-Zero Framework decision delayed: https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/decision-on-the-imo-net-zero-framework-delayed-for-one-year/</li><li>AMSA — Garbage Record Book: https://www.amsa.gov.au/marine-environment/marine-pollution/garbage-record-book</li><li>Lithuania TSA — MEPC.360(79) text: https://ltsa.lrv.lt/uploads/ltsa/documents/files/TJO/MEPC_360(79).pdf</li><li>IMO — Status of IMO Treaties (Parties &amp; tonnage): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/StatusOfConventions/Status%20of%20IMO%20Treaties.pdf</li><li>USCG — MARPOL: US Annexes and US laws: https://www.dco.uscg.mil/Our-Organization/Assistant-Commandant-for-Prevention-Policy-CG-5P/Inspections-Compliance-CG-5PC-/Commercial-Vessel-Compliance/MARPOL</li><li>Ship &amp; Bunker — North American ECA (200 nm): https://shipandbunker.com/news/am/377041-north-american-eca-comes-into-effect</li><li>CARB — Ocean-Going Vessel Fuel Regulation (24 nm): https://ww2.arb.ca.gov/our-work/programs/ocean-going-vessel-fuel-regulation</li><li>International Registries — US ballast water rules (US not Party to BWM): https://register-iri.com/wp-content/uploads/Update-on-United-States-Ballast-Water-Treatment-Requirements.pdf</li><li>Holland &amp; Knight — EPA VIDA final rule (Oct 2024): https://hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2024/10/epa-to-cruise-ships-cargo-ships-barges-tankers-tugs-and-tows</li><li>Skuld — US non-tank Vessel Response Plans: https://www.skuld.com/topics/environment/oil-pollution/america/us-nontank-vessel-response-plan-requirements/</li><li>American Club — EU 0.1% sulphur at berth: https://www.American-Club.com/files/files/MA_EU_Sulphur_Content.pdf</li><li>Kuehne+Nagel — EU ETS &amp; FuelEU Maritime: https://mykn.kuehne-nagel.com/news/article/eu-maritime-regulations-to-reduce-ghg-emissio-19-Dec-2024</li><li>Lloyd&#39;s Register — FuelEU shore power at berth: https://www.lr.org/en/services/statutory-compliance/fueleu-regulation/zero-emission-requirements-for-energy-used-at-berth/</li><li>DieselNet — China DECA sulphur rules: https://dieselnet.com/standards/cn/marine.php</li></ul>"}, {"id": "note-stcw-1978", "v": 2, "title": "STCW 1978", "category": "Maritime › IMO › Conventions", "tags": ["stcw", "imo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>STCW 1978 — International Convention on Standards of Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Seafarers</b></p><p><b>What is STCW</b></p><ul><li>IMO convention for minimum training, certification &amp; watchkeeping standards for seafarers on seagoing ships</li><li>Convention (Articles) + Annex (Regulations, Ch I–VIII) + STCW Code<ul><li>Code Part A: mandatory (competence tables: function, competence, knowledge, assessment)</li><li>Code Part B: guidance</li></ul></li><li>Philippines: MARINA is the single STCW Administration (RA 10635, 2014) · MARINA STCW Office issues CoC/CoP</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1978: adopted 7 Jul 1978 · in force 28 Apr 1984 · first global standard for seafarers</li><li>1995 amendments: full revision · STCW Code created · White List (I/7) · in force 1 Feb 1997</li><li>2010 Manila amendments: adopted 25 Jun 2010 · in force 1 Jan 2012 · transition to 1 Jan 2017<ul><li>Rest hours tightened · alcohol limits · ECDIS, BRM/ERM, leadership &amp; teamwork</li><li>New grades: Able Seafarer Deck/Engine, ETO, ETR</li><li>Refresher training every 5 years · security training (VI/6)</li></ul></li><li>2016–2018: IGF Code ships (V/3) · Polar waters (V/4, in force 1 Jul 2018)</li><li>1 Jan 2026: harassment &amp; bullying (incl. sexual harassment &amp; assault) prevention added to basic &amp; officer training — MSC.560(108)</li><li>Comprehensive review (started MSC 105, 2022): 400+ gaps found · revised STCW targeted for MSC approval 2029–2030</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Seafarers serving on seagoing ships of Party flags</li><li>Not covered (Art III): warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships non-commercial · fishing vessels (see STCW-F 1995) · pleasure yachts not in trade · wooden ships of primitive build</li></ul><p><b>Articles (main)</b></p><ul><li>Art I General obligations · Art II Definitions · Art III Application</li><li>Art VI Certificates: issued after required training, service &amp; competence</li><li>Art VIII Dispensation: Master may be permitted to serve with a lower-grade officer — max 6 months, not for radio officer/master/chief engineer except emergency</li><li>Art IX Equivalents · Art X Control (PSC)</li><li>Art XII Amendments (tacit acceptance)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch I — General Provisions</b></p><ul><li>I/1 Definitions (CoC, CoP, documentary evidence, seagoing service)</li><li>I/2 Certificates &amp; endorsements:<ul><li>CoC (officers) needs flag State endorsement · original certificates carried on board</li></ul></li><li>I/3 Near-coastal voyages</li><li>I/4 Control procedures (PSC): check certificates, rest hours, watchkeeping competence — detain if deficiency endangers persons/ship/environment</li><li>I/5 National provisions (fraud, penalties)</li><li>I/6 Training &amp; assessment: qualified instructors &amp; assessors</li><li>I/7 Communication of information → &quot;White List&quot; of Parties giving full effect</li><li>I/8 Quality standards: QSS for training &amp; certification · independent evaluation every ≤5 years</li><li>I/9 Medical standards:<ul><li>Medical certificate max 2 years · under 18: max 1 year · colour vision max 6 years</li><li>Expiry during voyage: valid to next port with doctor, max 3 months</li></ul></li><li>I/10 Recognition of foreign certificates (endorsement of recognition)</li><li>I/11 Revalidation of CoC every 5 years:<ul><li>Sea service ≥12 months in last 5 years, or ≥3 months in last 6 months, or approved course/test</li></ul></li><li>I/12 Simulators: performance standards (radar/ARPA, ECDIS)</li><li>I/13 Trials · I/14 Company responsibilities:<ul><li>Seafarers certified &amp; familiarized · ship-specific familiarization before duties · effective communication language</li></ul></li><li>I/15 Transitional provisions</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch II — Master and Deck Department</b></p><ul><li>II/1 OOW (navigational watch) ≥500 GT:<ul><li>Age ≥18 · ≥12 months approved seagoing service within approved training (training record book) or ≥36 months</li><li>≥6 months bridge watchkeeping under master/qualified officer</li><li>GMDSS GOC/ROC · basic training VI/1</li></ul></li><li>II/2 Master &amp; Chief Mate:<ul><li>≥3,000 GT: CM — ≥12 months as OOW · Master — ≥36 months as OOW, or ≥24 months if ≥12 of them as CM</li><li>500–3,000 GT: CM — ≥12 months as OOW · Master — ≥36 months as OOW, or ≥24 months if ≥12 as CM</li></ul></li><li>II/3 OOW &amp; Master &lt;500 GT (near-coastal / not near-coastal)</li><li>II/4 Rating forming part of navigational watch (RFPNW):<ul><li>Age ≥16 · ≥6 months seagoing service, or ≥2 months with special training</li></ul></li><li>II/5 Able Seafarer Deck: age ≥18 · RFPNW + ≥18 months service, or ≥12 months with approved training</li></ul><p><b>Code A-II key competences (OOW)</b></p><ul><li>Plan &amp; conduct passage, position fixing · keep safe watch (COLREG, BRM)</li><li>Radar/ARPA &amp; ECDIS · respond to emergencies &amp; distress · IAMSAR · SMCP English</li><li>Cargo handling &amp; stowage · stability, trim &amp; stress · fire &amp; LSA · MARPOL &amp; leadership</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch III — Engine Department</b></p><ul><li>III/1 OOW engineering watch (≥750 kW): age ≥18 · ≥12 months workshop + sea service (approved training) · ≥30 months education &amp; training</li><li>III/2 Chief &amp; Second Engineer ≥3,000 kW:<ul><li>2/E: ≥12 months as assistant engineer/OOW</li><li>C/E: ≥36 months, or ≥24 months if ≥12 as 2/E</li></ul></li><li>III/3 Chief &amp; Second Engineer 750–3,000 kW:<ul><li>2/E: ≥12 months · C/E: ≥24 months, of which ≥12 as 2/E</li></ul></li><li>III/4 Rating forming part of engine watch: age ≥16 · ≥6 months service, or 2 months with training</li><li>III/5 Able Seafarer Engine</li><li>III/6 Electro-Technical Officer (ETO) · III/7 Electro-Technical Rating (ETR)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch IV — Radiocommunication &amp; Radio Operators</b></p><ul><li>IV/1 Application (GMDSS ships)</li><li>IV/2 GMDSS radio operators:<ul><li>GOC: sea areas A1–A4 · ROC: A1 only</li><li>Required for deck officers (OOW and above)</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch V — Special Training for Certain Ship Types</b></p><ul><li>V/1-1 Oil &amp; chemical tankers:<ul><li>Basic: cargo operation training</li><li>Advanced (Master, C/E, CM, 2/E, anyone in charge of cargo): ≥3 months service on that tanker type + course</li></ul></li><li>V/1-2 Liquefied gas tankers: basic + advanced (same idea)</li><li>V/2 Passenger ships: crowd management, safety training, crisis management &amp; human behaviour, passenger safety/cargo safety &amp; hull integrity (ro-ro)</li><li>V/3 Ships under IGF Code (gas/low-flashpoint fuels): basic + advanced</li><li>V/4 Polar waters: basic (Master, CM, OOW) + advanced (Master, CM)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VI — Emergency, Occupational Safety, Security, Medical Care, Survival</b></p><ul><li>VI/1 Basic Training (all seafarers with safety duties) — Code A-VI/1:<ul><li>Personal survival techniques (PST)</li><li>Fire prevention &amp; firefighting (FPFF)</li><li>Elementary first aid (EFA)</li><li>Personal safety &amp; social responsibilities (PSSR) — now incl. prevention of &amp; response to violence and harassment (Table A-VI/1-4, MSC.560(108), 1 Jan 2026)</li><li>Philippines: MARINA STCW Advisory 2025-40 / 2025-40A — BT-PSSR updating training for existing certificate holders</li><li>Refresher every 5 years: PST &amp; FPFF</li></ul></li><li>VI/1 Familiarization: safety familiarization before duties (all persons on board)</li><li>VI/2 Proficiency in survival craft &amp; rescue boats (PSCRB) · fast rescue boats · refresher 5 years</li><li>VI/3 Advanced firefighting · refresher 5 years</li><li>VI/4 Medical first aid (A-VI/4-1) · Medical care (A-VI/4-2, person in charge of medical care)</li><li>VI/5 Ship Security Officer (SSO)</li><li>VI/6 Security awareness (all) · designated security duties (crew with security tasks)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VII — Alternative Certification</b></p><ul><li>Function-based certificates (navigation, cargo, engineering, etc.) as alternative to Ch II/III</li><li>Same competence standards · rarely used</li></ul><hr><p><b>Ch VIII — Watchkeeping</b></p><ul><li>VIII/1 Fitness for duty:<ul><li>Rest: ≥10 h in any 24 h · ≥77 h in any 7 days</li><li>Max 2 rest periods, one ≥6 h · interval between rest periods ≤14 h</li><li>Exceptions: emergencies &amp; drills; Party may allow 70 h/7 days for ≤2 consecutive weeks</li><li>Drills: minimize rest disturbance</li><li>Watch schedules posted · rest-hour records kept &amp; endorsed</li><li>Alcohol: max 0.05% BAC or 0.25 mg/l in breath · drug &amp; alcohol abuse prevention</li></ul></li><li>VIII/2 Watchkeeping arrangements &amp; principles (Code A-VIII/2):<ul><li>Part 3-1 Navigational watch: lookout, master&#39;s standing orders, calling the master, handover, restricted visibility, pilot on board</li><li>Part 3-2 Engineering watch: UMS, handover, calling the C/E</li><li>Part 3-3 Radio watch</li><li>Part 4 Watch in port: deck &amp; engine · hazardous cargo</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>STCW = who is qualified to do what on board, and how rested they must be</li><li>Promotion path (≥3,000 GT): OOW → 12 months → CM → 12 months as CM (or 36 as OOW) → Master</li><li>PSC focus: valid CoC &amp; endorsements, CoPs, medical certificate, rest-hour records vs. log, watch schedules, familiarization records, language/communication</li><li>Keep track: 5-year CoC revalidation · 5-year refreshers (PST, FPFF, PSCRB, AFF) · 2-year medical</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — STCW Convention (dates of adoption, 1995 &amp; 2010 amendments): https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-standards-of-training,-certification-and-watchkeeping-for-seafarers-(stcw).aspx</li><li>IMO — Rules in force from 1 January 2026 (MSC.560(108)): https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 718: IMO amendment to PSSR (Table A-VI/1-4): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-718-mf-2026-imo-amendment-to-pssr/mgn-718-mf-2026-imo-amendment-to-pssr</li><li>IMO — Comprehensive review of STCW FAQ: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/comprehensive-review-of-the-stcw-convention-and-code-faq.aspx</li><li>MARINA STCW Office — advisories: https://stcw.marina.gov.ph/marina-stcw-advisories/</li></ul>"}, {"id": "note-mlc-2006", "v": 2, "title": "MLC 2006 (ILO)", "category": "Maritime › ILO › Conventions", "tags": ["mlc", "ilo", "convention", "core"], "body": "<p><b>MLC 2006 — Maritime Labour Convention (ILO)</b></p><p><b>What is MLC</b></p><ul><li>ILO convention, the &quot;seafarers&#39; bill of rights&quot;: decent work &amp; living conditions on board</li><li>&quot;4th pillar&quot; of maritime law with SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW</li><li>Replaces 37 older ILO maritime Conventions and their Recommendations (68 instruments since 1920)</li><li>Structure: Articles → Regulations → Code (Part A mandatory Standards · Part B Guidelines)</li><li>5 Titles · &quot;No more favourable treatment&quot; (Art V.7): PSC applies it to ships of non-ratifying flags too</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Adopted 23 Feb 2006 (94th International Labour Conference, Geneva)</li><li>In force 20 Aug 2013 — after 30 ratifications covering ≥33% of world tonnage</li><li>Philippines ratified 20 Aug 2012 — the 30th registered ratification (with Russia the same day) → in force 20 Aug 2013</li><li>Amendments (via Special Tripartite Committee, Art XIII):<ul><li>2014: financial security for abandonment, death &amp; long-term disability · in force 18 Jan 2017</li><li>2016: harassment &amp; bullying guidance · MLC certificate extension (up to 5 months) · in force 8 Jan 2019</li><li>2018: wages &amp; SEA continue if held by pirates/armed robbers · in force 26 Dec 2020</li><li>2022: social connectivity, free food &amp; water, sized PPE, prompt repatriation · in force 23 Dec 2024</li><li>2025: &quot;key workers&quot;, shore leave, repatriation, harassment rules · adopted Apr 2025 · in force ~Dec 2027</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships, public or private, ordinarily engaged in commercial activities</li><li>Seafarer = any person employed, engaged or working on board in any capacity (incl. hotel staff)</li><li>Not covered: fishing vessels (ILO C188) · traditional ships (dhows, junks) · warships &amp; naval auxiliaries · ships only in inland/sheltered waters</li><li>Ships &lt;200 GT not on international voyages: flag State may apply some rules differently</li></ul><p><b>Articles (main)</b></p><ul><li>Art I General obligations · Art II Definitions &amp; scope</li><li>Art III Fundamental rights: freedom of association &amp; collective bargaining · no forced labour · no child labour · no discrimination</li><li>Art IV Seafarers&#39; employment &amp; social rights (safe workplace, fair terms, decent living conditions, health &amp; welfare)</li><li>Art V Implementation &amp; enforcement (flag + port State · no more favourable treatment)</li><li>Art VI Regulations &amp; Code (Part A mandatory, Part B guidance; &quot;substantially equivalent&quot;)</li><li>Art XIII Special Tripartite Committee (governments, shipowners, seafarers)</li><li>Art XIV–XV Amendments (Code: tacit acceptance)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 1 — Minimum Requirements to Work on a Ship</b></p><ul><li>Reg 1.1 Minimum age:<ul><li>≥16 years · ≥18 for night work and hazardous work</li><li>Night = ≥9 h, starting not later than midnight, ending not earlier than 5 am</li></ul></li><li>Reg 1.2 Medical certificate:<ul><li>Max 2 years · under 18: max 1 year · colour vision max 6 years</li><li>Expires at sea → valid until next port with doctor, max 3 months</li></ul></li><li>Reg 1.3 Training &amp; qualifications: trained/certified (STCW) · personal safety training</li><li>Reg 1.4 Recruitment &amp; placement:<ul><li>No fees charged to seafarers for placement</li><li>Licensed/regulated agencies (Philippines: DMW, formerly POEA) · agency protection system (insurance/compensation if agency fails)</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 2 — Conditions of Employment</b></p><ul><li>Reg 2.1 Seafarers&#39; Employment Agreement (SEA):<ul><li>Signed by seafarer &amp; shipowner (or representative) · copy on board · seafarer keeps original</li><li>Time to review &amp; get advice before signing · record of employment (no comment on quality of work)</li><li>Minimum notice for early termination: ≥7 days</li><li>2018: SEA continues while held by pirates</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.2 Wages:<ul><li>Paid at least monthly · monthly account (statement) · allotments to family · reasonable exchange-rate charges</li><li>2018: wages continue during piracy captivity</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.3 Hours of work &amp; rest (either system):<ul><li>Max work: 14 h in any 24 h · 72 h in any 7 days, OR</li><li>Min rest: 10 h in any 24 h · 77 h in any 7 days</li><li>Rest max 2 periods, one ≥6 h · interval ≤14 h</li><li>Table of shipboard working arrangements posted · records kept, signed by seafarer · emergencies override</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.4 Leave:<ul><li>Paid annual leave ≥2.5 calendar days per month of employment</li><li>Shore leave for health &amp; well-being (2025: no unjustified denial; key-worker access)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.5 Repatriation:<ul><li>Free repatriation: SEA expiry, termination, illness/injury, shipwreck, ship sale, piracy</li><li>Max service period on board &lt;12 months</li><li>2014: financial security for abandonment (Std A2.5.2) — 4 months&#39; wages, repatriation, essential needs (food, water, fuel, medical care)</li><li>2022: prompt repatriation, also when SEA expires abroad</li><li>2025: clearer repatriation duties incl. luggage allowance (30 kg)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 2.6 Compensation for ship loss or foundering: unemployment indemnity (max 2 months&#39; wages, Part B)</li><li>Reg 2.7 Manning levels: sufficient for safety, efficiency, fatigue (Safe Manning Document)</li><li>Reg 2.8 Career &amp; skill development, employment opportunities</li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 3 — Accommodation, Recreational Facilities, Food &amp; Catering</b></p><ul><li>Reg 3.1 Accommodation &amp; recreational facilities:<ul><li>Ships built ≥20 Aug 2013: cabin floor areas, headroom ≥203 cm, separate sleeping rooms, no cabins forward of collision bulkhead</li><li>Heating, ventilation, lighting, noise &amp; vibration limits · sanitary facilities · separate hospital if ≥15 seafarers on voyages &gt;3 days</li><li>Frequent inspections by master (record kept)</li><li>2022: social connectivity — internet access, reasonable cost (Reg 3.1 para 17)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 3.2 Food &amp; catering:<ul><li>Food &amp; drinking water free of charge · adequate quantity, nutrition, quality, variety (religion &amp; culture respected)</li><li>Ship&#39;s cook trained &amp; qualified · min age 18</li><li>Ships with &lt;10 crew: trained food handler instead of cook</li><li>Frequent documented inspections of food, water &amp; catering spaces</li><li>2022: nutritionally balanced food &amp; good quality water, free</li></ul></li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 4 — Health Protection, Medical Care, Welfare &amp; Social Security</b></p><ul><li>Reg 4.1 Medical care on board &amp; ashore:<ul><li>Free medical care, as close as possible to shore care · right to see doctor in port</li><li>Doctor required: ≥100 persons on international voyages &gt;3 days</li><li>No doctor: officer trained in medical care (STCW VI/4) · medicine chest · medical guide · radio medical advice</li><li>Standard medical report form</li><li>2022: prompt disembarkation of sick/injured · repatriation of remains</li></ul></li><li>Reg 4.2 Shipowners&#39; liability:<ul><li>Costs of sickness &amp; injury from start of duty to repatriation</li><li>Wages paid while sick/injured on board · ashore ≥16 weeks (national law)</li><li>Burial expenses</li><li>2014: financial security for contractual claims (death &amp; long-term disability)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 4.3 Health &amp; safety protection, accident prevention:<ul><li>Safety &amp; health policy, risk assessment · ship safety committee (≥5 seafarers)</li><li>Report &amp; investigate occupational accidents</li><li>2016 guidance: harassment &amp; bullying prevention</li><li>2022: PPE in proper sizes (incl. women) · deaths reported to ILO</li></ul></li><li>Reg 4.4 Access to shore-based welfare facilities (welfare boards, seafarer centres)</li><li>Reg 4.5 Social security: protection in at least 3 of 9 branches (medical care, sickness, unemployment, old age, employment injury, family, maternity, invalidity, survivors)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Title 5 — Compliance &amp; Enforcement</b></p><ul><li>Reg 5.1 Flag State:<ul><li>5.1.1 General principles: effective inspection &amp; certification system</li><li>5.1.2 Recognized Organizations (class societies) may do surveys</li><li>5.1.3 Maritime Labour Certificate &amp; DMLC:<ul><li>Required: ships ≥500 GT on international voyages (or between foreign ports)</li><li>MLC Certificate: 5 years · intermediate inspection between 2nd &amp; 3rd anniversary</li><li>Interim certificate: max 6 months (new ship, flag change, new shipowner)</li><li>DMLC Part I (flag State: national requirements) · Part II (shipowner: how they comply)</li><li>16 areas inspected: min age · medical cert · qualifications · SEA · recruitment &amp; placement · hours of work/rest · manning · accommodation · recreational facilities · food &amp; catering · health &amp; safety · medical care · on-board complaints · payment of wages · financial security for repatriation · financial security for shipowner liability</li><li>Certificates on board &amp; posted: MLC, DMLC, financial security certificates (2014)</li></ul></li><li>5.1.4 Inspection &amp; enforcement (inspectors&#39; powers, detention)</li><li>5.1.5 On-board complaint procedures: fair, quick, no victimization · complaint can go to master or ashore · copy of procedures given to seafarer</li><li>5.1.6 Marine casualties: official inquiry for serious casualties (2025: seafarers&#39; fair treatment in inquiries)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5.2 Port State:<ul><li>5.2.1 Inspections in port: check MLC &amp; DMLC · detailed inspection if documents missing/false, clear grounds, or complaint · detention for serious/repeated breaches</li><li>5.2.2 Onshore complaint handling</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5.3 Labour-supplying responsibilities: control of recruitment agencies &amp; social security for nationals (important for the Philippines)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>MLC = the seafarer&#39;s rights: contract, wages, rest, leave, repatriation, food, cabin, medical care, complaints</li><li>PSC &amp; ITF focus: SEA copies vs. crew list, wage records &amp; allotments, rest-hour records, MLC/DMLC &amp; financial security certificates, food &amp; water inspection records, complaint procedure, cabin condition</li><li>Abandonment: financial security must pay 4 months&#39; wages + repatriation — ILO/IMO abandonment database records cases</li><li>STCW &amp; MLC rest hours are the same (10 h / 77 h) — work-hour option exists only in MLC</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 7 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>ILO — MLC FAQ A44: amendments and their status (2014, 2016, 2018): https://faqmlc.ilo.org/en/?p=20318</li><li>ILO — 2022 amendments in force 23 Dec 2024: https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/new-important-set-amendments-mlc-2006-will-enter-force-23-december-2024</li><li>DNV — 2025 amendments (STC-5, Apr 2025; in force ~Dec 2027): https://www.dnv.com/news/2025/new-amendments-to-the-mlc-2006/</li><li>ILO — Philippines&#39; ratification, 30th, in force 20 Aug 2013: https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/philippines-ratification-marks-global-milestone-decent-work-seafarers</li><li>ILO — Russia &amp; Philippines ratifications (37 Conventions replaced): https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/milestone-ratifications-seafarers-labour-rights-charter</li><li>ILO — MLC 2006 text and preparatory reports: https://www.ilo.org/international-labour-standards/maritime-labour-convention-2006-0/text-and-preparatory-reports-maritime-labour-convention-2006</li></ul>"}];
// IMO conventions and codes (Obsidian notes in notes/imo, made by notes/imo-to-js.mjs).
const IMO_NOTES = [{"id":"note-imo-imo-index","v":199800693,"title":"IMO Index","category":"Maritime › IMO","tags":["imo","index"],"body":"<p><b>IMO Index (Conventions and Codes)</b></p><p><b>Already in Max (full notes)</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS 1974, MARPOL 73/78, STCW 1978, MLC 2006</li></ul><p><b>Conventions in this draft</b></p><ul><li>BWM 2004</li><li>Hong Kong Convention 2009</li><li>COLREG 1972</li><li>Load Lines 1966</li><li>Tonnage 1969</li><li>AFS 2001</li><li>CLC and Fund 1992</li><li>Bunkers 2001</li><li>Nairobi WRC 2007</li><li>SAR 1979</li><li>SUA 1988</li><li>FAL 1965</li><li>LLMC 1976</li><li>OPRC 1990</li><li>London Convention and Protocol</li><li>Salvage 1989</li><li>Intervention 1969</li><li>HNS 2010</li><li>Cape Town Agreement 2012</li><li>STCW-F 1995</li><li>Athens 2002</li></ul><p><b>Codes in this draft</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS: ISM Code, ISPS Code, LSA Code, FSS Code, FTP Code, IMDG Code, IBC Code, IGC Code, IMSBC Code, IGF Code, Polar Code, HSC Code, CSS Code, TDC Code, Grain Code, BLU Code, INF Code, IP Code, III Code, RO Code, ESP Code, Casualty Investigation Code, IS Code 2008, MASS Code</li><li>MARPOL: NOx Technical Code</li></ul><p><b>Not yet written</b></p><ul><li>SPS Code, CTU Code, GMDSS, MARPOL guidelines (EEDI/SEEMP, fuel oil quality), CSC 1972, Torremolinos 1977, STP/Space STP, Nuclear 1971, IMSO</li></ul><p><b>Verification status (updated 11 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>All 46 notes now use the same detailed format: what it is, history with dates and resolutions, who it applies to, chapter-by-chapter contents, key numbers, practical points on board, Key Point, sources checked 10–11 Nov 2026</li><li>Checked against IMO resolution texts: MASS Code (MSC.595(111)), IS Code (MSC.267(85)), BLU Code (A.862(20)), IP Code (MSC.527(106)), INF Code (MSC.88(71)), HSC 2000 (MSC.97(73)), NOx Technical Code (MEPC.177(58))</li><li>Checked against IMO press briefings and class/P&amp;I summaries: HNS 2010 (in force 29 Nov 2027, limits), Cape Town Agreement (in force Feb 2027), STCW-F revision (in force 1 Jan 2026), LLMC 2012 limits, MSC 111 and MEPC 84 (2026) amendments</li><li>Still worth a check against the full official text before an exam or a legal answer: rule-by-rule wording of COLREG, freeboard tables in Load Lines, CLC/Fund figures in SDR (change only by amendment)</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-afs-2001","v":69520768,"title":"AFS 2001","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["afs","anti-fouling","tbt","cybutryne","hull-coating","certificate","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>AFS 2001 — International Convention on the Control of Harmful Anti-fouling Systems on Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>5 Oct 2001</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>17 Sep 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Organotin (TBT)</td><td>Not applied after 1 Jan 2003 · not borne on hulls after 1 Jan 2008 (or covered by a barrier coating)</td></tr><tr><td>Cybutryne</td><td>MEPC.331(76), in force 1 Jan 2023 · not applied or re-applied from 1 Jan 2023</td></tr><tr><td>Ships ≥ 400 GT, international voyages</td><td>International Anti-fouling System Certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Ships 24 m to &lt; 400 GT, international voyages</td><td>Declaration on Anti-fouling System</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is AFS</h2><ul><li>Parties must prohibit or restrict harmful anti-fouling systems on ships flying their flag, on ships operating under their authority, and on ships entering their ports, shipyards or offshore terminals (Art 3(1), Art 4(1))</li><li>Anti-fouling paints stop marine organisms attaching to hulls, which would slow ships and raise fuel use; the metallic compounds in them leach into seawater and can persist (IMO)</li><li>TBT caused deformities in oysters and sex changes in whelks (IMO)</li><li>The prohibited substances are in Annex 1, updated as needed; a technical group reviews proposals for other substances</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 5 Oct 2001 · open for signature 1 Feb 2002 – 31 Dec 2002 (Art 17) · in force <b>17 Sep 2008</b></li><li>Art 18: in force 12 months after at least 25 States, whose merchant fleets are at least 25% of world gross tonnage</li><li><b>Cybutryne amendment MEPC.331(76)</b>: adopted 17 Jun 2021 · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2022 · in force <b>1 Jan 2023</b> (Art 16(2))</li></ul><hr><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"afs-timeline\"><figcaption>Key dates of AFS 2001</figcaption></figure><h2>The Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Title / content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General obligations — give full effect to the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions (10 terms)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Control of anti-fouling systems</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Control of waste materials</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Process for proposing amendments to Annex 1</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Technical groups</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Research and monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Surveys and certification</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Inspection of ships</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Violations</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Undue delay or detention of ships</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Settlement of disputes</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Relationship to international law of the sea (UNCLOS)</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Amendments</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Key articles</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 3(1)</b>: applies to ships flying a Party's flag, ships operating under a Party's authority, and ships entering a Party's port, shipyard or offshore terminal</li><li><b>Art 3(2)</b>: not warships, naval auxiliaries and other government ships on non-commercial service (Parties still ensure they act consistently where reasonable and practicable)</li><li><b>Art 3(3)</b>: Parties prevent non-Party ships from getting more favourable treatment</li><li><b>Art 4(2)</b>: a ship with a system that becomes controlled may keep it until its next scheduled renewal, but <b>not longer than 60 months after application</b>, unless the Committee finds exceptional circumstances</li><li><b>Art 5</b>: waste from applying or removing controlled systems is handled safely and in an environmentally sound way</li><li><b>Art 6</b>: a Party sends an initial proposal to IMO; the Committee decides if an in-depth review is needed; the proposing Party submits a comprehensive proposal; a technical group evaluates it; the Committee decides</li><li><b>Art 11 inspection</b>: (1) port inspection is limited to checking the certificate or declaration, and brief sampling that does not affect the system's integrity, structure or operation — sample processing time must not prevent the ship leaving; (2) a thorough inspection if there are clear grounds; (3) a Party may warn, detain, dismiss or exclude a ship in violation, and tells its Administration; (4) co-operation on enforcement</li><li><b>Art 12</b>: sanctions adequate in severity to discourage violations</li><li><b>Art 13</b>: a ship unduly detained or delayed is entitled to compensation</li><li><b>Art 16</b>: Annex amendment deemed accepted after 12 months unless more than one third of Parties object; generally in force six months after acceptance</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex 1 — Controls</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Substance</th><th>Control</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Organotin compounds acting as biocides</td><td>Ships shall not apply or re-apply</td><td>1 Jan 2003</td></tr><tr><td>Organotin compounds acting as biocides</td><td>Ships shall either not bear such compounds on hulls or external surfaces, <b>or bear a coating that forms a barrier</b> to them</td><td>1 Jan 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Cybutryne</td><td>Ships shall not apply or re-apply anti-fouling systems containing cybutryne</td><td>1 Jan 2023</td></tr><tr><td>Cybutryne (existing)</td><td>Ships with cybutryne in the external coating layer must remove it or apply a barrier (sealer) coat at the next scheduled renewal after 1 Jan 2023, and not later than <b>60 months</b> after the last application</td><td>from 1 Jan 2023</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Organotin 1 Jan 2008 control: does not apply to fixed and floating platforms, FSUs and FPSOs built before 1 Jan 2003 that have not been in dry dock on or after that date</li><li>Cybutryne exceptions: fixed and floating platforms, FSUs and FPSOs built before 1 Jan 2023 and not dry-docked on or after that date · ships not on international voyages · ships under 400 GT on international voyages, if accepted by the coastal State(s)</li><li>Annexes 2 and 3: information needed in an initial proposal (five elements) and a comprehensive proposal (properties, environmental fate and effects, non-target organisms, human health, methods and peer review)</li></ul><h2>Annex 4 — Surveys and certification</h2><p><b>Regulation 1 — Surveys</b> (ships of 400 GT and above on international voyages, except platforms, FSUs and FPSOs)</p><ul><li>(a) an <b>initial survey</b> before the ship is put into service, or before a certificate is first issued</li><li>(b) a survey when the anti-fouling systems are <b>changed or replaced</b></li></ul><p><b>Regulation 2 — Certificate</b></p><ul><li>Issued after a successful survey; the issuing authority has full responsibility</li><li>Cybutryne amendment: for a ship with a system applied before the control took effect, the Administration issues a certificate not later than two years after entry into force of the control (MEPC.331(76) guidance: affected flagged ships obtain a valid certificate within 24 months)</li></ul><p><b>Regulation 3</b> — another Party may survey and certify at the Administration's request; no certificate to a ship of a non-Party State</p><p><b>Regulation 4 — Validity</b>: the certificate ceases to be valid if the anti-fouling system is changed without endorsement, or on transfer to another flag</p><p><b>Regulation 5 — Declaration</b>: ships of 24 m or more but under 400 GT on international voyages (except platforms, FSUs and FPSOs): the owner or authorized agent signs a <b>Declaration on Anti-fouling System</b>, with supporting documents</p><p><b>Certificate:</b> the International Anti-fouling System Certificate is supplemented by a <b>Record of Anti-fouling Systems</b>; Reg 1(4) allows survey and certification to be delegated to Recognized Organizations</p><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>At every dry dock with new hull paint: keep the paint documents, and get the Record of Anti-fouling Systems updated</blockquote><blockquote>Check the record shows no organotin (or a barrier coat) and no cybutryne (or a barrier coat)</blockquote><blockquote>A port State may check the certificate or declaration and take brief samples, without delaying the ship</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>AFS Convention articles (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-05/harmful-anti-fouling-ships.html</li><li>AFS Convention annexes (Australian Parliament, treaty text): https://aph.gov.au/~/media/wopapub/house/committee/jsct/march2003/treaties/Antifouling_text_pdf.ashx</li><li>MEPC.331(76) cybutryne amendment (Dutch treaty publication): https://zoek.officielebekendmakingen.nl/trb-2022-8.html</li><li>IMO — AFS Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-the-Control-of-Harmful-Anti-fouling-Systems-on-Ships-(AFS).aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-athens-2002","v":216000347,"title":"Athens 2002","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["athens","pal","passengers","luggage","liability","insurance","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Athens Convention 2002 (PAL)</h1><p><b>Athens Convention relating to the Carriage of Passengers and their Luggage by Sea, 1974, as amended by the 2002 Protocol</b></p><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subject</td><td>Carrier's liability for passengers' death or injury and for luggage</td></tr><tr><td>Convention</td><td>Adopted 13 Dec 1974 · in force 28 Apr 1987</td></tr><tr><td>2002 Protocol</td><td>Adopted 1 Nov 2002 · in force 23 Apr 2014</td></tr><tr><td>Main changes in 2002</td><td>Strict liability for shipping incidents, higher limits, compulsory insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Unit of account</td><td>SDR</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>History</h2><ul><li>The 1990 Protocol never entered into force.</li><li>A State that ratifies the 2002 Protocol must denounce the 1974 Convention and its 1976 and 1990 Protocols, if bound by them.</li><li>The Protocol lets States keep or set higher limits for carriers under their jurisdiction (Art 7).</li><li><b>IMO Reservation and Guidelines (Oct 2006):</b> adopted by the Legal Committee at its 92nd session to help States ratify. A State may limit war and terrorism liability to 250,000 SDR per passenger per occasion, or 340 million SDR per ship per occasion. The Guidelines say war cover should include war-related acts, capture or seizure, derelict weapons, terrorist or malicious acts, and confiscation or expropriation.</li></ul><h2>Application (Art 2)</h2><p>Applies to international carriage when:</p><ul><li>the ship flies the flag of, or is registered in, a State Party; or</li><li>the contract of carriage was made in a State Party; or</li><li>the place of departure or destination is in a State Party.</li></ul><p>It does not apply where another convention's mandatory civil liability regime for a different transport mode governs the carriage.</p><h2>Key definitions (Art 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Carrier</td><td>A person by or on behalf of whom a contract of carriage was concluded</td></tr><tr><td>Performing carrier</td><td>A person other than the carrier (owner, charterer or operator) who actually performs all or part of the carriage</td></tr><tr><td>Ship</td><td>A seagoing vessel only, not an air-cushion vehicle</td></tr><tr><td>Cabin luggage</td><td>Luggage in the passenger's cabin, or otherwise in the passenger's possession, custody or control</td></tr><tr><td>International carriage</td><td>Departure and destination in two different States, or in one State with a scheduled intermediate port of call in another</td></tr><tr><td>Shipping incident</td><td>Shipwreck, capsizing, collision or stranding; explosion or fire in the ship; or a defect in the ship</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Liability for death and personal injury</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"athens-liability\"><figcaption>Death and injury: liability tiers and compulsory insurance (Arts 3, 4bis, 7)</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shipping incident, up to 250,000 SDR per passenger per occasion</td><td>Carrier liable; it avoids this only by proving war, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, an exceptional natural phenomenon, or that a third party wholly caused it by an act or omission done with intent to cause the incident</td></tr><tr><td>Shipping incident, above 250,000 SDR</td><td>Carrier liable unless it proves it was without fault or neglect</td></tr><tr><td>Not a shipping incident</td><td>Carrier liable only for fault or neglect; the claimant must prove it</td></tr><tr><td>Overall limit (Art 7)</td><td>400,000 SDR per passenger per distinct occasion</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>A State Party may set a higher national limit, not lower than 400,000 SDR, and must inform the Secretary-General.</li><li>Contributory fault (Art 6): if the carrier proves the passenger's fault or neglect caused or contributed to the harm, the court may exonerate the carrier wholly or partly under its own law.</li></ul><h2>Luggage and vehicles (Art 8, per carriage)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Limit (SDR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cabin luggage, per passenger</td><td>2,250</td></tr><tr><td>Vehicle, including luggage in or on it</td><td>12,700</td></tr><tr><td>Other luggage, per passenger</td><td>3,375</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Deductible:</b> the carrier and passenger may agree a deductible of up to 330 SDR for vehicle damage, and up to 149 SDR per passenger for other luggage.</li><li><b>Valuables (Art 5):</b> money, negotiable securities, gold, silverware, jewellery, works of art and other valuables are covered only if deposited with the carrier for safekeeping, and then up to the Art 8(3) limit unless a higher limit is agreed.</li><li>Cabin luggage: liability needs fault, and fault is presumed for shipping incidents. Other luggage: carrier liable unless it proves it was without fault.</li></ul><h2>Compulsory insurance (Art 4bis)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Which ships</td><td>Registered in a State Party and licensed to carry more than 12 passengers</td></tr><tr><td>Cover</td><td>Death and personal injury, at least 250,000 SDR per passenger per occasion</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Issued after the authority confirms compliance; shows ship, carrier, IMO number, type and duration of security, insurer and validity; <b>carried on board</b></td></tr><tr><td>Termination</td><td>Security cannot end early until 3 months after notice is given to the authorities</td></tr><tr><td>Direct action</td><td>Claims may be brought directly against the insurer; the 250,000 SDR limit applies even if the carrier cannot limit liability</td></tr><tr><td>Insurer's defences</td><td>May use the carrier's defences (except bankruptcy or winding up) and the assured's wilful misconduct</td></tr><tr><td>State duties</td><td>A flag State may not let a ship operate without a certificate; each Party must ensure insurance for such ships entering or leaving its ports</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Time limits and jurisdiction</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Time bar (Art 16)</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Starts</td><td>Injury: disembarkation. Death during carriage: when the passenger should have disembarked. Luggage: disembarkation, or when it should have occurred, whichever is later</td></tr><tr><td>Outer limit</td><td>5 years from disembarkation; or 3 years from when the claimant knew, or should have known, of the injury, loss or damage, if earlier</td></tr><tr><td>Extension</td><td>By the carrier's written declaration or a written agreement made after the cause of action arose</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction (Art 17)</td><td>Claimant's choice among: defendant's residence or principal place of business; departure or destination; claimant's residence (if defendant does business there); place of contract (same condition). After the incident the parties may agree another court or arbitration</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Strict liability for shipping incidents up to 250,000 SDR per passenger; up to 400,000 SDR unless the carrier proves no fault. Ships with more than 12 passengers must carry the insurance certificate on board. Claims can arrive up to 2 years later (5 at most), so record every passenger injury.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 22 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Consolidated Athens text (University of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/carriage-passangers-luggage-sea-consolidated.html</li><li>IMO — Athens Convention (PAL): https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Athens-Convention-relating-to-the-Carriage-of-Passengers-and-their-Luggage-by-Sea-(PAL).aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-bwm-2004","v":136698516,"title":"BWM 2004","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["bwm","ballast-water","d-1","d-2","bwms","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>BWM 2004 — International Convention for the Control and Management of Ships' Ballast Water and Sediments</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>13 Feb 2004</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>8 Sep 2017</td></tr><tr><td>Standards</td><td>D-1 exchange · D-2 performance</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>≥ 400 GT: International Ballast Water Management Certificate</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is BWM</h2><ul><li>Aim (Art 2): prevent, minimize and ultimately eliminate the transfer of harmful aquatic organisms and pathogens</li><li>Ships manage ballast water to the <b>D-1 exchange</b> or <b>D-2 performance</b> standard, carry a Ballast Water Management Plan and a Ballast Water Record Book; ships ≥ 400 GT also carry the International Ballast Water Management Certificate</li><li>D-2 deadline: all ships by 8 Sep 2024 at the latest (see B-3)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 13 Feb 2004 · in force <b>8 Sep 2017</b> (Art 18: 12 months after 30 States representing 35% of world merchant tonnage; the 35% was reached 8 Sep 2016)</li><li>MEPC.296(72) (amends A-1 and D-3) + MEPC.300(72): <b>BWMS Code</b> made mandatory (adopted MEPC 72, Apr 2018; in force Oct 2019); it superseded the G8 Guidelines from Oct 2019</li><li>MEPC.297(72): revised D-2 implementation schedule (Reg B-3) · MEPC.299(72): amended E-1 and E-5 (endorsement of additional surveys)</li><li>MEPC.325(75): <b>commissioning testing</b> made mandatory (amends Reg E-1 and the Appendix I certificate form) · adopted Nov 2020 · in force 1 Jun 2022</li><li>MEPC.369(80): new Appendix II (Ballast Water Record Book form) · MEPC.383(81): amends A-1 and B-2 to allow electronic record books · IMO listed them as expected in force Feb 2025 and 1 Oct 2025</li><li>Experience-building phase set up at MEPC 71 (Jul 2017, MEPC.290(71)) → data analysis report MEPC 78 (2022) → Convention Review Plan MEPC 80 (2023) → list of provisions to amend endorsed MEPC 81 (Mar 2024)</li><li>MEPC 84 (27 Apr–1 May 2026): approved the draft amendments from the Correspondence Group on the review of the Convention; entry into force to be decided at MEPC 85 so they take effect together with the BWMS Code. Direction: ships must prove the BWMS is working, maintained and meeting D-2, not just installed and type-approved</li><li>MEPC 84 also dealt with the 2026 G4 Guidelines (ballast water management and BWM Plans; they replace the earlier G4) and approved a unified interpretation of Reg D-3 (to go into BWM.2/Circ.66): systems approved under the 2016 G8 Guidelines are treated as equal to the BWMS Code and may still be installed</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><ul><li>Annex Regulations (A-, B-, D-, E- series) + Appendix I (form of the International BWM Certificate) + Appendix II (form of the Ballast Water Record Book)</li><li>Key IMO guidelines: G1 sediment reception facilities · G2 sampling · G3 equivalent compliance · G4 BWM plans (MEPC.127(53), amended; 2026 revision at MEPC 84) · G5 reception facilities · G6 ballast water exchange (2017, MEPC.288(71)) · G7 risk assessment under A-4 (2017, MEPC.289(71)) · G8 BWMS approval (2016, MEPC.279(70); superseded by the BWMS Code Oct 2019) · G9 active substances (MEPC.169(57)) · G10 prototype technology (MEPC.140(54)) · G11 exchange design and construction standards (MEPC.149(55)) · G12 sediment control design (MEPC.209(63)) · G13 additional measures incl. emergency situations (MEPC.161(56)) · G14 designation of exchange areas (MEPC.151(55))</li></ul><hr><h2>Section A — General provisions</h2><ul><li>A-1 Definitions</li><li>A-3 <b>Exceptions</b> — the standards do not apply to: uptake or discharge needed for the ship's safety in an emergency · accidental discharge or ingress from damage to the ship or equipment · operations to avoid or minimise pollution from the ship · uptake and discharge of the same ballast water on the high seas · discharge at the place where all the ballast water originated, if no unmanaged water from elsewhere was mixed in</li><li>A-4 <b>Exemptions</b> — for ships on voyages between specified locations (or in a defined area); max 5 years; only if the ballast water is not mixed outside the named locations; based on a risk assessment under G7</li><li>A-5 Equivalent compliance — for pleasure craft used solely for recreation or competition, and craft used primarily for search and rescue (G3: craft &lt; 50 m long with max ballast capacity of 8 m³)</li></ul><h2>Section B — Management and control requirements for ships</h2><ul><li>B-1 <b>Ballast Water Management Plan</b> (specific to the ship)</li><li>B-2 <b>Ballast Water Record Book</b> (kept on board at least 2 years, then under company control at least 3 more years)</li><li>B-3 Implementation schedule: D-2 is tied to the IOPP renewal survey (renewal survey after 8 Sep 2019 → D-2 by that survey, depending on the previous survey); ships without an IOPP renewal survey: date set by the flag State, not later than 8 Sep 2024; ships built on or after 8 Sep 2017: D-2 from delivery</li><li>B-4 <b>Ballast water exchange</b> (D-1): at least 200 nm from the nearest land and in water ≥ 200 m deep; if impossible, at least 50 nm from land and ≥ 200 m deep</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bwm-exchange-distance\"><figcaption>Reg B-4: ballast water exchange at least 200 nm from the nearest land in water at least 200 m deep; if that is not possible, at least 50 nm from land and at least 200 m deep. Drawn from the Convention text.</figcaption></figure><h2>Section D — Standards</h2><ul><li><b>D-1 Exchange standard</b>: at least 95% volumetric exchange (typically by pumping through 3 × the tank volume)</li><li><b>D-2 Performance standard</b> (discharge):<ul><li>&lt; 10 viable organisms per m³ of size ≥ 50 µm</li><li>&lt; 10 viable organisms per mL of size 10 to &lt; 50 µm</li><li>Toxicogenic *Vibrio cholerae* (O1 and O139) &lt; 1 cfu per 100 mL (or &lt; 1 cfu per g wet weight of zooplankton)</li><li>*Escherichia coli* &lt; 250 cfu per 100 mL</li><li>Intestinal enterococci &lt; 100 cfu per 100 mL</li></ul></li><li>D-3 Approval of BWMS: BWMS Code; systems using Active Substances need approval by IMO (G9 procedure)</li></ul><h2>Section E — Survey and certification</h2><ul><li>Ships ≥ 400 GT: <b>International Ballast Water Management Certificate</b>, valid up to 5 years (shows the expiry date)</li><li>Ships &lt; 400 GT: no survey/certificate requirement, but they must carry a Ballast Water Management Plan and a Record Book</li><li>Art 9: port State control officers may verify that the ship has a valid certificate</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Plan and Record Book on board; certificate if ≥ 400 GT</blockquote><blockquote>D-2 is the standard; D-1 exchange only follows the B-4 distance and depth rules</blockquote><blockquote>PSC can verify the certificate</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IRClass — MEPC 84 report: https://www.irclass.org/media/8526/mepc-84-report.pdf</li><li>Türk Loydu — MEPC 84 key outcomes: https://www.turkloydu.org/en-us/publications/articles/imo-marine-environment-protection-committee-mepc-84-key-regulatory-outcomes/</li><li>DNV — mandatory BWMS commissioning test from 1 Jun 2022: https://www.dnv.com/news/2022/mandatory-bwms-commissioning-test-required-from-1-june-2022-225958/</li><li>IMO — BWM Convention and Guidelines (G1–G14, amendments, EBP): https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/BWMConventionandGuidelines.aspx</li><li>IMO — MEPC 81 summary: https://imo.org/en/MediaCentre/MeetingSummaries/Pages/MEPC-81.aspx</li><li>IMO — Implementing the BWM Convention (D-2 numbers): https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/implementing-the-bwm-convention.aspx</li><li>IMO — FAQ implementing BWM: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Documents/FAQ%20-%20Implementing%20the%20Ballast%20Water%20Management%20Convention.pdf</li><li>UK gov — control and management of ballast water (exceptions, exemptions): https://www.gov.uk/control-and-management-of-ballast-water</li><li>IMO — BWM Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-for-the-Control-and-Management-of-Ships'-Ballast-Water-and-Sediments-(BWM).aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution MEPC.300(72) BWMS Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MEPCDocuments/MEPC.300(72).pdf</li><li>Lloyd's Register — MEPC 84 summary report: https://www.lr.org/en/knowledge/regulatory-updates/imo-meetings-and-future-legislation/mepc-84-summary-report/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-bunkers-2001","v":34140687,"title":"Bunkers 2001","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["bunkers","bunker-oil","liability","insurance","certificate","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Bunkers 2001 — International Convention on Civil Liability for Bunker Oil Pollution Damage</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>23 Mar 2001</td></tr><tr><td>Opened for signature</td><td>1 Oct 2001 to 30 Sep 2002 (Art 12), then accession</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>21 Nov 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Entry-into-force rule</td><td>1 year after 18 States, including 5 with ships totalling at least 1 million GT (Art 14)</td></tr><tr><td>Liability</td><td>Strict, joint and several on all \"shipowners\" (Art 3)</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Ships over 1,000 GT (Art 7)</td></tr><tr><td>Time limits</td><td>3 years from the damage · 6 years from the incident (Art 8)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>19 Articles and an Annex (certificate model)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is Bunkers 2001</h2><ul><li>Compensation for people harmed by spills of <b>bunker oil</b>: oil carried as a ship's fuel</li><li>A standalone convention covering pollution damage only</li><li>Modelled on the 1969 Civil Liability Convention</li><li>Three tools: strict liability of the shipowner, compulsory insurance, and direct action against the insurer</li><li>Ships over 1,000 GT carry a certificate on board</li></ul><h2>Scope (Art 2)</h2><p>Applies only to:</p><ul><li>pollution damage in the territory, including the territorial sea, of a State Party, or in its exclusive economic zone (or equivalent zone up to 200 nautical miles from the baselines); and</li><li>preventive measures, <b>wherever taken</b>, to prevent or minimise such damage.</li></ul><h2>Key definitions (Art 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ship</td><td>Any seagoing vessel or seaborne craft, of any type</td></tr><tr><td>Shipowner</td><td>The owner (including the registered owner), bareboat charterer, manager and operator</td></tr><tr><td>Registered owner</td><td>The person registered as owner or, if unregistered, the owner. For a State ship run by a company registered as operator, that company</td></tr><tr><td>Bunker oil</td><td>Hydrocarbon mineral oil, including lubricating oil, used or intended for operating or propelling the ship, and its residues</td></tr><tr><td>Incident</td><td>An occurrence, or series with the same origin, that causes pollution damage or a grave and imminent threat of it</td></tr><tr><td>Preventive measures</td><td>Reasonable measures taken after an incident to prevent or minimise pollution damage</td></tr><tr><td>Pollution damage</td><td>(a) Loss or damage outside the ship from contamination by bunker oil escaping or discharged, wherever it occurs; environmental impairment compensation is limited to reasonable reinstatement costs. (b) The costs of preventive measures and further loss or damage they cause</td></tr><tr><td>Gross tonnage</td><td>Calculated under Annex I of the Tonnage Convention 1969</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Liability (Art 3)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bunkers-liability\"><figcaption>Who is liable, who can be sued and what insurance covers</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The shipowner at the time of the incident is liable for pollution damage from bunker oil on board or originating from the ship. For a series of occurrences, liability attaches to the owner at the first occurrence.</li><li>Where several persons are liable, liability is <b>joint and several</b>.</li><li>Claims against the shipowner may be made only under this Convention. The shipowner's independent rights of recourse are preserved.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>No liability if the shipowner proves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>War, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, or an exceptional, inevitable and irresistible natural phenomenon</td></tr><tr><td>Damage wholly caused by a third party's intentional act or omission</td></tr><tr><td>Damage wholly caused by the negligence or wrongful act of a government authority responsible for lights or navigational aids</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>If the claimant's intentional act or negligence caused the damage wholly or partly, the shipowner may be wholly or partly exonerated towards that claimant.</li><li><b>Two or more ships (Art 5):</b> all shipowners concerned (unless exonerated) are jointly and severally liable for damage that is not reasonably separable.</li></ul><h2>Exclusions (Art 4)</h2><ul><li>Damage that is \"pollution damage\" under the CLC, whether or not CLC compensation is payable</li><li>Warships, naval auxiliaries and other State-owned or operated ships used only on non-commercial government service. A State may elect to apply the Convention to them.</li><li>For State-owned ships used commercially, the State may be sued in the Art 9 courts and must waive defences based on its sovereign status</li></ul><h2>Limitation (Art 6)</h2><ul><li>The shipowner, insurer and security provider keep the right to limit liability under any applicable national or international regime, <b>such as the 1976 LLMC Convention, as amended</b>.</li></ul><h2>Compulsory insurance (Art 7)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td>The registered owner of a ship over 1,000 GT registered in a State Party</td></tr><tr><td>Cover</td><td>Insurance or other financial security (e.g. a bank guarantee) for pollution liability up to the applicable limitation amount, never more than the LLMC 1976 amount, as amended</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Issued after the State authority checks compliance. The registry State issues or certifies it; for an unregistered ship, any State Party may</td></tr><tr><td>Contents</td><td>Ship name, distinctive number or letters and port of registry · registered owner's name and principal place of business · <b>IMO number</b> · type and duration of security · insurer or guarantor's name and principal place of business · validity period, not longer than the security</td></tr><tr><td>Language</td><td>The issuing State's official language; if that is not English, French or Spanish, a translation into one of them is needed</td></tr><tr><td>On board</td><td><b>Carried on board</b>; a copy is deposited with the registry authorities</td></tr><tr><td>Early termination</td><td>Security does not count if it can end, other than by expiry, within 3 months of notice of termination to the authorities, unless the certificate is surrendered or replaced in that period</td></tr><tr><td>Recognised bodies</td><td>A State may authorise a recognised organisation to issue certificates, but remains fully responsible for them</td></tr><tr><td>Mutual acceptance</td><td>Certificates issued by one Party are accepted by the others with equal force</td></tr><tr><td>Flag State</td><td>Must not allow a ship under its flag to operate without a certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Port State</td><td>Each Party must ensure by national law that the cover is in force for any ship over 1,000 GT, wherever registered, entering or leaving its ports or an offshore facility in its territorial sea</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic records</td><td>A State may notify that ships need not carry or produce the certificate in its ports, if the issuing State keeps electronic records open to all Parties</td></tr><tr><td>State-owned ships</td><td>No insurance needed, but the ship carries a registry-State certificate stating the ship is State-owned and liability is covered within the limit</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic trade</td><td>A State may declare Art 7 not applicable to ships operating only in its own territory and territorial sea</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Direct action (Art 7(10)):</b></p><ul><li>Claimants may sue the insurer or security provider directly.</li><li>The insurer may use the shipowner's defences (except bankruptcy or winding up), including Art 6 limitation, and the shipowner's <b>wilful misconduct</b>. No other defences.</li><li>Even if the owner cannot limit, the insurer may limit to the required insurance amount.</li><li>The insurer may require the shipowner to be joined.</li></ul><h2>Time limits (Art 8)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"bunkers-timebar\"><figcaption>Action within 3 years of the damage, and never later than 6 years after the incident</figcaption></figure><h2>Jurisdiction and judgments (Arts 9–10)</h2><ul><li>Actions against the shipowner, insurer or security provider may be brought <b>only in the courts of States Parties</b> where the incident caused damage (territory, territorial sea or EEZ area), or where preventive measures were taken.</li><li>Each defendant must get reasonable notice. Each Party must ensure its courts can hear such claims.</li><li>A final, enforceable judgment from such a court is recognised in every Party, unless it was obtained by fraud or the defendant had no reasonable notice and fair chance to present its case. The merits cannot be reopened.</li></ul><h2>Final clauses</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11</td><td>Supersession: replaces earlier conventions only where they conflict</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval, accession</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>States with more than one legal system</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Denunciation: effective 1 year after deposit, or any longer period stated</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Revision or amendment: IMO convenes a conference at the request of one-third of the Parties</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Depositary: the IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Transmission to the United Nations</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Languages: Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian, Spanish</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Ships over 1,000 GT carry the Bunkers certificate on board, and PSC checks it. The shipowner is strictly liable and the insurer can be sued directly. Claims are barred 3 years after the damage, and never later than 6 years after the incident. Cargo oil from tankers falls under the CLC, not Bunkers 2001.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Treaty text (Australian Parliament, JSCT): https://aphref.aph.gov.au/house/committee/jsct/28march2006/treaties/bunkers_text.pdf</li><li>IMO — Bunkers Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-Civil-Liability-for-Bunker-Oil-Pollution-Damage-(BUNKER).aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-clc-and-fund-1992","v":118029072,"title":"CLC and Fund 1992","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["clc","fund","iopc","oil-pollution","liability","compensation","tanker","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>CLC and Fund 1992 — Civil Liability Convention and Fund Convention for Oil Pollution Damage (with the 2003 Supplementary Fund)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Pays</th><th>Limit per incident (SDR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 — CLC 1992</td><td>Shipowner (insured)</td><td>4.51 million up to 5,000 GT; +631 per GT above; max 89.77 million</td></tr><tr><td>2 — Fund 1992</td><td>Oil receivers</td><td>203 million in total (including CLC)</td></tr><tr><td>3 — Supplementary Fund 2003</td><td>Oil receivers (optional)</td><td>750 million in total</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is CLC / Fund</h2><ul><li><b>CLC 1992</b>: International Convention on Civil Liability for Oil Pollution Damage — the shipowner is liable for pollution damage, with compulsory insurance (tier 1)</li><li><b>Fund 1992</b>: International Convention on the Establishment of an International Fund for Compensation for Oil Pollution Damage — pays where full compensation cannot be obtained under the CLC (tier 2); financed by contributions from receivers of oil</li><li><b>Supplementary Fund Protocol 2003</b>: optional third tier</li><li>Covers pollution damage from <b>persistent oil</b> carried by ships constructed or adapted to carry oil in bulk — laden or unladen, including the bunker oil of such ships</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>CLC 1969: adopted 29 Nov 1969 · in force 19 Jun 1975</li><li>Fund 1971: adopted 18 Dec 1971 · in force 16 Oct 1978</li><li>1992 Protocols (CLC and Fund): adopted 27 Nov 1992 · in force 30 May 1996<ul><li>Extended coverage to the EEZ · preventive measures covered even without a spill, where there is a grave and imminent threat of pollution damage · environmental damage limited to the reasonable cost of reinstatement</li><li>States Parties to the 1992 CLC ceased to be Parties to CLC 1969 from 16 May 1998</li><li>CLC 1992 entry into force: 12 months after 10 States, including four each with at least 1 million units of gross tanker tonnage, deposited instruments (Protocol Art 13)</li></ul></li><li>Original 1992 limits: CLC 3 million SDR (up to 5,000 GT) + 420 SDR per extra GT, max 59.7 million SDR · Fund max 135 million SDR per incident (including CLC)</li><li>2000 amendments raised the limits by about 50% (see below) · apply to incidents on or after 1 Nov 2003</li><li>2000 Protocol (adopted 27 Sep 2000, in force 27 Jun 2001) to end the Fund 1971 · Fund 1971 ceased to be in force 24 May 2002</li><li>Supplementary Fund Protocol: adopted 16 May 2003 · in force 3 Mar 2005</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>Pollution damage in the territory, territorial sea and EEZ of a Contracting State, and preventive measures wherever taken (CLC Art II)</li><li>\"Oil\" = any persistent hydrocarbon mineral oil such as crude oil, fuel oil, heavy diesel oil and lubricating oil, whether carried as cargo or in the bunkers of such a ship (CLC Art I)</li></ul><hr><h2>CLC 1992 — Tier 1 (shipowner)</h2><ul><li>The owner is liable for pollution damage caused by the ship as a result of the incident (Art III)</li><li>Exceptions: act of war or hostilities (and similar) · wholly caused by a third party's act done with intent to cause damage · wholly caused by the negligence or wrongful act of a government or authority responsible for maintaining lights or navigational aids</li><li>Channelling: no claim against the owner except under the Convention; no claim against listed persons such as crew, charterers and salvors, unless the damage resulted from their personal act or omission with intent, or recklessly and with knowledge that damage would probably result</li><li><b>Limits (incidents from 1 Nov 2003)</b>:<ul><li>Ship up to 5,000 GT: <b>4.51 million SDR</b></li><li>Above 5,000 GT: 4.51 million + <b>631 SDR per additional GT</b></li><li>Maximum: <b>89.77 million SDR</b></li></ul></li><li>The owner loses the right to limit if the damage resulted from his personal act or omission, committed with intent to cause such damage, or recklessly and with knowledge that such damage would probably result (Art V(2))</li><li><b>Compulsory insurance</b> for ships carrying <b>more than 2,000 tons of oil in bulk as cargo</b> (Art VII) · claims can be brought <b>directly against the insurer</b> or other provider of financial security (Art VII(8))</li><li>Time limits (Art VIII): action within 3 years from the date the damage occurred; never later than 6 years from the incident</li></ul><h2>Fund 1992 — Tier 2 (IOPC Fund)</h2><ul><li>Aim (Art 2): compensation to the extent that the protection under the CLC is inadequate</li><li>Pays any person suffering pollution damage where full compensation cannot be obtained under the CLC (Art 4)</li><li>Maximum per incident (including CLC): <b>203 million SDR</b>; a higher cap of 300.74 million SDR applies in certain conditions set in the Convention</li><li>Contributions (Art 10): paid by persons who received in total more than 150,000 tons of contributing oil (crude oil and fuel oil received after sea transport)</li></ul><h2>Supplementary Fund 2003 — Tier 3</h2><ul><li>Maximum payable for any one incident: <b>750 million SDR</b>, including amounts paid under the 1992 CLC and Fund</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"clc-fund-tiers\"><figcaption>Three tiers per incident in million SDR: CLC owner limit up to 89.77, Fund 1992 total 203, Supplementary Fund 2003 total 750. Bar lengths are to scale.</figcaption></figure><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Tankers carrying more than 2,000 tons of oil in bulk as cargo must have CLC insurance; the CLC covers the tanker's bunker oil as well as cargo oil</blockquote><blockquote>Three tiers per incident: CLC owner limit (max 89.77 million SDR) → Fund 1992 (203 million SDR total) → Supplementary Fund (750 million SDR total)</blockquote><blockquote>Persistent oil (crude, fuel oil, heavy diesel, lubricating oil) is covered</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IOPC Funds — Text of the Conventions: https://iopcfunds.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Text-of-Conventions_e.pdf</li><li>IMO — CLC: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-civil-liability-for-oil-pollution-damage-(clc).aspx</li><li>IMO — Fund: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-the-establishment-of-an-international-fund-for-compensation-for-oil-pollution-damage-%28fund%29.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-colreg-1972","v":133810552,"title":"COLREG 1972","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["colreg","rules-of-the-road","collision","lights","sound-signals","watchkeeping","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>COLREG 1972 — Convention on the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Done at London</td><td>20 Oct 1972</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>15 Jul 1977</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Articles I–IX · Rules 1–41 · Annexes I–IV</td></tr><tr><td>Parts</td><td>A General · B Steering and Sailing · C Lights and Shapes · D Sound and Light Signals · E Exemptions</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is COLREG</h2><ul><li>The \"rules of the road\" at sea: look-out, safe speed, risk of collision, action to avoid collision, steering and sailing rules, lights and shapes, sound and light signals, distress signals</li><li>The Convention (Articles I–IX) obliges Parties to give effect to the International Regulations (Rules 1–41 + Annexes I–IV) (Art I)</li><li>On entry into force the Regulations replaced the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, 1960 (Art IV(5))</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Done at London 20 Oct 1972 · in force <b>15 Jul 1977</b></li><li>Entry into force (Art IV): 12 months after at least 15 States whose merchant fleets make up at least 65% of the world fleet (vessels of 100 GT and over) became Parties</li><li>Art II: open for signature until 1 Jun 1973, then accession</li><li>Amendments (Art VI): proposed → two-thirds majority in the Maritime Safety Committee → two-thirds majority in the Assembly → in force on the date set by the Assembly unless more than one-third of Parties object (tacit acceptance)</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1981</td><td>A.464(12)</td><td>19 Nov 1981</td><td>1 Jun 1983</td></tr><tr><td>1987</td><td>A.626(15)</td><td>19 Nov 1987</td><td>19 Nov 1989</td></tr><tr><td>1989</td><td>A.678(16)</td><td>19 Oct 1989</td><td>19 Apr 1991</td></tr><tr><td>1993</td><td>A.736(18)</td><td>4 Nov 1993</td><td>4 Nov 1995</td></tr><tr><td>2001</td><td>A.910(22)</td><td>29 Nov 2001</td><td>29 Nov 2003</td></tr><tr><td>2007</td><td>A.1004(25)</td><td>29 Nov 2007</td><td>1 Dec 2009</td></tr><tr><td>2013</td><td>A.1085(28)</td><td>4 Dec 2013</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>1981: many rules, incl. Rules 1(c), 3(g), 10, 13(a), 22, 23, 24, 25, 27, 29, 30, 33, 34, 35, 36, 37, 38 and Annexes I and III</li><li>1987: Rules 1(e), 3(h), new 8(f), 10(a) and (c); Annex I; Annex IV new para 1(o)</li><li>1989: Rule 10(d) · 1993: Rule 26 (fishing vessel lights), Annexes I, II and IV</li><li>2001: Rule 3(a) and (m), Rule 8(a), Rule 18(f), Rule 23(c), Rule 31, Rule 33(a), Rule 35(i) (wing-in-ground craft), Annex I Sec 13, Annex III</li><li>2007: Annex IV (distress signals) revised</li><li>2013: new <b>Part F, Rules 39–41</b> (verification of compliance)</li><li>Note: the UK MCA amendment table gives 10 Dec 2013 for the 2013 amendments; the IMO resolution A.1085(28) itself states 4 Dec 2013</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>All vessels on the high seas and in all waters connected therewith navigable by seagoing vessels (Rule 1(a))</li><li>Rule 1(b): special rules for roadsteads, harbours, rivers, lakes or inland waterways connected with the high seas must conform as closely as possible to these Rules</li><li>Rule 1(c): a State may make special rules for additional station or signal lights, shapes or whistle signals for warships, vessels under convoy, fishing vessels fishing in a fleet — they must not be mistaken for any signal in the Rules</li><li>Rule 1(d): traffic separation schemes may be adopted by IMO</li><li>Rule 1(e): a vessel of special construction or purpose may comply as closely as practicable with the Rules on lights, shapes and signals, as determined by its Government</li></ul><hr><h2>Part A — General (Rules 1–3)</h2><ul><li>Rule 1 Application (above)</li><li>Rule 2 <b>Responsibility</b><ul><li>(a) nothing exonerates a vessel, owner, master or crew from the consequences of neglect to comply with the Rules or neglect of any precaution required by the ordinary practice of seamen or the special circumstances</li><li>(b) due regard to all dangers of navigation and collision and any special circumstances, including the limitations of the vessels involved, which may make a departure necessary to avoid <b>immediate danger</b></li></ul></li><li>Rule 3 General definitions:<ul><li>(a) vessel: every description of water craft, including non-displacement craft, WIG craft and seaplanes, used or capable of being used as a means of transportation on water</li><li>(b) power-driven vessel: propelled by machinery · (c) sailing vessel: under sail provided propelling machinery, if fitted, is not being used</li><li>(d) vessel engaged in fishing: fishing with nets, lines, trawls or other apparatus which restrict manoeuvrability (not trolling lines or other gear that do not restrict manoeuvrability)</li><li>(e) seaplane: any aircraft designed to manoeuvre on the water</li><li>(f) vessel not under command: through some exceptional circumstance unable to manoeuvre as required by the Rules because of some exceptional circumstance</li><li>(g) vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre: restricted by the nature of her work — includes vessels laying, servicing or picking up a navigation mark, submarine cable or pipeline; dredging, surveying or underwater operations; replenishing or transferring persons, provisions or cargo while underway; launching or recovering aircraft; mine clearance; towing operations that severely restrict the towing vessel and tow in their ability to deviate from their course</li><li>(h) vessel constrained by her draught: a power-driven vessel which, because of her draught in relation to the available depth and width of navigable water, is severely restricted in her ability to deviate from the course she is following</li><li>(i) underway: not at anchor, or made fast to the shore, or aground</li><li>(j) length and breadth: length overall and greatest breadth</li><li>(k) in sight of one another: only when one can be observed visually from the other</li></ul></li></ul><h2>Part B — Steering and sailing rules</h2><ul><li><b>Section I — Conduct of vessels in any condition of visibility (Rules 4–10)</b><ul><li>Rule 4 Application: Rules in Section I apply in any condition of visibility</li><li>Rule 5 <b>Look-out</b>: at all times by sight and hearing as well as by all available means appropriate in the prevailing circumstances and conditions, so as to make a full appraisal of the situation and of the risk of collision</li><li>Rule 6 <b>Safe speed</b>: at all times, so that proper and effective action can be taken to avoid collision and the vessel can be stopped within a distance appropriate to the circumstances<ul><li>(a) all vessels: visibility · traffic density · manoeuvrability (stopping distance and turning ability) · at night, background light · state of wind, sea and current, and proximity of navigational hazards · draught in relation to available depth of water</li><li>(b) vessels with operational radar: characteristics, efficiency and limitations of the radar · limitations imposed by the radar range scale in use · effect on radar detection of sea state, weather and other sources of interference · possibility that small vessels, ice and other floating objects may not be detected at adequate range · number, location and movement of vessels detected · more exact assessment of visibility possible when radar is used to determine the range of vessels or other objects in the vicinity</li></ul></li><li>Rule 7 <b>Risk of collision</b><ul><li>(a) all available means appropriate to the circumstances; if there is any doubt, such risk shall be deemed to exist</li><li>(b) proper use of radar equipment if fitted and operational, including long-range scanning to obtain early warning, and radar plotting or equivalent systematic observation of detected objects</li><li>(c) no assumptions on the basis of scanty information, especially scanty radar information</li><li>(d) in determining whether risk exists: (i) such risk shall be deemed to exist if the compass bearing of an approaching vessel does not appreciably change; (ii) such risk may sometimes exist even when an appreciable bearing change is evident, particularly when approaching a very large vessel or a tow or at close range</li></ul></li><li>Rule 8 <b>Action to avoid collision</b><ul><li>(a) if the circumstances of the case admit, positive, made in ample time and with due regard to the observance of good seamanship (and, since 2001, in compliance with the other Rules of Part B)</li><li>(b) any alteration of course and/or speed large enough to be readily apparent to another vessel observing visually or by radar; avoid a succession of small alterations</li><li>(c) if there is sufficient sea room, alteration of course alone may be the most effective action, provided it is made in good time, is substantial and does not result in another close-quarters situation</li><li>(d) action should result in passing at a safe distance; its effectiveness shall be carefully checked until the other vessel is finally past and clear</li><li>(e) if necessary to avoid collision or allow more time to assess the situation, slacken speed or take all way off by stopping or reversing the means of propulsion</li><li>(f) (i) a vessel required by these Rules not to impede the passage or safe passage of another vessel shall, when required by the circumstances of the case, take early action to allow sufficient sea room for the safe passage of the other vessel; (ii) this vessel is not relieved of the obligation if approaching so as to involve risk of collision, and shall have full regard to the actions which may be required by the Rules of Part B; (iii) when her passage is not to be impeded, the vessel remains fully obliged to comply with the Rules of Part B when the two vessels are approaching one another so as to involve risk of collision</li></ul></li><li>Rule 9 <b>Narrow channels</b><ul><li>(a) keep as near to the outer limit of the channel or fairway which lies on her starboard side as is safe and practicable</li><li>(b) a vessel of less than 20 m or a sailing vessel shall not impede the passage of a vessel which can safely navigate only within a narrow channel or fairway</li><li>(c) a vessel engaged in fishing shall not impede the passage of any other vessel navigating within a narrow channel or fairway</li><li>(d) shall not cross a narrow channel or fairway if such crossing impedes a vessel which can safely navigate only within it; the latter may use the Rule 34(d) doubt signal if in doubt about the intention of the crossing vessel</li><li>(e) overtaking: (i) where overtaking can take place only if the vessel to be overtaken has to take action to permit safe passing, the overtaking vessel indicates her intention by the appropriate signal of Rule 34(c)(i); the vessel to be overtaken, if in agreement, sounds the Rule 34(c)(ii) signal and takes steps to permit safe passing; if in doubt, she may sound the Rule 34(d) signals; (ii) this Rule does not relieve the overtaking vessel of her obligation under Rule 13</li><li>(f) a vessel nearing a bend or an area of a channel or fairway where other vessels may be obscured shall navigate with particular alertness and caution and sound the appropriate Rule 34(e) signal</li><li>(g) any vessel shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, avoid anchoring in a narrow channel</li></ul></li><li>Rule 10 <b>Traffic separation schemes</b><ul><li>(a) applies to schemes adopted by IMO; does not relieve any vessel of her obligation under any other Rule</li><li>(b) a vessel using a scheme shall: (i) proceed in the appropriate traffic lane in the general direction of traffic flow for that lane; (ii) so far as practicable keep clear of a traffic separation line or separation zone; (iii) normally join or leave a lane at the termination of the lane, but when joining or leaving from either side shall do so at as small an angle to the general direction of traffic flow as practicable</li><li>(c) so far as practicable avoid crossing traffic lanes, but if obliged to do so shall cross as nearly as practicable at right angles to the general direction of traffic flow</li><li>(d) (i) a vessel shall not use an inshore traffic zone when she can safely use the appropriate lane within the adjacent scheme; vessels of less than 20 m, sailing vessels and vessels engaged in fishing may use the inshore traffic zone at all times; (ii) notwithstanding (d)(i), a vessel may use an inshore traffic zone when en route to or from a port, offshore installation or structure, pilot station or any other place within the inshore traffic zone, or to avoid immediate danger</li><li>(e) a vessel other than a crossing vessel or one joining or leaving a lane shall not normally enter a separation zone or cross a separation line except in cases of emergency to avoid immediate danger, or to engage in fishing within a separation zone</li><li>(f) a vessel navigating in areas near the terminations of schemes shall do so with particular caution</li><li>(g) a vessel shall so far as practicable avoid anchoring in a traffic separation scheme or in areas near its terminations</li><li>(h) a vessel not using a scheme shall avoid it by as wide a margin as is practicable</li><li>(i) a vessel engaged in fishing shall not impede the passage of any vessel following a traffic lane</li><li>(j) a vessel of less than 20 m or a sailing vessel shall not impede the safe passage of a power-driven vessel following a traffic lane</li><li>(k) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre when engaged in an operation for the maintenance of safety of navigation in a traffic separation scheme is exempted from complying with this Rule to the extent necessary to carry out the operation</li><li>(l) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre when engaged in an operation for the laying, servicing or picking up of a submarine cable, within a traffic separation scheme, is exempted from complying with this Rule to the extent necessary to carry out the operation</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><b>Section II — Conduct of vessels in sight of one another (Rules 11–18)</b><ul><li>Rule 11 Application: vessels in sight of one another</li><li>Rule 12 <b>Sailing vessels</b> (risk of collision): (i) wind on different sides → the vessel with the wind on her port side keeps out of the way; (ii) wind on the same side → the windward vessel keeps out of the way of the leeward vessel; (iii) a vessel with the wind on the port side that sees a vessel to windward and cannot determine whether the other has the wind on the port or starboard side keeps out of the way; windward side = the side opposite to that on which the mainsail is carried (square-rigged: opposite the largest fore-and-aft sail)</li><li>Rule 13 <b>Overtaking</b><ul><li>(a) any vessel overtaking any other shall keep out of the way of the vessel being overtaken, notwithstanding anything in Sections I and II</li><li>(b) a vessel is overtaking when coming up with another from a direction more than <b>22.5 degrees abaft her beam</b>, i.e. in such a position that at night she would be able to see only the sternlight of that vessel but neither of her sidelights</li><li>(c) when in doubt whether she is overtaking, a vessel shall assume that this is the case and act accordingly</li><li>(d) any subsequent alteration of the bearing does not make the overtaking vessel a crossing vessel or relieve her of the duty to keep clear until finally past and clear</li></ul></li><li>Rule 14 <b>Head-on</b>: two power-driven vessels meeting on reciprocal or nearly reciprocal courses so as to involve risk of collision each alters course to <b>starboard</b> so that each passes on the port side of the other; exists when a vessel sees the other ahead or nearly ahead (at night: both masthead lights in a line or nearly in a line and/or both sidelights; by day: the corresponding aspect); in doubt, assume it exists</li><li>Rule 15 <b>Crossing</b>: two power-driven vessels crossing so as to involve risk of collision — the vessel which has the other on her own starboard side shall keep out of the way and, if the circumstances admit, avoid crossing ahead of the other vessel</li><li>Rule 16 <b>Give-way vessel</b>: take early and substantial action to keep well clear</li><li>Rule 17 <b>Stand-on vessel</b><ul><li>(a)(i) keep her course and speed · (ii) may take action to avoid collision by her manoeuvre alone, as soon as it becomes apparent that the vessel required to keep out of the way is not taking appropriate action</li><li>(b) when so close that collision cannot be avoided by the action of the give-way vessel alone, shall take such action as will best aid to avoid collision</li><li>(c) a power-driven vessel taking action in a crossing situation under (a)(ii) shall, if the circumstances admit, not alter course to port for a vessel on her own port side</li><li>(d) does not relieve the give-way vessel of her obligation to keep out of the way</li></ul></li><li>Rule 18 <b>Responsibilities between vessels</b> (except Rules 9, 10 and 13)<ul><li>(a) a power-driven vessel underway keeps out of the way of: not under command · restricted in ability to manoeuvre · engaged in fishing · a sailing vessel</li><li>(b) a sailing vessel underway keeps out of the way of: not under command · restricted in ability to manoeuvre · engaged in fishing</li><li>(c) a vessel engaged in fishing, when underway, shall so far as possible keep out of the way of: not under command · restricted in ability to manoeuvre</li><li>(d) (i) any vessel other than one not under command or restricted in her ability to manoeuvre shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, avoid impeding the safe passage of a vessel constrained by her draught, exhibiting the Rule 28 signals; (ii) a vessel constrained by her draught shall navigate with particular caution having full regard to her special condition</li><li>(e) a seaplane on the water shall in general keep well clear of all vessels and avoid impeding their navigation; where risk of collision exists she shall comply with the Rules of this Part</li><li>(f) a WIG craft: (i) when taking off, landing and in flight near the surface shall keep well clear of all other vessels and avoid impeding their navigation; (ii) when operating on the water surface shall comply with the Rules of this Part as a power-driven vessel</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><b>Section III — Conduct of vessels in restricted visibility (Rule 19)</b><ul><li>(a) applies to vessels not in sight of one another when navigating in or near an area of restricted visibility</li><li>(b) every vessel proceeds at a safe speed adapted to the prevailing circumstances and conditions of restricted visibility; a power-driven vessel shall have her engines ready for immediate manoeuvre</li><li>(c) due regard to the prevailing circumstances and conditions of restricted visibility when complying with Section I</li><li>(d) a vessel which detects by radar alone the presence of another vessel shall determine if a close-quarters situation is developing and/or risk of collision exists; if so, she shall take avoiding action in ample time; if the action consists of an alteration of course, so far as possible avoid: (i) an alteration of course to port for a vessel forward of the beam, other than for a vessel being overtaken; (ii) an alteration of course towards a vessel abeam or abaft the beam</li><li>(e) except where it has been determined that risk of collision does not exist, every vessel which hears apparently forward of her beam the fog signal of another vessel, or which cannot avoid a close-quarters situation with another vessel forward of her beam, shall reduce her speed to the minimum at which she can be kept on her course; she shall if necessary take all her way off and in any event navigate with extreme caution until danger of collision is over</li><li>There is no stand-on/give-way role between vessels not in sight of one another — each vessel acts under Rule 19</li></ul></li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"colreg-situations\"><figcaption>Rule 13 overtaking (more than 22.5 degrees abaft the beam, the overtaking vessel keeps clear), Rule 14 head-on (both alter to starboard, pass port to port), Rule 15 crossing (the vessel with the other on her starboard side gives way).</figcaption></figure><h2>Part C — Lights and shapes (Rules 20–31)</h2><ul><li>Rule 20 Application: in all weathers; lights from sunset to sunrise (no other lights unless they cannot be mistaken for the prescribed lights and do not impair them); prescribed lights, if carried, also from sunrise to sunset in restricted visibility, and may be shown in all other circumstances when considered necessary; shapes by day; lights and shapes must comply with Annex I</li><li>Rule 21 Definitions:<ul><li>masthead light: white, over the fore-and-aft centreline, <b>225°</b> arc, from right ahead to 22.5° abaft the beam on either side</li><li>sidelights: green on starboard and red on port, each <b>112.5°</b> arc, from right ahead to 22.5° abaft the beam on its side; in a vessel of less than 20 m the sidelights may be combined in one lantern on the centreline</li><li>sternlight: white, as near the stern as practicable, <b>135°</b> arc, 67.5° from right aft on each side</li><li>towing light: yellow, same characteristics as the sternlight · all-round light: 360° · flashing light: flashes at regular intervals at a frequency of <b>120 flashes or more per minute</b></li></ul></li><li>Rule 22 <b>Visibility of lights</b> (minimum ranges)<ul><li>Vessels of <b>50 m or more</b>: masthead 6 nm · sidelight 3 nm · sternlight 3 nm · towing light 3 nm · white, red, green or yellow all-round light 3 nm</li><li>Vessels of <b>12 m or more but less than 50 m</b>: masthead 5 nm (3 nm if less than 20 m) · sidelight, sternlight, towing and all-round lights 2 nm</li><li>Vessels of <b>less than 12 m</b>: masthead 2 nm · sidelight 1 nm · sternlight, towing and all-round lights 2 nm</li><li>Inconspicuous, partly submerged vessels or objects being towed: white all-round light 3 nm</li></ul></li><li>Rule 23 <b>Power-driven vessels underway</b>: masthead light forward · second masthead light abaft and higher than the forward one (vessels of less than 50 m need not, but may) · sidelights · sternlight; air-cushion vessels in non-displacement mode also an all-round flashing yellow light; WIG craft only when taking off, landing and in flight near the surface also a high-intensity all-round flashing red light; vessels of less than 12 m may show an all-round white light and sidelights instead; vessels of less than 7 m whose maximum speed does not exceed 7 knots may show an all-round white light and, if practicable, sidelights</li><li>Rule 24 <b>Towing and pushing</b><ul><li>Towing astern: two masthead lights in a vertical line (<b>three if the tow exceeds 200 m</b>, measured from the stern of the towing vessel) · sidelights · sternlight · towing light above the sternlight · a diamond shape where best seen if the tow exceeds 200 m</li><li>Pushing ahead or towing alongside (not a composite unit): two masthead lights in a vertical line, sidelights, sternlight</li><li>A pusher and pushed vessel rigidly connected as a composite unit are regarded as a power-driven vessel (Rule 23 lights)</li><li>Towing vessel also shows the Rule 23(a)(ii) light (second masthead light)</li><li>Other towed vessel or object: sidelights, sternlight and (if the tow exceeds 200 m) a diamond shape</li><li>Inconspicuous, partly submerged tow: all-round white lights at or near each end (two more at the extremities if 25 m wide or more; additional white lights not more than 100 m apart if over 100 m long); a diamond at or near the aftermost extremity (and another forward if the tow exceeds 200 m)</li><li>A vessel not normally engaged in towing, towing a vessel in distress, need not show the towing lights, but must take all possible measures to indicate the relationship (e.g. by illuminating the tow line)</li></ul></li><li>Rule 25 <b>Sailing vessels underway and vessels under oars</b>: sidelights and sternlight (under 20 m may combine in one lantern at or near the masthead) · optional at or near the top of the mast two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>red over green</b> (not with the combined lantern) · under 7 m: show the lights if practicable, otherwise have ready a white light (torch or lantern) · sailing vessel also using machinery: a cone, apex downwards, forward</li><li>Rule 26 <b>Fishing vessels</b> (underway or at anchor)<ul><li>Trawling: two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>green over white</b> (or a shape of two cones apex to apex); masthead light abaft and higher than the green light (optional under 50 m); sidelights and sternlight when making way</li><li>Fishing other than trawling: two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>red over white</b> (or two cones apex to apex); if outlying gear extends more than <b>150 m</b> horizontally, an all-round white light or a cone apex upward in the direction of the gear; sidelights and sternlight when making way</li><li>Annex II signals for fishing vessels fishing in close proximity · a vessel not fishing shows the lights for her length</li></ul></li><li>Rule 27 <b>Not under command / restricted in ability to manoeuvre</b><ul><li>Not under command: two all-round red lights in a vertical line (two balls); sidelights and sternlight when making way</li><li>Restricted in ability to manoeuvre (except mine clearance): three all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>red–white–red</b> (shapes: <b>ball–diamond–ball</b>); masthead lights, sidelights, sternlight when making way; at anchor also the Rule 30 signals</li><li>A towing vessel severely restricted shows the Rule 24(a) lights and the red–white–red lights / ball–diamond–ball shapes</li><li>Dredging or underwater operations: red–white–red lights; if an obstruction exists, two all-round red lights (or two balls) on the side of the obstruction and two all-round green lights (or two diamonds) on the side on which another vessel may pass</li></ul></li><li>Rule 28 <b>Vessels constrained by their draught</b>: may show three all-round red lights in a vertical line (or a cylinder) in addition to the Rule 23 lights</li><li>Rule 29 <b>Pilot vessels</b> on pilotage duty: two all-round lights in a vertical line, <b>white over red</b>; sidelights and sternlight when underway; anchor lights/shape when at anchor</li><li>Rule 30 <b>Anchored vessels and vessels aground</b>: all-round white light or one ball in the fore part, and an all-round white light at or near the stern lower than the forward one (a vessel of less than 50 m may show only one all-round white light); working lights may be used; vessels of <b>100 m or more must</b> illuminate their decks; aground: anchor lights plus two all-round red lights in a vertical line (shapes: three balls in a vertical line); vessels under 12 m aground need not show the red lights or balls; vessels under 7 m at anchor not in or near a narrow channel, fairway, anchorage or where other vessels normally navigate need not show anchor lights</li><li>Rule 31 <b>Seaplanes and WIG craft</b> that cannot show lights and shapes with the characteristics or positions prescribed show lights and shapes as closely similar as possible</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"colreg-light-arcs\"><figcaption>Rule 21 light arcs: masthead light white 225 degrees, sidelights green starboard and red port 112.5 degrees each, sternlight white 135 degrees.</figcaption></figure><h2>Part D — Sound and light signals (Rules 32–37)</h2><ul><li>Rule 32 Definitions: <b>short blast</b> about 1 second · <b>prolonged blast</b> 4–6 seconds</li><li>Rule 33 Equipment: vessels of 12 m or more — whistle · 20 m or more — whistle and bell · 100 m or more — whistle, bell and gong (tone and sound not to be confused with the bell) · under 12 m — not obliged, but must have some other means of making an efficient sound signal</li><li>Rule 34 <b>Manoeuvring and warning signals</b><ul><li>(a) in sight, power-driven vessel underway manoeuvring: <b>1 short blast</b> = I am altering my course to starboard · <b>2 short</b> = to port · <b>3 short</b> = my engines are going astern</li><li>(b) optional light signals: one, two or three flashes with the same meanings; flash about 1 second, interval about 1 second, at least 10 seconds between signals; all-round white light visible at least 5 nm</li><li>(c) in a narrow channel or fairway: <b>2 prolonged + 1 short</b> = I intend to overtake on your starboard side · <b>2 prolonged + 2 short</b> = on your port side · agreement: <b>prolonged–short–prolonged–short</b></li><li>(d) in doubt about the other vessel's intentions or actions: <b>at least 5 short and rapid blasts</b> (may be supplemented by at least 5 short and rapid flashes)</li><li>(e) nearing a bend or area where other vessels may be obscured: <b>1 prolonged blast</b>, answered by 1 prolonged blast by any approaching vessel within hearing</li><li>(f) if whistles are more than 100 m apart, only one is used for manoeuvring and warning signals</li></ul></li><li>Rule 35 <b>Sound signals in restricted visibility</b> <ul><li>Power-driven vessel making way: <b>1 prolonged blast</b> at intervals of not more than 2 minutes</li><li>Power-driven vessel underway but stopped, making no way: <b>2 prolonged blasts</b> (about 2 s between) at intervals of not more than 2 minutes</li><li>Not under command, restricted in ability to manoeuvre, constrained by draught, sailing, fishing, towing or pushing: <b>1 prolonged + 2 short</b> at intervals of not more than 2 minutes</li><li>A vessel towed (or the last vessel of a tow, if manned): <b>1 prolonged + 3 short</b>, if possible immediately after the towing vessel's signal</li><li>At anchor: rapid ringing of the bell for about <b>5 seconds</b> at intervals of not more than <b>1 minute</b>; vessel of <b>100 m or more</b> also gong sounded rapidly aft for about 5 seconds; may add <b>1 short + 1 prolonged + 1 short</b></li><li>Aground: bell (and gong) as at anchor plus <b>3 separate and distinct strokes on the bell</b> immediately before and after the rapid ringing</li><li>Vessels of 12 m to under 20 m are not obliged to give the bell signals but must give another efficient sound signal at intervals of not more than 2 minutes · vessels under 12 m likewise</li><li>Pilot vessel on pilotage duty may also sound an identity signal of <b>4 short blasts</b></li></ul></li><li>Rule 36 Signals to attract attention: any vessel may make light or sound signals, or direct the beam of a searchlight in the direction of the danger, not mistakable for any authorized signal or navigational aid; avoid high-intensity intermittent or revolving lights (e.g. strobe lights)</li><li>Rule 37 <b>Distress signals</b>: a vessel in distress and requiring assistance uses or exhibits the signals in Annex IV</li></ul><p><b>Part E — Exemptions (Rule 38)</b>: vessels whose keel was laid before the Regulations entered into force (15 Jul 1977) — exempt for a period for lights ranges (Rule 22) and colours (Annex I Sec 7) (4 years), masthead light repositioning (Annex I Sec 3(a), Sec 2(b)), sidelights (Sec 2(g), 3(b)) and sound appliances (Annex III) (9 years); some metric repositioning of lights is permanently exempt</p><p><b>Part F — Verification of compliance (Rules 39–41)</b> (in force 1 Jan 2016, A.1085(28))</p><ul><li>Rule 39 Definitions: Audit · Audit Scheme (IMO Member State Audit Scheme) · Code for Implementation (III Code, A.1070(28)) · Audit Standard</li><li>Rule 40 Application: Parties use the III Code to discharge their obligations under the Convention</li><li>Rule 41 Verification of compliance: every Party is subject to periodic IMO audits against the audit standard; the Secretary-General administers the scheme; Parties facilitate the audit and carry out a programme of actions to address findings; audits follow an overall schedule set by the Secretary-General</li></ul><h2>Annexes</h2><ul><li><b>Annex I — Positioning and technical details of lights and shapes</b> (per the US publication of the International Rules)<ul><li>Forward masthead light: at least 6 m above the hull for vessels of 20 m or more (not more than 12 m); the after masthead at least 4.5 m higher than the forward · vessels of 12 m to under 20 m: masthead at least 2.5 m above the gunwale</li><li>Masthead lights horizontally at least half the vessel's length apart (max 100 m)</li><li>Sidelights not more than three-quarters of the height of the forward masthead light</li><li>Forward anchor light (50 m or more): at least 6 m above the hull</li><li>Minimum luminous intensity for visibility ranges (K = 0.8): 1 nm = 0.9 cd · 2 nm = 4.3 cd · 3 nm = 12 cd · 4 nm = 27 cd · 5 nm = 52 cd · 6 nm = 94 cd</li><li>Sidelights of vessels of 20 m or more need matt black inboard screens · shapes are black; a ball at least 0.6 m diameter; at least 1.5 m between shapes</li></ul></li><li><b>Annex II — Additional signals for fishing vessels fishing in close proximity</b>: lights at least 0.9 m apart, lower than the Rule 26 lights, visible all round at least 1 nm · trawlers of 20 m or more: shooting nets = two white lights in a vertical line; hauling = white over red; net fast on an obstruction = two red lights in a vertical line · purse seiners hampered by gear: two yellow lights in a vertical line flashing alternately every second</li><li><b>Annex III — Technical details of sound signal appliances</b>: whistle fundamental frequency 70–700 Hz (200 m or more: 70–200 Hz; 75–200 m: 130–350 Hz; under 75 m: 250–700 Hz) · sound pressure level at 1 m: 143 dB (200 m or more), 138 dB (75–200 m), 130 dB (20–75 m) · audibility range 2 / 1.5 / 1 / 0.5 nm by length band · bell or gong: at least 110 dB at 1 m; bell mouth at least 300 mm for vessels of 20 m or more</li><li><b>Annex IV — Distress signals</b> (as revised by A.1004(25), in force 1 Dec 2009): (a) gun or other explosive signal fired at intervals of about a minute · (b) continuous sounding of any fog-signalling apparatus · (c) rockets or shells throwing red stars fired one at a time at short intervals · (d) Morse code SOS · (e) the spoken word \"MAYDAY\" by radiotelephone · (f) International Code signal N.C. · (g) a square flag with a ball (or anything resembling a ball) above or below it · (h) flames on the vessel (e.g. burning tar barrel) · (i) a rocket parachute flare or hand flare showing a red light · (j) a smoke signal giving off orange-coloured smoke · (k) slowly and repeatedly raising and lowering arms outstretched to each side · (l) a DSC distress alert on VHF channel 70, or on MF/HF · (m) a ship-to-shore distress alert transmitted by Inmarsat or another mobile satellite service ship earth station · (n) signals transmitted by EPIRBs · (o) approved signals transmitted by radiocommunication systems, including survival craft radar transponders · using these signals for any other purpose, or other signals that may be confused with them, is prohibited · aids: orange canvas with a black square and circle, and a dye marker</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Rules 5–8 (look-out, safe speed, risk of collision, action to avoid collision) and 13–19 are the core of watchkeeping</blockquote><blockquote>Rule 2: depart from the Rules only to avoid immediate danger; Rule 17(b): the stand-on vessel must act when collision cannot be avoided by the give-way vessel alone</blockquote><blockquote>Rule 19: vessels not in sight of one another — safe speed, engines ready, radar-only detection → avoid turning to port for a vessel forward of the beam (except overtaking) and turning towards a vessel abeam or abaft the beam</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Convention articles (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/08/8-03/preventing-collisions-sea.html</li><li>UK MCA — original COLREG and amendments table: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/msn-1781-mf-amendment-3-the-merchant-shipping-regulations-1996-colreg/appendix-2-original-colreg-and-amendments</li><li>US Coast Guard — Navigation Rules (International and Inland): https://www.whoi.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/USCG-Navigation-Rules_International-Inland.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution A.1004(25) (Annex IV): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1004%2825%29.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution A.1085(28) (Part F): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1085(28).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-cape-town-agreement-2012","v":95980874,"title":"Cape Town Agreement 2012","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["cape-town","fishing-vessels","torremolinos","safety","imo","agreement","convention"],"body":"<h1>Cape Town Agreement 2012 — Agreement on the Implementation of the Provisions of the 1993 Torremolinos Protocol relating to the Torremolinos International Convention for the Safety of Fishing Vessels, 1977</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>11 Oct 2012, Cape Town</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>February 2027 (12 months after the conditions were met)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Fishing vessels of 24 m in length and over (Protocol Art 3(3))</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate — up to 5 years (Reg I/13(1))</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>28 States (24 Feb 2026)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Cape Town Agreement</h2><ul><li>Makes the technical rules of the 1993 Torremolinos Protocol work: the 1977 Convention and its 1993 Protocol never entered into force</li><li>Mandatory safety standards for fishing vessels of 24 m and over (IMO says more than 45,000 vessels, FAO data)</li><li>Covers design, construction, equipment, inspection, stability and seaworthiness, machinery and electrical installations, life-saving appliances, fire protection and communications equipment</li><li>IMO calls it the fourth pillar of fishing vessel safety, with STCW-F (training), the ILO Work in Fishing Convention 2007 and the FAO Port State Measures Agreement 2009</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 11 Oct 2012 (\"Done at Cape Town this eleventh day of October two thousand and twelve\")</li><li>Open for signature at IMO HQ 11 Feb 2013 – 10 Feb 2014, then accession (Art 3)</li><li>Art 4(1): in force 12 months after <b>not less than 22 States</b>, whose fishing vessels of 24 m and over operating on the high seas total <b>not less than 3,600</b>, have consented to be bound</li><li>Conditions met when Argentina deposited its instrument of accession at IMO on 24 Feb 2026: <b>28 States, 3,754 vessels</b> (24 m and over)</li><li><b>In force February 2027</b></li></ul><h2>How the Agreement is built</h2><ul><li>Art 1(1): Parties give effect to the Agreement and to the 1993 Protocol, except Protocol Art 1(1)(a), 1(2), 1(3), Art 9 and Art 10</li><li>Art 2: Protocol Arts 2–8 and 11–14 apply, read as references to this Agreement</li><li>Annex = integral part of the Agreement (Art 1(3))</li><li>Once in force, States must put it into national law; flag States make sure their vessels comply; port States may inspect foreign vessels in their ports (IMO)</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"capetown-thresholds\"><figcaption>Length and gross tonnage thresholds</figcaption></figure><h2>Where it applies</h2><ul><li>Protocol Art 3(3): the Annex applies to fishing vessels of <b>24 m in length and over</b>, unless a provision says otherwise</li><li>Art 3(4): where a chapter sets a limit above 24 m, the Administration decides which of its regulations apply to vessels of 24 m and over below that limit</li><li>Art 3(2): not applied to vessels used only for sport or recreation, fish processing, research and training, or as fish carriers</li><li>Reg I/1(1): the Annex applies to <b>new vessels</b>, unless it says otherwise</li><li>Reg I/1(2): the Administration may use gross tonnage instead of length:</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Length</th><th>Gross tonnage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>24 m</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>45 m</td><td>950</td></tr><tr><td>60 m</td><td>2,000</td></tr><tr><td>75 m</td><td>3,000</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>New vessel (Reg I/2)</h2><ul><li>A vessel for which, on or after entry into force of the Protocol: (a) the building or major conversion contract is placed; or (b) the contract was placed earlier but delivery is 3 years or more after entry into force; or (c) with no contract, the keel is laid, construction identifiable with the vessel begins, or assembly of at least 50 tonnes or 1% of the estimated structural mass (whichever is less) has started</li><li>Existing vessel = a vessel that is not a new vessel</li><li>Length (L) = 96% of the total waterline length at 85% of the least moulded depth, or the length from the foreside of the stem to the rudder stock axis on that waterline if greater</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex — the ten chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Main application rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General provisions</td><td>New vessels (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Construction, watertight integrity and equipment</td><td>Length limits in individual regulations (45 m, 75 m)</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Stability and associated seaworthiness</td><td>No general length limit; vessels designed and built to satisfy it</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Machinery and electrical installations and periodically unattended machinery spaces</td><td>45 m and over (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Fire protection, fire detection, fire extinction and fire fighting</td><td>New vessels 45 m and over (Reg 1(1))</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Protection of the crew</td><td>See regulations</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Life-saving appliances and arrangements</td><td>New vessels 45 m and over (Reg 1(1))</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Emergency procedures, musters and drills</td><td>New and existing vessels 24 m and over (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>Radiocommunications</td><td>New and existing vessels 45 m and over (Reg 1)</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>Shipborne navigational equipment and arrangements</td><td>New and existing vessels; each regulation sets its own limit</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter I — Surveys and certificates</h2><ul><li>Reg titles: 1 Application · 2 Definitions · 3 Exemptions · 6 Inspection and survey · 7 Surveys of life-saving appliances and other equipment · 8 Surveys of radio installations · 9 Surveys of structure, machinery and equipment · 10 Maintenance of conditions after survey · 11 Issue or endorsement of certificates · 12 Issue or endorsement by another Party · 13 Duration and validity · 14 Forms of certificates and records of equipment · 15 Availability · 16 Acceptance · 17 Privileges</li><li>Reg 6: inspections and surveys by Administration officers, nominated surveyors or recognized organizations; they must be able to require repairs and to carry out inspections requested by a port State</li><li>Reg 7: life-saving appliances — initial, renewal (not more than 5 years), periodical, annual (within 3 months before or after each anniversary date) and additional surveys</li><li>Reg 9: structure, machinery and equipment — includes <b>at least two bottom inspections in any five-year period, with not more than 36 months between any two</b></li><li>Reg 10(3): after an accident or a defect, the skipper or owner also reports at once to the port State authorities if in another Party's port</li><li>Reg 6(3): where a vessel is unfit, the port State makes sure it does not sail until it can go to sea without danger</li></ul><p><b>Certificate validity (Reg I/13)</b></p><ul><li>International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate: period set by the Administration, <b>not more than 5 years</b></li><li>If a new certificate cannot be put on board before expiry, the old one may be endorsed for up to <b>5 months</b></li><li>Extension of up to 3 months only to reach the port where the survey is done</li><li>Certificate stops being valid if surveys are not done in time, it is not endorsed, or the vessel changes flag (Reg 13(8))</li></ul><h2>Chapter III — Stability (Reg III/2 intact stability)</h2><ul><li>GZ curve area: at least 0.055 m-rad up to 30° heel; at least 0.09 m-rad up to 40° (or the flooding angle if lower); at least 0.03 m-rad between 30° and 40°</li><li>GZ at least 200 mm at heel of 30° or more</li><li>Maximum GZ preferably above 30°, and not less than 25°</li><li>Initial GM at least 350 mm for single-deck vessels</li><li>Reg III/7: stability calculated for set operating conditions — departure with full fuel and stores, arrival with 10% stores and a minimum catch</li><li>Reg III/8: icing allowance in ice-prone areas — 30 kg/m² on exposed weather decks</li><li>Reg III/9 and III/10: inclining test on completion and stability information for the skipper</li><li>Reg III/14: subdivision and damage stability for vessels of 100 m and over carrying 100 or more persons</li></ul><h2>Chapter V — Fire protection</h2><ul><li>Part A Reg 1(1): new vessels of 45 m and over · Part B (Regs 3–27): vessels of 60 m and over · Part C (Reg 28 on): vessels of 45 m to under 60 m</li><li>Reg 1(2): accommodation and service spaces use one of three methods — IF (non-combustible divisions), IIF (automatic sprinkler and fire alarm), IIIF (automatic fire alarm and detection; spaces bounded by A or B class divisions limited to 50 m²)</li><li>Regs 17–19: at least two fire pumps and a fire main with hydrants, hoses and nozzles; vessels of 75 m and over need an independently driven emergency fire pump</li><li>Reg 22: machinery spaces — fixed extinguishing systems; new halogenated hydrocarbon systems prohibited</li><li>Regs 24–25: at least two fireman's outfits in widely separated places; fire control plan displayed permanently</li></ul><h2>Chapter VI — Protection of the crew</h2><ul><li>Reg 1: deck openings with coamings or sills under 600 mm need guards; skylights with protective bars not more than 350 mm apart; non-skid decks in working areas</li><li>Reg 2: access hatches at least 600 mm × 600 mm (or 600 mm diameter); escape hatch covers open from both sides</li><li>Reg 3: bulwarks or guard rails at least <b>1 m</b> high; gap under the lowest course not more than 230 mm; other courses not more than 380 mm apart; stanchions not more than 1.5 m apart</li><li>Reg 4: stairways and ladders — adequate size and strength, handrails, non-slip treads</li></ul><h2>Chapter VII — Life-saving appliances (new vessels 45 m and over)</h2><ul><li>Reg 5: at least two survival craft; vessels under 75 m carry craft for everyone on board on each side</li><li>Reg 8: one approved lifejacket for each person on board</li><li>Reg 9: immersion suits — one for each person assigned to the rescue boat crew, and at least three per lifeboat</li><li>Reg 10: lifebuoys — 8 on vessels of 75 m and over, 6 on vessels under 75 m; at least half with self-igniting lights, at least two with smoke signals</li><li>Reg 11: one line-throwing appliance · Reg 12: at least 12 rocket parachute flares</li><li>Reg 13: at least three two-way VHF radiotelephone sets · Reg 14: at least one radar transponder on each side</li><li>Reg 16: weekly and monthly inspections; falls turned end for end at intervals of not more than 30 months and renewed at least every 5 years; inflatable liferafts serviced every 12 months (extendable to 17)</li><li>Lifeboats (Reg 17): capacity not more than 150 persons; full boarding in not more than 3 min; fuel flashpoint above 43 °C</li><li>Liferafts (Regs 20–21): must stay afloat 30 days and survive a drop from 18 m; minimum capacity six persons</li><li>Lifejackets (Reg 24): donned within 1 min; light of at least 0.75 cd for 8 h</li><li>Immersion suits (Reg 25): donned within 2 min</li><li>Rocket parachute flares (Reg 29): reach at least 300 m and burn at least 40 s</li><li>Hand flares (Reg 30): at least 15,000 cd for at least 1 min</li></ul><h2>Chapter VIII — Emergency procedures, musters and drills (24 m and over)</h2><ul><li>Reg 2: muster list; general alarm of <b>seven or more short blasts followed by one long blast</b></li><li>Reg 3(1)(a): abandon ship and fire drills — monthly for each crew member; vessels under 45 m may follow an approved schedule of at least every three months</li><li>Reg 3(1)(f), (g): lifeboat and rescue boat launching — at least once every three months</li><li>Reg 3(3): drills recorded in a log-book</li></ul><h2>Chapter IX — Radiocommunications (45 m and over)</h2><ul><li>Functional requirements (Reg 4), general radio equipment (Reg 6), sea-area equipment (Regs 7–10), watches (Reg 11), reserve power (Reg 12), radio personnel (Reg 15)</li><li>Certified sea areas are recorded on the certificate (Reg IX/2)</li></ul><h2>Chapter X — Navigational equipment</h2><ul><li>Standard magnetic compass: vessels of 24 m and over (Reg 3(1)(a))</li><li>Gyro-compass: vessels of 45 m and over built on or after 1 Sep 1984 (Reg 3(3))</li><li>Radar: 45 m and over (Reg 3(6)) · echo-sounder (Reg 3(9)) · speed and distance device (Reg 3(11))</li><li>Bridge visibility (Reg 6): new vessels of 45 m and over — view of the sea surface ahead not blocked for more than the lesser of two vessel lengths or 500 m</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>From February 2027, fishing vessels of 24 m and over of Party States need the International Fishing Vessel Safety Certificate (up to 5 years), and Party port States may inspect foreign vessels</blockquote><blockquote>Many requirements start at 45 m — fire protection, life-saving appliances, radiocommunications, radar</blockquote><blockquote>Drills: abandon ship and fire drills monthly; bottom inspections at least twice in five years</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO consolidated text of the Agreement: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/About/Conventions/Documents/Consolidated%20text%20of%20the%20Agreement.pdf</li><li>Treaty text (Dutch Treaty Series 2014, 49): https://zoek.officielebekendmakingen.nl/trb-2014-49.html</li><li>1993 Torremolinos Protocol (articles, NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1993-Torremolinos-Protocol-Relating-to-1977-Convention-on-the-Safety-of-Fishing-Vessels-1.pdf</li><li>IMO — Cape Town Agreement to enter into force in 2027: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/cape-town-agreement-global-treaty-fishing-vessel-safety-enter-into-force-2027.aspx</li><li>Treaty text (Japan MOFA): https://www.mofa.go.jp/files/100318355.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-fal-1965","v":259860802,"title":"FAL 1965","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["fal","facilitation","port-clearance","single-window","fal-forms","stowaways","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>FAL 1965 — Convention on Facilitation of International Maritime Traffic</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>9 Apr 1965, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>5 Mar 1967 (60 days after 10 States, Art XI)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>16 Articles · an Annex of Standards and Recommended Practices</td></tr><tr><td>Annex</td><td>7 sections and 3 appendices, replaced in full by FAL.14(46)</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic exchange</td><td>Mandatory from 9 Apr 2019 (2016 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Single window</td><td>Mandatory from 1 Jan 2024 (2022 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Not applied to</td><td>Warships and pleasure yachts (Art II(3))</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is FAL</h2><ul><li>Aim: to <b>facilitate and expedite international maritime traffic</b> and prevent unnecessary delays to ships, persons and cargo (Art I)</li><li>Parties work for the highest practicable uniformity of formalities, documents and procedures (Art III)</li><li>Public authorities may require only the declarations listed in the Annex (Standard 2.1)</li><li>Parties cooperate directly or through the IMO (Art IV)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fal-dates\"><figcaption>The Convention and its Annex amendments, with adoption and entry-into-force dates</figcaption></figure><ul><li>The 2002 amendments added the stowaway standards and practices.</li><li><b>2002:</b> the Dangerous Goods Manifest (FAL Form 7) became the basic document for dangerous goods information.</li><li><b>2005:</b> electronic transmission was encouraged, and submitting all information at a single point was recommended.</li><li><b>2009:</b> new IMO FAL forms were approved.</li><li><b>2016:</b> all IMO FAL forms were revised except the Ship's Stores Declaration (Form 3). Electronic exchange became mandatory from 9 Apr 2019, with a transition period of at least 12 months, and the \"Single Window\" wording was introduced.</li><li><b>2022 (FAL.14(46), 13 May 2022):</b> the complete Annex was replaced, and single window systems became mandatory. In force 1 Jan 2024.</li><li><b>2023 (17 Mar 2023):</b> an amendment to Recommended Practice 7.11 on combating illicit activities. The IMO page gives its expected entry into force as 1 Jan 2025.</li></ul><h2>The Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General undertaking: facilitate and expedite international maritime traffic</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Co-operation and scope. Measures should be, as far as practicable, no less favourable than for other international transport; apply equally to ships of coastal and non-coastal Parties; <b>warships and pleasure yachts excluded</b></td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Uniformity of formalities, documents and procedures</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Co-operation directly or through the IMO</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Saving clauses: wider facilities allowed; temporary measures for public morality, order, security or health; matters not covered stay under national law</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>Definitions: <b>Standards</b> are measures whose uniform application is necessary and practicable; <b>Recommended Practices</b> are measures whose application is desirable</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>Amending the Annex</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>Differences from Standards (notification)</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>Revision of the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>Signature, acceptance, accession</td></tr><tr><td>XI</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>XII</td><td>Denunciation</td></tr><tr><td>XIII</td><td>Territorial extension</td></tr><tr><td>XIV</td><td>Notifications</td></tr><tr><td>XV</td><td>Deposit and registration</td></tr><tr><td>XVI</td><td>Languages and date</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Differences (Art VIII and IMO page):</b></p><ul><li>A Government that cannot comply with a Standard, or needs different rules, must notify the Secretary-General of the differences.</li><li>For a Recommended Practice, notification is required once the Government fully complies.</li></ul><p><b>Amending the Annex (Art VII):</b></p><ul><li>By proposal to the Secretary-General: in force 6 months after a majority of the Parties accept it.</li><li>By a conference called at the request of one third of the Parties: needs a two-thirds majority of those present and voting.</li><li>Art IX allows the Convention itself to be revised by a conference.</li></ul><p><b>Entry into force and denunciation (Arts XI–XII):</b></p><ul><li>In force 60 days after at least 10 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments.</li><li>Denunciation is possible after 3 years in force for a Party, and takes effect 1 year after notice.</li></ul><h2>The Annex</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fal-annex-map\"><figcaption>Sections and appendices of the Annex, as replaced by FAL.14(46)</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Definitions and general provisions (including electronic exchange of information)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Arrival, stay and departure of the ship</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Arrival and departure of persons</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Stowaways</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Arrival, stay and departure of cargo and other articles</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Public health and quarantine, including sanitary measures for animals and plants</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Miscellaneous provisions: A Bonds and other forms of security · B Services at ports · C Emergency assistance · D National facilitation committees</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Appendix</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Maximum information public authorities may require for the declarations (Standard 2.1bis)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Declarations that may be required in paper form in exceptional circumstances (Standard 2.11), with the paper forms</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Stowaway details (Recommended Practice 4.6.2)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Electronic exchange and single window (Section 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1.3bis</td><td>Public authorities shall establish, maintain and use systems for the electronic exchange of information</td></tr><tr><td>1.3ter</td><td>A transition period of no less than 12 months, counted from when all functional and technical specifications are published</td></tr><tr><td>1.3quart</td><td>Alternative means for clearance information during the transition; existing message formats stay accepted</td></tr><tr><td>1.3quin</td><td>Introduce arrangements so all the information required under Standard 2.1 can be given to a <b>single window</b> by electronic means</td></tr><tr><td>1.3sext</td><td>Data for arrival, stay and departure is submitted once and reused as far as possible</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Single window:</b> an environment that allows standardized, harmonized information to be submitted to a single entry point, typically electronic.</li><li>The IMO says all Member States must use a Maritime Single Window since 1 Jan 2024. It also reports that authorities still required paper or mixed submissions in 64% of port calls in one survey, so adoption is uneven.</li></ul><h2>Declarations authorities may require (Standard 2.1)</h2><p>Public authorities shall <b>not require more than</b> these:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Declaration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>General Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>b</td><td>Cargo Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>c</td><td>Ship's Stores Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>d</td><td>Crew's Effects Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>e</td><td>Crew List</td></tr><tr><td>f</td><td>Passenger List</td></tr><tr><td>g</td><td>Dangerous Goods Manifest</td></tr><tr><td>h</td><td>Special declaration for postal items (Universal Postal Union Acts)</td></tr><tr><td>i</td><td>Maritime Declaration of Health (International Health Regulations)</td></tr><tr><td>j</td><td>Ship Sanitation Control Exemption Certificate, Ship Sanitation Control Certificate or extension (IHR)</td></tr><tr><td>k</td><td>Security-related information under SOLAS regulation XI-2/9.2.2</td></tr><tr><td>l</td><td>Advance electronic cargo information for customs risk assessment (WCO SAFE Framework of Standards)</td></tr><tr><td>m</td><td>Advance Notification Form for Waste Delivery to Port Reception Facilities</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Standard IMO FAL forms (Appendix 2):</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Document</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FAL 1</td><td>General Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 2</td><td>Cargo Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 3</td><td>Ship's Stores Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 4</td><td>Crew's Effects Declaration</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 5</td><td>Crew List</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 6</td><td>Passenger List</td></tr><tr><td>FAL 7</td><td>Dangerous Goods Manifest</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Ports may ask only for the declarations in Standard 2.1, and since 1 Jan 2024 must take them electronically through a single window. Keep the crew list, stores and cargo data accurate, because the same data is reused. For stowaways, follow Section 4 and Appendix 3.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>FAL Convention text, 1965 (NUS Centre for International Law): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1965-Convention-on-Facilitation-of-International-Maritime-Traffic-1.pdf</li><li>Resolution FAL.14(46), Annex as replaced (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/FALDocuments/FAL.14(46).pdf</li><li>IMO — FAL Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Convention-on-Facilitation-of-International-Maritime-Traffic-(FAL).aspx</li><li>IMO — Maritime Single Window: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Facilitation/Pages/MaritimeSingleWindow-default.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hns-2010","v":21384881,"title":"HNS 2010","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["hns","liability","compensation","chemicals","lng","lpg","insurance","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>HNS 2010 — International Convention on Liability and Compensation for Damage in Connection with the Carriage of Hazardous and Noxious Substances by Sea, 1996, as amended by the 2010 Protocol</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>3 May 1996 · 2010 Protocol 30 Apr 2010</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td><b>29 Nov 2027</b></td></tr><tr><td>Tier 1</td><td>Shipowner — bulk max 100 million SDR · packaged max 115 million SDR</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 2</td><td>HNS Fund — total max 250 million SDR</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is HNS 2010</h2><ul><li>A two-tier compensation system for damage caused by hazardous and noxious substances carried by sea as cargo</li><li>Tier 1: the shipowner — strict liability, compulsory insurance, limited by ship size</li><li>Tier 2: the HNS Fund — financed by contributions from receivers of HNS cargo</li><li>Total compensation for one incident: up to <b>250 million units of account (SDR)</b>, including what the shipowner pays (Art 14(5)(a))</li><li>Once in force, the 1996 Convention as amended by the 2010 Protocol is called the \"2010 HNS Convention\"</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hns-tiers\"><figcaption>Two tiers of HNS compensation (million SDR)</figcaption></figure><h2>History</h2><ul><li>HNS Convention adopted 3 May 1996 (IMO, London) — never entered into force; practical problems stopped States from ratifying</li><li>2010 Protocol adopted 30 Apr 2010 — solved those problems</li><li>Entry-into-force conditions met on 29 May 2026: at least 12 States, including four each with at least 2 million GT of ships, and more than 40 million tonnes of contributing cargo</li><li><b>Enters into force 29 Nov 2027</b> (18 months after the conditions were met)</li><li>West P&amp;I (July 2026) lists 13 States that had deposited instruments: Belgium, Canada, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Netherlands, Norway, Slovakia, South Africa, Sweden, Türkiye</li></ul><h2>Where it applies (Art 3)</h2><ul><li>Damage in the territory or territorial sea of a State Party</li><li>Damage in the exclusive economic zone (or equivalent area) of a State Party — environmental damage</li><li>Damage other than environmental damage, outside any State's territory, caused by HNS on a ship registered in a State Party</li><li>Preventive measures, wherever taken</li></ul><h2>Not covered (Art 4, Art 5)</h2><ul><li>Pollution damage from oil as defined in CLC 1969 (oil pollution is under CLC/Fund)</li><li>Damage caused by radioactive material (class 7)</li><li>Warships and State ships used only on government non-commercial service (unless a State opts in)</li><li>Damage covered by the Bunkers Convention</li><li>Plastic pellets (nurdles) are excluded (West P&amp;I)</li><li>Option (Art 5(1)(a)): a State may declare the Convention not applicable to ships of <b>200 GT or less</b> carrying only packaged HNS on domestic voyages, if other conditions are met. West P&amp;I says only Canada and Sweden have used this.</li></ul><hr><h2>What counts as HNS (Art 1(5))</h2><ul><li>Defined by reference to lists in other IMO conventions and codes:<ul><li>Oils (MARPOL Annex I)</li><li>Noxious liquid substances (MARPOL Annex II)</li><li>Dangerous liquids (IBC Code)</li><li>Liquefied gases</li><li>Liquid substances with flashpoint not exceeding 60 °C</li><li>Dangerous, hazardous and harmful materials in packaged form (IMDG Code)</li><li>Solid bulk materials with chemical hazards (IMSBC Code)</li><li>Residues from the previous carriage of these (other than packaged residues)</li></ul></li><li>The HNS Finder tool on hnsconvention.org helps to check a substance</li></ul><h2>Damage covered (Art 1(6))</h2><ul><li>Loss of life or personal injury, on board or outside the ship</li><li>Loss of or damage to property outside the ship</li><li>Loss or damage by contamination of the environment (limited to reasonable reinstatement measures; loss of profit from impairment is covered)</li><li>Costs of preventive measures, and further loss or damage caused by them</li><li>The Convention covers fire and explosion risks as well as pollution</li></ul><h2>Tier 1 — Shipowner (Art 7, 9, 12)</h2><ul><li>Owner at the time of the incident is strictly liable (Art 7(1))</li><li><b>Limits (Art 9(1)) — tonnage means gross tonnage:</b></li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Ship size</th><th>Bulk HNS (SDR)</th><th>Packaged HNS (SDR)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to 2,000 GT</td><td>10 million</td><td>11.5 million</td></tr><tr><td>2,001–50,000 GT</td><td>+ 1,500 per GT</td><td>+ 1,725 per GT</td></tr><tr><td>Above 50,000 GT</td><td>+ 360 per GT</td><td>+ 414 per GT</td></tr><tr><td><b>Maximum</b></td><td><b>100 million</b></td><td><b>115 million</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The packaged-HNS limits also apply where damage involves both bulk and packaged HNS, or the source cannot be determined</li><li>Owner loses the right to limit only for a personal act or omission done with intent, or recklessly and with knowledge that damage would probably result (Art 9(2))</li></ul><h2>Owner is not liable if the damage (Art 7(2))</h2><ul><li>Resulted from war, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, or an exceptional, inevitable and irresistible natural phenomenon</li><li>Was wholly caused by an intentional act or omission of a third party</li><li>Was wholly caused by negligence of a government authority responsible for lights or other navigational aids</li><li>Was caused by failure of the shipper or another person to give information on the hazardous nature of the HNS, if the owner neither knew nor ought reasonably to have known</li><li>Liability can be reduced or removed if the claimant's own intent or negligence contributed (Art 7(3))</li></ul><h2>Insurance and certificate (Art 12)</h2><ul><li>Owner of a ship registered in a State Party and actually carrying HNS must keep insurance or other financial security</li><li>Ship must carry a compulsory insurance <b>certificate</b> in the Annex I model</li><li>No general minimum tonnage (apart from the 200 GT option above), so small ships may need a certificate too</li><li>The flag State issues it; a State Party may also certify a ship registered in a non-Party State once insurance is checked</li><li>Claims can be made <b>directly against the insurer</b> (Art 12(8)); evidence is usually a P&amp;I \"Blue Card\"</li></ul><h2>Tier 2 — HNS Fund (Art 13–21)</h2><ul><li>Established to pay when Tier 1 is inadequate or not available (Art 13(1))</li><li>Total limit with Tier 1: <b>250 million SDR</b> (Art 14(5))</li><li>Run by an Assembly of States Parties, a claims Committee and a Secretariat headed by a Director (hnsconvention.org)</li><li><b>Accounts:</b> a general account (with sectors) plus separate accounts for <b>oil, LNG and LPG</b> (Art 16) — so one cargo type does not subsidise another</li><li><b>Who pays:</b> receivers of contributing cargo in Party States, by quantity received in the previous calendar year; the owner does not pay</li><li><b>Thresholds:</b><ul><li>General account: receivers of more than 20,000 tonnes of contributing cargo in a year (Art 18(1))</li><li>Oil account: more than 150,000 tonnes of contributing oil, or more than 20,000 tonnes of other bulk oils (Art 19(1)(a))</li><li>LPG account: more than 20,000 tonnes (Art 19(1)(b))</li><li>LNG account: receivers of any quantity — contribution is by the titleholder rules of Art 19(1bis)</li></ul></li><li>Separate accounts start only after contributing cargo reaches: oil 350 million tonnes, LNG 20 million tonnes, LPG 15 million tonnes (Art 19(3))</li><li>Each State Party must report to the Director the name and address of each person liable to contribute, and cargo data (Art 21)</li></ul><h2>Time limits (Art 37)</h2><ul><li>Claim must be brought within <b>3 years</b> from when the claimant knew (or should reasonably have known) of the damage and the owner's identity</li><li>And never later than <b>10 years</b> after the incident</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>From 29 Nov 2027 ships carrying HNS to or from Party States need an HNS certificate, as with CLC and Bunkers</blockquote><blockquote>Shipper must declare HNS properly — non-disclosure can free the owner from liability</blockquote><blockquote>Oil pollution from tankers stays under CLC/Fund; bunker oil under Bunkers 2001</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 22 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>2010 HNS Convention consolidated text: https://www.hnsconvention.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/2010-HNS-Convention-Consolidated-text_e.pdf</li><li>IMO — HNS Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-liability-and-compensation-for-damage-in-connection-with-the-carriage-of-hazardous-and-noxious-.aspx</li><li>HNS Convention — The Convention: https://www.hnsconvention.org/the-convention/</li><li>West P&amp;I — The 2010 HNS Convention: key provisions (July 2026): https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/July-2026/The-2010-HNS-Convention-Key-Provisions-and-What-Sh/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hong-kong-convention-2009","v":218132898,"title":"Hong Kong Convention 2009","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["hkc","ship-recycling","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Hong Kong Convention 2009 — International Convention for the Safe and Environmentally Sound Recycling of Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>15 May 2009 (Hong Kong)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>26 Jun 2025</td></tr><tr><td>Main documents</td><td>Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM) · Ship Recycling Plan · Ready for Recycling Certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Yard</td><td>Authorized Ship Recycling Facility</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the HKC</h2><ul><li>IMO convention for safe, environmentally sound ship recycling: covers the ship (design, operation, preparation for recycling) and the recycling yard</li><li>Ship: Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM) all its life · surveys and certificates</li><li>Yard: Ship Recycling Facility (SRF) authorized by its State · Ship Recycling Facility Plan (SRFP) · ship-specific Ship Recycling Plan (SRP)</li><li>Convention (21 Articles) + Annex (Regulations, Ch 1–4) + 7 Appendices + IMO guidelines</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hkc-flow\"><figcaption>Steps for a ship: IHM Part I in service (initial and renewal surveys), IHM Parts II and III before recycling, Ship Recycling Plan from the authorized yard, final survey, then the Ready for Recycling Certificate (Regs 5, 9, 10, 11, 16). Drawn from the Convention text.</figcaption></figure><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 15 May 2009, Hong Kong diplomatic conference</li><li>Entry-into-force conditions: 15 States · 40% of world tonnage · recycling capacity ≥3% of their tonnage</li><li>Bangladesh and Liberia ratified 26 Jun 2023 → conditions met</li><li>In force 26 Jun 2025 (24 months after the conditions were met) · IMO lists 24 Parties covering about 57% of world tonnage</li><li>Annex Reg 1: existing ship = a ship that is not a new ship. New ship = building contract on/after entry into force (26 Jun 2025), or (no contract) keel laid or similar stage on/after 6 months after (26 Dec 2025), or delivery on/after 30 months after (26 Dec 2027)</li><li>Entry-into-force conditions at June 2023: about 45.9% of world tonnage and 3.31% recycling capacity</li><li>Cybutryne: Appendix 1 follows AFS Convention Annex 1 as in force (AFS amended by MEPC.331(76)) · the 2023 IHM Guidelines MEPC.379(80) (adopted MEPC 80, Jul 2023) add cybutryne to the Appendix 1 list for the IHM, with limits of 1,000 mg/kg dry paint (hull sample) or 200 mg/kg (wet paint sample)</li><li>Older IHM guidelines: MEPC.179(59), MEPC.197(62), MEPC.269(68) → replaced by MEPC.379(80)</li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>Ships entitled to fly a Party's flag or operating under its authority (Art 3)</li><li>Ship Recycling Facilities under a Party's jurisdiction</li><li>Survey and certificate requirements: ships ≥500 GT on international voyages</li><li>Not covered: warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships on non-commercial service, ships &lt;500 GT, ships operating throughout their life only in waters of the State whose flag they fly (Party should still act consistently, as far as reasonable and practicable) — Art 3</li></ul><h2>Articles (21)</h2><ul><li>Art 1 General obligations · Art 2 Definitions · Art 3 Application</li><li>Art 4 Controls related to ship recycling · Art 5 Survey and certification of ships · Art 6 Authorization of Ship Recycling Facilities</li><li>Art 7 Exchange of information · Art 8 Inspection of ships · Art 9 Detection of violations · Art 10 Violations · Art 11 Undue delay or detention of ships</li><li>Art 12 Communication of information · Art 13 Technical assistance and co-operation · Art 14 Dispute settlement</li><li>Art 15 Relationship with international law and other agreements · Art 16 Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval, accession · Art 17 Entry into force</li><li>Art 18 Amendments · Art 19 Denunciation · Art 20 Depositary · Art 21 Languages</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex Ch 1 — General</h2><ul><li>Reg 1 Definitions (para 3 existing ship, para 4 new ship) · Reg 2 General applicability · Reg 3 Relationship with other standards, recommendations and guidance (take into account relevant ILO and Basel Convention standards)</li></ul><h2>Annex Ch 2 — Requirements for Ships</h2><p>*Part A — Design, construction, operation, maintenance*</p><ul><li>Reg 4 Controls of hazardous materials (Appendix 1 — prohibited or restricted):<ul><li>Asbestos: banned in all new installations</li><li>Ozone-depleting substances (Montreal Protocol list, e.g. halons, CFCs): new installations banned on all ships; exception: HCFCs were allowed until 1 Jan 2020</li><li>PCBs: new installations banned</li><li>Anti-fouling systems: organotin (TBT) not used; anything the AFS Convention prohibits (incl. cybutryne) not used</li><li>Each Party applies these controls to its ships and also in its ports, shipyards, repair yards and offshore terminals</li></ul></li><li>Reg 5 Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM):<ul><li>Part I: materials in the ship's structure and equipment (Appendix 1 and 2) — new ships from delivery</li><li>Existing ships: comply as far as practicable · Part I within 5 years of entry into force or before going for recycling, whichever is earlier (by 26 Jun 2030) · at least identify Appendix 1 materials · include a plan describing the visual/sampling checks used</li><li>Part I kept up to date during the ship's life (new equipment, repairs)</li><li>Part II operationally generated wastes · Part III stores — added and verified only before recycling</li></ul></li><li>Reg 6 Procedure for proposing amendments to Appendices 1 and 2 · Reg 7 Technical groups</li></ul><p>*Part B — Preparation for ship recycling*</p><ul><li>Reg 8 General requirements</li><li>Reg 9 Ship Recycling Plan (SRP): the owner gives the authorized yard the IHM; the yard prepares a ship-specific SRP before recycling<ul><li>Approved by the yard's competent authority, explicitly or tacitly (tacit: receipt acknowledged in 3 working days, 14-day review, no written objection = approved)</li></ul></li></ul><p>*Part C — Surveys and certification*</p><ul><li>Reg 10 Surveys:<ul><li>Initial survey (before the ship enters service or before the IHM certificate is first issued): checks IHM Part I</li><li>Renewal survey: interval set by the Administration, ≤5 years</li><li>Additional survey: at the owner's request, after a change, replacement or significant repair affecting the IHM</li><li>Final survey: before the ship leaves service and before recycling starts — checks IHM (Parts I–III), that the SRP reflects the IHM and includes safe-for-entry and safe-for-hot-work arrangements, and that the yard authorization is valid</li><li>Surveys by Administration officers, or delegated to nominated surveyors or ROs (Administration stays responsible)</li></ul></li><li>Reg 11 Issue and endorsement of certificates:<ul><li>International Certificate on Inventory of Hazardous Materials: after initial or renewal survey · validity ≤5 years</li><li>Renewal survey within 3 months before expiry → new certificate runs 5 years from the old expiry date; more than 3 months before → 5 years from the survey date</li><li>Extensions: up to 5 months (endorsement, if survey done but certificate not issued) · up to 3 months to complete a voyage · 1 month grace for short-voyage ships</li><li>International Ready for Recycling Certificate (IRRC): after final survey, taking the yard's authorization into account · validity ≤3 months</li></ul></li><li>Reg 12 Issuance or endorsement of a certificate by another Party (mutual acceptance among Parties) · Reg 13 Form of the certificates · Reg 14 Duration and validity</li></ul><h2>Annex Ch 3 — Requirements for Ship Recycling Facilities</h2><ul><li>Reg 15 Controls on ship recycling facilities</li><li>Reg 16 Authorization: Document of Authorization to conduct Ship Recycling (DASR) · ≤5 years</li><li>Reg 17 General requirements</li><li>Reg 18 Ship Recycling Facility Plan (SRFP) · Reg 19 Prevention of adverse effects to health and environment</li><li>Reg 20 Safe and environmentally sound management of hazardous materials</li><li>Reg 21 Emergency preparedness · Reg 22 Worker safety and training</li><li>Reg 23 Reporting on incidents, accidents, occupational diseases and chronic effects</li></ul><h2>Annex Ch 4 — Reporting Requirements</h2><ul><li>Reg 24 Initial notification and reporting · Reg 25 Reporting upon completion</li></ul><h2>Appendices</h2><ul><li>Appendix 1 — Controls (prohibited/restricted): asbestos, ozone-depleting substances (HCFC exception to 1 Jan 2020), PCBs, anti-fouling compounds (organotin, cybutryne)</li><li>Appendix 2 — Minimum list for the IHM: Appendix 1 items + cadmium, hexavalent chromium, lead, mercury (and their compounds), PBBs, PBDEs, polychlorinated naphthalenes (more than 3 chlorine atoms), radioactive substances, certain short-chain chlorinated paraffins (C10–C13)</li><li>Appendix 3 — form of the International Certificate on Inventory of Hazardous Materials</li></ul><h2>IMO guidelines (MEPC)</h2><ul><li>MEPC.379(80) — 2023 Guidelines for the development of the IHM</li><li>MEPC.196(62) Ship Recycling Plan · MEPC.210(63) safe recycling (yards) · MEPC.211(63) authorization of SRFs</li><li>MEPC.222(64) Survey and certification · MEPC.223(64) Inspection of ships (PSC)</li></ul><h2>EU Ship Recycling Regulation (EU 1257/2013) — on top of HKC</h2><ul><li>Large commercial seagoing EU-flag ships: recycle only at yards on the European List (from 2019)</li><li>IHM required: EU ships and non-EU ships calling at EU ports or anchorages (since 31 Dec 2020); non-EU-flag ships need a certificate of compliance</li><li>EU Annex I prohibits PFOS as well · EU IHM list (Annex II) includes HBCDD, which HKC does not require</li><li>HKC certificate does not automatically replace EU SRR compliance (EU SRR is based on HKC; check with flag and class)</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Onboard: keep IHM Part I updated with every new or changed equipment · ask for Material Declarations (MD) and Supplier Declarations of Conformity (SDoC) from suppliers</blockquote><blockquote>Before sale for recycling: final survey + IRRC (3 months) + yard with DASR</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO — Recycling of ships and HKC (guidelines list): https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Pages/Recycling-of-ships-and-Hong-Kong-Convention.aspx</li><li>EMSA — ship recycling (EU SRR): https://www.emsa.europa.eu/sustainable-shipping/ship-recycling.html</li><li>ABS — HKC and IHM Part I (MEPC.379(80), cybutryne): https://ww2.eagle.org/content/dam/eagle/regulatory-news/2023/ABS%20Regulatory%20News%20-%20Ship%20Recycling.pdf</li><li>Treaty text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/2009-HK-Convention-For-Safe-and-Environmentally-Sound-Ship-Recycling.pdf</li><li>IMO — HKC entry into force: https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/PressBriefings/Pages/Hong-Kong-Convention-entry-into-force.aspx</li><li>Lloyd's Register — IMO Hong Kong Ship Recycling Convention: https://www.lr.org/en/services/statutory-compliance/imo-hong-kong-ship-recycling-convention/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-intervention-1969","v":259977239,"title":"Intervention 1969","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["intervention","high-seas","coastal-state","oil-pollution","casualty","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Intervention 1969 — International Convention Relating to Intervention on the High Seas in Cases of Oil Pollution Casualties</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>29 Nov 1969, Brussels</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>6 May 1975</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>90 (as of 10 Jul 2024, NUS CIL)</td></tr><tr><td>1973 Protocol</td><td>Extends it to substances other than oil · in force 1983</td></tr><tr><td>Core rule</td><td>Coastal State may act on the high seas against grave and imminent danger from oil pollution after a maritime casualty</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Intervention Convention</h2><ul><li>Lets a coastal State take measures on the high seas after a maritime casualty, to prevent, mitigate or eliminate grave and imminent danger to its coastline or related interests from oil pollution or the threat of it (Art I(1))</li><li>Measures must be limited to what is necessary, after consulting the flag State, owners and, where feasible, independent experts (IMO)</li><li>A State that goes beyond what is permitted pays compensation (Art VI)</li><li>Covers seagoing vessels; warships and State ships on non-commercial government service are excluded (Art I(2))</li></ul><h2>Key definitions (Art II)</h2><ul><li><b>Maritime casualty</b>: collision, stranding, other incidents of navigation, and other occurrences causing material damage or the imminent threat of it</li><li><b>Ship</b>: any seagoing vessel of any type whatsoever, and any floating craft, except installations for exploring or exploiting sea-bed resources</li><li><b>Oil</b>: crude oil, fuel oil, diesel oil and lubricating oil</li><li><b>Related interests</b> of the coastal State directly affected or threatened:<ul><li>(a) coastal, port or estuarine activities, including fisheries that are an essential means of livelihood</li><li>(b) tourist attractions of the area</li><li>(c) the health of the coastal population and the well-being of the area, including conservation of living marine resources and wildlife</li></ul></li><li><b>Organization</b>: the Inter-Governmental Maritime Consultative Organization (now IMO)</li></ul><hr><h2>Procedure before acting (Art III)</h2><ul><li>(a) Consult the other States affected, in particular the flag State(s)</li><li>(b) Promptly notify known persons whose interests may be affected (owners, others) and consider their views</li><li>(c) May consult independent experts from the list kept by the Organization</li><li>(d) In case of <b>extreme urgency</b>, may act at once without prior notice or consultation</li><li>Must use best endeavours to avoid any risk to human life</li><li>Must notify the measures taken to the States and known persons concerned, and to the IMO Secretary-General</li></ul><h2>Experts (Art IV)</h2><ul><li>IMO keeps the list of experts and sets its rules; Member States and Parties may nominate experts; the States using the experts pay for them</li></ul><h2>Proportionality (Art V)</h2><ul><li>V(1): measures must be proportionate to the damage, actual or threatened</li><li>V(2): must not go beyond what is reasonably necessary; must stop once the aim is achieved; must avoid unnecessary interference with the rights of others</li><li>V(3): proportionality is judged by (a) the extent and probability of imminent damage if no measures are taken, (b) the likelihood of the measures being effective, (c) the extent of damage the measures may cause</li></ul><h2>Compensation and disputes</h2><ul><li><b>Art VI:</b> a Party that took measures beyond what is reasonably necessary pays compensation for the damage they caused</li><li><b>Art VII:</b> the Convention does not prejudice other applicable rights, duties, privileges, immunities or remedies</li><li><b>Art VIII:</b> disputes on whether measures broke the Convention, on compensation and its amount go to <b>conciliation, then arbitration</b> under the Annex if negotiation fails; the acting Party cannot refuse because its own courts' remedies are not exhausted</li><li>Annex, conciliation: commission of three (one per party + chairman by agreement) · respondent names its conciliator within 60 days, otherwise the IMO Secretary-General · recommendation: parties have up to 90 days to accept or reject</li><li>Annex, arbitration: request within 180 days after conciliation fails · tribunal of three · award is reasoned, final, no appeal, to be complied with at once</li></ul><h2>Final clauses</h2><ul><li>Art IX: open for signature until 31 Dec 1970, then accession</li><li>Art XI: in force on the 90th day after 15 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Art XII: denunciation takes effect one year after deposit</li><li>Art XIV: revision conference convened by IMO, or if one third of Parties ask</li><li>Art XVII: authentic languages English and French</li><li>IMO Secretary-General is the depositary</li></ul><hr><h2>1973 Protocol — substances other than oil</h2><ul><li>Adopted at the 1973 London Conference on Marine Pollution · in force 1983</li><li>Only States that are Parties to the 1969 Convention can join (Art IV); it cannot enter into force before the Convention (Art VI(1)); in force 90th day after 15 States</li><li>Art I(1): same right of intervention on the high seas for grave and imminent danger from pollution by non-oil substances, after a maritime casualty or related acts, reasonably expected to cause major harmful consequences</li><li>Art I(2): applies to (a) substances on the list in the Protocol's Annex, and (b) other substances liable to harm human health, living resources, amenities or other legitimate uses of the sea</li><li>Art I(3): for (b), the intervening Party must show the substance could reasonably pose a danger similar to the listed ones</li><li>Art II: Art I(2) and Arts II–VIII of the 1969 Convention, and its Annex, apply to non-oil substances; the expert list also covers non-oil substances</li><li>Art III — the list: an IMO body keeps it · amendments need a two-thirds majority of Parties present and voting · deemed accepted after six months unless one third of Parties object · in force three months after acceptance</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>After a casualty, a coastal State may require action on the high seas — only if the danger is grave and imminent, and the measures are proportionate</blockquote><blockquote>Compensation is due if the State went beyond what was reasonably necessary</blockquote><blockquote>Keep records of orders and of consultations with the coastal State</blockquote><blockquote>Related instruments: Salvage 1989 and Nairobi WRC 2007 (wrecks)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>1969 Convention text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1969-International-Convention-on-High-Seas-Oil-Pollution-Intervention-1.pdf</li><li>1969 Convention page (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/databasecil/1969-international-convention-relating-to-intervention-on-the-high-seas-in-cases-of-oil-pollution-casualties</li><li>1973 Protocol text (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-05/intervention-high-seas.html</li><li>IMO — Intervention Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-Relating-to-Intervention-on-the-High-Seas-in-Cases-of-Oil-Pollution-Casualties.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-llmc-1976","v":79594372,"title":"LLMC 1976","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["llmc","limitation","liability","sdr","p-and-i","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>LLMC 1976 — Convention on Limitation of Liability for Maritime Claims (with the 1996 Protocol and the 2012 limits)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1976 Convention</td><td>Adopted 19 Nov 1976 · in force 1 Dec 1986</td></tr><tr><td>1996 Protocol</td><td>Adopted 2 May 1996 · in force 13 May 2004</td></tr><tr><td>2012 limits</td><td>Adopted 19 Apr 2012 · in force 8 Jun 2015</td></tr><tr><td>Who can limit</td><td>Shipowners, salvors and their insurers</td></tr><tr><td>Right to limit lost only if</td><td>Intent, or reckless and knowing that loss would probably result (Art 4)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is LLMC</h2><ul><li>Lets shipowners and salvors limit their total liability for most maritime claims from one occasion to an amount based on the ship's gross tonnage</li><li>IMO describes the limitation as \"virtually unbreakable\"</li><li>Replaced the 1957 Brussels Convention (in force 1968)</li><li>The 1976 Convention raised limits by up to 250–300% (IMO)</li><li>The 1996 Protocol raised the amounts again and added a tacit-acceptance procedure to update them</li><li>The 2012 amendments raised the 1996 limits further</li></ul><h2>Who may limit (Art 1)</h2><ul><li>Shipowners and salvors (Art 1(1))</li><li><b>Shipowner</b> = owner, charterer, manager and operator of a seagoing ship (Art 1(2))</li><li><b>Salvor</b> = any person rendering services in direct connection with salvage operations (Art 1(3))</li><li>People for whose acts the shipowner or salvor is responsible may also limit (Art 1(4))</li><li>Liability includes an action brought against the vessel herself (Art 1(5))</li><li>Insurers get the same benefit as the insured (Art 1(6))</li><li>Invoking limitation is not an admission of liability (Art 1(7))</li></ul><h2>Claims subject to limitation (Art 2)</h2><ul><li>(a) Loss of life or personal injury, or loss of or damage to property (including harbour works, basins, waterways and aids to navigation)</li><li>(b) Loss from delay in carriage by sea of cargo, passengers or their luggage</li><li>(c) Other loss from infringement of rights other than contractual rights</li><li>(d) Raising, removal, destruction or rendering harmless of a ship (wreck)</li><li>(e) Removal, destruction or rendering harmless of the ship's cargo</li><li>(f) Claims of a person other than the person liable, for measures taken to avert or minimize loss</li><li>Art 2(2): these claims are limited even if brought by way of recourse; but (d), (e) and (f) do not apply to the extent they relate to remuneration under a contract with the person liable</li><li>Applies whatever the basis of liability</li></ul><h2>Claims not limited (Art 3, as amended by the 1996 Protocol)</h2><ul><li>(a) Salvage claims (including special compensation under the 1989 Salvage Convention) and general average contributions</li><li>(b) Oil pollution damage under the 1969 CLC</li><li>(c) Claims subject to any international convention or national law governing or prohibiting limitation for nuclear damage</li><li>(d) Claims against the shipowner of a nuclear ship for nuclear damage</li><li>(e) Claims by servants of the shipowner or salvor, whose duties are connected with the ship or salvage, where the law governing the contract of service does not allow limitation or sets a higher limit</li></ul><h2>Conduct barring limitation (Art 4)</h2><blockquote>A person is not entitled to limit if it is proved that the loss resulted from his personal act or omission, committed with the intent to cause such loss, or recklessly and with knowledge that such loss would probably result</blockquote><hr><h2>Limits of liability (SDR)</h2><p><b>Loss of life and personal injury</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship size</th><th>1976</th><th>1996 Protocol</th><th>2012 (in force 8 Jun 2015)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to 2,000 GT</td><td>333,000 (ships up to 500 tons) + tonnage amounts</td><td>2 million</td><td><b>3.02 million</b></td></tr><tr><td>2,001–30,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 800 per ton</td><td><b>+ 1,208 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>30,001–70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 600 per ton</td><td><b>+ 906 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>Over 70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 400 per ton</td><td><b>+ 604 per ton</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Other claims (property)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ship size</th><th>1976</th><th>1996 Protocol</th><th>2012 (in force 8 Jun 2015)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to 2,000 GT</td><td>167,000 (ships up to 500 tons) + tonnage amounts</td><td>1 million</td><td><b>1.51 million</b></td></tr><tr><td>2,001–30,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 400 per ton</td><td><b>+ 604 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>30,001–70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 300 per ton</td><td><b>+ 453 per ton</b></td></tr><tr><td>Over 70,000 GT</td><td></td><td>+ 200 per ton</td><td><b>+ 302 per ton</b></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Passengers (Art 7(1), 1996 Protocol)</b>: 175,000 units of account × the number of passengers the ship is certified to carry (1976 text: 46,666 per passenger, maximum 25 million)</li><li>Example, 30,000 GT ship under the 2012 limits: personal claims = 3,020,000 + 28,000 × 1,208 = 36,844,000 SDR · property claims = 1,510,000 + 28,000 × 604 = 18,422,000 SDR</li><li>Unit of account (Art 8): the Special Drawing Right of the IMF, converted to national currency on the date the fund is constituted, paid or secured</li><li>Art 9: limits apply to the total of all claims from one distinct occasion</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"llmc-per-ton\"><figcaption>Added limit per ton for other claims: 1996 Protocol and 2012</figcaption></figure><h2>Updating the limits (1996 Protocol Art 8, tacit acceptance)</h2><ul><li>A proposed amendment of the limits is deemed accepted 18 months after notification, unless at least one-fourth of the Contracting States object</li><li>Limits may not be raised by more than 6% a year (compound), or above three times the original amount</li><li>No amendment may be considered within five years of the relevant date set in the Protocol</li></ul><h2>Fund, procedure and scope</h2><ul><li><b>Limitation without a fund (Art 10)</b>: limitation can be invoked without constituting a fund; a State may require one</li><li><b>Constituting the fund (Art 11)</b>: any person alleged liable may constitute a fund with the court, as a deposit or acceptable guarantee, including interest from the date of the occurrence; a fund constituted by one liable person or their insurer is deemed constituted by all</li><li><b>Distribution (Art 12)</b>: in proportion to established claims</li><li><b>Bar to other actions (Art 13)</b>: a claimant against the fund cannot act against other assets of the fund holder; an arrested ship or security is released once the fund exists in the listed places</li><li><b>Governing law (Art 14)</b>: the law of the State where the fund is constituted</li><li><b>Counterclaims (Art 5)</b>: claims from the same occurrence are set off; the Convention applies only to the balance</li><li><b>Scope (Art 15)</b>: applies when limitation is sought before a court of a State Party. States may regulate inland waterway vessels and ships of less than 300 tons by national law. It does not apply to air-cushion vehicles or to floating platforms built to explore or exploit sea-bed resources</li><li><b>Reservations (Art 18, as amended 1996)</b>: a State may exclude Art 2(1)(d) and (e) (wreck and cargo removal) and claims under the 1996 HNS Convention; no other reservation to the substantive provisions</li></ul><h2>Entry into force</h2><ul><li>1976 Convention (Art 17): first day of the month after one year, after 12 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments → 1 Dec 1986</li><li>1996 Protocol: open for signature 1 Oct 1996 – 30 Sep 1997; in force 90 days after 10 States consented → 13 May 2004</li><li>Protocol Art 12: denunciation takes effect 12 months after the instrument is deposited</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The 2012 limits (in force 8 Jun 2015) are the current LLMC 1996 limits</blockquote><blockquote>Wreck removal claims are limited unless the State used its Art 18 reservation — see Nairobi WRC 2007</blockquote><blockquote>Oil pollution under CLC is outside LLMC (Art 3(b))</blockquote><blockquote>Limitation depends on the facts of conduct — report incidents fully to the P&amp;I Club</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>1976 LLMC treaty text (UK Treaty Series 1990 No 13): https://treaties.fcdo.gov.uk/data/Library2/pdf/1990-TS0013.pdf</li><li>1996 Protocol text: https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-07/limitation-liability-maritime.html</li><li>IMO — LLMC Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/convention-on-limitation-of-liability-for-maritime-claims-(llmc).aspx</li><li>IMO — new LLMC limits: https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/PressBriefings/Pages/24-LLMC-limits.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-load-lines-1966","v":92497146,"title":"Load Lines 1966","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["load-lines","freeboard","plimsoll","ll-protocol","certificate","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>LL 1966 — International Convention on Load Lines (with the 1988 Protocol)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>5 Apr 1966</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>21 Jul 1968</td></tr><tr><td>Annex I</td><td>Regulations for determining freeboard</td></tr><tr><td>Annex II</td><td>Zones, areas and seasonal periods</td></tr><tr><td>Annex III</td><td>Certificates</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is LL</h2><ul><li>Sets the <b>minimum freeboard</b> a ship must keep (Annex I) so that load lines are not submerged · zones and seasonal periods in Annex II · certificates in Annex III</li><li>Freeboard = vertical distance, measured amidships, from the upper edge of the deck line down to the upper edge of the load line (Reg 3)</li><li>Load lines and the deck line are marked amidships on each side of the ship</li><li>Conditions of assignment (Chapter II) keep the hull weathertight and watertight: doors, hatchways, openings, ventilators, air pipes, freeing ports, protection of the crew</li><li>Ships carrying timber deck cargo get a smaller (timber) freeboard (Chapter IV)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 5 Apr 1966 · in force 21 Jul 1968</li><li>Art 28: in force 12 months after at least 15 Governments, including 7 with at least 1 million GT of shipping, signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Amendments of 1971, 1975, 1979 and 1983 needed acceptance by two-thirds of Parties and never entered into force</li><li><b>1988 Protocol</b>: adopted 11 Nov 1988 · in force <b>3 Feb 2000</b> · harmonised survey and certification with SOLAS and MARPOL, revised certain technical annex regulations, and introduced the <b>tacit amendment</b> procedure (Annex B amendments deemed accepted after 2 years from communication, or a shorter period of at least 1 year set at adoption; in force 6 months later; a Party may exempt itself for up to 1 year)</li><li>2003 amendments: MSC.143(77), adopted 5 Jun 2003 · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2004 · in force 1 Jan 2005</li><li>Ships of 24 m and over built on or after 1 Jul 2010 must meet at least Part A of the 2008 IS Code (MSC.270(85)); ships built 21 Jul 1968 – 30 Jun 2010 must meet the stability standards in force before 1 Jan 2005 (per UK MCA guidance)</li><li>Later amendments include MSC.172(79) to MSC.375(93), A.1082(28), A.1083(28) and MSC.491(104) (in force 1 Jan 2024)</li><li><b>MSC.591(111)</b> (adopted 21 May 2026): amends Annex I Ch II <b>Reg 25 Protection of the crew</b> (guard rails: see below) · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027 · in force <b>1 Jan 2028</b> · applies to ships whose keel is laid, or which are at a similar stage of construction, on or after 1 Jan 2028</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b> (Arts 4–5)</p><ul><li>Ships of Contracting Governments (and unregistered ships flying their flag) engaged on international voyages</li><li>Annex I applies to new ships; existing ships need not increase freeboard; Annex II applies to new and existing ships</li><li>Art 5 exceptions: warships · new ships under 24 m · existing ships under 150 GT · pleasure yachts not engaged in trade · fishing vessels · ships solely on the Great Lakes/St Lawrence, Caspian Sea, and the Plate, Paraná and Uruguay Rivers (within stated limits)</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles (34)</h2><ul><li>Art 1 General obligation · Art 2 Definitions (<b>Length L = 96% of the total length on a waterline at 85% of the least moulded depth, or the stem-to-rudder-stock length on that waterline if greater</b>) · Art 3 General provisions (ships on international voyages are surveyed, marked and hold an International Load Line Certificate (1966) or an Exemption Certificate; the Administration may assign more than the minimum freeboard) · Art 4 Application · Art 5 Exceptions</li><li>Art 6 Exemptions: short voyages between neighbouring ports · ships with novel features · a single exceptional international voyage — reported to IMO</li><li>Art 7 Force majeure · Art 8 Equivalents · Art 9 Experimental approvals · Art 10 Repairs, alterations and modifications · Art 11 Zones and areas (Annex II)</li><li>Art 12 <b>Submersion</b>: load lines must not be submerged at sea, during the voyage, or on arrival; allowances for fresh water (adjusted relative to a density of 1.025) and for deeper loading when departing from rivers or inland waters</li><li>Art 13 Survey and marking (Administration officers, nominated surveyors or recognised organisations) · Art 14 <b>Surveys and inspections</b>: initial before service · periodical not exceeding 5 years · annual inspection within 3 months either side of the anniversary date (checks openings, guard rails, freeing ports and crew access; endorsed on the certificate)</li><li>Art 15 Maintenance of conditions after survey (no change without Administration sanction) · Art 16 Issue of certificates · Art 17 Issue by another Government (never to ships of non-Contracting States) · Art 18 Form of certificates (Annex III; English or French translation) </li><li>Art 19 <b>Duration</b>: up to 5 years; may be extended up to 5 months if no relevant alterations; cancelled for material alterations, fittings not maintained, missing inspection endorsements, or structure weakened so the ship is unsafe; ceases when the ship changes flag; single-voyage exemption certificate valid for that voyage</li><li>Art 20 Acceptance of certificates · Art 21 <b>Control</b>: foreign port control limited to verifying a valid certificate, that the ship is not overloaded, that the load line position matches the certificate, and that the ship has not been so materially altered as to be manifestly unfit for sea; intervention reported in writing to the flag State's consul or diplomatic representative</li><li>Art 22 Privileges only with a valid certificate · Art 23 Casualties · Art 24 Prior treaties · Art 25 Special rules by agreement · Art 26 Communication of information · Art 27 Signature, acceptance, accession · Art 28 Entry into force · Art 29 Amendments · Art 30 Denunciation (after 5 years, effective 1 year after notice) · Art 31 Suspension · Art 32 Territories · Art 33 Registration · Art 34 Languages</li></ul><p><b>Annex I — Regulations for determining load lines</b> (figures below are from the 1966 text; later amendments and the 1988 Protocol Annex B revised some regulations — check the current text before calculating)</p><ul><li><b>Chapter I General</b>: Reg 1 Strength of hull · Reg 2 Application · Reg 3 Definitions (freeboard deck, superstructure, block coefficient, perpendiculars, amidships, freeboard, length) · Reg 4 Deck line · Reg 5 Load line mark · Reg 6 Lines to be used with the load line mark · Reg 7 Mark of assigning authority · Reg 8 Details of marking · Reg 9 Verification of marks<ul><li><b>Deck line</b> (Reg 4): horizontal line 300 mm long × 25 mm wide, amidships on each side; its upper edge normally passes where the upper surface of the freeboard deck, extended outwards, meets the outer shell</li><li><b>Load line mark</b> (Reg 5): ring 300 mm outside diameter × 25 mm wide, crossed by a horizontal line 450 mm long × 25 mm wide whose upper edge passes through the ring's centre; the centre is amidships, below the deck line by the assigned summer freeboard</li><li><b>Lines</b> (Reg 6): horizontal lines 230 mm long × 25 mm wide, extending forward of a vertical line 25 mm wide set 540 mm forward of the ring centre: <b>S</b> Summer · <b>W</b> Winter · <b>WNA</b> Winter North Atlantic · <b>T</b> Tropical · <b>F</b> Fresh water in summer and <b>TF</b> Tropical fresh water (both marked abaft the vertical line) · timber load lines (LS, LW, LWNA, LT, LF, LTF) are 230 × 25 mm and extend abaft a vertical line 540 mm abaft the ring centre (LF and LTF forward) · lines that do not apply may be omitted</li><li><b>Mark of assigning authority</b> (Reg 7): not more than four initials, each about 115 mm high and 75 mm wide, alongside the ring</li><li><b>Painting</b> (Reg 8): white or yellow on a dark background, or black on a light one; also permanently marked on the sides · <b>Verification</b> (Reg 9): the certificate is not issued until the marks are verified correct and permanent</li></ul></li><li><b>Chapter II Conditions of assignment of freeboard</b>: Reg 10 Information to be supplied to the master (loading, ballasting, stability) · Reg 11 Superstructure end bulkheads · Reg 12 Doors (steel; weathertight; sills at least 380 mm above deck for superstructure access) · Reg 13 Position of hatchways, doorways and ventilators (Position 1 and Position 2) · Reg 14 Cargo and other hatchways · Reg 15 Hatchways closed by portable covers and tarpaulins · Reg 16 Hatchways closed by weathertight steel covers with gaskets and clamping devices · Reg 17 Machinery space openings · Reg 18 Miscellaneous openings in freeboard and superstructure decks · Reg 19 Ventilators · Reg 20 Air pipes · Reg 21 Cargo ports and similar openings · Reg 22 Scuppers, inlets and discharges · Reg 23 Side scuttles · Reg 24 Freeing ports · Reg 25 Protection of the crew · Reg 26 Special conditions of assignment for Type \"A\" ships<ul><li><b>Reg 25 guard rails</b> (1966 text): at least 1 m high; lowest opening not more than 230 mm; other courses not more than 380 mm apart. MSC.591(111) (ships with keel laid on/after 1 Jan 2028): guard rails or bulwarks around all exposed decks and sea access holes (e.g. moonpool edges) the crew can reach, minimum 1 m high (Administration may approve lower where 1 m interferes with normal operation); guard rails with <b>at least three courses</b>; opening below the lowest course not more than 230 mm; other courses not more than 380 mm apart; rounded-gunwale ships: supports on the flat of the deck; chains between two fixed stanchions/bulwarks may replace guard rails where necessary for normal operation (tightened as far as practicable, detachable)</li></ul></li><li><b>Chapter III Freeboards</b>: Reg 27 Types of ships (Type \"A\", Type \"B\"; in practice also Type B reduced, B increased, B timber) · Reg 28 Tabular freeboard (by length, interpolated) · Reg 29 Correction for Type B ships 24–100 m with enclosed superstructures: increase = 7.5 × (100 − L) × (0.35 − E/L) mm · Reg 30 Block coefficient correction (if Cb &gt; 0.68, multiply by (Cb + 0.68)/1.36) · Reg 31 Depth correction (if D &gt; L/15, add (D − L/15) × R; R = 0.48 L mm below 120 m, 250 mm at 120 m and over) · Reg 32 Deck line correction · Reg 33 Standard height of superstructure (raised quarter deck 0.90 m at ≤30 m, 1.20 m at 75 m, 1.80 m at ≥125 m; other superstructures 1.80 m, 1.80 m, 2.30 m) · Reg 34 Length of superstructure · Reg 35 Effective length · Reg 36 Trunks · Reg 37 Deduction for superstructures and trunks · Reg 38 Sheer · Reg 39 Minimum bow height (below 250 m: 56 L (1 − L/500) × (Cb + 0.68)/1.36 mm; 250 m and over: 7000 × (Cb + 0.68)/1.36 mm; Cb not less than 0.68) · Reg 40 Minimum freeboards<ul><li><b>Reg 40</b>: Summer = tabular freeboard with corrections (salt-water minimum 50 mm; 150 mm if non-compliant hatch covers in Position 1) · <b>Tropical</b> = summer − 1/48 of summer draught · <b>Winter</b> = summer + 1/48 of summer draught · <b>WNA</b> = winter + 50 mm, for ships of 100 m or less entering the North Atlantic winter area · <b>Fresh water</b> deduction = Δ/(4T) cm (Δ = salt-water displacement in tonnes, T = tonnes per cm immersion); if displacement cannot be certified, 1/48 of summer draught</li></ul></li><li><b>Chapter IV Timber freeboards (Regs 41–45)</b>: Reg 41 Application · Reg 42 Definitions (timber deck cargo) · Reg 43 Construction (forecastle at least standard height and 0.07 L; ships under 100 m need a poop or equivalent; bulwarks at least 1 m high) · Reg 44 Stowage (lashings at least 19 mm chain or equivalent; uprights and lashings not more than 3 m apart; in a winter seasonal zone in winter cargo height limited to one-third of extreme breadth; guard rails or life lines not more than 33 cm apart vertically) · Reg 45 Computation (winter timber = summer timber + 1/36 of moulded summer timber draught; tropical timber = summer timber − 1/48)</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"load-line-mark\"><figcaption>Load line mark (Annex I Regs 4 to 7): deck line 300 x 25 mm; ring 300 mm outside diameter, 25 mm wide, with a 450 mm line through its centre; load lines 230 x 25 mm; vertical line 540 mm forward of the ring centre. Not to scale.</figcaption></figure><p><b>Annex II — Zones, areas and seasonal periods</b> (Regs 46–52, 1966 text)</p><ul><li>Winter periods: North Atlantic Winter Zone I 16 Oct – 15 Apr · Zone II 1 Nov – 31 Mar · North Atlantic Winter Seasonal Area (over 100 m) 16 Dec – 15 Feb (100 m and under 1 Nov – 31 Mar) · North Pacific Winter Zone 16 Oct – 15 Apr · Southern Winter Zone 16 Apr – 15 Oct</li><li>Seasonal tropical areas (examples): North Atlantic 1 Nov – 15 Jul · Arabian Sea 1 Sep – 31 May · Bay of Bengal 1 Dec – 30 Apr</li><li>Enclosed seas, ships 100 m and under: Baltic winter 1 Nov – 31 Mar · Black Sea north of 44°N winter 1 Dec – end Feb · Sea of Japan north of 50°N winter 1 Dec – end Feb · Mediterranean (specified western part) winter 16 Dec – 15 Mar</li><li>Reg 52 Winter North Atlantic load line: part of Winter Zone II between 15°W and 50°W, and all of Winter Zone I</li><li>Art 11: a port on a zone boundary is treated as in the zone the ship arrives from or departs to</li></ul><h2>Annex III — Certificates</h2><ul><li><b>International Load Line Certificate (1966)</b>: ship name, distinctive number or letters, port of registry, length L · assigned type (A, B, B with reduced freeboard, B with increased freeboard) · freeboards from the deck line for T, S, W, WNA and the timber equivalents · fresh water allowance · survey date · reverse side: endorsements from periodical inspections and extensions · valid up to 5 years (Art 19)</li><li><b>International Load Line Exemption Certificate</b>: exemptions under Art 6(2) and any single-voyage exemption under Art 6(4)</li><li>Fresh water allowance adjusted in proportion to the difference of the water density from 1.025; allowance for river or inland-water departure for fuel and consumables used to reach the sea</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Never submerge the load line (for the zone and season) at departure, during the voyage or on arrival (Art 12)</blockquote><blockquote>Annual inspection within 3 months before or after each anniversary; renewal every ≤5 years</blockquote><blockquote>Port State control (Art 21): valid certificate, not overloaded, load line position matches the certificate, no material alteration making the ship unfit for sea</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Convention text, Arts 1–34 (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1966-International-Convention-on-Load-Lines.pdf</li><li>Convention text with Annexes (UK Treaty Series 1968): https://treaties.fcdo.gov.uk/data/Library2/pdf/1968-TS0058.pdf</li><li>IMO — Load Lines Convention: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-Load-Lines.aspx</li><li>UK MCA — MSIS41 International Load Line: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/644633e96dda69000d11e2e6/MSIS41_International_Load_Line_R05.23.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.591(111) (Reg 25 amendment): https://ltsa.lrv.lt/public/canonical/1787556830/2291/MSC.591(111).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-london-convention-and-protocol","v":34924803,"title":"London Convention and Protocol","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["london-convention","london-protocol","dumping","waste","co2","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>London Convention 1972 and London Protocol 1996 — Prevention of Marine Pollution by Dumping of Wastes and Other Matter</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>London Convention (LC)</th><th>London Protocol (LP)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>13 Nov 1972, London</td><td>7 Nov 1996</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>30 Aug 1975</td><td>24 Mar 2006</td></tr><tr><td>Approach</td><td>Lists: banned (Annex I), special permit (Annex II), general permit (all other)</td><td><b>All dumping banned except the \"reverse list\"</b> (Annex 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>15 ratifications (Art XIX)</td><td>30th day after 26 States, incl. 15 LC Parties (Art 25)</td></tr><tr><td>Administered by</td><td>IMO (since 1977)</td><td>IMO</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is LC / LP</h2><ul><li>Global control of the deliberate disposal of wastes at sea</li><li>LC aim (Art I): prevent pollution of the sea by dumping that could harm human health, living resources, amenities or other legitimate uses</li><li>LP is intended to eventually replace the LC (IMO)</li><li>LP principles: <b>precautionary approach</b> (Art 3.1 — action even without conclusive evidence of a causal link) · <b>polluter pays</b> (Art 3.2) · no transfer of pollution from one part of the environment to another (IMO)</li><li>LP Art 2: Parties act according to their scientific, technical and economic capabilities to prevent, reduce and where practicable eliminate pollution from dumping or incineration</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>LC adopted 13 Nov 1972 · in force 30 Aug 1975 · IMO administers it since 1977</li><li>1993 LC amendments (in force 1994): ban on dumping low-level radioactive waste · industrial waste dumping phased out by 31 Dec 1995 · ban on incineration at sea of industrial waste</li><li>LP adopted 7 Nov 1996 · in force <b>24 Mar 2006</b></li><li><b>2006 amendment</b> (LP.1(1)): adds CO₂ sequestration in sub-seabed geological formations to Annex 1 · adopted 2 Nov 2006 · in force 10 Feb 2007 (Canada 29 Jan 2007) · excludes use of CO₂ streams for enhanced oil recovery (IMO)</li><li><b>2009 amendment</b> (LP.3(4), adopted 30 Oct 2009): Art 6 — export of CO₂ streams for sub-seabed storage allowed between countries with an agreement · not yet in force: takes effect 60 days after two-thirds of Parties accept (IMO) · <b>2019 resolution LP.5(14)</b> allows provisional application, by a declaration to the IMO Secretary-General</li><li><b>2013 amendment</b> (LP.4(8), adopted 18 Oct 2013): marine geoengineering — new Art 6bis, Annex 4 (ocean fertilization), Annex 5 (assessment framework)</li></ul><hr><h2>London Convention 1972</h2><p><b>Definition of dumping (Art III)</b></p><ul><li>Deliberate disposal at sea of wastes or other matter from vessels, aircraft or platforms, and the disposal of vessels or structures themselves</li><li>Not dumping: waste incidental to or from normal operation of vessels and platforms (unless the vessel carries waste for disposal) · placement of matter for a purpose other than mere disposal · waste from seabed mineral exploration and exploitation</li></ul><p><b>Permits (Art IV)</b></p><ul><li>Annex I matter: <b>prohibited</b></li><li>Annex II matter: <b>prior special permit</b></li><li>All other matter: <b>prior general permit</b></li><li>Permits only after careful consideration of the Annex III factors</li><li>Art V: force majeure — dumping allowed to secure the safety of human life or vessels when it is the only way to avert the threat, and must be reported to IMO</li><li>Art VI: each Party designates an authority to issue permits, keep records of the nature and quantities of matter dumped, and monitor the sea</li><li>Art VII: Parties apply the Convention to their vessels and aircraft, those loading in their territory, and must prevent and punish contraventions</li></ul><p><b>Annex I — prohibited</b></p><ul><li>Organohalogen compounds · mercury and compounds · cadmium and compounds</li><li>Persistent plastics and other persistent synthetic materials (for example netting and ropes) that interfere with fishing or navigation</li><li>Crude oil and its wastes, refined petroleum products and distillate residues taken aboard for dumping</li><li>Radioactive wastes or other radioactive matter · materials produced for biological and chemical warfare</li><li>(Added later) incineration at sea of industrial waste and sewage sludge prohibited; industrial waste covered from 1 Jan 1996</li></ul><p><b>Annex II — special permit</b></p><ul><li>Wastes with significant amounts of arsenic, beryllium, chromium, copper, lead, nickel, vanadium, zinc, organosilicon compounds, cyanides, fluorides, and pesticides not covered by Annex I</li><li>Containers, scrap metal and bulky wastes that may sink and obstruct fishing or navigation</li><li>Materials that are non-toxic but may become harmful in quantity or seriously reduce amenities</li></ul><p><b>Annex III — permit factors</b></p><ul><li>A Characteristics and composition of the matter · B Characteristics of the dumping site and method of deposit · C General considerations and conditions</li></ul><hr><h2>London Protocol 1996 — the Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>2–3</td><td>Objectives and general obligations</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Dumping of wastes or other matter</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Incineration at sea</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Export of wastes or other matter</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Internal waters</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Exceptions</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Issuance of permits and reporting</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Application and enforcement</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Compliance procedures</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Regional co-operation</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Technical co-operation and assistance</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Scientific and technical research</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Responsibility and liability</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Meetings of Contracting Parties</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Duties of the Organization</td></tr><tr><td>21–22</td><td>Amendment of the Protocol and of the Annexes</td></tr><tr><td>25–26</td><td>Entry into force · transitional period</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Key articles</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 1 definitions:</b> dumping = any deliberate disposal into the sea of wastes or other matter from vessels, aircraft, platforms or other man-made structures; includes storage in the seabed and subsoil, and abandonment or toppling of platforms for the sole purpose of deliberate disposal · incineration at sea = combustion on board a vessel, platform or other man-made structure at sea (waste from normal operations excluded) · \"sea\" = all marine waters other than internal waters</li><li><b>Art 4.1.1:</b> Parties shall prohibit dumping of any wastes or other matter <b>except those listed in Annex 1</b>; Art 4.1.2: dumping of Annex 1 matter needs a permit that meets Annex 2</li><li><b>Art 5:</b> incineration at sea prohibited</li><li><b>Art 6:</b> no export of wastes to other countries for dumping or incineration at sea (2009 amendment adds a CO₂ exception)</li><li><b>Art 7:</b> in internal waters a Party may apply the Protocol or other effective permitting and regulatory measures</li><li><b>Art 8:</b> force majeure (\"stress of weather\") — Arts 4.1 and 5 do not apply when it is the only way to avert a threat; reported forthwith; emergency permits possible after consultation</li><li><b>Art 9:</b> each Party designates authorities that issue permits, keep records and monitor the sea; annual reports to IMO</li><li><b>Art 10:</b> applies to vessels and aircraft registered or flying the flag of a Party, and to those loading waste in its territory</li><li><b>Art 11:</b> compliance procedures set by the Meeting of Contracting Parties</li><li><b>Art 26:</b> a State may notify a transitional period, not longer than five years</li></ul><h2>Annex 1 — the \"reverse list\"</h2><p>Only these may be considered for dumping, and only with a permit meeting Annex 2:</p><ul><li>1.1 Dredged material</li><li>1.2 Sewage sludge</li><li>1.3 Fish waste, or material from industrial fish processing</li><li>1.4 Vessels and platforms or other man-made structures at sea</li><li>1.5 Inert, inorganic geological material</li><li>1.6 Organic material of natural origin</li><li>1.7 Bulky items primarily of iron, steel, concrete and similarly unharmful materials</li><li>1.8 Carbon dioxide streams from CO₂ capture processes for sequestration</li></ul><p><b>Conditions in Annex 1</b></p><ul><li>Items 1.4 and 1.7: material that could create floating debris must be removed, and the item must not seriously obstruct fishing or navigation</li><li>Materials with radioactivity above de minimis levels are not eligible; a scientific study is due every 25 years from 20 Feb 1994</li><li>CO₂ streams: only into a sub-seabed geological formation, must consist overwhelmingly of CO₂, no waste added for disposal</li></ul><h2>Annex 2 — assessment of wastes</h2><ul><li>General · waste prevention audit · consideration of waste management options · chemical, physical and biological properties · action list · dump-site selection · assessment of potential effects · monitoring · permit and permit conditions</li></ul><h2>Marine geoengineering (2013 amendment LP.4(8))</h2><ul><li>Marine geoengineering = deliberate intervention in the marine environment to manipulate natural processes, with potential deleterious effects (Art 1.5bis)</li><li>Annex 4 lists <b>ocean fertilization</b> only: activity intended mainly to stimulate ocean primary productivity; aquaculture, mariculture and artificial reefs excluded</li><li>All ocean fertilization is prohibited, <b>except legitimate scientific research</b>, which may get a permit after assessment under Annex 5 (the 2010 Assessment Framework)</li><li>Annex 5 requires a full project description, a purpose other than mere disposal, and proof of financial resources; research must be peer reviewed, results published, no direct financial gain</li><li>Entry into force: not stated in the text I checked, so it is not given here</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>LP bans all dumping except Annex 1 items, and then only with a permit</blockquote><blockquote>Ship garbage, sewage and oily waste are MARPOL matters, not LC/LP dumping — LC excludes waste incidental to normal vessel operation</blockquote><blockquote>Disposing of a vessel at sea is \"dumping\" — permitted under the LP only as an Annex 1 item (1.4) with a permit</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>1996 London Protocol text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1996-Prot-to-1972-Convention-on-Prevention-of-MP-by-Dumping-1.pdf</li><li>1972 London Convention text (US EPA): https://www.epa.gov/sites/production/files/2015-10/documents/lc1972.pdf</li><li>IMO — London Convention and Protocol: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Convention-on-the-Prevention-of-Marine-Pollution-by-Dumping-of-Wastes-and-Other-Matter.aspx</li><li>IMO — Carbon Capture and Sequestration: https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/environment/pages/ccs-default.aspx</li><li>2009 amendment LP.3(4): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/2009-Amd-to-London-Prot.pdf</li><li>2013 amendment LP.4(8): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Environment/Documents/LC_LP/LP.4(8).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-nairobi-wrc-2007","v":75905746,"title":"Nairobi WRC 2007","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["nairobi","wreck-removal","wrc","imo","convention","liability"],"body":"<h1>Nairobi WRC 2007 — Nairobi International Convention on the Removal of Wrecks</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>18 May 2007 (Nairobi)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>14 Apr 2015</td></tr><tr><td>Owner liability</td><td>Locate, mark and remove hazardous wreck (Art 10)</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Ships ≥ 300 GT (Art 12)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the WRC</h2><ul><li>IMO convention giving States a legal basis to remove, or arrange the removal of, wrecks that threaten safety at sea or the marine environment, with uniform rules beyond the territorial sea</li><li>Registered owner is liable for the costs of locating, marking and removing a hazardous wreck (Art 10)</li><li>Compulsory insurance or other financial security for ships ≥300 GT flying a Party's flag · certificate carried on board (Art 12)</li><li>The affected State may remove the wreck at the owner's expense if the owner does not (Art 9)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 18 May 2007 at an international conference in Nairobi, Kenya</li><li>Open for signature at IMO HQ 19 Nov 2007 – 18 Nov 2008, then accession (Art 17)</li><li>Art 18: in force 12 months after 10 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments · in force <b>14 Apr 2015</b></li></ul><h2>Applies to</h2><ul><li>Convention area: a Party's exclusive economic zone (or, if none, an area adjacent to the territorial sea extending no more than 200 nm from the baselines) — Art 1</li><li>Territory and territorial sea: only if the Party opts in by notifying the IMO Secretary-General (Art 3(2)); then only Arts 7–9 (locating, marking, removal) apply there</li><li>Ship = seagoing vessel of any type: hydrofoils, air-cushion vehicles, submersibles, floating craft and platforms — except platforms engaged in seabed mineral exploration, exploitation or production</li><li>Not covered (Art 4): measures under the 1969 Intervention Convention and its 1973 Protocol · warships and government non-commercial ships, unless the State decides otherwise</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"wrc-convention-area\"><figcaption>Convention area: the exclusive economic zone (up to 200 nm from the baselines if no EEZ is declared). The territory and territorial sea are covered only if the State opts in (Art 3(2)), and then only Arts 7 to 9 apply. Drawn from the Convention text.</figcaption></figure><h2>Key definitions (Art 1)</h2><ul><li>Wreck: after a maritime casualty — a sunken or stranded ship · any part of a ship · any object lost from a ship that is adrift or stranded · a ship about to sink or strand if effective assistance is not already being given</li><li>Hazard: a condition or threat that impedes navigation, or may reasonably cause major harmful consequences to the marine environment, the coastline or related interests of one or more States</li><li>Related interests: e.g. fisheries, tourism, public health, marine conservation, offshore infrastructure</li><li>Affected State: the State in whose Convention area the wreck lies</li><li>Registered owner: the person registered as owner, or if none the owner at the time of the casualty</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles (21)</h2><ul><li>Art 1 Definitions · Art 2 Objectives and general principles · Art 3 Scope of application · Art 4 Exclusions</li><li>Art 5 Reporting wrecks · Art 6 Determination of hazard · Art 7 Locating wrecks · Art 8 Marking of wrecks</li><li>Art 9 Measures to facilitate the removal of wrecks · Art 10 Liability of the owner · Art 11 Exceptions to liability</li><li>Art 12 Compulsory insurance or other financial security · Art 13 Time limits</li><li>Art 14 Amendment provisions · Art 15 Settlement of disputes · Art 16 Relationship to other conventions and international agreements</li><li>Art 17 Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession · Art 18 Entry into force · Art 19 Denunciation · Art 20 Depositary · Art 21 Languages</li><li>Annex: model certificate of insurance or other financial security</li></ul><h2>Art 2 — Objectives</h2><ul><li>Measures must be proportionate to the hazard, no more than reasonably necessary, and end once the wreck is removed</li><li>The Convention gives no State sovereignty or sovereign rights over the high seas</li></ul><h2>Art 5 — Reporting wrecks</h2><ul><li>The master and the operator of a ship flying the flag of a Party report to the affected State without delay (one report from either satisfies both)</li><li>Report contents:<ul><li>Name and principal place of business of the registered owner</li><li>Precise location of the wreck</li><li>Type, size and construction of the wreck</li><li>Nature of the damage and condition of the wreck</li><li>Nature and quantity of cargo, especially hazardous and noxious substances</li><li>Amount and types of oil on board, including bunker oil and lubricating oil</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Art 6 — Determination of hazard</b> (the affected State considers)</p><ul><li>Type, size and construction of the wreck · water depth · tidal range and currents</li><li>Particularly sensitive sea areas (and areas under UNCLOS Art 211(6) special measures)</li><li>Proximity to shipping routes or traffic lanes · traffic density and frequency · type of traffic</li><li>Type and quantity of cargo and oil, and the damage likely if released</li><li>Vulnerability of port facilities · prevailing weather and hydrographic conditions · submarine topography</li><li>Height of the wreck above or below the lowest astronomical tide</li><li>Acoustic and magnetic profiles of the wreck</li><li>Proximity to offshore installations, pipelines, telecommunications cables and similar structures</li><li>Any other circumstances that might require removal</li></ul><h2>Art 7 — Locating wrecks</h2><ul><li>On learning of a wreck, the affected State urgently uses all practicable means to warn mariners and concerned States</li><li>If it has reason to believe the wreck is a hazard, it takes all practicable steps to establish the exact location</li></ul><h2>Art 8 — Marking wrecks</h2><ul><li>If the wreck is a hazard, the affected State takes all reasonable steps to mark it, using the internationally accepted buoyage system for the area</li><li>It publicizes the marking details, including through nautical publications</li></ul><h2>Art 9 — Measures to facilitate removal</h2><ul><li>The affected State informs the registry State and the registered owner at once, and consults them</li><li>The registered owner must remove a wreck found to be a hazard, and give evidence of insurance or financial security (Art 12)</li><li>The owner may contract any salvor; the State may set conditions only as needed for safety and environmental protection</li><li>The State sets a reasonable deadline, tells the owner in writing, and says it will remove the wreck at the owner's expense if the deadline is missed; it may act immediately if the hazard becomes severe</li><li>If the owner misses the deadline or cannot be contacted, or in an emergency, the State may remove the wreck by the most practical and expeditious means, consistent with safety and the environment</li></ul><h2>Art 10 — Liability of the owner (strict)</h2><ul><li>The registered owner pays the costs of locating, marking and removing the wreck, unless the owner proves the casualty:<ul><li>resulted from an act of war, hostilities, civil war, insurrection, or an exceptional, inevitable and irresistible natural phenomenon</li><li>was wholly caused by an act or omission of a third party done with intent to cause damage</li><li>was wholly caused by the negligence or wrongful act of a government or other authority responsible for the maintenance of lights or other navigational aids</li></ul></li><li>The owner keeps the right to limit liability under national or international regimes (e.g. LLMC 1976)</li><li>Costs can be claimed against the owner only under this Convention · rights of recourse against third parties are kept</li></ul><h2>Art 11 — Exceptions to liability</h2><ul><li>The owner is not liable under this Convention where that would conflict with the applicable liability conventions: the CLC (oil pollution), the 1996 HNS Convention, the Bunker Oil Convention 2001, and nuclear liability conventions or national law</li><li>If the measures count as salvage under national law or another convention, that law or convention governs the salvor's reward</li></ul><h2>Art 12 — Compulsory insurance or other financial security</h2><ul><li>Registered owner of a ship ≥300 GT flying a Party's flag must have insurance or other financial security</li><li>Amount: the limitation amount under LLMC 1976 Art 6(1)(b)</li><li>Certificate: issued by the registry State (for an unregistered ship, any Party may issue it) · carried on board · a copy filed with the authority that keeps the registry</li><li>Certificate shows: ship name and port of registry, gross tonnage, IMO number, owner, type and duration of security, insurer, period of validity</li><li>Cover cannot end before expiry without 3 months' notice to the authorities (unless a replacement certificate is issued or the old one is surrendered)</li><li>Each Party ensures, under its national law, that ships ≥300 GT entering or leaving its ports, or arriving at or leaving its offshore facilities in the territorial sea, have this cover, whatever their flag</li><li>Direct action: claims for costs can be brought directly against the insurer · the insurer may use the owner's defences (including limitation) and wilful misconduct, but not the owner's bankruptcy</li><li>State-owned ships: no insurance needed; they carry a certificate from the registry State</li></ul><h2>Art 13 — Time limits</h2><ul><li>Right to recover costs lapses unless action is brought within 3 years of the date the hazard was determined</li><li>Never later than 6 years from the date of the casualty (for a series of occurrences, from the first one)</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Onboard: after a casualty resulting in a wreck, the master and operator report to the affected State without delay (owner, precise position, damage, cargo, oil on board)</blockquote><blockquote>Keep the wreck removal insurance certificate valid and on board (≥300 GT)</blockquote><blockquote>The owner pays for locating, marking and removal; the State may remove the wreck itself if the deadline is missed or in an emergency, at the owner's expense</blockquote><blockquote>The owner may still limit liability (e.g. LLMC 1976)</blockquote><h2>Sources (re-checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Treaty text (University of Oslo library): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-04/imo_removal_wrecks.html</li><li>IMO — Nairobi International Convention on the Removal of Wrecks: https://imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/Nairobi-International-Convention-on-the-Removal-of-Wrecks.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-oprc-1990","v":117579828,"title":"OPRC 1990","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["oprc","oprc-hns","oil-pollution","sopep","smpep","contingency","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>OPRC 1990 — International Convention on Oil Pollution Preparedness, Response and Co-operation (and the OPRC-HNS Protocol 2000)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>OPRC 1990</th><th>OPRC-HNS Protocol 2000</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>30 Nov 1990, London</td><td>15 Mar 2000, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>13 May 1995</td><td>14 Jun 2007</td></tr><tr><td>Covers</td><td>Oil pollution incidents</td><td>Hazardous and noxious substances (anything other than oil likely to harm health, living resources, amenities or other legitimate uses of the sea)</td></tr><tr><td>In force rule</td><td>12 months after 15 States (Art 16)</td><td>12 months after 15 States (Art 15)</td></tr><tr><td>Ship plan</td><td>SOPEP (MARPOL Annex I Reg 37)</td><td>Plan under MARPOL Annex II Reg 17; combined plan = SMPEP</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is OPRC</h2><ul><li>A global framework for international co-operation in preparing for and responding to major oil pollution incidents or threats</li><li>Started with a July 1989 conference of leading industrial nations in Paris, which asked IMO to develop further measures; the IMO Assembly endorsed this in Nov 1989 (IMO)</li><li>Parties: take all appropriate measures to prepare for and respond to oil pollution incidents (Art 1(1))</li><li>Annex is an integral part of the Convention (Art 1(2))</li><li>Warships, naval auxiliaries and other State ships on non-commercial service are excluded; Parties still ensure they act consistently where reasonable (Art 1(3))</li><li>IMO has an important coordinating role</li></ul><h2>Definitions (Art 2)</h2><ul><li><b>Oil</b>: petroleum in any form, including crude oil, fuel oil, sludge, oil refuse and refined products</li><li><b>Oil pollution incident</b>: an occurrence or series of occurrences with the same origin that results or may result in a discharge of oil, threatens the marine environment or the coastline or related interests of one or more States, and needs emergency or other immediate response</li><li><b>Ship</b>: any vessel operating in the marine environment, including hydrofoils, air-cushion vehicles, submersibles and floating craft</li><li><b>Offshore unit</b>: fixed or floating installation engaged in oil or gas exploration, exploitation or production, or loading or unloading of oil</li><li><b>Sea ports and oil handling facilities</b>: those that present a risk of an oil pollution incident, including ports, oil terminals and pipelines</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles</h2><p><b>Art 3 — Oil pollution emergency plans</b></p><ul><li>3(1)(a): ships flying a Party's flag carry a shipboard oil pollution emergency plan, as required by IMO</li><li>3(1)(b): such a ship may be inspected in a port or offshore terminal under a Party's jurisdiction</li><li>3(2): offshore unit operators under a Party's jurisdiction have plans coordinated with the national system (Art 6) and approved by the national authority</li><li>3(3): ports and oil handling facilities have plans or similar arrangements coordinated with the national system</li></ul><p><b>Art 4 — Reporting</b></p><ul><li>Masters or persons in charge of ships and offshore units report without delay any event involving a discharge or probable discharge of oil — ships to the nearest coastal State; offshore units to the coastal State they are under</li><li>Same duty for any observed event at sea involving a discharge or the presence of oil</li><li>Persons in charge of ports and oil handling facilities report to the competent national authority</li><li>Maritime inspection vessels and aircraft are instructed to report; civil aircraft pilots are requested to report to the nearest coastal State</li><li>Reports follow IMO guidelines</li></ul><p><b>Art 5 — Action on receiving a report</b></p><ul><li>The Party assesses whether an incident has occurred, and its nature, extent and consequences, then promptly informs affected or likely affected States</li><li>Where severity justifies, informs IMO directly or via regional bodies</li></ul><p><b>Art 6 — National and regional systems</b></p><ul><li>Each Party establishes a national response system, with: designated competent authority · national operational contact point · an authority entitled to request or grant assistance · a <b>national contingency plan</b></li><li>Within its capabilities: pre-positioned oil spill combating equipment · programme of exercises and training · detailed response plans and communications · mechanism to coordinate response</li><li>Keeps IMO informed of contact points, equipment, expertise and the national contingency plan</li></ul><p><b>Other articles</b></p><ul><li>Art 7 International co-operation: advice, technical support and equipment on request (subject to capabilities); costs follow the Annex; Parties facilitate entry, use and departure of response ships, aircraft, personnel and equipment</li><li>Art 8 Research and development · Art 9 Technical co-operation · Art 10 Bilateral and multilateral co-operation · Art 11 Relation to other conventions</li><li>Art 12 Institutional arrangements: IMO provides information services, education and training, technical services and technical assistance</li><li>Art 13 Evaluation · Art 14 Amendments (two-thirds of Parties accept; in force six months later for those that accepted)</li><li>Art 15 Signature: 30 Nov 1990 – 29 Nov 1991, then accession</li><li>Art 16 Entry into force: 12 months after 15 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Art 17 Denunciation after five years; takes effect 12 months after notice · Art 19: done at London 30 Nov 1990</li></ul><p><b>Annex — reimbursement of costs of assistance</b></p><ul><li>Without a prior agreement: if a Party acts at another Party's express request, the requester reimburses the assisting Party (it may cancel but pays costs already incurred); if a Party acts on its own initiative, it bears its own costs</li><li>Reimbursable costs are calculated under the assisting Party's law and practice, unless agreed otherwise</li><li>The requesting Party may ask for waivers, reductions or postponements, with regard to developing countries</li><li>Does not prejudice recovery of costs from third parties under other laws (CLC 1969, FUND 1971)</li></ul><hr><h2>Shipboard plans (SOPEP / SMPEP)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Rule</th><th>Ships</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SOPEP</td><td>MARPOL Annex I Reg 37</td><td>Oil tankers of 150 GT and above; all other ships of 400 GT and above</td></tr><tr><td>Marine pollution plan for noxious liquid substances</td><td>MARPOL Annex II Reg 17</td><td>Ships of 150 GT and above certified to carry noxious liquid substances in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>SMPEP</td><td>Combined SOPEP + NLS plan</td><td>Practical, because the two largely overlap</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Plans must be approved</li><li>The plan carries, as an appendix, the <b>List of National Operational Contact Points</b> — the agencies or officials that receive and process incident reports</li><li>IMO Guidelines for the Development of Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plans (2010 edition): SOPEP guidelines — MEPC.54(32) as amended by MEPC.86(44) · SMPEP guidelines — MEPC.85(44) as amended by MEPC.137(53)</li></ul><h2>OPRC-HNS Protocol 2000</h2><ul><li>Open for signature 15 Mar 2000 – 14 Mar 2001, then accession; only OPRC Parties may join (Art 13)</li><li>In force 12 months after 15 States; for later States, three months after deposit (Art 15)</li><li>Art 1: same duties, for HNS pollution incidents; warships and non-commercial State ships excluded</li><li>Art 2: HNS = any substance other than oil which, if introduced into the marine environment, is likely to create hazards to human health, harm living resources, damage amenities or interfere with other legitimate uses of the sea</li><li>Art 3: ships carry an on-board pollution incident emergency plan; masters follow reporting procedures; ports and HNS handling facilities have plans coordinated with the national system; a Party that learns of an incident notifies States likely to be affected. Floating production and storage units (FPSO, FSU) are not covered by this article</li><li>Art 4: national and regional systems (authorities, contact points, national contingency plan, equipment, exercises, training)</li><li>Art 5: international co-operation · Art 6 research · Art 7 technical co-operation · Art 8 bilateral/multilateral agreements</li><li>Denouncing the OPRC Convention also denounces the Protocol (Art 16(4))</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>SOPEP and, where certified for noxious liquids, a marine pollution plan (SMPEP) must be approved and on board — check the ship-size thresholds above</blockquote><blockquote>Report any discharge or sighting of oil at sea to the nearest coastal State without delay</blockquote><blockquote>Keep the list of national operational contact points up to date</blockquote><blockquote>A port State may inspect the shipboard plan (Art 3(1)(b))</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>OPRC Convention text (Univ. of Oslo): https://www.jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-05/convention-oil-pollution.xml</li><li>OPRC-HNS Protocol text (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/06/6-04/co-operation-pollution-incidents.html</li><li>IMO — OPRC: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-oil-pollution-preparedness,-response-and-co-operation-(oprc).aspx</li><li>IMO — Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plans: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/Shipboard-Marine-Pollution-Emergency-Plans.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-sar-1979","v":121470326,"title":"SAR 1979","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["sar","search-and-rescue","rcc","iamsar","place-of-safety","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>SAR 1979 — International Convention on Maritime Search and Rescue</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>27 Apr 1979, Hamburg</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>22 Jun 1985</td></tr><tr><td>Revised Annex</td><td>Adopted May 1998 · in force Jan 2000</td></tr><tr><td>2004 amendments</td><td>MSC.155(78), adopted 20 May 2004 · in force 1 Jul 2006</td></tr><tr><td>Global plan</td><td>13 SAR areas (provisional plans complete at Fremantle, Sep 1998)</td></tr><tr><td>Manual</td><td>IAMSAR Manual (IMO and ICAO), 3 volumes</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is SAR</h2><ul><li>An international SAR plan, so that a rescue anywhere at sea is coordinated by a SAR organization, helped by neighbouring organizations when needed (IMO)</li><li>IMO's Maritime Safety Committee divided the oceans into <b>13 SAR areas</b>; countries delimit their own SAR regions inside them</li><li>Art I: Parties adopt all measures needed to give full effect to the Convention and its Annex</li><li>The Convention has 8 Articles: I General obligations · II Other treaties and interpretation · III Amendments · IV Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession · V Entry into force · VI Denunciation · VII Deposit and registration · VIII Languages</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 27 Apr 1979 in Hamburg · in force 22 Jun 1985</li><li><b>1998 revised Annex</b> (adopted May 1998, in force Jan 2000): clearer government responsibilities, regional approach, better co-ordination between maritime and aeronautical SAR; RCCs to run 24 hours a day with English-speaking staff; encourages immediate entry of other States' rescue units into territorial waters for SAR; operations continue \"until all reasonable hope of rescuing survivors has passed\"</li><li><b>2004 amendments</b> (MSC.155(78)): adopted 20 May 2004 · deemed accepted 1 Jan 2006 unless more than one third of Parties objected · in force <b>1 Jul 2006</b></li><li>Global plan: provisional plans for all 13 areas completed when the Indian Ocean plans were finalized at a conference in Fremantle, Western Australia, in Sep 1998</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex — Chapter 1: Terms (1.3)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Search (1.3.1)</td><td>An operation, normally co-ordinated by an RCC or sub-centre, to locate persons in distress</td></tr><tr><td>Rescue (1.3.2)</td><td>An operation to retrieve persons in distress and <b>deliver them to a place of safety</b></td></tr><tr><td>SAR region (1.3.4)</td><td>An area of defined dimensions associated with an RCC</td></tr><tr><td>RCC (1.3.5)</td><td>A unit responsible for promoting efficient organization of SAR services</td></tr><tr><td>RSC (1.3.6)</td><td>A unit subordinate to an RCC</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty phase (1.3.11)</td><td>Uncertainty exists as to the safety of a person, vessel or other craft</td></tr><tr><td>Alert phase (1.3.12)</td><td>Apprehension exists as to the safety of a person, vessel or other craft</td></tr><tr><td>Distress phase (1.3.13)</td><td>Reasonable certainty that a person, vessel or other craft is threatened by grave and imminent danger</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter 2 — Organization and co-ordination</h2><ul><li>2.1.1: Parties ensure assistance is rendered to any person in distress at sea</li><li>2.1.2: Parties establish basic service elements — legal framework, responsible authority, resources, communications, operations, improvement processes</li><li>2.1.3–2.1.7: SAR regions, preferably contiguous and not overlapping, are set <b>by agreement among the Parties concerned</b>; delimitation does not prejudice State boundaries</li><li>2.1.10: assistance is provided to any person in distress at sea, regardless of nationality, status or the circumstances</li><li>2.3: RCCs operate on a <b>24-hour basis</b> with trained personnel</li><li>2.4: closest practicable co-ordination with aeronautical services; joint centres where practicable</li><li>2.5: Parties designate facilities able to take part, as SAR units</li></ul><h2>Chapter 3 — Co-operation between States</h2><ul><li>3.1.1: Parties co-ordinate their SAR organizations, and with neighbouring States where necessary</li><li>3.1.2: unless agreed otherwise, a Party should authorize immediate entry of other Parties' rescue units into its territory or territorial sea</li><li>3.1.3–3.1.4: the requesting Party sends a request with full details to the other Party's RCC; the receiving authority acknowledges promptly and states conditions</li><li>3.1.5: agreements with neighbouring States on entry conditions</li><li>3.1.6: RCCs may request assistance and grant entry permissions</li></ul><h2>Chapter 4 — Operating procedures</h2><ul><li>4.1 Preparatory measures: up-to-date facility data, vessel position data, operational plans, readiness</li><li>4.2 Information concerning emergencies: 24-hour capability to receive distress alerts</li><li>4.3 Initial action: a unit receiving distress information acts at once if it can help, and notifies the RCC or RSC without delay</li><li>4.4 Emergency phases: 4.4.1 uncertainty · 4.4.2 alert · 4.4.3 distress</li><li>4.5 Procedures during the phases (including unknown position)</li><li>4.6 Co-ordination when two or more Parties are involved</li><li>4.7 On-scene co-ordination: \"the most capable person\" is designated the on-scene co-ordinator</li><li>4.8 Termination and suspension: operations continue, when practicable, until all reasonable hope of rescuing survivors has passed</li></ul><h2>Chapter 5 — Ship reporting systems</h2><ul><li>5.1 General · 5.2 Operational requirements · 5.3 Types of reports: <b>sailing plan, position report, final report</b> · 5.4 Use of the systems</li></ul><hr><h2>2004 amendments (in force 1 Jul 2006) — place of safety</h2><ul><li><b>2.1.1</b>: adds a definition of \"person in distress at sea\" — includes people in need of assistance who reached a remote coast in an area that rescue facilities can reach only as the Annex provides</li><li><b>3.1.6.4</b> (new): RCCs co-operate to identify the most appropriate place(s) for disembarking people found in distress</li><li><b>3.1.9</b> (new): Parties co-ordinate and co-operate so that masters who provide assistance by embarking persons in distress at sea are released from their obligations with <b>minimum further deviation</b> from the voyage, provided this does not further endanger life at sea; the <b>Party responsible for the SAR region</b> in which the assistance is rendered has <b>primary responsibility</b> for this</li><li><b>4.8.5</b> (new): the RCC or RSC starts the process of identifying the most appropriate place(s) for disembarking persons found in distress at sea, and informs the ship and other parties concerned</li></ul><h2>IAMSAR Manual</h2><ul><li>Developed jointly by IMO and ICAO; three volumes: <b>Organization and Management · Mission Co-ordination · Mobile Facilities</b></li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The State responsible for the SAR region has primary responsibility to arrange the place of safety; the master is released with minimum further deviation</blockquote><blockquote>A rescue means delivery to a place of safety (1.3.2)</blockquote><blockquote>Phases: uncertainty → alert → distress</blockquote><blockquote>Operations continue until all reasonable hope of rescuing survivors has passed</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>SAR Convention text, as amended: https://sosmediterranee.org/app/uploads/2023/10/sar-convention-1979.pdf</li><li>IMO — SAR Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/pages/international-convention-on-maritime-search-and-rescue-(sar).aspx</li><li>Resolution MSC.155(78), text and dates (Norwegian Parliament proposition): https://www.regjeringen.no/no/dokumenter/stprp-nr-27-2005-2006-/id211703/?ch=10</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-stcw-f-1995","v":50714433,"title":"STCW-F 1995","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["stcw-f","fishing","training","certification","watchkeeping","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>STCW-F 1995 — International Convention on Standards of Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Fishing Vessel Personnel</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>7 Jul 1995, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>29 Sep 2012</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>15 Articles · 1 Annex (Ch I–IV) · STCW-F Code (from 1 Jan 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Revision</td><td>Annex amended by MSC.561(108) · STCW-F Code MSC.562(108) — both adopted 23 May 2024, in force <b>1 Jan 2026</b></td></tr><tr><td>Main threshold</td><td>Skipper and watch officers: fishing vessels of 24 m and over</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is STCW-F</h2><ul><li>The international standard for training, certification and watchkeeping of crews of seagoing fishing vessels</li><li>Art 6: fishing vessel personnel shall be certificated in line with the Annex</li><li>Art 3: applies to personnel serving on board seagoing fishing vessels entitled to fly the flag of a Party</li><li>\"Seagoing\" excludes vessels that navigate only in inland waters (Art 2)</li><li>IMO calls it one of the pillars of fishing vessel safety, with the Cape Town Agreement (vessel safety), the ILO Work in Fishing Convention 2007 and the FAO Port State Measures Agreement 2009</li><li>A Party may exempt certain provisions for vessels under 45 m that operate only from its ports and within its limited waters (IMO)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 7 Jul 1995 in London</li><li>Art 12: in force 12 months after not less than 15 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>In force <b>29 Sep 2012</b> (IMO)</li><li>Annex amendments follow a comprehensive review of the Convention, to meet the needs of the fishing industry (IMO)</li><li><b>MSC.561(108)</b> — revised Annex: adopted 23 May 2024 · deemed accepted 1 Jul 2025 unless more than one third of Parties objected · in force <b>1 Jan 2026</b></li><li><b>MSC.562(108)</b> — new <b>STCW-F Code</b>, Part A (mandatory) and Part B (guidance): adopted 23 May 2024 · Part A takes effect on the same date as MSC.561(108)</li></ul><h2>The 15 Articles</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General obligations</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Other treaties and interpretation</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Certification</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>National provisions</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Control</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Promotion of technical co-operation</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Amendments</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Signature, ratification, acceptance, approval and accession</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Denunciation</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Depositary</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Languages</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Art 8 Control: port State control of certification; the vessel may not sail if a danger is not corrected</li><li>Art 10 Amendments: annex amendments are deemed accepted after two years unless a different period is set</li><li>Art 13 Denunciation: possible after five years from entry into force for that Party; takes effect 12 months after notice</li><li>Art 14: the IMO Secretary-General is the depositary</li></ul><hr><h2>Annex (as revised by MSC.561(108), in force 1 Jan 2026)</h2><p><b>Chapter I — General provisions</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I/1</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>I/2</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>I/3</td><td>Certificates and endorsements</td></tr><tr><td>I/4</td><td>Control procedures</td></tr><tr><td>I/5</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>I/6</td><td>Administration of certification arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>I/7</td><td>Recognition of certificates</td></tr><tr><td>I/8</td><td>Transitional provisions</td></tr><tr><td>I/9</td><td>Dispensation</td></tr><tr><td>I/10</td><td>Equivalents</td></tr><tr><td>I/11</td><td>Use of simulators</td></tr><tr><td>I/12</td><td>Medical standards</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Reg I/2: 300 GT is equivalent to 24 m, and 950 GT to 45 m</li><li>Transitional: certificates of competency (I/8.2) — up to five years after entry into force; certificates of service (I/8.3) — may be issued within two years after entry into force</li></ul><p><b>Chapter II — Certification of skippers, officers in charge of a navigational watch (OICNW), engineer officers and radio operators</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>II/1</td><td>Skippers, 24 m and over, unlimited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/2</td><td>OICNW, 24 m and over, unlimited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/3</td><td>Skippers, 24 m and over, limited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/4</td><td>OICNW, 24 m and over, limited waters</td></tr><tr><td>II/5-1-1</td><td>Chief and second engineer officers, propulsion power 3,000 kW or more</td></tr><tr><td>II/5-1-2</td><td>Chief and second engineer officers, 750 kW to 3,000 kW</td></tr><tr><td>II/5-2</td><td>Officer in charge of an engineering watch or designated duty engineer, 750 kW or more</td></tr><tr><td>II/6</td><td>GMDSS radio operators</td></tr><tr><td>II/7</td><td>Revalidation for skippers and officers</td></tr><tr><td>II/8</td><td>Revalidation for GMDSS radio operators</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Chapter III — Basic training and onboard safety familiarization</b></p><ul><li>III/1: mandatory minimum requirements for all fishing vessel personnel</li></ul><p><b>Chapter IV — Watchkeeping</b></p><ul><li>IV/1 Fitness for duty · IV/2 Basic watchkeeping principles</li></ul><h2>Age, sea service and medical (MSC.561(108))</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Candidates for certification (I/12.4)</td><td>At least 16, or 15 if authorized by the competent authority</td></tr><tr><td>OICNW (II/2, II/4), OICEW (II/5-2), GMDSS radio operator (II/6)</td><td>At least 18</td></tr><tr><td>Skipper, unlimited waters (II/1)</td><td>12 months as OICNW or skipper on vessels of 12 m or more</td></tr><tr><td>OICNW, unlimited waters (II/2)</td><td>12 months in an approved training programme, or two years in the deck department</td></tr><tr><td>Second engineer, 3,000 kW or more (II/5-1-1)</td><td>12 months as qualified engineer officer</td></tr><tr><td>Chief engineer, 3,000 kW or more (II/5-1-1)</td><td>36 months, reducible to 24 months if at least 12 months were served as second engineer</td></tr><tr><td>Second engineer, 750–3,000 kW (II/5-1-2)</td><td>12 months as assistant or engineer officer</td></tr><tr><td>Chief engineer, 750–3,000 kW (II/5-1-2)</td><td>24 months, including at least 12 months qualified as second engineer</td></tr><tr><td>OICEW (II/5-2)</td><td>12 months of workshop training and sea service (at least six months on fishing or training vessels), or 12 months in the engine room</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Medical certificate (I/12.3): holders serving at sea must hold a valid medical certificate</li><li>Validity (I/12.5): maximum two years, or one year for crew under 18</li><li>Expiry during a voyage (I/12.6): valid until the next port with a recognized practitioner, for not more than three months</li><li>Urgent cases (I/12.7): work allowed for up to three months with a recently expired certificate</li><li>Revalidation (II/7.1, II/8.1): at intervals not exceeding five years, including medical fitness</li></ul><h2>Basic training (III/1)</h2><ul><li>Before any shipboard duty: approved basic training and onboard safety familiarization, to the standard of competence (III/1.1)</li><li>A certificate of proficiency is issued where basic training is not part of a certificate's qualification (III/1.2)</li><li>Code Table A-III/1 gives four areas:<ul><li>A-III/1-1 Personal survival techniques — including lifejacket and immersion suit, jumping from height, righting an inverted liferaft, streaming a drogue or sea anchor</li><li>A-III/1-2 Fire prevention and fire fighting — including fire triangle, detection, breathing apparatus, rescue in smoke-filled spaces</li><li>A-III/1-3 Elementary first aid — casualty assessment, resuscitation, bleeding, shock, burns, safe transport</li><li>A-III/1-4 Personal safety and social responsibilities — emergency procedures and muster duties, marine pollution precautions (including fishing gear and plastic waste), safe working practices, communication and working relationships, fatigue</li></ul></li></ul><h2>Watchkeeping (IV/1, IV/2)</h2><ul><li>Watch arrangements must prevent fatigue; the first watch and relieving watches must be rested and fit for duty (IV/1)</li><li>OICNW must be on the bridge, or a directly associated place, at all times (IV/2.2.1)</li><li>Radio operators keep a continuous radio watch on duty (IV/2.2.2)</li><li>Engineering watch officers must be immediately available and, when required, present in the machinery space (IV/2.2.3)</li><li>Very small fishing vessels in limited waters may be excluded from fully observing the basic principles (IV/2.3)</li><li>Code section A-IV/2 covers: navigational watch (to and from fishing grounds, pilot on board, while fishing, anchor watch) · engineering watch · radio watchkeeping</li></ul><h2>STCW-F Code (MSC.562(108))</h2><ul><li>Part A: mandatory standards. Part B: recommended guidance, numbered like the Convention's regulations</li><li>Competence standards grouped in seven functions (suffix -F): navigation · catch handling and stowage · controlling the operation of the vessel and care for persons on board · marine engineering · electrical, electronic and control engineering · maintenance and repair · radiocommunications</li><li>Three levels of responsibility: management, operational, support</li><li>Tables have four columns: competence · knowledge, understanding and proficiency · methods for demonstrating competence · criteria for evaluating competence</li><li>Table A-II/1 skippers (24 m and over, unlimited waters), A-II/2 OICNW (unlimited waters), A-II/3 skippers (limited waters) — competences include voyage planning, position fixing, watchkeeping, meteorology, manoeuvring, ECDIS, catch handling, stability, fire fighting, life-saving, medical care, maritime law</li><li>Table A-I/12: minimum in-service eyesight standards</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>Fishing skippers and officers need STCW-F certificates, not the STCW merchant certificates</blockquote><blockquote>From 1 Jan 2026 the STCW-F Code competence tables apply</blockquote><blockquote>Certificates are revalidated at intervals not exceeding five years; the medical certificate lasts at most two years (one year under 18)</blockquote><blockquote>Port States can check the certificates of foreign fishing vessel crews (Art 8)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>STCW-F Convention text (NUS CIL): https://cil.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/1995-STCW.pdf</li><li>IMO — STCW-F Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/HumanElement/Pages/STCW-F-Convention.aspx</li><li>IMO — Raft of shipping rules in force from 1 January 2026: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/raft-of-shipping-rules-in-force-from-1-january-2026.aspx</li><li>Revised Annex (MSC.561(108)): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/Revised%20annex%20of%20STCW-F%2095.pdf</li><li>STCW-F Code (MSC.562(108)): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/STCW-F%20Code.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-sua-1988","v":39675350,"title":"SUA 1988","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["sua","security","unlawful-acts","terrorism","boarding","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>SUA 1988 — Convention for the Suppression of Unlawful Acts against the Safety of Maritime Navigation (with the 1988 Fixed Platforms Protocol and the 2005 Protocols)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SUA 1988 and Fixed Platforms Protocol</td><td>Adopted 10 Mar 1988, Rome · in force 1 Mar 1992</td></tr><tr><td>2005 Protocols</td><td>Adopted 14 Oct 2005 · in force 28 Jul 2010</td></tr><tr><td>Core duty</td><td>Make the offences crimes, take jurisdiction, <b>prosecute or extradite</b></td></tr><tr><td>2005 additions</td><td>Art 3bis terrorism-related offences · Art 8bis boarding procedures</td></tr><tr><td>In force rule (2005 Protocol, Art 18)</td><td>90 days after 12 States</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is SUA</h2><ul><li>Makes serious unlawful acts against ships international offences, and requires States to prosecute or extradite offenders (Arts 10–11)</li><li>The Fixed Platforms Protocol applies the same system to fixed platforms on the continental shelf; the 2005 amendments add Art 2bis and Art 2ter to it, for similar intimidation-based offences</li><li>Why: hijackings, kidnappings and ships deliberately grounded or blown up increased in the 1980s (IMO)</li><li>IMO's 14th Assembly (Nov 1985) backed a US proposal for preventive measures (resolution A.584(14); MSC/Circ.443 in 1986); in Nov 1986 Austria, Egypt and Italy proposed a convention, which led to the 1988 treaty</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>1988: SUA Convention and Fixed Platforms Protocol adopted 10 Mar 1988 in Rome · in force 1 Mar 1992</li><li>2005: diplomatic conference 10–14 Oct 2005 adopted the 2005 Protocols · in force 28 Jul 2010</li><li>2005 Protocol: open for signature 14 Feb 2006 – 13 Feb 2007, then accession (Art 17)</li></ul><hr><h2>Application</h2><p><b>Scope (Art 4)</b></p><ul><li>Applies when a ship is navigating, or scheduled to navigate, beyond the outer limit of the territorial sea of a single State (or beyond its lateral limits with adjacent States)</li><li>Also applies when the offender is found in another Party's territory (Art 4(2))</li></ul><p><b>Not covered (Art 2)</b></p><ul><li>Warships · State-owned or State-operated ships used as naval auxiliaries or for customs or police purposes · ships withdrawn from navigation or laid up</li><li>Art 2bis: saves other rights under international law (UN Charter, human rights, refugee and humanitarian law); does not cover armed forces in armed conflict; rights under the NPT, BWC and CWC are unaffected</li></ul><p><b>Ship (Art 1(1)(a))</b>: a vessel of any type whatsoever not permanently attached to the sea-bed</p><h2>Offences</h2><p><b>Art 3(1) — unlawfully and intentionally:</b></p><ul><li>(a) Seizing or exercising control over a ship by force, threat or intimidation</li><li>(b) Violence against a person on board, if likely to endanger the safe navigation of the ship</li><li>(c) Destroying a ship, or damaging it or its cargo, so as to endanger safe navigation</li><li>(d) Placing on a ship a device or substance likely to destroy or damage it</li><li>(e) Destroying or seriously damaging maritime navigational facilities or interfering with their operation, so as to endanger safe navigation</li><li>(f) Communicating information known to be false, endangering safe navigation</li><li><b>Art 3(2)</b>: threatening to commit (b), (c) or (e) to compel a person to do or refrain from an act, if the threat is likely to endanger safe navigation</li></ul><p><b>Added by the 2005 Protocol</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 3bis(1)(a)</b>: to intimidate a population or compel a government or organization — using a ship against or on it, or discharging from it, explosive, radioactive or BCN weapons, oil, LNG or other hazardous or noxious substances (where death, serious injury or damage results or is likely); using a ship in a manner that causes death or serious injury; threats</li><li><b>Art 3bis(1)(b)</b>: transporting on a ship explosives, radioactive material, BCN weapons, nuclear material or related equipment, knowing the intended use</li><li><b>Art 3ter</b>: transporting a person, knowing they committed a listed offence, to help them evade prosecution</li><li><b>Art 3quater</b>: injuring or killing in connection with an offence · attempts · complicity · organizing or directing others · contributing to a group's offences</li><li><b>Art 5bis</b>: legal entities can be held liable (criminal, civil or administrative) when a manager or controller commits an offence; sanctions effective, proportionate and dissuasive</li></ul><hr><h2>Jurisdiction, custody and extradition</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>Penalties reflecting the grave nature of the offences</td></tr><tr><td>6(1)</td><td><b>Mandatory</b> jurisdiction: offence against or on a ship flying the State's flag · in its territory (including territorial sea) · by its national</td></tr><tr><td>6(2)</td><td><b>Optional</b> jurisdiction: stateless person with habitual residence · victim is a national · aimed at compelling the State to act</td></tr><tr><td>6(4)</td><td>The State where the alleged offender is present, and does not extradite, establishes jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><b>Custody</b>: the State where the offender is present takes custody or other measures, makes a preliminary inquiry, lets the person contact their State's representative, and informs interested States</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><b>Delivery by the master</b> (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><b>Prosecute without exception, or extradite</b>; fair treatment guaranteed</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Offences are deemed extraditable; the Convention can be the legal basis</td></tr><tr><td>11bis</td><td>Offences are not political offences for extradition or mutual assistance</td></tr><tr><td>11ter</td><td>No obligation to extradite or assist if the request aims to persecute on grounds such as race, religion or nationality</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Greatest measure of mutual assistance</td></tr><tr><td>13–14</td><td>Co-operation on prevention and exchange of information; avoid undue delay of ships</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Reporting to the IMO Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Disputes: negotiation, then arbitration; ICJ if arbitration cannot be organized within six months; States may opt out</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Delivery by the master (Art 8)</b></p><ul><li>The master of a flag State ship may deliver a person to the authorities of another Party if there are reasonable grounds to believe the person committed an offence</li><li>The master should notify the receiving State in advance (8(2)) and provide the evidence in the master's possession (8(4))</li><li>The receiving State generally must accept delivery (8(3))</li></ul><h2>Boarding procedures — Art 8bis (2005 Protocol)</h2><ul><li>8bis(5): boarding a ship of another Party seaward of any territorial sea requires (a) confirmation of nationality, (b) a request for authorization, and (c) a flag State response — authorize, board itself, join in boarding, or decline</li><li>Boarding without the flag State's express authorization is prohibited</li><li>States may notify an optional rule: authorization deemed given if the flag State does not respond within <b>four hours</b></li><li>8bis(9): use of force must be minimal and necessary</li><li>8bis(10) safeguards: safety of life at sea · human dignity and human rights · environmental soundness · notice to the master · States are liable for unfounded or unlawful measures and must give effective recourse · only identifiable government ships and aircraft may act · officials show government identification</li><li>8bis(15): Parties designate competent authorities and notify the Secretary-General within one month</li></ul><h2>Final clauses (2005 Protocol)</h2><ul><li>Art 18: in force 90 days after 12 States signed without reservation or deposited instruments</li><li>Art 19: denunciation takes effect one year after deposit</li><li>Art 20: revision conference by IMO, or at the request of one third of States Parties or ten, whichever is higher</li><li>Art 24: authentic in Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish</li><li>Annex: lists nine treaties (aviation, hostage-taking, nuclear material, terrorist bombings, terrorism financing and others) used for Art 3ter</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>SUA is criminal law after an attack — prevention is in SOLAS XI-2 and the ISPS Code</blockquote><blockquote>The master may deliver a suspect to a Party's authorities, giving evidence and advance notice</blockquote><blockquote>Boarding at sea needs the flag State's authorization (or the optional 4-hour rule)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>SUA Convention, consolidated with the 2005 Protocol (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/safety-maritime-consolidated.html</li><li>IMO — SUA Treaties: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/SUA-Treaties.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-salvage-1989","v":69645737,"title":"Salvage 1989","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["salvage","lof","special-compensation","environment","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Salvage 1989 — International Convention on Salvage</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>28 Apr 1989, London</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>14 Jul 1996</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>1910 Brussels salvage convention (\"no cure, no pay\")</td></tr><tr><td>New feature</td><td>Enhanced award and special compensation for protecting the environment</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force rule</td><td>1 year after 15 States (Art 29)</td></tr><tr><td>Time bar</td><td>2 years from the end of the salvage operation (Art 23)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is Salvage 1989</h2><ul><li>Rules for salvage operations: duties and rights of salvors, owners and masters, and how rewards are set</li><li>The 1910 rule ignored pollution: a salvor who prevented a major spill but could not save the ship or cargo got nothing, so there was little reason to try long-odds operations (IMO)</li><li>The 1989 Convention gives an enhanced award that counts the salvor's skill and efforts to prevent or minimize environmental damage</li><li>The Convention applies unless a contract provides otherwise (Art 6(1))</li></ul><h2>Applies to (Arts 2–5)</h2><ul><li>Art 2: when judicial or arbitral proceedings are brought in a State Party</li><li>Art 3: <b>not</b> fixed or floating platforms or mobile offshore drilling units engaged in sea-bed mineral work, when on location</li><li>Art 4: <b>not</b> warships and non-commercial State vessels with sovereign immunity, unless the State decides otherwise (and notifies the Secretary-General)</li><li>Art 5: does not affect national law or conventions on salvage controlled by public authorities</li></ul><hr><h2>Chapter I — General (Arts 1–7)</h2><p><b>Art 1 definitions</b></p><ul><li>(a) Salvage operation: any act or activity undertaken to assist a vessel or any other property in danger</li><li>(b) Vessel · (c) Property · (e) Payment · (f) Organization (IMO) · (g) Secretary-General</li><li>(d) <b>Damage to the environment</b>: substantial physical damage to human health or to marine life or resources in coastal or inland waters or areas adjacent to them, caused by pollution, contamination, fire, explosion or similar major incidents</li></ul><p><b>Contracts</b></p><ul><li>Art 6: the master has authority to conclude salvage contracts for the owner of the vessel</li><li>Art 7: a contract can be annulled or modified if made under undue influence or the influence of danger and its terms are inequitable, or if the payment is grossly too large or too small</li></ul><h2>Chapter II — Performance of salvage operations (Arts 8–11)</h2><ul><li><b>Art 8 — Salvor:</b> act with due care · take due care to prevent or minimize damage to the environment · seek help from other salvors when circumstances require · accept the intervention of other salvors when reasonably asked</li><li><b>Art 8 — Owner and master:</b> co-operate fully with the salvor · take due care to prevent or minimize damage to the environment · accept redelivery of the vessel or property when it is reasonably asked and the salvor has secured a place of safety</li><li><b>Art 9:</b> coastal State keeps its right to take measures to protect its coastline from pollution, including giving directions about salvage operations</li><li><b>Art 10 — Duty to render assistance:</b> every master must, as far as he can do so without serious danger to his vessel and persons on board, render assistance to any person in danger of being lost at sea; States Parties adopt measures to enforce this; the owner is not liable for the master's breach</li><li><b>Art 11:</b> States should consider the need for co-operation with salvors when deciding on admission of vessels in distress to ports</li></ul><h2>Chapter III — Rights of salvors (Arts 12–19)</h2><ul><li><b>Art 12:</b> a useful result is needed for a reward; no useful result, no payment unless otherwise provided</li><li><b>Art 13(1) — Criteria for the reward</b> (no order of priority):<ul><li>(a) salved value of the vessel and other property</li><li>(b) skill and efforts of the salvors in preventing or minimizing damage to the environment</li><li>(c) measure of success obtained</li><li>(d) nature and degree of the danger</li><li>(e) skill and efforts of the salvors in salving the vessel, other property and life</li><li>(f) time used and expenses and losses incurred</li><li>(g) risk of liability and other risks run by the salvors or their equipment</li><li>(h) promptness of the services</li><li>(i) availability and use of vessels or other equipment intended for salvage</li><li>(j) state of readiness and efficiency of the salvor's equipment, and its value</li></ul></li><li>Art 13(2): payment is shared by the vessel and other property interests in proportion to their salved values</li><li>Art 13(3): the reward, excluding interest and recoverable legal costs, cannot exceed the salved value of the vessel and other property</li></ul><p><b>Art 14 — Special compensation</b></p><ul><li>14(1): if the salvor worked on a vessel which, by itself or its cargo, threatened damage to the environment, and did not earn a reward under Art 13 at least equal to the special compensation, the salvor is entitled to special compensation from the owner, equal to the salvor's expenses</li><li>14(3): <b>salvor's expenses</b> = out-of-pocket expenses reasonably incurred, plus a fair rate for equipment and personnel actually and reasonably used</li><li>14(2): if the salvor prevented or minimized environmental damage, the compensation may be increased up to <b>30%</b> of expenses; a tribunal may increase it further if fair and just, but the total increase cannot be more than <b>100%</b> of expenses</li><li>14(4): special compensation is paid only to the extent it exceeds the Art 13 reward</li><li>14(5): if the salvor was negligent and failed to prevent or minimize environmental damage, special compensation may be denied or reduced</li><li>14(6): the owner keeps his right of recourse</li></ul><p><b>Other</b></p><ul><li>Art 15: apportionment among salvors by the Art 13 criteria; division between owner, master and crew of the salving vessel by the law of its flag</li><li>Art 16: no payment from persons whose lives are saved (national law may differ); life salvors share fairly in the reward for salving the vessel or property or preventing environmental damage</li><li>Art 17: no payment for services under a contract, unless they go beyond what the contract reasonably requires</li><li>Art 18: payment may be reduced or removed for fault, neglect, fraud or dishonest conduct</li><li>Art 19: no payment for services rendered against the express and reasonable prohibition of the owner or master</li></ul><h2>Chapter IV — Claims and actions (Arts 20–27)</h2><ul><li>Art 20: the salvor's maritime lien is not affected; it cannot be enforced once adequate security has been provided</li><li>Art 21: on request, the liable party provides security; salved property is not removed from the port or place where it is first brought, without the salvor's consent, until security is provided</li><li>Art 22: interim payment may be ordered by a tribunal</li><li>Art 23: actions must be started within <b>two years</b> from the day the salvage operations ended</li><li>Art 24: interest — by the law of the State where the tribunal sits</li><li>Art 25: no seizure of State-owned non-commercial cargo with sovereign immunity without the State's consent</li><li>Art 26: no seizure of donated humanitarian cargo if the donor State agreed to pay for the salvage</li><li>Art 27: States should encourage publication of arbitral awards, with the parties' consent</li></ul><h2>Chapter V — Final clauses (Arts 28–34)</h2><ul><li>Art 28: open for signature 1 Jul 1989 – 30 Jun 1990, then accession</li><li>Art 29: in force one year after 15 States consented</li><li>Art 30 reservations: a State may exclude (a) inland-waters salvage with only inland navigation vessels, (b) inland-waters salvage with no vessel involved, (c) cases where all interested parties are its nationals, (d) maritime cultural property of historic interest on the sea-bed</li><li>Art 31: denunciation after one year from entry into force for that State; effective one year after receipt</li><li>Art 32: revision conference — by IMO, or by the Secretary-General at the request of eight States or one quarter of the Parties, whichever is higher</li><li>Art 34: six authentic languages</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The master's duty to save life at sea (Art 10) is a separate duty from a property salvage contract</blockquote><blockquote>The master may conclude a salvage contract for the vessel's owner (Art 6) — inform the company and P&amp;I at once</blockquote><blockquote>Environmental protection is paid through special compensation (Art 14) even if the ship is lost</blockquote><blockquote>Services against an express and reasonable prohibition are not paid (Art 19)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Salvage 1989 treaty text (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/convention-salvage.html</li><li>IMO — International Convention on Salvage: https://www.imo.org/en/About/Conventions/Pages/International-Convention-on-Salvage.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-tonnage-1969","v":249177298,"title":"Tonnage 1969","category":"Maritime › IMO › Conventions","tags":["tonnage","gross-tonnage","net-tonnage","itc","imo","convention"],"body":"<h1>Tonnage 1969 — International Convention on Tonnage Measurement of Ships</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>23 Jun 1969</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>18 Jul 1982</td></tr><tr><td>Existing ships</td><td>Could keep old tonnage until 18 Jul 1994 (12 years)</td></tr><tr><td>Gross tonnage</td><td>GT = K1 × V</td></tr><tr><td>Net tonnage</td><td>Never less than 0.30 GT</td></tr><tr><td>Not applied to</td><td>Warships · ships under 24 m in length (Art 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Tonnage Certificate (1969)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is Tonnage 1969</h2><ul><li>IMO calls it \"the first successful attempt to introduce a universal tonnage measurement system\"</li><li>Replaced varying national methods, all descended from Moorsom's 1854 approach</li><li>Separate <b>gross tonnage (GT)</b> and <b>net tonnage (NT)</b>, each calculated independently; the terms changed from grt and nrt to GT and NT</li><li>GT depends on the moulded volume of all enclosed spaces; NT comes from a formula based on the moulded volume of cargo spaces</li><li>GT governs manning, safety and registration fees; both GT and NT are used for port dues (IMO)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>Adopted 23 Jun 1969 · open for signature six months from 23 Jun 1969, then accession (Art 16)</li><li>Art 17: in force <b>24 months after at least 25 Governments, whose merchant fleets are at least 65% of world gross tonnage</b>, signed without reservation or deposited instruments → in force <b>18 Jul 1982</b></li><li>Ships built earlier could keep their old tonnage for 12 years, until 18 Jul 1994, to protect operators from higher charges tied to tonnage</li></ul><hr><h2>Articles (the Convention has 23; Arts 1–18 below)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Art</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General obligation — the Annexes are an integral part</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Definitions</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Exceptions</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Force majeure</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Determination of tonnages</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Issue of certificate</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Certificate issued by another Government</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Form of certificate</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Cancellation</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Inspection</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Privileges</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Prior treaties</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Communication of information</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Signature and accession</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Coming into force</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Amendments</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Key articles</b></p><ul><li><b>Art 2</b>: nine defined terms — Regulations, Administration, international voyage, gross tonnage, net tonnage, new ship (keel laid on or after entry into force), existing ship, length, Organization. <b>Length</b> = 96% of the total length on a waterline at 85% of the least moulded depth, or the length from the foreside of the stem to the axis of the rudder stock on that waterline if greater (Art 2(8))</li><li><b>Art 3</b>: applies to ships on international voyages that are registered in Contracting States, or unregistered and flying their flag; covers new ships, existing ships with substantial alterations, existing ships on owner's request, and all existing ships 12 years after entry into force</li><li><b>Art 4</b>: excludes warships and ships under 24 m in length; also ships navigating only the Great Lakes and St Lawrence, the Caspian Sea, or the Plate, Paraná and Uruguay Rivers</li><li><b>Art 5</b>: deviation due to weather or other force majeure does not make a ship subject to the Convention</li><li><b>Art 6</b>: the Administration determines tonnages; may delegate to recognised persons or organizations but keeps full responsibility</li><li><b>Art 7</b>: an International Tonnage Certificate (1969) is issued to every ship whose tonnages were determined under the Convention</li><li><b>Art 8</b>: another Contracting Government may determine tonnages and issue the certificate at the flag State's request; none to ships of non-Contracting States</li><li><b>Art 9</b>: in the issuing country's language, with English or French translation; follows the Annex II model</li><li><b>Art 10</b>: cancelled when alterations increase gross or net tonnage; generally lapses on transfer to another flag (for a transfer between Contracting States, up to three months or until replaced)</li><li><b>Art 11</b>: other Contracting Governments accept the certificate as valid as their own</li><li><b>Art 12</b>: port inspection is limited to checking the certificate's validity and that the main characteristics match; must not delay the ship; flag State informed of any tonnage increase</li><li><b>Art 13</b>: Convention privileges only for ships with a valid certificate</li><li><b>Art 18</b>: amendments in force 12 months after acceptance by two-thirds of Contracting Governments</li></ul><hr><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"tonnage-k1\"><figcaption>K1 against volume, as listed in the Annex</figcaption></figure><h2>Annex I — Regulations</h2><p><b>Reg 2 — Definitions</b></p><ul><li><b>Upper deck</b>: the uppermost complete deck exposed to weather and sea, with permanent weathertight closings, and watertight closings for side openings below it</li><li><b>Moulded depth</b>: vertical distance from the top of the keel to the underside of the upper deck at side</li><li><b>Breadth</b>: maximum breadth amidships (moulded frame line for metal shells)</li><li><b>Enclosed spaces</b>: spaces bounded by the hull, fixed or portable partitions, decks or coverings other than permanent or movable awnings; breaks or openings do not by themselves exclude a space</li><li><b>Excluded spaces</b> (a)–(e): for example spaces within an erection opposite an end opening · spaces under an open overhead deck covering · spaces in a side-to-side erection opposite side openings · spaces immediately below an uncovered weather-exposed deck opening, limited to the opening's area · recesses in a bulkhead with weather-exposed deck-to-deck opening</li><li><b>Passenger</b>: any person other than the master, crew or others employed on the ship's business, and other than a child under one year old</li><li><b>Cargo spaces</b> (for NT): enclosed spaces for cargo to be discharged, already counted in GT, marked \"CC\" at least 100 mm high</li></ul><p><b>Reg 3 — Gross tonnage</b></p><blockquote>GT = K1 × V</blockquote><blockquote>V = total volume of all enclosed spaces, in m³ · K1 = 0.2 + 0.02 log₁₀ V (or the Appendix 2 table, with interpolation)</blockquote><table><thead><tr><th>V (m³)</th><th>K1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100</td><td>0.2400</td></tr><tr><td>1,000</td><td>0.2600</td></tr><tr><td>10,000</td><td>0.2800</td></tr><tr><td>100,000</td><td>0.3000</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>0.3200</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Reg 4 — Net tonnage</b></p><blockquote>NT = K2 × Vc × (4d/3D)² + K3 × (N1 + N2/10)</blockquote><ul><li>Vc = total volume of cargo spaces (m³) · K2 = 0.2 + 0.02 log₁₀ Vc</li><li>K3 = 1.25 × (GT + 10,000) / 10,000</li><li>D = moulded depth amidships (m) · d = moulded draught amidships (m)</li><li>N1 = passengers in cabins with not more than 8 berths · N2 = other passengers · N1 + N2 = passengers permitted by the passenger certificate; if less than 13, N1 and N2 are zero</li><li>Limits: (4d/3D)² not more than 1 · the first term K2 × Vc × (4d/3D)² not less than 0.25 GT · <b>NT not less than 0.30 GT</b></li><li><b>Moulded draught d</b>: (a) ships under the Load Line Convention — the Summer Load Line draught (excluding timber) · (b) passenger ships — deepest subdivision load line draught · (c) ships with a national load line — summer load line draught · (d) ships with a restricted draught and no load line — maximum permitted draught · (e) other ships — 75% of the moulded depth amidships</li></ul><p><b>Reg 5 — Change of net tonnage</b></p><ul><li>Increase: apply the new net tonnage without delay</li><li>Decrease: a new certificate with lower net tonnage cannot be issued until 12 months after the current certificate was issued, except on transfer to another flag, after major alterations approved by the Administration, or for passenger ships in special trades (such as the pilgrim trade)</li></ul><p><b>Reg 6 and 7</b></p><ul><li>Volumes measured to the inner side of metal shell or structural boundary plating, regardless of insulation; volume of appendages included; volumes of spaces open to the sea may be excluded</li><li>Measurements to the nearest centimetre; volumes by generally accepted methods acceptable to the Administration</li></ul><h2>Annex II — International Tonnage Certificate (1969)</h2><ul><li>Ship name, distinctive number or letters, port of registry</li><li>Date keel laid (or similar stage), or date of major alteration</li><li>Main dimensions: length, breadth, moulded depth to upper deck</li><li><b>Gross tonnage and net tonnage</b></li><li>Tables of spaces included in GT and NT; passengers (N1, N2); excluded spaces and moulded draught; dates and places of original and last measurement</li></ul><h2>Key Point</h2><blockquote>The International Tonnage Certificate (1969) must be on board and match the ship</blockquote><blockquote>GT is a number from volume, not weight; DWT is weight</blockquote><blockquote>Alterations that increase tonnage cancel the certificate (Art 10)</blockquote><blockquote>A lower net tonnage cannot be certified until 12 months after the current certificate (Reg 5)</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Convention text, Arts 1–23 (Univ. of Oslo): https://jus.uio.no/english/services/library/treaties/07/7-04/imo_tonnage.html</li><li>Annexes I–II (UK Treaty Series 1982 No 50): https://treaties.fcdo.gov.uk/data/Library2/pdf/1982-TS0050.pdf</li><li>Regulation 4 formula check (Dutch treaty text): https://inview.nl/openCitation/idd622cd2688aed2d5f2ae37f1ef323f8f/international-convention-on-tonnage-measurement-of-ships-1969-bijlage-i</li><li>IMO — Tonnage Convention: https://www.imo.org/en/about/conventions/page/international-convention-on-tonnage-measurement-of-ships.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-blu-code","v":60478508,"title":"BLU Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["blu","bulk-carrier","loading","unloading","terminal","solas-vi","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>BLU Code — Code of Practice for the Safe Loading and Unloading of Bulk Carriers</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>27 Nov 1997, Assembly resolution A.862(20)</td></tr><tr><td>Effective</td><td>1 Jul 1998</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Introduction · 6 Sections · 5 Appendices</td></tr><tr><td>Aim</td><td>Prevent loss of, or damage to, ships carrying solid bulk cargoes through improper loading and unloading</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo</td><td>Solid bulk cargoes, <b>excluding grain</b></td></tr><tr><td>Amendments</td><td>MSC.238(82) (2006) · MSC.304(87) (2010)</td></tr><tr><td>Linked to</td><td>SOLAS Chapter VI, regulation 7</td></tr><tr><td>Used with</td><td>IMSBC Code (cargo), BLU Manual (for terminals)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the BLU Code</h2><ul><li>Practical recommendations for <b>ship and terminal</b> to load and unload solid bulk cargoes safely</li><li>Main tools: the agreed <b>loading or unloading plan</b> and the <b>ship/shore safety checklist</b></li><li>The Code revoked MSC/Circ.690 and DSC/Circ.3</li><li>Its recommendations are subject to terminal, port or national requirements</li><li>SOLAS, MARPOL and Load Line issues are not specifically covered</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>27 Nov 1997</td><td>A.862(20) adopts the Code · effective 1 Jul 1998</td></tr><tr><td>1 Dec 2006</td><td>MSC.238(82): amends the Introduction, Section 5 and Appendix 4 · effective 1 Jan 2007</td></tr><tr><td>14 May 2010</td><td>MSC.304(87): amends Sections 1, 3 and 5 and Appendices 4 and 5 · effective 1 Jan 2011</td></tr><tr><td>May 2005</td><td>MSC 80 approves the <b>BLU Manual</b> for terminal representatives; amendments approved at MSC 87 (MSC.1/Circ.1356)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The Code is linked to SOLAS regulation VI/7 (loading, unloading and stowage of bulk cargoes).</li><li>SOLAS VI/7.3: a copy of the plan is lodged with the appropriate authority of the port State.</li><li><b>EU:</b> Directive 2001/96/EC, Article 8, requires the plan to follow the form in BLU Code Appendix 2 and the checklist to follow the Appendix 4 guidelines. The directive defines a bulk carrier as a ship of 500 GT or more of the bulk-carrier type described in it (UK MCA summary).</li></ul><h2>Procedure at a glance</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"blu-flow\"><figcaption>The four stages: before arrival, before cargo handling, loading or unloading, completion</figcaption></figure><h2>Sections</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Definitions (1.1–1.11)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Suitability of ships and terminals</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Procedures between ship and shore before arrival</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Procedures between ship and terminal before cargo handling</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Cargo loading and handling of ballast</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Unloading cargo and handling of ballast</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Section 1 — Definitions:</b> air draught, combination carriers (OBO or O/O), conveyor system, hot work, list indication lights, master, pour, terminal representative, and three types of trimming (loading cargo, unloading cargo, ship trimming).</p><p><b>Section 2 — Suitability</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Ships (2.1–2.2)</th><th>Terminals (2.3)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Valid statutory certificates, with validity covering loading, voyage and unloading plus a reserve for delays</td><td>Accept only ships that can berth safely, considering water depth, maximum ship size, mooring, fendering, safe access and obstructions</td></tr><tr><td>Weathertight; approved stability and loading booklet</td><td>Equipment maintained; weighing equipment calibrated at regular intervals</td></tr><tr><td>Adequate hatches, hatch numbers clearly shown</td><td>Personnel trained and rested to avoid fatigue</td></tr><tr><td>Approved loading instrument where stress calculations are needed</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Machinery and hatches working; list indication lights tested before operations</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Section 3 — Before arrival</b></p><ul><li>Information is exchanged early enough for preparations.</li><li>Loading rate and cargo distribution are agreed using one of three options: the restrictions in the ship's manual or booklet, an approved loading instrument, or a conservative procedure.</li><li><b>Ship to terminal:</b> ETA (updated), ship particulars, loading plan, draughts, de-ballasting time, hatch distances, accommodation ladder location, air draught, cargo gear, mooring details. Part-loaded ships report cargo on board and the distribution to be unloaded. Combination carriers report previous cargoes and gas-free status. The ship confirms holds are clean.</li><li><b>Terminal to ship:</b> berth details, loader or unloader characteristics and rates, water depth, water density, air draught, gangway arrangements, approach speed, tug availability, cargo sequence, cargo hazards, mooring requirements. The terminal confirms the ship has received the cargo declaration.</li></ul><p><b>Section 4 — Before cargo handling</b></p><ul><li><b>4.1:</b> the master is responsible at all times for safe loading and unloading.</li><li><b>4.2 loading:</b> the plan is signed by both parties; the master specifies hold order and weights; ballast and loading rates are coordinated.</li><li><b>4.2 unloading:</b> the terminal proposes the rate; the master sets hold order and weights.</li><li><b>4.3:</b> the plan follows Appendix 2. It changes only by a revised plan signed by both parties. Plans are kept for <b>6 months</b>, and a copy is lodged with the port State authority.</li></ul><h2>Who does what</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"blu-roles\"><figcaption>Duties of the master and of the terminal representative</figcaption></figure><p><b>Section 5 — Loading and ballast</b></p><ul><li>Confirm there is no excessive hull stress.</li><li>The terminal alerts the ship to high impact loads from heavy cargo until the tank top is covered.</li><li>Trimming follows the BC Code.</li><li>Both parties keep accurate records of total and per-pour quantities.</li><li>Completion of loading is agreed in writing.</li></ul><p><b>Section 6 — Unloading and ballast</b></p><ul><li>Confirm the method avoids excessive stress and damage; keep communication open.</li><li>On completion both parties agree in writing that the ship was unloaded to plan, holds were cleaned to the master's requirements, and any damage was recorded.</li><li>Flammable vapour risks apply when ballasting holds.</li><li>The ship warns the terminal at once of damage or hazards.</li><li>The terminal matches port and starboard unloading, reports when each hold is complete, avoids and reports damage, and forecasts adverse weather.</li><li>Damage affecting structural or watertight integrity is reported to the Administration and the port State authority.</li></ul><h2>Right to stop</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Master</td><td>May stop loading or unloading if the agreed plan is not followed or anything else endangers the ship (resolution para 3.4)</td></tr><tr><td>Port authorities</td><td>May stop operations when the ship's safety is endangered (para 3.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Checklist item 18</td><td>Both parties accept that cargo operations must stop if ballasting falls out of step with cargo operations</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Appendices</h2><table><thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Recommended contents of port and terminal information books</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Loading or unloading plan: sample form and worked examples; the heavy-lined box holds the minimum information; bending moments and shear forces stay within permitted limits</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ship/shore safety checklist: 21 questions, signed by the master and the terminal representative</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Guidelines for completing the checklist</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Form for cargo information from the shipper: cargo description, stowage factor, angle of repose, trimming procedures, hazards, signed declaration</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 21 checklist questions cover water depth, mooring, emergency departure, access, communications, crewing, bunkering, repairs, damage reporting, cargo information, atmosphere safety, loader limits, the plan, trimming, ballast-cargo alignment, residue removal, final trim and time to sail.</p><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> No loading or unloading starts until the master and the terminal representative have agreed and signed the plan and completed the checklist. Keep ballast in step with cargo, and watch hull stresses at every pour. The master can stop the operation when the ship is endangered.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO Assembly resolution A.862(20), BLU Code text: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.862(20).pdf</li><li>IMO — BLU Code and BLU Manual: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/BLU-Code-and-BLU-Manual.aspx</li><li>Amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vi/blu-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Directive 2001/96/EC, Article 8: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/eudr/2001/96/article/8</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 699 Annex B: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-699-m-guidance-on-the-merchant-shipping-carriage-of-cargoes-regulations-2024/annex-b-loading-unloading-and-stowage-of-solid-bulk-cargoes-and-the-blu-code</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-css-code","v":168056270,"title":"CSS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["css","cargo-securing","lashing","csm","solas-vi","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>CSS Code — Code of Safe Practice for Cargo Stowage and Securing</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>6 Nov 1991, Assembly resolution A.714(17) (effective 1 Jul 1996)</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>Resolution A.288(VIII)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>7 chapters · 14 annexes (Annexes 13 and 14 added later)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Cargoes other than solid and liquid bulk cargoes and timber stowed on deck</td></tr><tr><td>Main tool on board</td><td>Cargo Securing Manual (SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5)</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>The master remains responsible for the voyage, the ship, the crew and the cargo</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the CSS Code</h2><ul><li>An international standard for safe stowage and securing of cargo</li><li>Covers: ship suitability and equipment, general stowage advice, cargoes known to be hard to stow, actions in heavy weather, and what to do after cargo shifts</li><li>The Assembly urged Governments to implement it at the earliest opportunity and asked the MSC to keep it under review</li><li>SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5: cargo units must be loaded, stowed and secured throughout the voyage under a <b>Cargo Securing Manual approved by the Administration</b>, to a standard at least equivalent to the IMO guidelines</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Amendment</th><th>Circular</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1994 (MSC 64)</td><td>MSC/Circ.664</td><td>Paragraphs 1.9.1, 2.9.1, 3.2 and new <b>Annex 13</b></td></tr><tr><td>1995 (MSC 65)</td><td>MSC/Circ.691</td><td>Paragraph 1.6.2 and Annex 5 changes</td></tr><tr><td>1996 (MSC 66)</td><td>MSC/Circ.740</td><td>Annex 12: new 2.3, 2.4, 2.5</td></tr><tr><td>1997 (MSC 68)</td><td>MSC/Circ.812</td><td>Paragraphs 4.3.1, 6.1 and Annex 13 (Table 1)</td></tr><tr><td>2002 (MSC 75)</td><td>MSC/Circ.1026</td><td>Annex 13 sections 1, 4, 5, 6, 7 and new appendices</td></tr><tr><td>2010 (MSC 87)</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1352</td><td>New <b>Annex 14</b></td></tr><tr><td>2014 (MSC 94)</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1352/Rev.1 (15 Dec 2014)</td><td>Amended Annex 14; revoked Circ.1352</td></tr><tr><td>2020 (MSC 102)</td><td>MSC.1/Circ.1623 (7 Dec 2020)</td><td>Annex 13 replaced in full with its four appendices</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Annex 14 applies in full to ships with keels laid on or after 1 Jan 2015.</li><li>Cargo Securing Manual guidelines: MSC.1/Circ.1353/Rev.1 (15 Dec 2014), then Rev.2 (7 Dec 2020), which amended its Appendix 2 and revoked Rev.1.</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td><td>Application, definitions (cargo unit, portable tank, ro-ro ship, unit load), forces, cargo behaviour, risk criteria, the Cargo Securing Manual, equipment, special cargo, cargo information</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Principles of safe stowage and securing</td><td>Cargo suitability, distribution, securing arrangements, residual strength, friction, supervision, entry into enclosed spaces, pre-loading checks, cargo stowage and securing declarations (including dangerous goods)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Standardized systems</td><td>Ships for standardized cargo (containers, railway wagons) must be designed and equipped to secure it and give the master adequate information</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Semi-standardized systems</td><td>Securing points and arrangements for road vehicles and roll-trailers on ro-ro ships; acceptance of road vehicles</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Non-standardized systems</td><td>General advice and twelve cargo types with annexes; equivalent methods allowed if they give at least the same safety</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Heavy weather actions</td><td>Course or speed changes, heaving to, avoiding bad weather, timely ballasting or deballasting, voyage planning</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Actions after cargo shift</td><td>Course or speed changes, monitoring the ship's integrity, restowing or resecuring, route diversion; ballasting only if stability is adequate</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Forces:</b> longitudinal and transverse forces are predominant. Transverse forces increase with stow height, distance from the ship's centre of motion, and metacentric height.</p><h2>Annexes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Subject</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Containers on deck of ships not specially designed for them</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Portable tanks (tank-containers)</td><td>Over 450 litres · lashing angle against sliding not more than 25° · against tipping 45° to 60° or more</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Portable receptacles</td><td>1,000 litres or less (cylinders 150 litres or less)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Wheel-based (rolling) cargoes</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Heavy cargo items (locomotives, transformers)</td><td>Optimum lashing angle about 25° against sliding, 45° to 60° against tipping</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Coiled sheet steel</td><td>Coils typically over 10 tonnes gross</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Heavy metal products</td><td>Shoring on every frame, at intervals of at least 1 m</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Anchor chains</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Metal scrap in bulk</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Flexible intermediate bulk containers (FIBCs)</td><td>Up to 3 m³</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Under-deck stowage of logs</td><td>Sliding into the sides begins at about 2 m above the inner bottom</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Unit loads</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Methods to assess the efficiency of securing arrangements (semi- and non-standardized cargo)</td><td>See below</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Safe working conditions for securing containers on deck</td><td>See below</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annex 13 — assessing securing arrangements</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"css-msl\"><figcaption>MSL as a percentage of breaking strength, Annex 13 Table 1</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Timber: MSL is 0.3 kN/cm² normal to the grain.</li><li>A device marked with a permissible working load may use that value as its MSL (para 4.3).</li></ul><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"css-rule-of-thumb\"><figcaption>The rule-of-thumb check for transverse securing</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Rule of thumb (section 5):</b> the total MSL of the devices on each side, port and starboard, should equal the weight of the item in kN. It assumes 1 g (9.81 m/s²) transverse acceleration and ignores lashing-angle effects, uneven load sharing and friction.</li><li>Transverse lashing angles to the deck should not exceed 60°. Lashings above 60° can help against tipping but are not counted in the rule-of-thumb total.</li><li>The full method uses basic acceleration data (Table 2) with length and speed correction factors (Table 3) and B/GM correction factors (Table 4).</li></ul><h2>Annex 14 — safe working conditions for securing containers</h2><p>Applies to dedicated containerships and parts of other ships designed and fitted to carry containers on deck. It protects the people who lash and unlash.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>General: reduce work on container tops and heavy manual equipment; approved <b>Cargo Safe Access Plan (CSAP)</b>; training in lashing, defective gear, working at heights, reefer cable checks</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Responsibilities of Administrations, owners and operators, designers, shipbuilders and terminals</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Design: risk assessment, safe access, fencing (top rail about 1 m, gaps 300 mm or less), lashing system standards, <b>lighting at least 10 lux in access ways and 50 lux at work spaces</b>, at least 70 mm between turnbuckles</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Operations and maintenance: lashing in the ISM system, keep transit areas clear, work in pairs with heavy gear, PPE, safety cages for container tops, <b>no lashing where safe access cannot be provided</b>, record book</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Reefers: outlets interlocked, circuit-breaker protected, de-energized when unplugged; defective plugs locked and tagged out</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For existing containerships (keels laid before 1 Jan 2015), sections 4.4, 7.1, 7.3 and 8 apply, and sections 6 and 7.2 as far as practical.</p><h2>Cargo Securing Manual (MSC.1/Circ.1353/Rev.1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 General</td><td>Definitions (securing devices, <b>MSL</b>, standardized, semi-standardized, non-standardized cargo); written in the ship's working language, with an English, French or Spanish translation if needed; consistency with the trim and stability booklet, Load Line Certificate and IMDG Code; reserve devices; maintenance</td></tr><tr><td>2 Securing devices and arrangements</td><td>Fixed devices and portable devices with MSL; inspection and maintenance: the master's responsibility, routine and periodic examinations, a record book kept with the manual</td></tr><tr><td>3 Non-standardized and semi-standardized cargo</td><td>Forces, accelerations, calculation of devices and safety factors (refers to Annex 13); ro-ro ships</td></tr><tr><td>4 Containers and other standardized cargo</td><td>Stowage and securing plan: stack masses, sequences, heights, sight lines; alternative patterns; forces</td></tr><tr><td>5 Cargo Safe Access Plan</td><td>Required for containerships; refers to Annex 14</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>The full guidelines apply to containerships with keels laid on or after 1 Jan 2015; chapters 1 to 4 apply to existing containerships.</li><li>A manual drafted to the older MSC/Circ.385 is acceptable if it meets these guidelines.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Secure cargo as the approved Cargo Securing Manual says: right devices, correct MSL, records of inspection and maintenance. Check securing arrangements and act early in heavy weather (Chapter 6). On a containership, the Cargo Safe Access Plan and Annex 14 protect the lashing crew.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>IMO Assembly resolution A.714(17) (Code text): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.714(17).pdf</li><li>IMO — CSS Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/CSS-Code.aspx</li><li>CSS Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/vi/css-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1623, Annex 13: https://www.liscr.com/sites/default/files/MSC.1-Circ.1623.pdf</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1352/Rev.1, Annex 14: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.circ.1352.2014.pdf</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1353/Rev.1, Cargo Securing Manual guidelines: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/imo/imo.circ.1353.2014.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-casualty-investigation-code","v":81310328,"title":"Casualty Investigation Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["casualty-investigation","ci-code","accident","solas-xi-1","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Casualty Investigation Code</h1><p><b>Code of the International Standards and Recommended Practices for a Safety Investigation into a Marine Casualty or Marine Incident</b></p><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>16 May 2008, resolution MSC.255(84) (MSC 84)</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory</td><td>Parts I and II mandatory under SOLAS chapter XI-1 from 1 Jan 2010 (resolution MSC.257(84)) · Part III recommended</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>3 Parts · 26 chapters</td></tr><tr><td>Core duty</td><td>A marine safety investigation into every <b>very serious marine casualty</b></td></tr><tr><td>Reports</td><td>Final report to IMO and made public</td></tr><tr><td>Guidance</td><td>Resolution A.1075(28) (investigator guidelines)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Casualty Investigation Code</h2><ul><li>A common approach for States to investigate marine casualties and incidents, to <b>prevent future casualties and incidents</b></li><li>It expands SOLAS I/21, which only required an investigation when the Administration judged it could inform regulatory changes</li><li>The Code requires an investigation of every very serious marine casualty, and recommends investigating other casualties and incidents likely to prevent future accidents</li><li>Related provisions cited by IMO: MARPOL articles 8 and 12, Load Lines Convention article 23, UNCLOS article 94(7)</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li><b>MSC.255(84)</b> adopted 16 May 2008; entry into force tied to the SOLAS XI-1 amendments, 1 Jan 2010</li><li><b>A.1075(28)</b> provides guidelines to help investigators apply the Code, and revoked resolutions A.849(20) and A.884(21)</li><li>IMO's GISIS Maritime Casualties and Incidents module stores casualty data and uploaded investigation reports (some restricted to IMO Members)</li></ul><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ci-structure\"><figcaption>The three parts and 26 chapters of the Code</figcaption></figure><h2>Key definitions (Chapter 2)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marine casualty (2.9)</td><td>An event or sequence of events directly connected with a ship's operations that causes: death or serious injury; loss of a person from a ship; loss or abandonment of a ship; material damage to a ship; stranding, disabling or collision; damage to external marine infrastructure that could seriously endanger safety; or severe environmental damage (or its potential). Deliberate harmful acts are excluded</td></tr><tr><td>Marine incident (2.10)</td><td>A non-casualty event that endangered, or would endanger if not corrected, a ship, its occupants, other persons or the environment</td></tr><tr><td>Very serious marine casualty (2.22)</td><td>A casualty involving <b>total loss of the ship, a death, or severe environmental damage</b></td></tr><tr><td>Serious injury (2.18)</td><td>Incapacitation where a person cannot function normally for more than <b>72 hours</b>, starting within <b>7 days</b> of the injury</td></tr><tr><td>Marine safety investigation (2.11)</td><td>An investigation or inquiry, however a State names it, conducted to prevent future casualties and incidents: evidence collection and analysis, identification of causal factors, safety recommendations</td></tr><tr><td>Substantially interested State (2.20)</td><td>The flag State or coastal State involved; a State whose environment was severely or significantly damaged; whose serious interests were harmed or threatened; whose nationals died or were seriously injured; that holds important information; or that has another significant interest</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Code does not define \"serious marine casualty\"; it uses only \"very serious\".</p><h2>Part II — Mandatory standards</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>Marine safety investigation Authority</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Notification</td><td>On the high seas or in an EEZ: the flag State notifies other substantially interested States as soon as reasonably practicable. In a coastal State's territory or territorial sea: flag and coastal States notify each other, then other interested States. Not delayed for lack of complete information</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Requirement to investigate very serious marine casualties</td><td>An investigation into every very serious marine casualty; the flag State is responsible, subject to a Chapter 7 agreement</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Flag State's agreement with another substantially interested State</td><td>States consult to agree who investigates; full participation in another State's investigation fulfils the flag State's obligations</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Powers of an investigation</td><td>National law must let investigators board ships, interview the master, crew and others, and obtain evidence</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Parallel investigations</td><td>Other substantially interested States may run separate investigations; timing coordinated</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Co-operation</td><td>Interested States cooperate, with participation as far as practicable</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Not subject to external direction</td><td>Investigators impartial and free from external direction</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Obtaining evidence from seafarers</td><td>Taken at the earliest practical opportunity; seafarers return to their ship or are repatriated early; informed of their rights, including legal advice and protection against self-incrimination</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Draft reports</td><td>Sent to a substantially interested State on request, if it guarantees non-circulation; comments within <b>30 days</b> or another agreed period; accuracy and completeness verified</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Final reports</td><td>Submitted to IMO for every very serious marine casualty, and for other casualties or incidents where the information could prevent or lessen future occurrences; made available to the public and the shipping industry</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>From casualty to final report</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ci-path\"><figcaption>Notification, investigation, evidence, draft report and final report</figcaption></figure><h2>Part III — Recommended practices</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15</td><td>Administrative responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Principles of investigation</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Investigation of marine casualties (other than very serious) and marine incidents</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Factors for seeking agreement under Chapter 7 of Part II</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Acts of unlawful interference</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Notification to parties involved and commencement of an investigation</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Co-ordinating an investigation</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Collection of evidence</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Confidentiality of information</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Protection for witnesses and involved parties</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Draft and final report</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>Re-opening an investigation</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><b>Ch 17:</b> the flag State should investigate other casualties and incidents where this is likely to prevent future occurrences.</li><li><b>Ch 20:</b> the investigation starts as soon as reasonably practicable without unnecessarily delaying the ship.</li><li><b>Ch 22:</b> ships are not detained unnecessarily; copies of documents are made where practicable.</li><li><b>Ch 25:</b> reports completed as quickly as practicable; 30 days for comments on a draft; interim safety measures may be recommended at any stage; a State that disagrees with a final report may submit its own report to IMO.</li><li><b>Ch 26:</b> an investigation should be reconsidered and may be re-opened when new evidence may materially change the conclusions.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Every very serious marine casualty (total loss, a death, or severe pollution) must be investigated, and the final report goes to IMO and the public. The purpose is prevention, not blame. Seafarers must be told their rights, including legal advice and protection against self-incrimination. Other legal proceedings can run in parallel.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>MSC.255(84) Casualty Investigation Code text (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/MSAS/Documents/Res.%20MSC.255(84)%20Casualty%20Iinvestigation%20Code.pdf</li><li>IMO — Casualty investigation: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/MSAS/Pages/Casualties.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-esp-code","v":183823135,"title":"ESP Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["esp","survey","bulk-carrier","oil-tanker","solas-xi-1","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>ESP Code — 2011 International Code on the Enhanced Programme of Inspections during Surveys of Bulk Carriers and Oil Tankers</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>30 Nov 2011, Assembly resolution A.1049(27)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jan 2014 (takes effect with the SOLAS chapter XI-1 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Made mandatory by</td><td>SOLAS regulation XI-1/2</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Annex A (bulk carriers) · Annex B (oil tankers), each in Parts A and B</td></tr><tr><td>Consolidated text</td><td>Res. MSC.461(101), \"completely revised\", in force 1 Jan 2021</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendments</td><td>MSC.553(108), in force 1 Jan 2026 (thickness measurement firms) · MSC.584(111), adopted at MSC 111 (May 2026), expected in force 1 Jan 2028 (remote inspection techniques)</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk carrier survey cycle</td><td>Renewal every 5 years · annual · intermediate</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the ESP Code</h2><ul><li>The enhanced programme of inspections during surveys of <b>bulk carriers and oil tankers</b>, mainly the hull structure</li><li>Close-up surveys, thickness measurement, coating condition and corrosion assessment</li><li>Surveys are planned and documented, and the documents stay on board</li><li>The Maritime Safety Committee keeps the Code under review and updates it</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A.1049(27)</td><td>30 Nov 2011</td><td>1 Jan 2014</td><td>The 2011 Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.371(93)</td><td>22 May 2014</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td><td>Amended the Code</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.381(94)</td><td>21 Nov 2014</td><td>1 Jul 2016</td><td>Sections 5.3, 5.5, 5.6, 5.7, 6.1, 6.3, 6.4 (and 2.6 for oil tankers)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.405(96)</td><td>19 May 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2018</td><td>Survey preparation sections</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.412(97)</td><td>25 Nov 2016</td><td>1 Jul 2018</td><td>Sections 1.5, 2.4.4 and Annex 1 and others</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.461(101)</td><td>14 Jun 2019</td><td>1 Jan 2021</td><td>Completely revised (consolidated)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.483(103)</td><td>13 May 2021</td><td>1 Jan 2023</td><td>Annex B, Part A: Annex 2</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.553(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Approval and certification of firms doing thickness measurement</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.584(111)</td><td>MSC 111, 13–22 May 2026</td><td>1 Jan 2028 (expected; deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027 unless objections)</td><td>Remote inspection techniques (RIT) in ESP surveys</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Entry into force of amendments follows the SOLAS tacit acceptance procedure.</p><h2>Structure</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"esp-structure\"><figcaption>Annexes A and B, their Parts, and the eight sections in each part</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Part</th><th>Applies to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A: Bulk carriers</td><td>A</td><td>Self-propelled ships of 500 GT and above, <b>other than</b> double-side skin bulk carriers</td></tr><tr><td>A: Bulk carriers</td><td>B</td><td>Double-side skin bulk carriers</td></tr><tr><td>B: Oil tankers</td><td>A</td><td>Double-hull oil tankers</td></tr><tr><td>B: Oil tankers</td><td>B</td><td>Oil tankers other than double-hull</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Hybrid ships apply Part B to double-side skin holds and their wing spaces.</li><li>Each part has eight sections: <b>1</b> General · <b>2</b> Renewal survey · <b>3</b> Annual survey · <b>4</b> Intermediate survey · <b>5</b> Preparations for survey · <b>6</b> Documentation on board · <b>7</b> Procedures for thickness measurements · <b>8</b> Reporting and evaluation of survey.</li><li>Bulk carrier Part A has Annexes 1–15 and Part B has Annexes 1–12. Oil tanker Parts A and B each have Annexes 1–12, including tank testing and hull girder longitudinal strength criteria (Annex 12).</li></ul><h2>Key definitions (Annex A, Part A)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Substantial corrosion</td><td>Wastage in excess of <b>75% of allowable margins</b>, but within acceptable limits. For ships under the IACS Common Structural Rules, gauged thickness between tnet + 0.5 mm and tnet</td></tr><tr><td>Close-up survey</td><td>Structural details within close visual range, normally within reach of hand</td></tr><tr><td>Overall survey</td><td>Reports the hull's overall condition and determines the extent of close-up surveys</td></tr><tr><td>Suspect areas</td><td>Locations showing substantial corrosion, or considered prone to rapid wastage</td></tr><tr><td>Coating condition</td><td>Rated GOOD, FAIR or POOR by breakdown and rust percentages (POOR, for example: general breakdown over 20% of an area, or hard scale at 10% or more)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate survey</td><td>Carried out at the second or third annual survey, or between them</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Survey types (bulk carriers)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"esp-cycle\"><figcaption>Annual, intermediate and renewal surveys over the five-year cycle</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Survey</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Renewal</td><td>5-year cycle. May begin at the fourth annual survey and be completed by the fifth anniversary. At least two bottom inspections in the five years, with at most 36 months between them. For ships 15 years and older, the bottom inspection is done in dry-dock</td></tr><tr><td>Annual</td><td>Hull, weather decks, hatch covers, coamings and piping. Hatch cover sets in the forward 25% of the ship's length are surveyed open, closed and in operation each year</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate, 5–10 years</td><td>Overall survey of representative ballast tanks, plus close-up examination of at least 25% of frames in the forward hold and one other selected hold</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate, 10–15 years</td><td>Same extent as the previous renewal survey. Internal fuel tank examination and pressure testing not required unless the surveyor deems them necessary</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate, over 15 years</td><td>As 10–15 years, plus a dry-dock survey as part of the intermediate survey</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Planning, thickness measurement and documents</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey programme (5.1)</td><td>Agreed in writing with the owner before the survey, based on Annex 4A; the owner completes the planning questionnaire (Annex 4B) first</td></tr><tr><td>Planning meeting (5.6)</td><td>Held before any part of a renewal or intermediate survey starts</td></tr><tr><td>Surveyors (1.4)</td><td>Bulk carriers of 20,000 dwt and above: two surveyors for the third and later renewal and intermediate surveys. 100,000 dwt and above: two surveyors for the 10–15-year intermediate survey</td></tr><tr><td>Thickness measurement</td><td>Normally ultrasonic. Done by a <b>certified company</b>, certification renewed at intervals not exceeding 3 years. Reports give locations, measured and original thicknesses, equipment and personnel and are signed by the operator; the surveyor countersigns the cover page</td></tr><tr><td>Firm approval (MSC.553(108))</td><td>After reviewing a firm's documents, the Administration <b>must audit</b> the firm to confirm it is duly organized and managed and capable of hull thickness measurement. Applies to all annexes</td></tr><tr><td>Documents on board</td><td>The <b>survey report file</b> and supporting documents, kept on board for the ship's lifetime</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>The Condition Evaluation Report must include an English translation and be endorsed by the Administration or recognized organization</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Remote inspection techniques (MSC.584(111))</h2><ul><li>Adopted unanimously at <b>MSC 111</b> (13 to 22 May 2026) to permit the use of <b>remote inspection techniques (RIT)</b> in ESP Code surveys. The guidelines MSC.1/Circ.1700 were approved alongside.</li><li>Expected in force <b>1 Jan 2028</b>: deemed accepted 1 Jul 2027 unless objections are communicated to the Secretary-General before then.</li><li>Applies to periodical surveys that commence on or after 1 Jan 2028 when RIT is used, whatever the ship's construction date.</li><li>RIT can serve as an alternative means of access for close-up surveys and related thickness measurements.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>System</td><td>Assessed and certified against Appendix 4 of MSC.1/Circ.1700, with a Statement of Capability (SoC)</td></tr><tr><td>Firm</td><td>Approved, using a certified system with a valid SoC, within its limits</td></tr><tr><td>Survey programme</td><td>Includes an RIT inspection plan and a copy of the SoC</td></tr><tr><td>Limits</td><td>Depend on the ship's condition history, defect records and the company's performance; flag Administrations may add restrictions from the third special survey onward</td></tr><tr><td>Thickness measurement</td><td>Still taken alongside close-up surveys; alternative arrangements if RIT limitations prevent its use</td></tr><tr><td>Checks</td><td>Random confirmatory surveys validate RIT results</td></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>The RIT firm submits a detailed report with labelled videos and photos</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The ship keeps the survey report file and supporting documents on board for life. Prepare safe access and the owner's inspection records before surveys. Thickness measurement must be done by a certified firm. Substantial corrosion means wastage over 75% of the allowable margin.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution A.1049(27), 2011 ESP Code (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1049(27).pdf</li><li>ESP Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/xi-1/esp-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>Report of MSC 111, MSC.584(111) (IMO via MPA Singapore): https://www.mpa.gov.sg/api/media/e783b0c8-e1f3-44bf-8462-d2357ff91654/MSC-111-22---Report-Of-The-Maritime-Safety-Committee-On-Its-111Th-Session-(Secretariat).pdf</li><li>Cyprus Shipping News, RIT conditions: https://cyprusshippingnews.com/2026/10/15/new-imo-esp-code-framework-for-remote-inspection-techniques/</li><li>Resolution MSC.553(108) (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.553(108).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-fss-code","v":45727224,"title":"FSS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["fss","fire-safety","solas-ii-2","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>FSS Code — International Code for Fire Safety Systems</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>5 Dec 2000, resolution MSC.98(73)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 2002, with the revised SOLAS chapter II-2</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory under revised SOLAS chapter II-2</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>17 chapters (1–15 original; 16 added 2012, 17 added 2020)</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendment</td><td>MSC.555(108), in force 1 Jan 2026 (chapters 7 and 9)</td></tr><tr><td>Role</td><td>SOLAS II-2 says which systems a ship needs; the FSS Code says how they are built and perform</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the FSS Code</h2><ul><li>Gives <b>international standards for the fire safety systems</b> required by SOLAS chapter II-2</li><li>The technical system requirements are in the Code, while the statutory requirements stay in the Convention</li><li>Some original technical provisions were moved from the Convention into the Code</li><li>Chapter 1 also covers general provisions: applicability, definitions, equivalents and alternative designs, and a ban on toxic extinguishing media</li></ul><h2>History</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.98(73)</td><td>5 Dec 2000</td><td>1 Jul 2002</td><td>The Code (chapters 1–15)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.206(81)</td><td>18 May 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2010</td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.217(82)</td><td>8 Dec 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2008 / 1 Jul 2010</td><td>Chapters 4, 6, 7 (cabin balconies), 9 (individual remote identification of detectors on passenger ships)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.292(87)</td><td>21 May 2010</td><td>1 Jan 2012</td><td>Chapter 10 and new <b>Chapter 16</b></td></tr><tr><td>MSC.311(88)</td><td>3 Dec 2010</td><td>1 Jul 2012</td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.327(90)</td><td>25 May 2012</td><td>1 Jan 2014</td><td>Chapters 6 and 8</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.339(91)</td><td>30 Nov 2012</td><td>1 Jul 2014</td><td>Chapters 3, 5, 7 (new 2.4), 8, 9, 12, 13, 14</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.367(93)</td><td>22 May 2014</td><td>1 Jan 2016</td><td>Chapter 15</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.403(96)</td><td>19 May 2016</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Chapter 8 (2.4.1) and new <b>Chapter 17</b></td></tr><tr><td>MSC.457(101)</td><td>14 Jun 2019</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Chapter 15 (2.2.3.2 and 2.2.4)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.484(103)</td><td>13 May 2021</td><td>1 Jan 2024</td><td>Chapter 9 (new 2.1.8)</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.555(108)</td><td>23 May 2024</td><td>1 Jan 2026</td><td>Chapters 7 and 9</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Chapter 1 applies to ships whose keels are laid on or after 1 Jul 2002, as in the 2000 text; later amendments set their own application dates.</li></ul><h2>The chapters</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fss-chapters\"><figcaption>The 17 chapters, with the two later additions marked</figcaption></figure><h2>Chapter 2 — International shore connection</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"fss-shore-connection\"><figcaption>Flange dimensions from Table 2.1</figcaption></figure><ul><li>Kept on board with a gasket, four bolts, four nuts and eight washers.</li></ul><h2>Chapter 3 — Personnel protection</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire-fighter's outfit</td><td>Protective clothing with a water-resistant outer surface; rubber or non-conducting boots; rigid impact-resistant helmet; electric safety lamp with at least <b>3 h</b> burning time (explosion-proof on tankers and hazardous areas); insulated axe</td></tr><tr><td>Breathing apparatus</td><td>Compressed-air SCBA with at least <b>1,200 litres</b> of air in the cylinders, or another SCBA that runs at least <b>30 min</b>; cylinders interchangeable</td></tr><tr><td>Lifeline</td><td>Fireproof, at least 30 m long, proof-tested at 3.5 kN for 5 min, with a snap-hook</td></tr><tr><td>EEBD</td><td>At least <b>10 min</b> service duration; flame-resistant hood or full face piece with a clear viewing window; for <b>escape only</b>, not for fire-fighting or entering oxygen-deficient spaces</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapter 4 — Fire extinguishers</h2><ul><li>Powder or CO₂ extinguisher: at least 5 kg. Foam extinguisher: at least 9 litres.</li><li>Portable extinguishers must not exceed <b>23 kg</b> and must be at least as effective as a 9-litre fluid extinguisher.</li><li>Portable foam applicator: inductor-type nozzle on the fire main, a tank of at least 20 litres of foam liquid plus one spare tank, output at least 1.5 m³/min.</li></ul><h2>Chapter 5 — Fixed gas systems (CO₂)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cargo spaces</td><td>Free gas volume at least <b>30%</b> of the largest protected cargo space</td></tr><tr><td>Machinery spaces</td><td>The larger of <b>40%</b> of the largest machinery space (casing excluded above the 40% horizontal-area level) or <b>35%</b> including the casing. Cargo ships under 2,000 GT with two or more non-separate machinery spaces: 35% and 30%</td></tr><tr><td>Calculation</td><td>Free CO₂ volume at 0.56 m³/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Discharge</td><td>Machinery spaces: <b>85%</b> of the gas within <b>2 min</b></td></tr><tr><td>Controls</td><td>Two separate controls in a release box: one opens the space valve, the other releases the gas. Key in a break-glass enclosure</td></tr><tr><td>Alarm</td><td>Audible pre-discharge alarm in ro-ro and occupied spaces, at least <b>20 s</b> before release</td></tr><tr><td>Automatic release</td><td>Not permitted unless the Administration allows it</td></tr><tr><td>Pipes</td><td>Through accommodation: welded, substantial thickness, pressure-tested at not less than 5 N/mm²</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 6 to 8 — Foam, water-spray, sprinklers</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Key figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6 Fixed foam</td><td><b>High-expansion:</b> fill the largest space at least 1 m depth per minute; foam volume five times the largest space; expansion ratio not above 1,000:1. <b>Low-expansion:</b> 150 mm depth over the largest oil-spread area within 5 min; ratio not above 12:1</td></tr><tr><td>7 Water-spray and water-mist</td><td>Average at least 5 l/m²/min in machinery spaces; pump starts automatically on pressure drop; pump and controls outside the protected spaces</td></tr><tr><td>8 Sprinklers, detection and alarm</td><td>Wet-pipe systems (saunas dry-pipe, heads up to 140 °C); spare heads 6 (fewer than 300 installed), 12 (300–1,000), 24 (over 1,000); operating range 68–79 °C in accommodation; sections of at most 200 sprinklers, and on passenger ships at most two decks; two power sources</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 9 and 10 — Detection</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Heat detectors</td><td>Operate before <b>78 °C</b>, not below <b>54 °C</b>, at a rise under 1 °C/min; drying rooms up to 130 °C, saunas up to 140 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Smoke detectors</td><td>In stairways, corridors and escape routes: operate before 12.5%/m obscuration, not below 2%/m</td></tr><tr><td>Spacing</td><td>Heat: 37 m² each, 9 m apart, 4.5 m from bulkheads. Smoke: 74 m², 11 m, 5.5 m</td></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td>Two sources, one of them emergency</td></tr><tr><td>Alarm</td><td>An unacknowledged alarm sounds throughout accommodation, service, control and category A machinery spaces after <b>2 min</b></td></tr><tr><td>Panel</td><td>On the bridge or in a manned central control station</td></tr><tr><td>Sample extraction (Ch 10)</td><td>Sensing unit operates before 6.65%/m obscuration; duplicate fans and alternative power; sampling pipes at least 12 mm internal diameter</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Chapters 11 to 13</h2><ul><li><b>Ch 11 Low-location lighting:</b> approved under IMO guidelines (A.752(18)) or ISO 15370:2001.</li><li><b>Ch 12 Emergency fire pumps:</b> capacity at least <b>40%</b> of the required fire pump capacity, and not less than 25 m³/h (passenger ships under 1,000 GT and cargo ships of 2,000 GT and above) or 15 m³/h (cargo ships under 2,000 GT). Diesel drive must start by hand at 0 °C, at least six times within 30 min, including twice in the first 10 min. Service tank holds 3 h at full load, with reserve fuel outside the machinery space for 15 h more.</li><li><b>Ch 13 Means of escape:</b> passenger ships' stairways at least 900 mm wide, plus 10 mm per person above 90; at most 3.5 m rise without a landing; inclination at most 45°; handrails on both sides. Cargo ships: stairways and corridors at least 700 mm wide, handrail on one side (both sides at 1,800 mm and over).</li></ul><h2>Chapters 14 to 17 — Tankers and helicopters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Key figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>14 Fixed deck foam</td><td>Solution rate: the greatest of 0.6 l/min/m² of cargo tank deck area, 6 l/min/m² of the largest tank's sectional area, or 3 l/min/m² of the monitor-protected area (at least 1,250 l/min). Foam supply <b>20 min</b> with inert gas, <b>30 min</b> without. Expansion ratio generally not above 12:1. Applicators at least 400 l/min each, throw at least 15 m, at least four</td></tr><tr><td>15 Inert gas</td><td>Cargo tank atmosphere at <b>8% oxygen or less</b> by volume and positive pressure. Supply at 125% of maximum discharge rate, with no more than <b>5%</b> oxygen in the supply main. At least two blowers and two non-return devices, one a water seal. Alarms for oxygen above 8%, gas pressure below 100 mm water gauge, and high pressure; automatic shutdown of blowers and the gas regulating valve</td></tr><tr><td>16 Fixed hydrocarbon gas detection</td><td>Central gas measurement unit with sampling pipes in ballast tanks and void spaces of double-hull and double-bottom spaces next to cargo tanks, including the forepeak. At least two sampling points per space (upper at least 1 m from tank top, lower at least 0.5 m from tank bottom). Each line analysed in turn at intervals not over 30 min. Alarm at a pre-set value not over <b>30% of the LFL</b>; separate alarm for clogged lines</td></tr><tr><td>17 Helicopter facility foam</td><td>Foam systems protecting helidecks and landing areas: discharge rates, foam concentrate quantities, nozzle types, equipment height limits</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>The 2026 amendments (MSC.555(108), with SOLAS MSC.550(108))</h2><p>Both resolutions take effect 1 Jan 2026. MSC.555(108) updates FSS chapters 7 and 9.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>New requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ch 7, new 2.5</td><td>Fixed water-based systems on weather decks carrying vehicles on ro-ro passenger ships built on or after 1 Jan 2026: monitors at least 2.0 l/min per m² of protected area, none below 1,250 l/min; monitors outside the protected area; far end within 75% of the monitor's still-air throw; areas monitors cannot reach need nozzles giving 5.0 l/min per m²; four jets where dangerous goods are carried on the weather deck</td></tr><tr><td>Ch 9, 2.3.1.3 to 2.3.1.5</td><td>Heat detector tests to EN 54:2001 and IEC 60092-504; <b>new</b> linear heat detectors tested to EN 54-22:2015 and IEC 60092-504</td></tr><tr><td>Ch 9, 2.4.2.2</td><td>Revised spacing: heat 37 m², 9 m, 4.5 m · smoke 74 m², 11 m, 5.5 m · combined smoke and heat 74 m², 9 m, 4.5 m · linear heat cable not more than 9.0 m apart and 4.5 m from bulkheads</td></tr><tr><td>Ch 9, 2.5.1.2 to 2.5.1.4</td><td>Ro-ro passenger ships built from 1 Jan 2026: alarms shown consistently and recognisable on the bridge; alarm addressability, history, latest alarm and suppression; smoke detection in special category and ro-ro spaces may be disconnected during loading and unloading (time-limited, resets automatically, shown on the central unit); heat detection and manual call points cannot be disconnected</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>SOLAS II-2 side (UK MGN 713):</b> passenger ships built from 1 Jan 2026 need individually addressable smoke and heat detection in vehicle, special category and ro-ro spaces, and continuous video monitoring with footage replayable for at least 7 days. Older passenger ships need water monitors by their first survey from 1 Jan 2028 and video monitoring by 1 Jan 2028 with 24 hours of replay.</p><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Keep the international shore connection on board and ready. EEBDs are for escape only. CO₂ is released only from two separate controls, after the pre-discharge alarm has run at least 20 seconds. Inert gas: at most 8% oxygen in the tanks and at most 5% in the supply main.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.98(73), FSS Code text (via US Government Printing): https://corpora.tika.apache.org/base/docs/govdocs1/668/668672.pdf</li><li>IMO — History of fire protection requirements: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/History-of-fire-protection-requirements.aspx</li><li>FSS Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/ii-2/fss-code-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>MSC.217(82): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.217(82).pdf</li><li>MSC.292(87), Chapter 16: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.292(87).pdf</li><li>MSC.403(96), Chapter 17: https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/file/MSC.403%2896%29.pdf</li><li>MSC.555(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution%20MSC.555(108).pdf</li><li>UK MCA MGN 713: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-713-m-fire-protection-regulations-adoptions-of-amendments-in-imo-resolutions-in-force-on-1-jan-2026/mgn-713-m-fire-protection-regulations-adoptions-of-amendments-in-imo-resolutions-in-force-on-1-jan-2026</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ftp-code","v":73822518,"title":"FTP Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ftp","fire-test","solas-ii-2","materials","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>FTP Code — International Code for Application of Fire Test Procedures (2010 FTP Code)</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>3 Dec 2010, resolution MSC.307(88)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jul 2012 (with the SOLAS II-2 amendments)</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory under SOLAS chapter II-2, for products required to be tested, evaluated and approved on or after 1 Jul 2012</td></tr><tr><td>Replaced</td><td>The 1998 FTP Code, MSC.61(67)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>9 chapters · Annex 1 (11 Parts) · Annexes 2–4</td></tr><tr><td>Amendment</td><td>MSC.437(99), adopted 24 May 2018, in force 1 Jan 2020 (Annex 3, Table 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Used by</td><td>Administrations, competent authorities and testing laboratories</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the FTP Code</h2><ul><li>The <b>test procedures</b> used to approve fire-resistant and fire-restricting materials and products for ships</li><li>Used by the Administration to approve products and by laboratories to test them</li><li>Applies where SOLAS requires products to be tested \"in accordance with the Fire Test Procedures Code\"</li><li>On board you meet the result: approved products with their certificates</li></ul><h2>History</h2><ul><li>The 1998 Code was made mandatory by MSC.57(67) for ships constructed on or after 1 Jul 1998.</li><li>MSC.307(88) replaced it, taking effect 1 Jul 2012.</li><li>Type approvals based on tests under the previous Code were accepted if the tests were made no later than one year after entry into force; renewals without retesting were allowed for reports up to 15 years old.</li><li>MSC.437(99) amended Annex 3, Table 1 (adopted 24 May 2018, in force 1 Jan 2020).</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th><th>Key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Scope</td><td>Use by Administrations, authorities and testing laboratories</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Application</td><td>Where the Convention requires products to be tested to the FTP Code</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Definitions</td><td>Standard time-temperature curve; \"sustained flaming\" = flames lasting 5 s or longer</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Testing</td><td>Test procedures (Annex 1); laboratories recognized by the Administration (quality control based on ISO/IEC 17025); test reports</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Approval</td><td>Type approval: certificates valid up to <b>5 years</b>, test reports under 5 years old when submitted. Case-by-case approval: valid only for the specific ship</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Products installed without testing and/or approval</td><td>Listed in Annex 2</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Equivalents and modern technology</td><td>Alternative tests need written analyses</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Period of grace</td><td>See History</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>List of references</td><td>ISO and IEC standards, including ISO 834-1, ISO 1182, ISO 5659-2, ISO 5660-1/-2, ISO 9705, ISO 19702</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ftp-fire-curve\"><figcaption>The standard time-temperature curve used in the fire resistance tests</figcaption></figure><h2>Annex 1 — Fire test procedures</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Non-combustibility (ISO 1182)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Smoke and toxicity</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>\"A\", \"B\" and \"F\" class divisions</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Fire door control systems</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Surface flammability (low flame-spread surfaces, primary deck coverings)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Blank</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Vertically supported textiles and films</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Upholstered furniture</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Bedding components</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Fire-restricting materials for high-speed craft</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Fire-resisting divisions of high-speed craft</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 1 — Non-combustibility</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Furnace temperature</td><td>750 ± 5 °C, test up to 30 min</td></tr><tr><td>Average furnace and surface temperature rise</td><td>≤ 30 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Average sustained flaming</td><td>≤ 10 s</td></tr><tr><td>Average mass loss</td><td>≤ 50%</td></tr><tr><td>Specimens</td><td>5 (homogeneous) or 10 (non-homogeneous), each 43–45 mm diameter and 50 ± 3 mm high</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 2 — Smoke and toxicity</b></p><ul><li>Appendix 1: smoke test to ISO 5659-2 at 25 kW/m² (with and without pilot flame) and 50 kW/m² (no pilot).</li><li>Appendix 2: toxic gases measured by FTIR (ISO 19702).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Smoke density Dm (max)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bulkhead, lining and ceiling surfaces</td><td>200</td></tr><tr><td>Primary deck coverings</td><td>400</td></tr><tr><td>Floor coverings</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Plastic pipes</td><td>400</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Toxic gas</th><th>Limit (ppm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CO</td><td>1,450</td></tr><tr><td>HCl, HBr, HF</td><td>600 each</td></tr><tr><td>HCN</td><td>140</td></tr><tr><td>SO₂</td><td>120 (200 for floor coverings)</td></tr><tr><td>NOx</td><td>350</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Part 3 — \"A\", \"B\" and \"F\" class divisions</b></p><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ftp-class-divisions\"><figcaption>Insulation criteria for the unexposed side</figcaption></figure><ul><li>\"A\" class: average rise ≤ 140 °C, any single point ≤ 180 °C. Durations: A-60, A-30, A-15, A-0.</li><li>\"B\" and \"F\" class: average rise ≤ 140 °C, any single point ≤ 225 °C. Durations: B-15, B-0, F-15, F-0.</li><li>Integrity: no flaming on the unexposed face, no ignition of the cotton-wool pad, and gap gauges must not pass.</li><li>Aluminium cores may rise no more than 200 °C.</li><li>Appendices cover fire resistance (1), windows, dampers and penetrations (2), thermal radiation through windows (3), and continuous \"B\" ceilings and linings (4).</li></ul><p><b>Part 4 — Fire door control systems</b></p><ul><li>The system is tested as a prototype, independent of its power supply (pneumatic, hydraulic or electrical), in the Part 3 furnace.</li><li>The door starts open, and the system must show it can close the door at the start of the test.</li><li>Furnace temperature raised and held at <b>200 ± 50 °C</b> within 5 min, held for the first <b>60 min</b>, then following the standard curve up to 945 °C.</li><li>The opening and closing function is activated every 5 min for the first 60 min.</li><li>Power to the control system is isolated at average furnace temperatures up to 300 °C. The door must stay closed at least up to 945 °C.</li><li>Criteria: the system must not fail in the first 60 min; from 60 min to the end of the test the door must remain closed.</li></ul><p><b>Part 5 — Surface flammability</b></p><ul><li>Averages must meet Table 1 for materials to be considered low flame-spread.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Linings (bulkhead, wall, ceiling)</th><th>Floor coverings</th><th>Primary deck coverings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qt (MJ)</td><td>≤ 0.7</td><td>≤ 2.0</td><td>≤ 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Qp (kW)</td><td>≤ 4.0</td><td>≤ 10.0</td><td>≤ 10.0</td></tr><tr><td>Burning droplets</td><td>Not produced</td><td>No more than 10 burning drops</td><td>Not produced</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Burning droplets make a lining or primary deck covering a reject, whatever the other criteria.</li></ul><p><b>Part 10:</b> full-scale room test per ISO 9705 and heat release, smoke and mass loss per ISO 5660-1/-2.</p><h2>Annex 2 — Products that may be installed without testing or approval</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Products</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-combustible materials</td><td>Only glass, concrete, ceramics, natural stone, masonry units, common metals or metal alloys: treated as non-combustible, no testing or approval</td></tr><tr><td>Low smoke and toxicity</td><td>Non-combustible materials meet Part 2. Surface materials and primary deck coverings with total heat release ≤ 0.2 MJ and peak heat release rate ≤ 1 kW meet Part 2</td></tr><tr><td>Steel divisions</td><td><b>\"A-0\" bulkhead:</b> 4 mm plating with 60 × 60 × 5 mm stiffeners at 600 mm spacing, or a structural equivalent. <b>\"A-0\" deck:</b> 4 mm plating with 95 × 65 × 7 mm stiffeners at 600 mm spacing, or a structural equivalent</td></tr><tr><td>Low flame-spread surfaces</td><td>Non-combustible materials meet Part 5 (but consider the method of fixing, for example glue). Primary deck coverings rated not readily ignitable meet the floor covering requirements</td></tr><tr><td>High-speed craft</td><td>Fire-restricting materials meet Parts 2 and 5 without further testing</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Annexes 3 and 4</h2><ul><li><b>Annex 3:</b> fire protection materials and the required approval test methods. Table 1 is for passenger ships carrying more than 36 passengers and high-speed craft; Table 2 is for cargo ships (method IC).</li><li><b>Annex 4:</b> interpretation of SOLAS II-2 regulations 5.3 and 6.2 (MSC/Circ.1120), with tables for passenger ship accommodation bulkheads and cargo ship accommodation spaces (methods IC, IIC and IIIC).</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Use only products with a valid FTP approval when you replace a bulkhead panel, door, floor covering or curtain. Annex 2 lists the few products that need no test. Fire door control systems are tested to keep the door closed up to 945 °C.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.307(88), 2010 FTP Code text: https://traficom.fi/sites/default/files/media/regulation/21812-MSC_307%2888%29.pdf</li><li>FTP Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/ii-2/ftp-code-amendment-status.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-grain-code","v":217140164,"title":"Grain Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["grain","bulk","stability","solas-vi","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>Grain Code — International Code for the Safe Carriage of Grain in Bulk</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>23 May 1991, resolution MSC.23(59)</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1 Jan 1994</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Mandatory under SOLAS chapter VI, part C</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Part A (specific requirements) · Part B (calculation of assumed heeling moments and general assumptions)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>All ships regardless of size, including under 500 GT, carrying grain in bulk</td></tr><tr><td>Key documents</td><td>Document of Authorization · grain loading manual</td></tr><tr><td>Stability criteria</td><td>Heel ≤ 12° · residual area ≥ 0.075 m·rad · GM ≥ 0.30 m</td></tr><tr><td>Latest amendment</td><td>MSC.552(108), in force 1 Jan 2026</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the Grain Code</h2><ul><li>An international standard for the safe carriage of grain in bulk: stability, stowage, fittings and securing</li><li><b>Grain</b> means wheat, maize, oats, rye, barley, rice, pulses, seeds and processed forms with similar behaviour (A 2.1)</li><li>Each ship loaded under the Code needs a <b>Document of Authorization</b> showing it can meet the Code</li><li>The document accompanies or is included in the <b>grain loading manual</b>, which gives the master the stability information</li><li>A copy of the document, with the stability data and plans, is kept on board for inspection by the loading port's authorities</li></ul><h2>Part A — Specific requirements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Title and key content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A 1</td><td>Application: all ships regardless of size, including under 500 GT</td></tr><tr><td>A 2</td><td>Definitions (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>A 3</td><td>Document of authorization: issued for each ship loaded under the Code; accompanies the grain loading manual; copies on board; a ship without one must demonstrate compliance before loading</td></tr><tr><td>A 4</td><td>Equivalents: recorded in the document or manual</td></tr><tr><td>A 5</td><td>Exemptions: the Administration may exempt ships or classes for sheltered voyages</td></tr><tr><td>A 6</td><td>Stability and loading information: booklet with ship particulars, lightship data, KG, free surface corrections, capacities, flooding angle curve (where less than 40°), hydrostatic curves and cross curves of stability at 12° and 40°; approved data on volumes and heeling moments per compartment and maximum permissible heeling moments</td></tr><tr><td>A 7</td><td>Stability requirements (see below)</td></tr><tr><td>A 8</td><td>Existing ships (keel laid before 25 May 1980)</td></tr><tr><td>A 9</td><td>Optional requirements for partial cargoes (ships without documents)</td></tr><tr><td>A 10</td><td>Stowage</td></tr><tr><td>A 11</td><td>Strength of grain fittings</td></tr><tr><td>A 12</td><td>Divisions loaded on both sides</td></tr><tr><td>A 13</td><td>Divisions loaded on one side only</td></tr><tr><td>A 14</td><td>Saucers</td></tr><tr><td>A 15</td><td>Bundling</td></tr><tr><td>A 16</td><td>Overstowing arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>A 17</td><td>Strapping or lashing</td></tr><tr><td>A 18</td><td>Securing with wire mesh</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Definitions (A 2)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Filled compartment, trimmed (2.2)</td><td>Grain at its highest possible level after loading and trimming under A 10.2</td></tr><tr><td>Filled compartment, untrimmed (2.3)</td><td>Filled to the maximum in way of the hatch but not trimmed outside the hatch periphery</td></tr><tr><td>Partly filled compartment (2.4)</td><td>Grain not loaded as in 2.2 or 2.3</td></tr><tr><td>Angle of flooding (2.5)</td><td>Heel angle at which openings that cannot be closed weathertight immerse</td></tr><tr><td>Stowage factor (2.6)</td><td>Volume per unit weight, with no allowance for lost space</td></tr><tr><td>Specially suitable compartment (2.7)</td><td>At least two grain-tight longitudinal divisions, coincident with hatch side girders or positioned to limit transverse shift; sloping divisions at least 30° to the horizontal</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Stability requirements (A 7)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"grain-criteria\"><figcaption>The three stability criteria, with a schematic righting and heeling arm diagram</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7.1.1 Angle of heel from grain shift</td><td>Not more than <b>12°</b>, or for ships constructed on or after 1 Jan 1994 the deck-edge immersion angle, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>7.1.2 Residual area</td><td>Between the heeling arm and righting arm curves, up to the smaller of the angle of maximum difference, 40° or the flooding angle, at least <b>0.075 m·rad</b> in all loading conditions</td></tr><tr><td>7.1.3 Initial GM</td><td>Corrected for free surfaces in tanks, at least <b>0.30 m</b></td></tr><tr><td>7.2</td><td>The master may be required to demonstrate stability at all stages of the voyage before loading</td></tr><tr><td>7.3</td><td>The ship must be upright after loading and before sailing</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Existing ships (A 8):</b> ships loaded under previously approved documents (SOLAS 1960 regulation 12, resolutions A.184(VI) or A.264(VIII)) are considered equivalent to A 7. Ships without a document may use A 9 with no deadweight limit.</p><p><b>Partial cargoes without documents (A 9):</b></p><ul><li>Grain not more than <b>one third of deadweight</b></li><li>Centreline divisions in filled, trimmed compartments, extending below deck at least 1/8 of the breadth or 2.4 m, whichever is greater (saucers may replace them in and beneath a hatch, except for linseed and similar seeds)</li><li>Hatches to trimmed compartments closed and secured</li><li>Free grain surfaces in partly filled spaces levelled and secured under A 16, 17 or 18</li><li>GM at least 0.3 m, or the greater value from the formula in the Code</li><li>The master demonstrates compliance to the Administration or port State</li></ul><h2>Stowage (A 10)</h2><p>Free surfaces are trimmed and shifting minimized (10.1).</p><ul><li>Trimmed compartments (10.2):<ul><li>filled under decks and hatch covers as far as possible</li></ul></li><li>Untrimmed compartments (10.3):<ul><li>filled in way of the hatch</li><li>grain may rest at its natural angle outside the hatch periphery</li><li>only for compartments with feeder ducts or perforated decks, or specially suitable compartments</li></ul></li><li>Hatch covers:<ul><li>non-grain-tight 'tween-deck hatch covers are sealed by taping, tarpaulins or similar</li></ul></li><li>Partly filled compartments:<ul><li>surface levelled</li><li>secured against overstowing (A 16), or strapped or lashed (A 17 or A 18), unless the heeling effect is accounted for</li></ul></li></ul><h2>Securing the grain surface (A 11 to A 18)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Key figures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11 Strength of fittings</td><td>Working stresses: 19.6 kN/cm² for steel, 1.57 kN/cm² for wood. Upright housing at least 75 mm at each end</td></tr><tr><td>12 Shifting boards</td><td>At least <b>50 mm</b> thick, grain-tight. Maximum unsupported span 2.5 m (50 mm), 3.0 m (60 mm), 3.5 m (70 mm), 4.0 m (80 mm). Ends housed at least 75 mm. Steel wire stays: working load not more than one third of breaking load, designed for 4.9 kN/m². Shores not steeper than 45°</td></tr><tr><td>13 One-sided loading</td><td>Loads from Tables A 13-1 to A 13-6; stays and shores not over one third of breaking load</td></tr><tr><td>14 Saucers</td><td>Only in filled, trimmed compartments, not for linseed-type seeds. Depth from saucer bottom to deck line at least 1.2 m (moulded breadth up to 9.1 m) to at least 1.8 m (18.3 m or more)</td></tr><tr><td>15 Bundling</td><td>Alternative to saucer filling; lining of 2,687 N per 5 cm strip tensile strength, or 1,344 N per 5 cm with athwartship lashings at 2.4 m or less</td></tr><tr><td>16 Overstowing</td><td>Separation cloth or platform on levelled grain; bearers at most 1.2 m apart; boards 25 mm thick with gaps of at most 100 mm; topped with tightly stowed bagged grain to at least 1/16 of the free surface breadth or 1.2 m, whichever is greater</td></tr><tr><td>17 Strapping or lashing</td><td>Grain slightly crowned; separation cloths overlapping at least 1.8 m; lashings of 19 mm steel wire, 50 × 1.3 mm steel strapping (breaking load at least 49 kN) or equivalent chain; 32 mm turnbuckles; attached about 450 mm below the final surface, at most 2.4 m apart; inspect and tighten during the voyage</td></tr><tr><td>18 Wire mesh</td><td>Burlap (at least 1,344 N per 5 cm), then two layers of 3 mm wire mesh with 150 mm squares; hold-down lashings at most 2.4 m apart, first and last within 300 mm of bulkheads</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Part B — Assumed heeling moments</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"grain-shift-angles\"><figcaption>Assumed grain surface angles after shifting for the three cases</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trimmed (B 2)</td><td>Grain surface after shifting assumed at <b>15°</b></td></tr><tr><td>Untrimmed (B 3)</td><td><b>25°</b> generally, or 15° where the void area in a section is no greater than the trimmed-equivalent void. End-untrimmed: 15° abreast the hatch, 25° in the ends</td></tr><tr><td>Trunks (B 4 in the 1991 text)</td><td>25° shift where wing spaces cannot be trimmed</td></tr><tr><td>Partly filled (B 5 in the 1991 text)</td><td><b>25°</b> if not secured under A 16, 17 or 18; total heeling moment × <b>1.12</b> (B 1.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Void depth (B 1.1.1)</td><td>Vd = Vd1 + 0.75(d − 600) mm, Vd1 from Table B 1-1 (430–570 mm), never less than 100 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Credited underdeck voids (B 1.3)</td><td>Total heeling moment multiplied by 1.06</td></tr><tr><td>Divisions in partly filled compartments (B 5.2)</td><td>Extend 1/8 of the maximum breadth above and below the grain surface</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>2026 amendments (MSC.552(108))</h2><ul><li>Adopted 23 May 2024 at MSC 108; deemed accepted 1 Jul 2025 unless objections; <b>in force 1 Jan 2026</b>.</li><li><b>New definition A 2.8:</b> a \"specially suitable compartment, partly filled in way of the hatch opening, with ends untrimmed\": filled to at least the bottom edge of the hatch end beams and not trimmed outside the hatch periphery under the new A 10.4.</li><li><b>New A 10.4:</b> grain outside the hatch periphery may rest at its natural angle of repose; end trimming may be waived for specially suitable compartments. Only the surface in way of the hatch must be level (A 10.7).</li><li><b>New B 1.1.5:</b> grain surfaces assumed to slope at <b>30°</b> from the lower edge of the hatch end beam.</li><li><b>B 1.5 replaced:</b> total heeling moment = 1.12 × the calculated transverse heeling moment, for partly filled compartments and the new category.</li><li><b>New B section 4:</b> these compartments follow the B 2 trimmed rules, but the surface in way of the hatch and in the ends is assumed at <b>25°</b> after shifting. The old section 4 is renumbered section 5 (section numbers above are those of the 1991 text).</li><li>Practical point (Gard): the grain loading manual and loading computer must carry the new heeling moment data and be approved before the new condition is used.</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> No grain loading without a Document of Authorization and the approved grain loading manual. Check the three criteria: heel at most 12°, residual area at least 0.075 m·rad, GM at least 0.30 m. A partly filled hold must have its surface levelled and secured, or the larger 25° shift moment applies.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.23(59), Grain Code text (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.23(59).pdf</li><li>IMO — Grain Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/Grain-Code.aspx</li><li>Resolution MSC.552(108) (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/SOLAS%201974%20(202)%20-%20Resolution%20MSC.552(108).pdf</li><li>Gard — The Grain Code is being revised: https://gard.no/insights/the-grain-code-is-being-revised-are-you-ready/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-hsc-code","v":238518943,"title":"HSC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["hsc","high-speed-craft","fast-ferry","solas-x","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>HSC Code — International Code of Safety for High-Speed Craft</h1><p><b>1994 HSC Code and 2000 HSC Code</b></p><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1994 HSC Code</td><td>MSC.36(63), adopted 20 May 1994 · mandatory from 1 Jan 1996 · craft built on or after 1 Jan 1996 and before 1 Jul 2002</td></tr><tr><td>2000 HSC Code</td><td>MSC.97(73), adopted 5 Dec 2000 · in force 1 Jul 2002 · craft built on or after 1 Jul 2002</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory by</td><td>SOLAS chapter X (MSC.99(73) for the 2000 Code)</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Preamble · 19 chapters · 11 annexes</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>High-Speed Craft Safety Certificate, up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Also needed</td><td>Permit to Operate High-Speed Craft</td></tr><tr><td>Voyage limit</td><td>Passenger craft up to 4 h, cargo craft up to 8 h at operational speed from a place of refuge</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the HSC Code</h2><ul><li>Complete safety standards for <b>high-speed craft</b> on international voyages: design, equipment, operation and crew</li><li>Requires the Code to be applied in its entirety, a quality-management system, operation only by persons qualified for the craft type, operating limits, proximity to a place of refuge, and no enclosed sleeping berths for passengers (1.2)</li><li>Introduced with SOLAS chapter X on 1 Jan 1996</li><li>The 1994 Code introduced \"assisted craft\" (basis for Category A) and \"unassisted craft\" (basis for cargo craft and Category B)</li></ul><h2>What counts as a high-speed craft (1.4.30)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hsc-speed-line\"><figcaption>The speed threshold, from 3.7 × displacement^0.1667</figcaption></figure><ul><li>A craft whose maximum speed in m/s is equal to or greater than <b>3.7 ∇^0.1667</b>, where ∇ is the displacement volume at the design waterline in m³</li><li>Craft supported entirely clear of the water by ground-effect aerodynamic forces are excluded</li><li><b>Maximum speed (1.4.37):</b> at maximum continuous propulsion power certified, at maximum operational weight, in smooth water</li><li><b>Operational speed (1.4.44):</b> 90% of maximum speed</li></ul><h2>Application (1.3)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Construction date (1.3.1)</td><td>Keels laid, or similar stage of construction, on or after 1 Jul 2002 (2000 Code)</td></tr><tr><td>\"Similar stage\" (1.3.2)</td><td>Construction identifiable with a specific craft has begun and assembly has started of at least <b>50 tonnes or 3%</b> of the estimated mass of all structural material, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Conversions (1.3.3)</td><td>A cargo craft converted to passenger use is treated as a passenger craft constructed when the conversion begins</td></tr><tr><td>Passenger craft (1.3.4)</td><td>Not more than <b>4 hours</b> at operational speed from a place of refuge</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo craft (1.3.4)</td><td>500 GT and above; not more than <b>8 hours</b> at operational speed from a place of refuge, fully laden</td></tr><tr><td>Excluded (1.3.5–1.3.6)</td><td>Craft of war and troopcraft, unpropelled craft, primitive wooden craft, non-trading pleasure craft, fishing craft, and craft operating solely on the Great Lakes and the lower St. Lawrence</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Craft types</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"hsc-craft-types\"><figcaption>Category A, Category B and cargo craft</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>Passenger craft (1.4.47):</b> carries more than <b>12 passengers</b>. A passenger excludes the master, crew, persons employed on the craft's business, and children under one year.</li><li><b>Place of refuge (1.4.48):</b> a naturally or artificially sheltered area a craft can use under conditions likely to endanger it.</li><li><b>Worst intended conditions (1.4.61):</b> the specified environmental limits within which the craft's operation is certified.</li></ul><h2>Chapters and annexes</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General comments and requirements</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Buoyancy, stability and subdivision</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Structures</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Accommodation and escape measures</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Directional control systems</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Anchoring, towing and berthing</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Fire safety</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Life-saving appliances and arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Machinery</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Auxiliary systems</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Remote control, alarm and safety systems</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Electrical installations</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Shipborne navigational systems and equipment and voyage data recorders</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Radiocommunications</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Operating compartment layout</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Stabilization systems</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Handling, controllability and performance</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Operational requirements</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Inspection and maintenance requirements</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Annex</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Form of High-Speed Craft Safety Certificate and Record of Equipment</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Form of Permit to Operate High-Speed Craft</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Use of probability concept</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Procedures for failure mode and effects analysis</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ice accretion applicable to all types of craft</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Stability of hydrofoil craft</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Stability of multihull craft</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Stability of monohull craft</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Definitions, requirements and compliance criteria related to operational and safety performance</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Criteria for testing and evaluation of revenue and crew seats</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Open reversible liferafts</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Certificates and permits (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>High-Speed Craft Safety Certificate (1.8)</td><td>Issued after an initial or renewal survey to a craft that complies; the Administration assumes full responsibility. Valid <b>up to 5 years</b> (1.8.4); renewal surveys at intervals not over 5 years (1.5.1.2); periodical surveys within 3 months before or after each anniversary (1.5.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term endorsement (1.8.9)</td><td>An existing certificate may be endorsed for up to 5 months after expiry</td></tr><tr><td>Extension (1.8.10)</td><td>To reach a survey location, limited to one month</td></tr><tr><td>Permit to Operate (1.9)</td><td>Needed for commercial operation in addition to the Safety Certificate (transit voyages without passengers or cargo exempt). Sets the operating conditions from the route operational manual (Ch 18); the Administration consults each port State before issuing; port States may inspect, and deficiencies make the Permit invalid until corrected</td></tr><tr><td>Type rating</td><td>Type rating training: 18.6 (passenger craft) and 18.8 (cargo craft)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Selected requirements</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 Stability</td><td>Displacement mode, intact: inclination not over <b>10°</b> in still water (2.11). Non-displacement: heel from turning not over 8°, total heel from beam wind and turning not over 12° (2.12.2). Damaged passenger craft: normally not over 10°, up to 15° allowed right after damage if reduced to 10° within 15 min (2.13.1). Category B, raking damage: not over 20°, positive righting lever range at least 15°, positive area at least 0.015 m·rad (2.13.2). Damaged cargo craft: normally not over 15°, up to 20° right after damage reducing to 15° within 15 min (2.15). Passenger craft lightweight survey at intervals not over 5 years; re-incline if lightweight displacement deviates more than 2% or LCG more than 1% of L (2.14.1)</td></tr><tr><td>4 Accommodation</td><td>A seat for each passenger and crew member (4.5.1). At least two unobstructed evacuation paths per person (4.7.12) and two exits from each enclosed public space (4.7.4). Corridors, doorways and stairs at least <b>900 mm</b> (passenger craft) and <b>700 mm</b> (cargo craft) (4.7.13). Guard rails at least 1 m high (4.11.1). Noise: public spaces and crew accommodation generally not above 75 dB(A), operating compartment not above 65 dB(A) (4.10)</td></tr><tr><td>7 Fire safety</td><td>Manual call points: no part of the space more than 20 m from one (7.8.3.2). Ro-ro spaces: no point more than about 15 m walking distance from an extinguisher (7.8.4.3). Hinged fire doors close in no more than 40 s and no less than 10 s (7.9.3.3.1). Fire-fighter's breathing apparatus works at least 30 min (7.10.3.2.2)</td></tr><tr><td>8 Life-saving</td><td>Survival craft for at least <b>100%</b> of persons certified, minimum two craft (8.10.1.1), plus additional craft for at least <b>10%</b> (8.10.1.2). Davit-launched craft not fitted: marine evacuation system or equivalent (8.7.4). MES serviced at intervals not over 12 months (8.9.7) and deployed at least once every six years (8.9.8)</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Evacuation time (4.8.1):</b> the craft is arranged so that evacuation time = <b>(SFP − 7) / 3 minutes</b>, where SFP is the structural fire protection time from 7.4.2 for areas of major fire hazard, and 7 minutes is deducted for initial detection and extinguishing. Worked example: the SFP times are based on protection for 60 minutes, so (60 − 7) / 3 is about 17.7 minutes.</p><h2>Amendments to the 2000 Code</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Changed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSC.175(79)</td><td>10 Dec 2004</td><td>1 Jul 2006</td><td>2.2.1; Annex 1</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.222(82)</td><td>8 Dec 2006</td><td>1 Jul 2008</td><td>Largest amendment: Chapters 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 13, 14, 18 and Annexes 1, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; new Annex 12</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.260(84)</td><td>16 May 2008</td><td>1 Jan 2010</td><td>8.2.1.2; 14.7.1.3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.271(85)</td><td>4 Dec 2008</td><td>1 Jan 2011</td><td>7.17.1 and tables 7.17-1 and 7.17-3</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.326(90)</td><td>24 May 2012</td><td>1 Jan 2014</td><td>14.15.10</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.352(92)</td><td>21 Jun 2013</td><td>1 Jan 2015</td><td>18.5.4, 18.5.8, 18.5.12</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.424(98)</td><td>15 Jun 2017</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>8.10</td></tr><tr><td>MSC.439(99)</td><td>24 May 2018</td><td>1 Jan 2020</td><td>Chapter 14 and Annex 1</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Operational requirements (Chapter 18)</h2><ul><li><b>Part A (general):</b> craft operational control · craft documentation · training and qualifications · manning of survival craft and supervision · emergency instructions and drills</li><li><b>Part B (passenger craft):</b> type rating training (18.6) · emergency instructions and drills (18.7)</li><li><b>Part C (cargo craft):</b> type rating training (18.8)</li></ul><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> The craft must hold both a valid HSC Safety Certificate and a Permit to Operate, and stay within the Permit's limits. Crew need training for the craft type (type rating), not only general certificates. Evacuation time is set by (SFP − 7) / 3 minutes.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.97(73), 2000 HSC Code text (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.97(73).pdf</li><li>HSC Code amendment status (Deutsche Flagge): https://www.deutsche-flagge.de/de/redaktion/dokumente/interpretationen/solas/x/hsc-code-2000-amendment-status.pdf</li><li>H. Hoppe (IMO), overview of the DSC and HSC Codes, FAST 2005: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/Safety/Documents/International.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ibc-code","v":219561633,"title":"IBC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ibc","chemical-tanker","nls","marpol-ii","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<h1>IBC Code — International Code for the Construction and Equipment of Ships Carrying Dangerous Chemicals in Bulk</h1><h2>At a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>17 Jun 1983, resolution MSC.4(48)</td></tr><tr><td>Mandatory under</td><td>SOLAS chapter VII (MSC.6(48)) and MARPOL Annex II (MEPC.19(22), Dec 1985)</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Chemical tankers of any size, keels laid on or after <b>1 Jul 1986</b></td></tr><tr><td>Older ships</td><td>BCH Code (MARPOL Annex II regulation 11)</td></tr><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of Dangerous Chemicals in Bulk, up to 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>21 chapters and an appendix (certificate model)</td></tr><tr><td>Current text</td><td>2020 edition, with amendments in force 1 Jan 2021 and 1 Jul 2024</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What is the IBC Code</h2><ul><li>An international standard for the <b>safe bulk carriage of dangerous and noxious liquid chemicals</b> listed in Chapter 17</li><li>Covers ship design, construction, equipment and operation, to minimize risk to the ship, crew and environment</li><li>SOLAS covers products marked S or S/P in column d of Chapter 17; MARPOL covers NLS tankers carrying products marked X, Y or Z in column c</li></ul><h2>History</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ibc-dates\"><figcaption>From adoption in 1983 to the 2024 amendment</figcaption></figure><ul><li><b>BCH Code:</b> the predecessor. Chemical tankers built before 1 Jul 1986 must comply with it under MARPOL Annex II regulation 11.</li><li><b>2004 amendments (MSC.176(79)):</b> adopted 10 Dec 2004, deemed accepted 1 Jul 2006, in force <b>1 Jan 2007</b>. They go with the revised MARPOL Annex II (in force 1 Jan 2007), which brought a four-category system for noxious liquid substances (X, Y, Z, OS).</li><li><b>MEPC.318(74) and MSC.460(101):</b> in force 1 Jan 2021; amend chapters 1, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19 and 21, with a complete revision of the product information in chapters 17 and 18.</li><li><b>MEPC.302(72) and MSC.440(99):</b> new model form of the Certificate of Fitness, in force 1 Jan 2020.</li><li><b>MEPC.345(78) and MSC.526(106):</b> adopted 10 Jun 2022 and 10 Nov 2022; in force <b>1 Jul 2024</b>; replace paragraph 2.9.2.1 on the final waterline in damage survival.</li></ul><h2>Application and definitions (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Size</td><td>All ships regardless of size, including under 500 GT</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo</td><td>Bulk dangerous chemicals and noxious liquid substances, excluding petroleum and similar flammable products, where the cargo has greater fire hazards than petroleum products or other significant hazards</td></tr><tr><td>Vapour pressure</td><td>Covered liquids do not exceed 0.28 MPa absolute at 37.8 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Construction date</td><td>Keels laid, or similar stage, on or after 1 Jul 1986; the stage is when identifiable construction begins and assembly reaches <b>at least 50 tonnes or 1%</b> of the estimated structural mass, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Conversions</td><td>A ship of any age converted to a chemical tanker on or after 1 Jul 1986 counts as constructed when the conversion begins</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chemical tanker</td><td>A cargo ship built or adapted to carry in bulk any liquid product listed in Chapter 17</td></tr><tr><td>Cargo area</td><td>Cargo tanks, slop tanks, cargo pump-rooms, cofferdams, adjacent ballast or void spaces, and the deck areas above them</td></tr><tr><td>Cofferdam</td><td>An isolating space between two adjacent steel bulkheads or decks</td></tr><tr><td>Noxious liquid substance (NLS)</td><td>A substance listed in the Pollution Category column of Chapter 17 or 18, or in the current MEPC circular, or provisionally assessed as category X, Y or Z</td></tr><tr><td>Products</td><td>The collective term for NLS and dangerous chemicals</td></tr><tr><td>Length L</td><td>96% of the waterline length at 85% of the least moulded depth</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Certificate and surveys (Chapter 1)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Certificate</td><td>Issued after an initial or renewal survey; valid for a period set by the Administration, <b>not more than 5 years</b></td></tr><tr><td>Renewal</td><td>Completed within 3 months before expiry: valid up to 5 years from the existing expiry date. Completed after expiry: same. More than 3 months before expiry: up to 5 years from the survey completion</td></tr><tr><td>Extension</td><td>An existing certificate may be endorsed for up to 5 further months</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid if</td><td>Surveys or endorsements are missed, or the ship changes flag</td></tr><tr><td>Initial</td><td>Before service, a full examination for compliance</td></tr><tr><td>Renewal survey</td><td>At intervals set by the Administration, not more than 5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>Within 3 months before or after the 2nd or 3rd anniversary; replaces one annual survey</td></tr><tr><td>Annual</td><td>Within 3 months before or after each anniversary</td></tr><tr><td>Additional</td><td>After an investigation or important repairs or renewals</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Ship types and cargo tank location (Chapter 2)</h2><figure class=\"note-diagram\" data-diagram=\"ibc-ship-types\"><figcaption>Where cargo tanks may be placed for each ship type</figcaption></figure><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Hazard</th><th>Tank location (2.6.1)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>Most severe hazards; maximum preventive measures; survives the most severe damage</td><td>Side and bottom setbacks equal to the damage extents, and at least <b>760 mm</b> from the shell</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>Appreciably severe hazards; significant preventive measures</td><td>Bottom setback equal to the vertical damage extent, and at least 760 mm from the shell</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>Sufficiently severe hazards; moderate containment</td><td>No requirement</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Diluted slops tanks from tank washing are exempt from the 760 mm rule.</li></ul><p><b>Damage extents (2.5.1)</b></p><table><thead><tr><th>Damage</th><th>Extent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Side, transverse</td><td>B/5 or 11.5 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Side, longitudinal</td><td>1/3 L^(2/3) or 14.5 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, vertical</td><td>B/15 or 6 m, whichever is less</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, forward of 0.3L</td><td>Longitudinal 1/3 L^(2/3) or 14.5 m; transverse B/6 or 10 m</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom, elsewhere</td><td>Longitudinal 1/3 L^(2/3) or 5 m; transverse B/6 or 5 m</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Other Chapter 2 figures</b></p><ul><li>Suction wells (non-Type 1 ships) may protrude into bottom damage zones by no more than 25% of the double-bottom depth or 350 mm, whichever is less.</li><li>Permeability: stores 0.60, accommodation 0.95, machinery 0.85, voids 0.95.</li><li>Damage standard: Type 1 ships survive damage anywhere along their length; Type 2 and 3 requirements depend on length (thresholds at 150 m, 125 m and 225 m).</li><li>Final equilibrium (2.9): heel not more than 25° (30° if no deck is immersed); righting lever range at least 20° beyond equilibrium, residual righting lever at least 0.1 m, area at least 0.0175 m·rad.</li></ul><h2>Chapters</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Title</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>General</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ship survival capability and location of cargo tanks</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ship arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Cargo containment</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Cargo transfer</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Materials of construction, protective linings and coatings</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Cargo temperature control</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Cargo tank venting and gas-freeing arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Environmental control</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Electrical installations</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Fire protection and fire extinction</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Mechanical ventilation in the cargo area</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Instrumentation</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Personnel protection</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Special requirements</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Operational requirements</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Summary of minimum requirements (the product table)</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Products reviewed and found not to warrant inclusion in the Code (pollution only)</td></tr><tr><td>19 to 21</td><td>Further chapters (19 and 21 amended in 2021)</td></tr></tbody></table><table><thead><tr><th>Ch</th><th>Figures stated in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11</td><td>Cargo-area deck foam: solution rate at least <b>2 l/min per m²</b> (11.3.5.1); at least <b>four</b> foam applicators (11.3.10)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Pump-rooms normally entered: at least <b>20 air changes per hour</b> (12.2). Cargo spaces generally at least 30 changes per hour (12.1.3); 45 for certain products (15.17)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Cargo tank gauging is open, restricted or closed (13.1.1); overflow control in 15.19</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Protective equipment includes aprons, long-sleeved gloves, footwear, chemical-resistant coveralls, goggles or face shields (14.1.1); ships carrying certain cargoes need at least <b>three</b> complete sets of safety equipment (14.2.1)</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Applies where column o of Chapter 17 refers to it; molten sulphur temperature limit 155 °C (15.10.6)</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Maximum cargo per tank: <b>1,250 m³</b> on Type 1 ships (16.1.1), <b>3,000 m³</b> on Type 2 ships (16.1.2); tanks with liquids at ambient temperature must not become liquid-full during the voyage (16.1.3)</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>The product table: pollution category (column c), safety hazards (column d), ship type, and further requirements by column</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><b>Key Point:</b> Before loading, check the product against Chapter 17 and the ship's Certificate of Fitness: ship type, tank requirements and special requirements. Type 1 and Type 2 tanks must stay at least 760 mm from the shell. Pump-rooms normally entered need at least 20 air changes per hour.</blockquote><h2>Sources (checked 23 Nov 2026)</h2><ul><li>Resolution MSC.4(48) (IMO): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.4(48).pdf</li><li>Resolution MSC.176(79), 2004 IBC Code text (MLIT Japan copy): https://www.mlit.go.jp/kaiji/naikouchemical/dai1kai/sanko3e.pdf</li><li>IMO — IBC Code: https://imo.org/en/OurWork/Environment/Pages/IBCCode.aspx</li><li>IMO publication flyer, IBC Code 2020 edition: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/publications/Documents/Flyers/Flyers/IE100E.pdf</li><li>IMO supplement July 2024, MEPC.345(78) and MSC.526(106): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/publications/Documents/Supplements/English/QE100E_supplement_July2024_PQ.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-igc-code","v":247909282,"title":"IGC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["igc","gas-carrier","lng","lpg","ammonia","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>IGC Code — International Code for the Construction and Equipment of Ships Carrying Liquefied Gases in Bulk</b></p><p><b>What is the IGC Code</b></p><ul><li>IMO code for design, construction and equipment of gas carriers (LNG, LPG, ammonia, ethylene, chemical gases)</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch VII Part C</li><li>Certificate: International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of Liquefied Gases in Bulk (CoF) — lists permitted cargoes</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Gas Carrier Codes: Existing Ships Code (before 1976), GC Code (1976–1986)</li><li>IGC Code adopted 1983 (MSC.5(48)) · ships built on/after 1 Jul 1986</li><li>Revised IGC Code adopted 22 May 2014 (MSC.370(93)) · in force 1 Jan 2016 · applies to ships with keel laid on/after 1 Jul 2016</li><li>Which Code applies (keel-laying date): before 1 Jul 1986 = GC Code · 1 Jul 1986 to 30 Jun 2016 = IGC Code as amended up to MSC.220(82) · from 1 Jul 2016 = revised IGC Code</li><li>MSC.476(102) Ch 6 weld testing · MSC.492(104) watertight openings — in force 1 Jan 2024</li><li>MSC.523(106): high-manganese austenitic steel for cargo tanks (−55 to −165 °C) — 1 Jan 2026</li><li>MSC.566(109): toxic cargo (e.g., ammonia) may be used as fuel — in force 1 Jul 2026 (MSC.1/Circ.1681 allows early use)</li><li>MSC.1/Circ.1702 (Jul 2026): interim guidelines for ammonia cargo as fuel</li><li>Comprehensive IGC amendments approved MSC 111 (May 2026) · adoption expected Dec 2026 · in force 1 Jul 2028 (\"one ship, one code\" for gas carriers burning cargo)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Ships of any size carrying liquefied gases with vapour pressure &gt;0.28 MPa absolute at 37.8 °C, and other products listed in Ch 19</li><li>Regardless of size (incl. &lt;500 GT)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Chapters (revised Code)</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 General: definitions, surveys, CoF (≤5 years)</li><li>Ch 2 Ship survival capability and location of cargo tanks: ship types <b>1G</b> (most hazardous, e.g., chlorine), <b>2G</b>, <b>2PG</b>, <b>3G</b> · damage assumptions and tank distances from shell</li><li>Ch 3 Ship arrangements: segregation of cargo area, accommodation, air locks, access</li><li>Ch 4 Cargo containment:<ul><li>Independent tanks type A (full secondary barrier), type B (partial secondary barrier, e.g., Moss/SPB), type C (pressure vessels, no secondary barrier)</li><li>Membrane tanks (full secondary barrier) · integral, semi-membrane</li></ul></li><li>Ch 5 Process pressure vessels, liquid, vapour and pressure piping</li><li>Ch 6 Materials of construction and quality control</li><li>Ch 7 Cargo pressure/temperature control: reliquefaction, gas combustion unit, boil-off use as fuel</li><li>Ch 8 Vent systems for cargo containment (PRVs, vent masts)</li><li>Ch 9 Cargo containment system atmosphere control (inerting, nitrogen)</li><li>Ch 10 Electrical installations</li><li>Ch 11 Fire protection and extinction: water spray on tank domes and manifolds, dry chemical powder for deck, CO₂ for compressor rooms</li><li>Ch 12 Artificial ventilation in the cargo area</li><li>Ch 13 Instrumentation and automation: level, pressure, temperature, overflow (high-high alarm, ESD), gas detection</li><li>Ch 14 Personnel protection: protective clothing, SCBA, decontamination showers, eye wash</li><li>Ch 15 Filling limits for cargo tanks (generally ≤98% at reference temperature)</li><li>Ch 16 Use of cargo as fuel (methane boil-off; from 2026 other cargoes like ammonia under conditions)</li><li>Ch 17 Special requirements (toxic, flammable, ammonia, chlorine, ethylene oxide, vinyl chloride…)</li><li>Ch 18 Operating requirements: cargo information, compatibility, training, cargo transfer, ESD/ship-shore link</li><li>Ch 19 Summary of minimum requirements — product list (ship type, tank type, vapour detection, gauging, special requirements)</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Cargo must be on the CoF list; check Ch 19 row for that product</li><li>ESD tested before transfer · ship-shore safety checklist · filling limit</li><li>Ammonia: toxic — gas detection, PPE and water spray ready; from 2026 can be burned as fuel only with flag approval and equivalent safety</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>ABS — Regulatory Lessons: IGC Code (Oct 2026): https://ww2.eagle.org/content/dam/eagle/publications/Regulatory%20Lessons-IGC%20Code-%20October%202026.pdf</li><li>PRS — IMO requirements entering into force 2026: https://prs.pl/wp-content/uploads/Biulletin_IMO_nr_15_2025_IMO-Requirements-2026_ENG.pdf</li><li>UK P&amp;I — IMO regulatory update 2026: https://www.ukpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/article/imo-regulatory-update-2026/</li><li>Maritime Cyprus — SOLAS amendments and codes update (Oct 2025): https://maritimecyprus.com/2025/10/05/maritime-compliance-solas-amendments-and-relevant-codes-update-3/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-igf-code","v":232954259,"title":"IGF Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["igf","lng-fuel","alternative-fuels","solas-ii-1","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>IGF Code — International Code of Safety for Ships Using Gases or Other Low-flashpoint Fuels</b></p><p><b>What is the IGF Code</b></p><ul><li>IMO code for ships using gas or low-flashpoint fuels (flashpoint &lt;60 °C) — mainly LNG today</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch II-1 Part G</li><li>Goal-based: each chapter has a goal and functional requirements; detailed rules exist for natural gas, others need \"alternative design\" approval (SOLAS II-1/55) using IMO interim guidelines</li><li>Gas carriers burning cargo use the IGC Code instead (not IGF)</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Interim Guidelines for gas-fuelled engine installations (MSC.285(86), 2009)</li><li>Adopted 11 Jun 2015 at MSC 95 (MSC.391(95)) · in force 1 Jan 2017</li><li>Fuel-specific interim guidelines (non-mandatory, MSC.1/Circ.):<ul><li>Methyl/ethyl alcohol: MSC.1/Circ.1621 (2020)</li><li>Fuel cells: MSC.1/Circ.1647 (2022)</li><li>Low-flashpoint diesel: MSC.1/Circ.1666 (2023)</li><li>Ammonia: MSC.1/Circ.1687 (approved MSC 109, circulated Feb 2025)</li><li>Hydrogen (liquefied and compressed): MSC.1/Circ.1701 (approved MSC 111, circulated Jul 2026)</li></ul></li><li>MSC.524(106): high-manganese austenitic steel for fuel tanks — 1 Jan 2026</li><li>MSC.551(108): tank design, pressure relief, gas supply, hazardous areas, bunkering, fire safety, redundancy — 1 Jan 2026</li><li>MSC.567(109): further design, fire safety, explosion prevention, ventilation (e.g., fuel tank suction wells) — 1 Jan 2028</li><li>Draft amendments approved MSC 111 (May 2026): Code covers gaseous fuels whether low-flashpoint or not — adoption expected Dec 2026, in force expected 1 Jul 2028</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS ships using gases or other low-flashpoint fuels, built (contract/keel/delivery) on/after 1 Jan 2017</li><li>Existing ships converting to such fuels</li><li>Not: gas carriers under the IGC Code</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part A — General</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 Preamble · Ch 2 General: definitions (bunkering, double barrier, ESD-protected machinery space, gas-safe machinery space, hazardous areas zones 0/1/2) · alternative design</li><li>Ch 3 Goal and functional requirements</li><li>Ch 4 General requirements: risk assessment (HAZID) · limitation of explosion consequences</li></ul><p><b>Part A-1 — Specific requirements for natural gas (LNG/CNG)</b></p><ul><li>Ch 5 Ship design and arrangement: fuel tank location (≥B/5 or 11.5 m from side shell for most cases, or probabilistic method) · machinery space concepts — gas-safe (double-walled piping) or ESD-protected</li><li>Ch 6 Fuel containment system: independent tanks type A/B/C, membrane · pressure control (boil-off handling, holding time ≥15 days) · max filling limit</li><li>Ch 7 Material and general pipe design</li><li>Ch 8 Bunkering: bunkering station, manifold, ESD link ship–shore, dry-disconnect couplings · bunkering procedures and checklist</li><li>Ch 9 Fuel supply to consumers: redundancy (single failure does not cause loss of propulsion) · master gas valve outside the machinery space</li><li>Ch 10 Power generation including propulsion and other gas consumers (dual-fuel engines, boilers, GCU)</li><li>Ch 11 Fire safety: tank room A-60 · water spray on bunkering station and tank · dry powder at bunkering station</li><li>Ch 12 Explosion prevention: hazardous area zones · equipment certified for zone</li><li>Ch 13 Ventilation: tank connection space, double-wall piping (≥30 air changes/hour), fuel preparation room</li><li>Ch 14 Electrical installations</li><li>Ch 15 Control, monitoring and safety systems: gas detection (alarm 20% LEL, shutdown 40% LEL), ESD, bilge level, leak detection</li><li>Ch 16 Manufacture, workmanship and testing</li></ul><p><b>Part B — Manufacture, workmanship and testing</b> (part B-1 for natural gas)</p><p><b>Part C — Drills and emergency exercises</b></p><ul><li>Ch 17 Drills and emergency exercises: gas-related drills in the SMS (bunkering leak, fire)</li></ul><p><b>Part D — Training</b></p><ul><li>Ch 18 Operation: bunkering procedures, maintenance, enclosed space entry, hot work, approved Fuel Handling Manual on board</li><li>Ch 19 Training: STCW V/3 — Basic and Advanced training for ships subject to the IGF Code (in force 1 Jan 2017)</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Crew on IGF ships need STCW V/3 Basic (all with duties on fuel) and Advanced (master, engineers, persons in charge of fuel)</li><li>Bunkering: ESD link and checklist, safety zone agreed, water curtain on hull, no simultaneous operations without assessment</li><li>Ammonia, hydrogen and methanol ships still built under \"alternative design\" with IMO interim guidelines</li><li>From 2026 (MSC.551) and 2028 (MSC.567): more detailed design rules — check build date</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>ABS — Regulatory Lessons: IGF Code (Oct 2026): https://ww2.eagle.org/content/dam/eagle/publications/Regulatory%20Lessons-IGF%20Code-October-2026-Revised.pdf</li><li>PRS — IMO requirements 2026 (MSC.524(106), MSC.551(108)): https://prs.pl/wp-content/uploads/Biulletin_IMO_nr_15_2025_IMO-Requirements-2026_ENG.pdf</li><li>Ammonia Energy Association — IMO interim guidelines for ammonia fuel: https://ammoniaenergy.org/articles/paving-the-way-for-ammonia-as-a-marine-fuel-insights-into-the-imos-interim-guidelines</li><li>UK P&amp;I — IMO regulatory update 2026: https://www.ukpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/article/imo-regulatory-update-2026/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-iii-code","v":103599465,"title":"III Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["iii-code","flag-state","port-state","imsas","audit","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>III Code — IMO Instruments Implementation Code</b></p><p><b>What is the III Code</b></p><ul><li>Standard for how States implement and enforce IMO conventions — as flag State, coastal State and port State</li><li>Basis of the mandatory IMO Member State Audit Scheme (IMSAS): every Member State is audited on its performance</li><li>Not for ships or companies directly — for governments (maritime administrations like MARINA, USCG, AMSA)</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Voluntary Member State Audit Scheme 2005 (A.946(23)) · Code for the implementation of mandatory IMO instruments (A.996(25), 2007)</li><li>III Code adopted 4 Dec 2013 by Assembly resolution A.1070(28) · replaced the 2011 Code for the Implementation of Mandatory IMO Instruments (A.1054(27))</li><li>Made mandatory through amendments to SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW, Load Lines, Tonnage, COLREG (and their protocols) — in force 1 Jan 2016</li><li>IMSAS: framework and procedures A.1067(28) · audits started 2016 (about 7-year cycle)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All Contracting Governments to the mandatory instruments: SOLAS 74 and 88 Protocol · MARPOL · STCW · LL 66 and 88 Protocol · Tonnage 69 · COLREG 72</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part 1 — Common areas</b></p><ul><li>Objective · strategy (policy, legislation, resources to implement and enforce)</li><li>General obligations: law to give full effect to the instruments · communication of information to IMO (mandatory reporting)</li><li>Initial actions: when a State becomes Party — legislation, administration, procedures</li><li>Records · improvement (continuous review, corrective actions from audits)</li></ul><p><b>Part 2 — Flag States</b></p><ul><li>Implementation: laws, regulations, guidance · delegation of authority to Recognized Organizations (RO Code) with oversight</li><li>Enforcement: inspections, surveys, investigations, penalties strong enough to deter violations</li><li>Flag surveyors: qualified, trained, independent</li><li>Investigations of marine casualties (Casualty Investigation Code) and reporting to IMO (GISIS)</li><li>Evaluation and review of flag performance (detention rates, casualties)</li></ul><p><b>Part 3 — Coastal States</b></p><ul><li>Implementation of coastal duties: hydrographic services, SAR, navigational warnings, ships' routeing, VTS, pollution response</li><li>Enforcement in territorial sea and EEZ within UNCLOS limits</li></ul><p><b>Part 4 — Port States</b></p><ul><li>Port State control in line with IMO procedures (resolution A.1185(33) Procedures for PSC) · regional MoUs (Tokyo, Paris, etc.)</li><li>Reception facilities for ship wastes (MARPOL) · reporting of deficiencies and detentions to flag and IMO</li><li>No more favourable treatment for ships of non-Parties</li></ul><p><b>Annex — mandatory obligations by instrument</b> (list of what each instrument requires of States)</p><p><b>Why it matters on board</b></p><ul><li>Your flag's performance (IMSAS results, PSC record) affects how ships under that flag are targeted by PSC</li><li>Flags on Paris/Tokyo MoU white/grey/black lists use the III Code framework for improvement</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>III Code = the \"ISM Code for governments\": policy, procedures, records, audits, continuous improvement</li><li>Philippines: MARINA is audited as flag/port/coastal State under IMSAS (with PCG for coastal/port duties)</li><li>Results of IMO audits are confidential summaries shared with Member States (GISIS)</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Member State Audit Scheme and III Code: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/MSAS/Pages/default.aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution A.1070(28) III Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1070(28).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-imdg-code","v":144880927,"title":"IMDG Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["imdg","dangerous-goods","packaged","solas-vii","marpol-iii","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>IMDG Code — International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code</b></p><p><b>What is the IMDG Code</b></p><ul><li>IMO code for the safe carriage of dangerous goods in packaged form (packages, IBCs, portable tanks, road tank vehicles, containers) by sea</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch VII Part A and MARPOL Annex III (marine pollutants)</li><li>Based on the UN Recommendations on the Transport of Dangerous Goods (\"Orange Book\") · updated every 2 years</li><li>2 volumes + Supplement (EmS Guide, MFAG, IMO packing/securing guidance)</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1965 first adopted (recommendatory) · adopted as mandatory Code 24 May 2002 (MSC.122(75); SOLAS VII amended by MSC.123(75)) · voluntary 1 Jan 2003 · mandatory 1 Jan 2004</li><li>2-year amendment cycle: each amendment voluntary from 1 Jan of the odd year, mandatory from 1 Jan of the next year</li><li>Amdt 41-22 (MSC.501(105)): mandatory 1 Jan 2024</li><li>Amdt 42-24 (MSC.556(108)): voluntary 1 Jan 2025 · mandatory 1 Jan 2026</li><li>Amdt 43-26 (adopted MSC 111, May 2026): voluntary 1 Jan 2027 · mandatory 1 Jan 2028</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships to which SOLAS applies carrying dangerous goods in packaged form, and all ships under MARPOL Annex III</li><li>Also: shippers, packers, freight forwarders, terminals (training in Ch 1.3 is mandatory for shore staff)</li><li>Not covered: bulk cargoes (IMSBC, IBC, IGC), ship's own stores and equipment</li></ul><hr><p><b>Volume 1 — Parts 1, 2, 4–7</b></p><p>*Part 1 — General provisions, definitions, training*</p><ul><li>1.1 Application · 1.2 Definitions, units, abbreviations</li><li>1.3 Training (mandatory for shore-side personnel; records kept)</li><li>1.4 Security provisions (high-consequence dangerous goods, security plans)</li><li>1.5 Radioactive material general provisions</li></ul><p>*Part 2 — Classification*</p><ul><li>Class 1 Explosives (Divisions 1.1–1.6, compatibility groups A–S)</li><li>Class 2 Gases: 2.1 flammable · 2.2 non-flammable, non-toxic · 2.3 toxic</li><li>Class 3 Flammable liquids (flashpoint ≤60 °C)</li><li>Class 4: 4.1 flammable solids, self-reactive, desensitized explosives · 4.2 spontaneously combustible · 4.3 dangerous when wet</li><li>Class 5: 5.1 oxidizing substances · 5.2 organic peroxides</li><li>Class 6: 6.1 toxic · 6.2 infectious</li><li>Class 7 Radioactive material</li><li>Class 8 Corrosive substances</li><li>Class 9 Miscellaneous (incl. lithium and sodium-ion batteries, environmentally hazardous substances)</li><li>2.10 Marine pollutants (MARPOL Annex III) — \"Marine Pollutant\" mark</li><li>Packing groups: I high danger · II medium · III low</li></ul><p>*Part 4 — Packing and tank provisions*</p><ul><li>4.1 Packagings, IBCs, large packagings (packing instructions P/IBC/LP)</li><li>4.2 Portable tanks and MEGCs (T codes) · 4.3 Bulk containers (BK)</li></ul><p>*Part 5 — Consignment procedures*</p><ul><li>5.1 General · 5.2 Marking and labelling of packages (UN number, proper shipping name, labels, marine pollutant mark)</li><li>5.3 Placarding and marking of cargo transport units (placards ≥250 × 250 mm on each side)</li><li>5.4 Documentation:<ul><li>Dangerous Goods Declaration (UN number, PSN, class, PG, quantity, marine pollutant, flashpoint)</li><li>Container/Vehicle Packing Certificate (signed by packer)</li><li>Dangerous goods manifest or stowage plan on board (from 42-24: shows subsidiary hazards too)</li><li>5.4.4.2 (42-24): exemption certificates go with the cargo information</li></ul></li><li>5.5 Special provisions (fumigated units — FUMIGATED mark; CTUs with coolant/conditioner)</li></ul><p>*Part 6 — Construction and testing of packagings, IBCs, tanks*</p><ul><li>UN specification marks · drop, stacking, leakproofness, hydraulic pressure tests</li></ul><p>*Part 7 — Operations (shipboard)*</p><ul><li>7.1 Stowage: categories A–E (passenger and cargo ships); \"on deck only\" / \"under deck\" codes</li><li>7.2 Segregation: terms \"away from\", \"separated from\", \"separated by a complete compartment or hold from\", \"separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment or hold from\" · Segregation Table 7.2.4</li><li>7.3 Consigning operations for CTUs (packing, securing, CTU Code)</li><li>7.4–7.7 Container ships, ro-ro ships, general cargo ships, barge-carrying ships</li><li>7.8 Special requirements in the event of an incident and fire precautions</li><li>7.9 Exemptions, approvals and certificates</li></ul><p><b>Volume 2 — Part 3 and Appendices</b></p><ul><li>3.1 General · 3.2 Dangerous Goods List (by UN number: PSN, class, subsidiary risk, PG, special provisions, limited/excepted quantities, packing, tanks, EmS, stowage &amp; handling, segregation, properties)</li><li>3.3 Special provisions · 3.4 Limited quantities · 3.5 Excepted quantities</li><li>Appendix A Generic and N.O.S. names · Appendix B Glossary · Index</li></ul><p><b>Supplement</b></p><ul><li>EmS Guide: emergency schedules — Fire (F-A to F-J) and Spillage (S-A to S-Z)</li><li>MFAG: Medical First Aid Guide for accidents involving dangerous goods</li><li>Reporting procedures · CTU Code reference · IMO Packing guidance · Safe use of pesticides (fumigation)</li><li>INF Code (irradiated nuclear fuel, plutonium, high-level radioactive waste)</li></ul><p><b>Amendment 42-24 (mandatory 1 Jan 2026) — what changed</b></p><ul><li>Sodium-ion batteries (Class 9, UN 3551/3552) now handled in line with lithium battery entries</li><li>New vehicle entries: UN 3556 (lithium-ion), 3557 (lithium metal), 3558 (sodium-ion) — packing instruction P912</li><li>UN 1361 Carbon: SP925 deleted; new SP978 (weathering, temperature checks, packing, documents) · UN 1362 activated carbon SP979</li><li>Stowage moved to Category D (deck only; not on passenger ships): UN 3129, 3130, 3148, and UN 3536 (lithium batteries installed in CTUs)</li><li>UN 3423 reclassified Class 6.1 (sub 8), PG I</li><li>Stowage plan must show subsidiary hazards</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Check every DG unit against the Declaration: UN number, PSN, class, labels/placards, marine pollutant mark, segregation</li><li>Stowage plan and DG manifest on the bridge; EmS numbers ready for fire/spill</li><li>Most container fires come from mis-declared cargo (charcoal, lithium batteries, calcium hypochlorite)</li><li>Record the amendment in use: 42-24 from 1 Jan 2026</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>AM Nautical — IMDG Code Amendment 42-24, what changed: https://www.amnautical.com/blogs/the-mariners-blog/imdg-code-amendment-42-24</li><li>West P&amp;I — SOLAS amendments to the IMDG Code (42-24): https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/September-2024/SOLAS-Amendments-to-the-IMDG-Code-(Amendment-42-24/</li><li>GAC — IMDG amendment becoming mandatory: https://www.gac.com/hot-port-news/international-maritime-dangerous-goods-code-amendment-becoming-mandatory</li><li>PRS — IMO requirements 2026 (MSC.556(108)): https://prs.pl/wp-content/uploads/Biulletin_IMO_nr_15_2025_IMO-Requirements-2026_ENG.pdf</li><li>UK MCA — MIN 715 (IMSBC 07-23 and IMDG 42-24): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/min-715-m-cargoes-amendments-to-the-imsbc-code-07-23-and-imdg-code-42-24</li><li>DNV — IMO MSC 111: amendments adopted (May 2026): https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-mcs-111-new-mass-code-adopted/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-imsbc-code","v":177181622,"title":"IMSBC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["imsbc","solid-bulk","liquefaction","solas-vi","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>IMSBC Code — International Maritime Solid Bulk Cargoes Code</b></p><p><b>What is the IMSBC Code</b></p><ul><li>IMO code for the safe stowage and shipment of solid bulk cargoes (excluding grain)</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch VI (Reg 1-1) and Ch VII Part A-1 (dangerous goods in solid form in bulk)</li><li>Core: shipper's cargo information, test certificates, Individual Schedules per cargo (BCSN)</li><li>Updated every 2 years (like IMDG)</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Replaced the BC Code (1965, voluntary)</li><li>Adopted 4 Dec 2008 by MSC.268(85) · voluntary from 1 Jan 2009 · mandatory from 1 Jan 2011</li><li>Amdt 07-23 (MSC.539(107)): mandatory 1 Jan 2025</li><li>Amdt 08-25 (MSC.575(110), June 2025): voluntary 1 Jan 2026 · mandatory 1 Jan 2027</li><li>Amdt 09-27: in preparation (CCC sub-committee)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships to which SOLAS applies carrying solid bulk cargoes (other than grain — see Grain Code)</li><li>Shippers, terminals and competent authorities (testing, certification, approvals)</li></ul><p><b>Cargo groups</b></p><ul><li>Group A: may liquefy if shipped above its Transportable Moisture Limit (TML) — e.g., iron ore fines, nickel ore, bauxite (some), mineral concentrates</li><li>Group B: chemical hazard (may be MHB — Materials Hazardous only in Bulk, or IMDG class) — e.g., coal (self-heating, methane), DRI, sulphur, ammonium nitrate fertilizers</li><li>Group C: neither liquefies nor chemical hazard — e.g., iron ore (lump), limestone, sand</li><li>A cargo can be both A and B</li></ul><hr><p><b>Sections</b></p><ul><li>Sec 1 General provisions and definitions (BCSN, TML, FMP, angle of repose, MHB, competent authority)</li><li>Sec 2 General loading, carriage and unloading precautions:<ul><li>Distribute cargo to avoid excessive stress (loading/unloading plan agreed by master and terminal — SOLAS VI/7, BLU Code)</li><li>Trimming to minimize shift · hatch covers weathertight</li></ul></li><li>Sec 3 Safety of personnel and ship:<ul><li>Entry into enclosed spaces · fumigation in transit · gas detection and oxygen meters on board</li></ul></li><li>Sec 4 Assessment of acceptability of consignments:<ul><li>Shipper gives cargo information before loading (BCSN, group, IMDG class, total quantity, moisture content, TML, angle of repose, chemical properties)</li><li>Certificate of TML and moisture content: TML test ≤6 months old · moisture sample ≤7 days before loading (≤3 days if heavy rain)</li><li>Sampling and testing procedures by the shipper</li></ul></li><li>Sec 5 Trimming procedures (cohesive / non-cohesive cargo, angle of repose ≤30° shifting rule)</li><li>Sec 6 Methods of determining angle of repose</li><li>Sec 7 Cargoes that may liquefy (Group A):<ul><li>Moisture content must be &lt; TML · specially constructed or fitted ships may carry above TML</li><li>Can test (master's simple check) is no substitute for lab tests</li></ul></li><li>Sec 8 Test procedures for cargoes that may liquefy (Appendix 2: flow table, penetration, Proctor/Fagerberg)</li><li>Sec 9 Materials possessing chemical hazards (classes 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 5.1, 6.1, 7, 8, 9 and MHB) — segregation table 9.3.3</li><li>Sec 10 Carriage of solid bulk wastes (Basel Convention documents)</li><li>Sec 11 Security provisions</li><li>Sec 12 Stowage factor conversion tables</li><li>Sec 13 References to related information and recommendations</li></ul><p><b>Appendices</b></p><ul><li>Appendix 1 Individual Schedules (alphabetical by BCSN): description, characteristics (group, class, angle of repose, bulk density), hazard, stowage &amp; segregation, hold cleanliness, weather precautions, loading, precautions, ventilation, carriage, discharge, clean-up, emergency procedures</li><li>Appendix 2 Laboratory test procedures · Appendix 3 Properties of solid bulk cargoes · Appendix 4 Index · Appendix 5 BCSN in other languages</li><li>Supplement: BLU Code &amp; Manual · Recommendations on fumigation · Emergency schedules (EmS) · MFAG</li></ul><p><b>Amendment 08-25 (mandatory 1 Jan 2027)</b></p><ul><li>12 new schedules: incl. Apatite Concentrate, Asphalt Granulates, Fish Meal (Stabilized), Iron Ore Briquettes, Phosphate Rock Fines (uncalcined), Zinc Slag (Coarse)</li><li>11 schedules amended: incl. Ferrosilicon, Iron Ore Pellets, Castor Beans/Meal, DRI (A) and (B)</li><li>Sec 3 fumigation guidance updated · Sec 9 segregation table 9.3.3 revised (radioactive separation clearer, \"MHB\" references removed)</li></ul><p><b>Cargoes not listed</b></p><ul><li>A cargo with no schedule needs a tripartite agreement: competent authorities of the loading port, the flag State and the unloading port (provisional assessment)</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Never load Group A cargo without a valid TML + moisture certificate; reject if wet, if sample dates are old, or if can test shows free water</li><li>Liquefaction = sudden list and capsize (nickel ore, bauxite, iron ore fines casualties)</li><li>Coal: monitor temperature, CH₄, O₂, CO; surface-ventilate only per schedule</li><li>Carry the latest amendment on board (08-25 voluntary 2026, mandatory 2027)</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>West P&amp;I — SOLAS amendments to the IMSBC Code (08-25): https://www.westpandi.com/News-and-Resources/News/October-2025/SOLAS-Amendments-to-the-IMSBC-Code-(Amendment-08-2/</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.575(110): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.575(110).pdf</li><li>UK P&amp;I — IMO regulatory update 2026: https://www.ukpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/article/imo-regulatory-update-2026/</li><li>UK MCA — MIN 715 (IMSBC 07-23): https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/min-715-m-cargoes-amendments-to-the-imsbc-code-07-23-and-imdg-code-42-24</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-inf-code","v":251568545,"title":"INF Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["inf-code","nuclear","radioactive","class-7","solas-vii","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>INF Code — International Code for the Safe Carriage of Packaged Irradiated Nuclear Fuel, Plutonium and High-Level Radioactive Wastes on Board Ships</b></p><p><b>What is the INF Code</b></p><ul><li>Extra ship requirements for carrying <b>INF cargo</b>: packaged irradiated nuclear fuel, plutonium and high-level radioactive wastes (IMDG Class 7, in Type B or similar packages)</li><li>The packages follow the IMDG Code and IAEA transport regulations; the INF Code covers the <b>ship</b>: stability, fire safety, cooling, securing, power, radiological protection, emergency plans</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Chapter VII Part D</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>INF Code first adopted as recommendations, A.748(18) (1993)</li><li>Made mandatory: Code adopted 27 May 1999, resolution MSC.88(71); SOLAS VII Part D by MSC.87(71)</li><li>In force 1 Jan 2001</li><li>Amended later (e.g. alignment with IMDG Code and IAEA regulations; ship notification)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships, regardless of date of build and size (including cargo ships under 500 GT), carrying INF cargo</li><li>Not: warships and naval auxiliaries (flag States should apply it as far as reasonable)</li></ul><hr><p><b>INF ship classes (by total radioactivity carried)</b></p><ul><li><b>INF 1</b>: aggregate activity less than <b>4,000 TBq</b></li><li><b>INF 2</b>: irradiated nuclear fuel or high-level wastes less than <b>2 × 10⁶ TBq</b>; plutonium less than <b>2 × 10⁵ TBq</b></li><li><b>INF 3</b>: no restriction on aggregate activity</li><li>Passenger ships may not carry INF cargo that needs an INF 3 ship</li></ul><p><b>Chapters</b></p><ul><li><b>1 General</b>: definitions, application, INF classes, survey and certification — <b>International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of INF Cargo</b> after initial survey (with SOLAS certificates)</li><li><b>2 Damage stability</b>: INF 1 — to the Administration's satisfaction; INF 2 — SOLAS II-1 Part B (passenger standard) or Part B-1 (cargo standard, any length); INF 3 — IBC Code <b>type 1 ship</b> survival capability and cargo location, or SOLAS II-1 B-1 with a higher index (R-INF)</li><li><b>3 Fire safety measures</b>: INF 2/3 — water spray or fire-fighting capability for cargo spaces, fixed extinguishing in machinery spaces, detection and alarm</li><li><b>4 Temperature control of cargo spaces</b>: average temperature not above <b>55 °C</b>; ventilation or refrigeration, independent and duplicated where essential</li><li><b>5 Structural considerations</b>: strength of deck and supporting structure for the packages</li><li><b>6 Cargo securing arrangements</b>: permanent fittings for accelerations of <b>1.5 g</b> longitudinal and transverse, <b>1.0 g</b> vertical up, <b>2.0 g</b> vertical down</li><li><b>7 Electrical power supplies</b>: INF 2/3 — alternative power source for at least <b>36 hours</b> for cooling, flooding and emergency services</li><li><b>8 Radiological protection</b>: shielding, radiation monitoring, keep crew doses as low as reasonably achievable</li><li><b>9 Management and training</b>: crew trained on INF cargo risks and emergency actions</li><li><b>10 Shipboard emergency plan</b>: approved by the Administration (with SMPEP-like structure: reporting, response, co-ordination)</li><li><b>11 Notification</b> of incidents involving INF cargo (SOLAS VII/7-1) to the nearest coastal State and the flag State</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Know the three classes: INF 1 &lt; 4,000 TBq · INF 2 · INF 3 no limit</li><li>55 °C cargo space average · 36 h alternative power (INF 2/3) · securing 1.5 g / 1.0 g up / 2.0 g down</li><li>Any release or probable release is reported at once under SOLAS VII/7-1</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 11 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — INF Code: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/INF-Code.aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.88(71) INF Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.88(71).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ip-code","v":43776357,"title":"IP Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ip-code","industrial-personnel","offshore-wind","transfer","solas-xv","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>IP Code — International Code of Safety for Ships Carrying Industrial Personnel</b></p><p><b>What is the IP Code</b></p><ul><li>Safety standards for ships that carry <b>industrial personnel</b> — persons transported or accommodated on board for offshore industrial activities (offshore wind farms, oil and gas, construction)</li><li>Fills the gap between cargo ship rules (≤12 passengers) and passenger ship rules</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Chapter XV, with special attention to <b>personnel transfer</b> to other ships and offshore facilities</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Developed after the growth of offshore wind service operation vessels (SOVs) and crew transfer vessels</li><li>Adopted 10 Nov 2022 by MSC 106: Code MSC.527(106); SOLAS Ch XV MSC.521(106)</li><li>In force 1 Jul 2024</li><li>MSC 111 (May 2026): amendment — dimensioning weight of an industrial person (with PPE) <b>90 kg</b> (instead of 75 kg) · in force 1 Jan 2028</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Cargo ships and high-speed cargo craft of <b>500 GT and above</b> on international voyages carrying more than 12 industrial personnel (counted together with passengers and special personnel)</li><li>Ships under 500 GT: the Administration decides; the goals and functional requirements apply as far as reasonable</li><li>Not: passenger ships certified for the persons carried</li></ul><hr><p><b>Structure</b></p><ul><li><b>Part I General</b>: goal, definitions (industrial personnel, offshore industrial activities, personnel transfer), application, <b>Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate</b> and surveys</li><li><b>Part II Goals and functional requirements</b>: 1 Industrial personnel · 2 Safe transfer · 3 Subdivision and stability · 4 Machinery installations · 5 Electrical installations · 6 Periodically unattended machinery spaces · 7 Fire safety · 8 Life-saving appliances · 9 Dangerous goods</li><li><b>Part III Regulations</b>: Reg 1 Industrial personnel · Reg 2 Safe transfer</li><li><b>Part IV</b> Additional requirements for ships certified under SOLAS Ch I (stability, fire, LSA by number of persons — ships carrying up to 60, and over 60 up to 240 persons)</li><li><b>Part V</b> Additional requirements for high-speed craft (SOLAS Ch X)</li><li>Appendix: Form of Industrial Personnel Safety Certificate and Record of Equipment</li></ul><p><b>Industrial personnel (Reg 1)</b> — means must be provided so they:</p><ul><li>Are medically fit</li><li>Can communicate with the ship's crew (understand safety information, symbols, signs and alarms)</li><li>Have appropriate safety training (personal survival, fire safety, first aid — or a qualification acceptable to the Administration)</li><li>Receive <b>ship-specific safety familiarization</b> before leaving port or right after boarding: ship layout; location of lifejackets, muster and embarkation stations, escape routes, first aid; signs and alarms; action on alarm</li><li>Receive <b>familiarization with the transfer arrangements</b> before being transferred</li><li>Master must have documents confirming their training before they sail</li></ul><p><b>Safe transfer (Reg 2)</b></p><ul><li>Transfer appliances (walk-to-work gangways, boat landings, baskets) kept clean, maintained, permanently marked with limits; rigging checked</li><li>Risk assessment of each transfer; weather limits; communication between ship and facility; rescue of a person in the water</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Industrial personnel are not passengers and not crew — count them for LSA and stability (90 kg each for new ships from 2028)</li><li>No industrial personnel on board without training documents and ship familiarization</li><li>Transfers only within the assessed weather limits, with recovery means ready</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 11 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.527(106) IP Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.527(106).pdf</li><li>DNV — MSC 111 (90 kg dimensioning weight, in force 2028): https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-mcs-111-new-mass-code-adopted/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-is-code-2008","v":181112263,"title":"IS Code 2008","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["is-code","intact-stability","gz","gm","weather-criterion","solas-ii-1","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>2008 IS Code — International Code on Intact Stability, 2008</b></p><p><b>What is the IS Code</b></p><ul><li>Intact stability criteria and guidance so ships do not capsize in normal operation</li><li><b>Part A</b> mandatory (through SOLAS II-1 and the 1988 Load Lines Protocol); <b>Part B</b> recommendations and guidance</li><li>Basis for the ship's stability booklet and loading instrument checks</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Replaced the Code on Intact Stability A.749(18) (1993) and older A.167/A.562 (weather criterion)</li><li>Adopted 4 Dec 2008, resolution MSC.267(85) (Part A mandatory via MSC.269(85) SOLAS and MSC.270(85) LL amendments)</li><li>Mandatory from 1 Jul 2010</li><li>Amended several times since (e.g. 2015–2016 criteria for ships engaged in anchor handling, lifting and towing)</li><li>Second-generation intact stability criteria: Interim Guidelines MSC.1/Circ.1627 (2020) — vulnerability checks for parametric roll, pure loss of stability, dead ship, excessive acceleration, surf-riding/broaching (voluntary)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Ships and other marine vehicles of 24 m in length and over: cargo ships, cargo ships with timber deck cargo, grain ships, passenger ships, fishing vessels, special purpose ships, offshore supply vessels, MODUs, pontoons, containerships</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part A — Mandatory criteria</b></p><ul><li><b>Ch 1 General</b>: purpose, definitions, dynamic stability phenomena in waves</li><li><b>Ch 2 General criteria (2.2) — righting lever (GZ) curve, after free-surface correction</b><ul><li>Area under GZ up to 30°: <b>≥ 0.055 m·rad</b></li><li>Area up to 40° (or downflooding angle φf if less): <b>≥ 0.09 m·rad</b></li><li>Area between 30° and 40° (or φf): <b>≥ 0.03 m·rad</b></li><li>GZ at 30° or more: <b>≥ 0.20 m</b></li><li>Maximum GZ at an angle <b>≥ 25°</b> (preferably above 30°)</li><li>Initial metacentric height GM₀: <b>≥ 0.15 m</b></li></ul></li><li><b>2.3 Severe wind and rolling (weather) criterion</b><ul><li>Steady wind heeling lever lw1 (wind pressure 504 Pa); angle of heel under steady wind φ0 <b>≤ 16° or 80% of deck-edge immersion angle</b>, whichever is less</li><li>Ship rolls to windward by φ1 (calculated from tables), then a gust lever lw2 = 1.5 × lw1</li><li>Area \"b\" (reserve) must be <b>≥ area \"a\"</b> (heeling energy)</li></ul></li><li><b>Ch 3 Special criteria for certain types of ships</b><ul><li>3.1 Passenger ships: heel from crowding of passengers to one side and from turning ≤ 10°</li><li>3.2 Oil tankers ≥ 5,000 DWT: MARPOL Annex I Reg 27 criteria</li><li>3.3 Cargo ships carrying timber deck cargo: area up to 40° ≥ 0.08 m·rad; max GZ ≥ 0.25 m; GM ≥ 0.10 m throughout the voyage (allowing for water absorption and icing)</li><li>3.4 Grain ships: International Grain Code</li><li>3.5 High-speed craft: HSC Code</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Part B — Recommendations and guidelines</b></p><ul><li>Ch 2 Design criteria: fishing vessels, pontoons, containerships &gt; 100 m, offshore supply vessels, special purpose ships, MODUs, anchor handling, lifting, towing</li><li>Ch 3 Preparing stability information: free surface, permanent ballast, standard loading conditions, stability booklet, timber deck cargo operations</li><li>Ch 4 Stability instruments (loading computers)</li><li>Ch 5 <b>Operational provisions against capsizing</b>: precautions in heavy weather, ship handling, avoid resonance with waves (see MSC.1/Circ.1228)</li><li>Ch 6 Icing considerations · Ch 7 Watertight and weathertight integrity (hatches, doors, freeing ports)</li><li>Ch 8 Determination of lightship parameters (inclining test, lightweight check)</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Every departure (and worst voyage) condition must meet the 2.2 GZ criteria and 2.3 weather criterion — check with the approved loading computer</li><li>Free-surface correction is part of GM and GZ — slack tanks reduce stability</li><li>Timber deck cargo, grain and passenger ships have extra criteria</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 11 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.267(85) 2008 IS Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.267(85).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ism-code","v":59640491,"title":"ISM Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ism","sms","safety-management","solas-ix","imo","code","core"],"body":"<p><b>ISM Code — International Safety Management Code</b></p><p><b>What is the ISM Code</b></p><ul><li>International standard for the safe management and operation of ships and for pollution prevention</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch IX</li><li>The Company builds a Safety Management System (SMS); the flag State (or RO) audits it</li><li>Certificates: Document of Compliance (DOC) — Company · Safety Management Certificate (SMC) — ship</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Born from accidents caused by poor management: Herald of Free Enterprise (1987), Scandinavian Star (1990)</li><li>Adopted 4 Nov 1993 (Assembly resolution A.741(18))</li><li>Mandatory 1 Jul 1998: passenger ships, tankers, bulk carriers, high-speed craft ≥500 GT</li><li>1 Jul 2002: other cargo ships and MODUs ≥500 GT</li><li>Amendments: MSC.104(73) (2002), MSC.179(79), MSC.195(80) (2006), MSC.273(85) (2010: risk assessment, near-miss emphasis), MSC.353(92) (2015)</li><li>Guidelines on implementation by Administrations: A.1118(30) (2017) · Revised guidelines on implementation by Companies</li><li>MSC.428(98) (2017): cyber risks addressed in the SMS by first annual DOC verification after 1 Jan 2021</li><li>2026: IMO III 12 started a comprehensive revision of the ISM implementation guidelines (roadmap to III 14)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS ships ≥500 GT on international voyages: passenger ships (incl. HSC), oil/chemical tankers, gas carriers, bulk carriers, cargo HSC, other cargo ships, MODUs</li><li>Not covered: government ships on non-commercial service</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part A — Implementation</b></p><ul><li>1 General:<ul><li>1.1 Definitions (Company, Administration, SMS, DOC, SMC, objective evidence, observation, non-conformity, major non-conformity, anniversary date)</li><li>1.2 Objectives: safety at sea, prevent injury/loss of life, avoid damage to environment and property · assess all identified risks, set safeguards · improve skills and emergency preparedness</li><li>1.4 Functional requirements of an SMS</li></ul></li><li>2 Safety and environmental-protection policy (Company writes it; implemented at all levels)</li><li>3 Company responsibilities and authority (if not the owner, Company details reported to flag; defined responsibilities; adequate resources and shore support)</li><li>4 Designated Person(s) Ashore (DPA): direct access to highest management; monitors safety of each ship</li><li>5 Master's responsibility and authority:<ul><li>Implements policy, motivates crew, gives orders simply, verifies requirements, reviews SMS and reports deficiencies</li><li><b>Overriding authority</b> to make decisions on safety and pollution prevention, and to request Company help</li></ul></li><li>6 Resources and personnel: qualified master, crew familiarization, SMS in a working language understood by crew, training needs identified</li><li>7 Shipboard operations: plans, procedures, instructions with checklists for key operations</li><li>8 Emergency preparedness: identify emergencies, drills and exercises, Company response at any time</li><li>9 Reports and analysis of non-conformities, accidents and hazardous occurrences (near misses) — corrective action</li><li>10 Maintenance of the ship and equipment: inspections, records, critical equipment and systems identified and tested</li><li>11 Documentation: controlled documents, Safety Management Manual on board</li><li>12 Company verification, review and evaluation: internal audits on board and ashore at least every 12 months (≤3 months extension in exceptional cases); management review</li></ul><p><b>Part B — Certification and verification</b></p><ul><li>13 Certification and periodical verification:<ul><li>DOC: valid ≤5 years · annual verification (±3 months of anniversary)</li><li>SMC: valid ≤5 years · intermediate verification between 2nd and 3rd anniversary</li><li>Ship SMC only if Company holds a valid DOC for that ship type</li></ul></li><li>14 Interim certification:<ul><li>Interim DOC: ≤12 months (new Company or new ship type)</li><li>Interim SMC: ≤6 months (new ship, new Company, change of flag) — may extend 6 months more in special cases</li></ul></li><li>15 Verification (audits per procedures acceptable to the Administration)</li><li>16 Forms of certificates</li></ul><p><b>Audit findings</b></p><ul><li>Observation: statement of fact</li><li>Non-conformity (NC): objective evidence that a requirement is not met — corrective action within agreed time (usually ≤3 months)</li><li>Major non-conformity: serious threat to safety/environment, or failure to implement a requirement — must be downgraded before the ship sails; may lead to withdrawal of SMC</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Master's overriding authority is written into the SMS — use it</li><li>Near misses must be reported (Element 9) — PSC and auditors look for real reports</li><li>Critical equipment (steering, emergency generator, fire pumps…) tested per SMS and recorded</li><li>Cyber risk is part of the SMS since 2021</li><li>Many PSC detentions are \"ISM-related\": repeated deficiencies show the SMS is not working</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>DNV — IMO III 12: ISM implementation guidelines revision (Jul 2026): https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-iii-12-initiation-of-ism-implementation-guidelines-revision-and-progression-of-survey-requirements-revision/</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.353(92) (ISM amendments): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/OurWork/HumanElement/Documents/353(92).pdf</li><li>UK MCA — MGN 708 guidance on the ISM Code Regulations 2026: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-708m-guidance-on-the-merchant-shipping-international-safety-management-ism-code-regulations-2026</li><li>ICS — III 12/12 Proposals to improve ISM implementation by Administrations: https://www.ics-shipping.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/III-12-12-Proposals-to-improve-the-implementation-of-the-ISM-Code-by-Administrations-ICS.pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-isps-code","v":233925309,"title":"ISPS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["isps","security","solas-xi-2","ssp","imo","code","core"],"body":"<p><b>ISPS Code — International Ship and Port Facility Security Code</b></p><p><b>What is the ISPS Code</b></p><ul><li>International framework for ships and port facilities to detect security threats and take preventive measures</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch XI-2 · Part A mandatory · Part B guidance (some flags/US/EU make parts of B mandatory)</li><li>Ship: Ship Security Assessment (SSA) → Ship Security Plan (SSP) → International Ship Security Certificate (ISSC)</li><li>Port: Port Facility Security Assessment (PFSA) → Port Facility Security Plan (PFSP) → Statement of Compliance of a Port Facility</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Response to 11 Sep 2001 attacks</li><li>Adopted 12 Dec 2002 (SOLAS Diplomatic Conference, with SOLAS XI-1 and XI-2 amendments)</li><li>In force 1 Jul 2004</li><li>Related: SOLAS XI-1/3 IMO ship number, XI-1/5 Continuous Synopsis Record, V/19 AIS, XI-2/6 Ship Security Alert System (SSAS)</li><li>Cyber risk: addressed via ISM (MSC.428(98)) and IMO guidelines MSC-FAL.1/Circ.3/Rev.3</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Passenger ships (incl. HSC) on international voyages</li><li>Cargo ships (incl. HSC) ≥500 GT on international voyages</li><li>Mobile offshore drilling units</li><li>Port facilities serving these ships</li><li>Not covered: warships, naval auxiliaries, State ships on non-commercial service</li></ul><p><b>Security levels</b></p><ul><li>Level 1: normal — minimum protective measures at all times</li><li>Level 2: heightened risk — additional measures for a period</li><li>Level 3: probable or imminent incident — specific measures, limited period (follow government instructions)</li><li>Ship's level must be at least the port's level</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part A — Mandatory</b></p><ul><li>1 General · 2 Definitions (SSP, SSO, CSO, PFSO, Declaration of Security)</li><li>3 Application</li><li>4 Responsibilities of Contracting Governments (set security levels, approve SSPs, issue ISSC)</li><li>5 Declaration of Security (DoS): agreement ship–port or ship–ship on security measures (when levels differ, ship–ship activity, threat, government request) · kept ≥ period set by flag</li><li>6 Obligations of the Company: master's overriding authority on security · support CSO, SSO and master</li><li>7 Ship security: measures at each level for access control, restricted areas, handling of cargo and stores, monitoring, embarkation of persons and baggage</li><li>8 Ship Security Assessment (by CSO): on-scene survey, identify threats, weaknesses, existing measures</li><li>9 Ship Security Plan: approved by flag/RSO · protected from unauthorized disclosure · contents include:<ul><li>Measures to prevent weapons and dangerous substances on board · prevent unauthorized access</li><li>Procedures for responding to threats, evacuation, reporting incidents</li><li>Duties of personnel, audits, training, drills</li><li>SSAS activation points and procedures (confidential)</li></ul></li><li>10 Records (kept ≥ period set by flag; in working language): training and drills · security threats and incidents · breaches · changes in security level · communications · internal audits · reviews of SSA/SSP · maintenance of security equipment · DoS</li><li>11 Company Security Officer (CSO)</li><li>12 Ship Security Officer (SSO): regular inspections, maintains SSP, coordinates with PFSO, reports incidents</li><li>13 Training, drills and exercises:<ul><li>Drills at least every 3 months · when &gt;25% crew changed with no prior drill on that ship, drill within 1 week</li><li>Exercises at least once each calendar year, ≤18 months between</li></ul></li><li>14 Port facility security · 15 PFSA · 16 PFSP · 17 Port Facility Security Officer (PFSO) · 18 Port facility training and drills</li><li>19 Verification and certification for ships:<ul><li>Initial, renewal (≤5 years), intermediate (between 2nd and 3rd anniversary) verifications</li><li>ISSC valid ≤5 years · Interim ISSC ≤6 months (no extension)</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Part B — Guidance</b></p><ul><li>Detailed guidance on SSA, SSP contents, DoS, PFSA, information to be provided for control measures</li><li>B/4: CSR and pre-arrival security information</li><li>Many PSC regimes check Part B items (e.g., US 33 CFR 104)</li></ul><p><b>Linked SOLAS XI-2 regulations</b></p><ul><li>XI-2/5 Specific responsibility of companies (master has information on who appoints crew, charterers)</li><li>XI-2/6 Ship Security Alert System (covert, to flag/company; no alarm on board)</li><li>XI-2/8 Master's discretion for ship safety and security</li><li>XI-2/9 Control and compliance measures (pre-arrival information, delay, detention, denial of entry)</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Know the port's security level and match it; DoS when levels differ</li><li>Gangway watch, visitor log, ID checks, restricted areas locked</li><li>Drill every 3 months; 25% new crew rule; one exercise a year</li><li>SSP is confidential — PSC may only see it with flag consent (except listed sections)</li><li>SSAS tested as per flag instructions; activation points known only to master/SSO and few others</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Maritime security: SOLAS XI-2 and the ISPS Code: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Security/Pages/SOLAS-XI-2%20ISPS%20Code.aspx</li><li>IMO — Guidelines on maritime cyber risk management MSC-FAL.1/Circ.3/Rev.3: https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Security/Pages/Cyber-security.aspx</li><li>USCG — 33 CFR Part 104 Maritime security: vessels: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-33/chapter-I/subchapter-H/part-104</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-lsa-code","v":78122688,"title":"LSA Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["lsa","life-saving","lifeboat","solas-iii","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>LSA Code — International Life-Saving Appliance Code</b></p><p><b>What is the LSA Code</b></p><ul><li>Technical standards for life-saving appliances required by SOLAS Ch III</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS III/3.10 (from 1 Jul 1998)</li><li>Says how equipment must be built and perform; SOLAS III says what each ship carries, drills and maintenance</li><li>Testing: Revised Recommendation on Testing of LSA — MSC.81(70) (prototype and production tests)</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Adopted 4 Jun 1996 (MSC.48(66)) · in force 1 Jul 1998</li><li>MSC.320(89) (2011): new release-and-retrieval system (on-load hook) requirements — existing hooks evaluated/replaced by first dry-dock after 1 Jul 2014</li><li>MSC.535(107): totally enclosed lifeboat ventilation — lifeboats installed on/after 1 Jan 2029</li><li>MSC.554(108): in force 1 Jan 2026 — lifejacket in-water performance (righting), release hooks, launching appliance lowering speeds</li><li>MSC.402(96): maintenance, thorough examination, operational testing, overhaul and repair of survival craft, rescue boats, launching appliances and release gear — in force 1 Jan 2020</li><li>Amendments adopted MSC 111 (May 2026): simulated launching of free-fall lifeboats — in force 1 Jan 2028</li><li>MSC.559(108) (amends MSC.402(96) maintenance): annual thorough examination includes the lifeboat ventilation system — 1 Jan 2026</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Life-saving appliances on SOLAS ships, installed per SOLAS Ch III</li><li>Each amendment usually applies to appliances installed on/after its date (existing ones may stay)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Chapters</b></p><ul><li>Ch I General: definitions · general requirements (resistant to corrosion, highly visible colour, retro-reflective tape, operate −30 °C to +65 °C stowage, −1 to +30 °C sea water)</li></ul><ul><li>Ch II Personal life-saving appliances<ul><li>2.1 Lifebuoys: outer diameter ≤800 mm, inner ≥400 mm · mass ≥2.5 kg · supports 14.5 kg iron for 24 h · self-igniting lights (≥2 cd, 2 h) · self-activating smoke signals (15 min) · buoyant lifeline (≥30 m)</li><li>2.2 Lifejackets:<ul><li>Adult: turn an unconscious person face-up within 5 s (from 2026: MSC.554 removes allowance for failed righting) · mouth ≥120 mm above water</li><li>Person can jump 4.5 m into water wearing it · don correctly within 1 min without help</li><li>Light (≥0.75 cd, 8 h) and whistle · sizes: infant (&lt;15 kg), child (15–43 kg), adult (≥43 kg)</li></ul></li><li>2.3 Immersion suits: don unaided within 2 min · body core temperature falls ≤2 °C — uninsulated suit 1 h in 5 °C water, insulated suit 6 h in 0–2 °C water · jump 4.5 m</li><li>2.4 Anti-exposure suits (rescue boat crews) · 2.5 Thermal protective aids (thermal conductance ≤7,800 W/(m²·K))</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li>Ch III Visual signals<ul><li>3.1 Rocket parachute flares: height ≥300 m · ≥30,000 cd · burn ≥40 s</li><li>3.2 Hand flares: ≥15,000 cd · ≥1 min</li><li>3.3 Buoyant smoke signals: orange smoke ≥3 min</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li>Ch IV Survival craft<ul><li>4.1 General liferaft requirements: withstand 18 m drop (or stowage height) · jump from 4.5 m onto it · capacity ≥6 persons · canopy</li><li>4.2 Inflatable liferafts: two separate buoyancy tubes · container · painter · hydrostatic release unit (float-free)</li><li>4.3 Rigid liferafts · 4.4 General lifeboat requirements:<ul><li>Launch with full complement in still water at 5 knots ahead and 10° trim / 20° list</li><li>Boarding: cargo ships all persons ≤3 min from the order · passenger ships ≤10 min</li><li>Engine: start at −15 °C within 2 min · speed ≥6 knots in calm water (≥2 knots towing a 25-person liferaft) · fuel for 24 h</li><li>Release mechanism: off-load and on-load release, protected against accidental operation; hydrostatic interlock</li></ul></li><li>4.5 Partially enclosed lifeboats · 4.6 Totally enclosed lifeboats (self-righting; from 2029 ventilation 5 m³/h per person for 24 h) · 4.7 Free-fall lifeboats (certified free-fall height) · 4.8 Lifeboats with self-contained air support · 4.9 Fire-protected lifeboats (8 min in oil fire, water spray)</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li>Ch V Rescue boats<ul><li>Length 3.8–8.5 m · carry 5 seated + 1 on stretcher · speed ≥6 knots for 4 h · tow 25-person liferaft at 2 knots</li><li>Fast rescue boats: 6–8.5 m · ≥20 knots (with 3 crew), ≥8 knots (with 6 persons)</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li>Ch VI Launching and embarkation appliances<ul><li>Davit-launched: lower at speed per MSC.554 (safe max and min) · gravity or stored mechanical power · manual brake · recovery</li><li>Free-fall launching, marine evacuation systems (MES), embarkation ladders</li><li>Release hooks: new release-and-retrieval system requirements (MSC.320(89), MSC.554(108))</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li>Ch VII Other life-saving appliances<ul><li>7.1 Line-throwing appliances: ≥4 projectiles · each line ≥230 m · range ≥230 m in calm weather</li><li>7.2 General alarm and public address systems (sound ≥80 dB(A) in accommodation, 10 dB above ambient)</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Linked: maintenance and servicing</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS III/20 · MSC.402(96): lifeboats, rescue boats, davits, release gear — weekly/monthly ship checks; annual thorough examination and 5-yearly overhaul and load test by manufacturer or authorized provider</li><li>Liferafts and HRUs: serviced every 12 months (≤17 months approved) at approved service station</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Lifejacket, immersion suit and lifeboat standards here; drill frequency and on-board numbers in SOLAS Ch III</li><li>From 1 Jan 2026: new lifejackets must right an unconscious person; new hooks/launching appliances meet MSC.554</li><li>From 1 Jan 2029: new totally enclosed lifeboats need ventilation — annual check includes it (from 2026)</li><li>PSC hot spots: release gear, falls, engine start, liferaft service dates, HRU expiry</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>UK MCA — MGN 714 LSA Code amendments: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mgn-714-mf-adoption-of-amendments-to-the-life-saving-appliances-lsa-code/mgn-714-mf-adoption-of-amendments-to-the-life-saving-appliances-lsa-code</li><li>PRS — IMO requirements 2026 (MSC.554(108), MSC.559(108), MSC.535(107)): https://prs.pl/wp-content/uploads/Biulletin_IMO_nr_15_2025_IMO-Requirements-2026_ENG.pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.554(108): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/Resolution%20MSC.554(108).pdf</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.535(107): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/MediaCentre/Documents/MSC.535(107).pdf</li><li>DNV — IMO MSC 111: amendments adopted (May 2026): https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-mcs-111-new-mass-code-adopted/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-mass-code","v":97871668,"title":"MASS Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["mass","autonomous","remote-operation","roc","cyber","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>MASS Code — International Code of Safety for Maritime Autonomous Surface Ships</b></p><p><b>What is the MASS Code</b></p><ul><li>First international framework for ships whose key functions are autonomous or remotely operated from a Remote Operations Centre (ROC)</li><li>Goal-based and technology-neutral; aims at a level of safety and security expected of a conventional ship</li><li><b>Supplementary</b>: covers MASS operations and functions only where SOLAS, COLREG, STCW, MARPOL etc. are not adequate</li><li>Non-mandatory first version, written so it can later become mandatory</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Regulatory scoping exercise 2017–2021 (MSC 98–103); MSC–LEG–FAL Joint Working Group</li><li>Adopted 21 May 2026 at MSC 111, resolution MSC.595(111)</li><li>Effective (voluntary) 1 Jul 2026, followed by an experience-building phase</li><li>Mandatory MASS Code planned: entry into force 1 Jan 2032 at the earliest (timeline may be revisited)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Cargo ships to which SOLAS Chapter I applies, and their ROCs, that have systems enabling autonomous or remote operation — when the Administration finds the other instruments impracticable or insufficient</li><li>Not passenger ships (first version)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part I — Introduction</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 Purpose, principles (supplementary, holistic, goal-based, non-mandatory, technology-neutral) and objectives (human oversight, safe coexistence with all ships, responsibility)</li><li>Ch 2 Application · Ch 3 Code structure · Ch 4 Definitions (MASS, MASS function, ROC, remote operator, operational context, fallback / minimum risk condition)</li></ul><p><b>Part II — Principles for MASS and MASS functions</b></p><ul><li>Ch 5 Surveys and certificates: <b>MASS Safety Certificate</b>, <b>MASS ROC Certificate</b>, provisional certificates, validity (forms in the Appendix)</li><li>Ch 6 Approval process (by the Administration, function by function)</li><li>Ch 7 Risk assessment · Ch 8 Operational context (where, when and in what conditions the ship may operate)</li><li>Ch 9 System design · Ch 10 Software principles</li><li>Ch 11 Management of safe operations (link to ISM; overriding authority of the master)</li><li>Ch 12 Special measures to enhance maritime security (incl. cyber)</li><li>Ch 13 Alert management · Ch 14 Manning, training and watchkeeping · Ch 15 Maintenance</li></ul><p><b>Part III — Goals, functional requirements and expected performances</b></p><ul><li>Ch 16 Safety of navigation (lookout, COLREG compliance, voyage planning)</li><li>Ch 17 Connectivity (redundant links; safe behaviour when a link is lost)</li><li>Ch 18 Remote operations (ROC design, staffing, hand-over)</li><li>Ch 19 Structure, subdivision, stability and watertight integrity</li><li>Ch 20 Fire protection, detection and extinction</li><li>Ch 21 Search and rescue (how a MASS assists persons in distress)</li><li>Ch 22 Carriage of cargoes · Ch 23 Anchoring, towing and mooring · Ch 24 Machinery and electrical installations</li></ul><p><b>The master (Ch 14)</b></p><ul><li>Regardless of the mode of operation, <b>the master is responsible for the safe operation of the MASS</b></li><li>The master may be on board or in an ROC; must receive alerts and be able to intervene</li></ul><p><b>Degrees of autonomy (IMO scoping, for reference)</b></p><ul><li>1 crewed ship with automated processes · 2 remotely controlled with seafarers on board · 3 remotely controlled without seafarers on board · 4 fully autonomous</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Voluntary from 1 Jul 2026; mandatory version targeted for 2032</li><li>Certificates: MASS Safety Certificate (ship) and MASS ROC Certificate (control centre)</li><li>One master always responsible — on board or ashore; COLREG still applies to every ship</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 11 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.595(111) MASS Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.595(111).pdf</li><li>IMO — Autonomous shipping (MASS): https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/MASS.aspx</li><li>DNV — IMO MSC 111: new MASS Code adopted: https://www.dnv.com/news/2026/imo-mcs-111-new-mass-code-adopted/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-nox-technical-code","v":248244866,"title":"NOx Technical Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["nox","nox-technical-code","eiapp","marpol-annex-vi","engines","tier-iii","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>NOx Technical Code 2008 — Technical Code on Control of Emission of Nitrogen Oxides from Marine Diesel Engines</b></p><p><b>What is the NOx Technical Code</b></p><ul><li>How marine diesel engines are tested, surveyed and certified to show they meet the NOx limits of MARPOL Annex VI Regulation 13</li><li>Mandatory under MARPOL Annex VI</li><li>Gives the <b>EIAPP certificate</b>, the engine <b>Technical File</b> and on-board verification methods</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1997: first NOx Technical Code with MARPOL Annex VI (in force 19 May 2005)</li><li>Revised 2008 Code adopted 10 Oct 2008, resolution MEPC.177(58) · in force 1 Jul 2010 (with revised Annex VI)</li><li>Later amendments: e.g. MEPC.251(66) (dual-fuel and gas-fuelled engines, SCR testing)</li><li>MEPC 84 (Apr–May 2026): amendments aligning Annex VI and the Code for engines with <b>multiple engine operational profiles</b> (entry into force after adoption procedure)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Marine diesel engines with power output <b>over 130 kW</b>, installed on a ship (new engines, and engines with a major conversion)</li><li>Not: engines used only in emergencies or only for lifeboats/emergency equipment</li></ul><hr><p><b>Chapters</b></p><ul><li>Introduction · abbreviations, subscripts and symbols</li><li><b>Ch 1 General</b>: purpose, application, definitions (engine family, engine group, parent engine, rated power and speed)</li><li><b>Ch 2 Surveys and certification</b>: pre-certification survey → <b>EIAPP Certificate</b>; initial survey on board; periodical and intermediate surveys (with the IAPP certificate); Technical File and <b>Record Book of Engine Parameters</b>; on-board NOx verification procedures</li><li><b>Ch 3 NOx emission standards</b>: limits from Reg 13 and the <b>test cycles</b> (E2, E3 for propulsion; D2 for constant-speed auxiliaries; C1 for variable-speed auxiliaries)</li><li><b>Ch 4 Approval of serially manufactured engines</b>: engine family and engine group concepts, parent engine selection</li><li><b>Ch 5 Test-bed measurements</b>: test conditions, fuels, analysers, calibration, data evaluation</li><li><b>Ch 6 Demonstrating compliance on board</b>: engine parameter check method · simplified measurement method · direct measurement and monitoring method</li><li><b>Ch 7 Certification of an existing engine</b> (pre-2000 engines over 5,000 kW and 90 L/cyl, Reg 13.7 approved methods)</li><li>Appendices I–VIII: EIAPP certificate form; flow charts; analyser specifications; calibration; parent engine test report; exhaust mass flow (carbon balance); parameter check checklist; direct measurement</li></ul><p><b>NOx Tiers (MARPOL Annex VI Reg 13) — g/kWh by rated speed n (rpm)</b></p><ul><li><b>Tier I</b> (ships built 1 Jan 2000 – 31 Dec 2010): n &lt; 130 → 17.0 · 130 ≤ n &lt; 2,000 → 45 × n^(-0.2) · n ≥ 2,000 → 9.8</li><li><b>Tier II</b> (built from 1 Jan 2011): 14.4 · 44 × n^(-0.23) · 7.7</li><li><b>Tier III</b> (built from 1 Jan 2016, in <b>NOx ECAs</b> only): 3.4 · 9 × n^(-0.2) · 2.0<ul><li>NOx ECAs: North American and US Caribbean Sea (ships built from 2016); Baltic and North Sea (built from 2021); Norwegian Sea (built from 1 Mar 2026); later ECAs (Canadian Arctic, NE Atlantic) on their own dates</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Documents on board</b></p><ul><li>EIAPP certificate for each engine · Technical File (approved) · Record Book of Engine Parameters · Tier III SCR/EGR documentation; Tier change-over log when entering/leaving NOx ECAs</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>The engine must match its Technical File — any change to components or settings (injectors, turbochargers, timing) must be recorded and allowed by the file</li><li>Tier III only applies inside NOx ECAs; log the time and position of Tier changes</li><li>PSC checks EIAPP, Technical File, record book and NOx-critical component markings</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 11 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Resolution MEPC.177(58) NOx Technical Code 2008: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MEPCDocuments/MEPC.177(58).pdf</li><li>Lloyd's Register — MEPC 84 summary report: https://www.lr.org/en/knowledge/regulatory-updates/imo-meetings-and-future-legislation/mepc-84-summary-report/</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-polar-code","v":192742385,"title":"Polar Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["polar","arctic","antarctic","solas-xiv","marpol","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>Polar Code — International Code for Ships Operating in Polar Waters</b></p><p><b>What is the Polar Code</b></p><ul><li>IMO code for safety and pollution prevention of ships in Arctic and Antarctic waters</li><li>Mandatory under SOLAS Ch XIV (Part I — safety) and MARPOL Annexes I, II, IV, V (Part II — environment)</li><li>Goal-based: each chapter has a goal, functional requirements and regulations</li><li>Certificate: Polar Ship Certificate (PSC — Category A, B or C) · Polar Water Operational Manual (PWOM) on board</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Before: IMO Guidelines for ships in Arctic ice-covered waters (2002), polar waters (2009, A.1024(26))</li><li>Safety part adopted 21 Nov 2014 (MSC.385(94)); SOLAS Ch XIV added by MSC.386(94)</li><li>Environment part adopted 15 May 2015 (MEPC.264(68))</li><li>In force 1 Jan 2017 (new ships) · existing ships by first intermediate or renewal survey after 1 Jan 2018</li><li>STCW amendments: basic and advanced polar training (in force 1 Jul 2018)</li><li>MARPOL Annex I: Arctic heavy fuel oil use/carriage ban from 1 Jul 2024 (waivers for some ships until 1 Jul 2029)</li><li>MSC.538(107): Chapters 9-1 and 11-1 for non-SOLAS ships — in force 1 Jan 2026</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>SOLAS ships (cargo ≥500 GT, passenger ships) operating in polar waters</li><li>From 1 Jan 2026 (Part I-A Ch 9-1 navigation and Ch 11-1 voyage planning): fishing vessels ≥24 m LOA · pleasure yachts ≥300 GT not in trade · cargo ships 300–&lt;500 GT<ul><li>Built ≥1 Jan 2026: comply before operating in polar waters · built before: comply by 1 Jan 2027</li></ul></li><li>Polar waters: Arctic waters (defined boundary around 60° N, varies) and Antarctic area (south of 60° S)</li></ul><p><b>Ship categories</b></p><ul><li>Category A: medium first-year ice with old ice inclusions (PC1–PC5)</li><li>Category B: thin first-year ice with old ice inclusions (PC6–PC7)</li><li>Category C: open water/ice conditions less severe than A and B (may be not ice-strengthened)</li></ul><hr><p><b>Introduction</b></p><ul><li>Definitions (ice-free waters, ice of land origin, Polar Service Temperature (PST) = 10 °C below lowest Mean Daily Low Temperature, Polar Class)</li><li>Hazards: ice, low temperature, extended darkness/daylight, high latitude (navigation and communication), remoteness, lack of crew experience, lack of SAR, ecosystem sensitivity</li></ul><p><b>Part I-A — Safety measures (mandatory)</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 General: Polar Ship Certificate · operational assessment (POLARIS — Polar Operational Limit Assessment Risk Indexing System)</li><li>Ch 2 Polar Water Operational Manual (PWOM): capabilities and limitations, procedures for normal and emergency operations, ice operations, contacting icebreakers</li><li>Ch 3 Ship structure: Polar Class structure for Cat A/B · materials suitable for PST</li><li>Ch 4 Subdivision and stability: ice accretion allowances (30 kg/m² on exposed decks, 7.5 kg/m² on lateral area) · damage stability for ice damage (Cat A/B)</li><li>Ch 5 Watertight and weathertight integrity: operable in ice and low temperature</li><li>Ch 6 Machinery installations: protection from ice and snow, sea-water intake for ice</li><li>Ch 7 Fire safety/protection: equipment works at PST, freeze protection of fire mains and extinguishers</li><li>Ch 8 Life-saving appliances and arrangements: survival for ≥5 days (maximum expected time of rescue) · personal and group survival equipment · immersion suits/thermal protection for all</li><li>Ch 9 Safety of navigation: ice detection (two independent echo-sounders, radar for ice), visual aids, two non-magnetic heading sources for high latitudes · Ch 9-1 for non-SOLAS ships (2026)</li><li>Ch 10 Communication: voice and data to RCC/SAR in the area · emergency communication in survival craft</li><li>Ch 11 Voyage planning: ice and weather information, routeing, places of refuge, marine mammals, PSSA, SAR capability · Ch 11-1 for non-SOLAS ships (2026)</li><li>Ch 12 Manning and training: masters, chief mates and OOWs with STCW Basic or Advanced Polar training per category and ice conditions</li></ul><p><b>Part I-B — Additional guidance</b> (recommendatory)</p><p><b>Part II-A — Pollution prevention measures (mandatory)</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 Oil (MARPOL I): no discharge of oil or oily mixtures in polar waters · Cat A/B built ≥2017: fuel and cargo tanks separated from shell (≥0.76 m)</li><li>Ch 2 Noxious liquid substances (MARPOL II): no discharge of NLS in polar waters · Cat A/B new ships — tanks away from shell</li><li>Ch 3 (reserved — packaged harmful substances)</li><li>Ch 4 Sewage (MARPOL IV): discharge limits — distance from ice shelf/fast ice; treatment plant standards</li><li>Ch 5 Garbage (MARPOL V): food waste only comminuted (≤25 mm), ≥12 nm from land and ice shelf/fast ice, not onto ice · cargo residues limited (Arctic)</li></ul><p><b>Part II-B — Additional guidance</b> (recommendatory)</p><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>On board: Polar Ship Certificate + PWOM + POLARIS assessment for the route</li><li>Master and officers need polar training (STCW V/4) for the ship category and ice</li><li>Zero oil and NLS discharge; garbage rules stricter than outside polar waters</li><li>No HFO in the Arctic for most ships from 2024 (all by 2029); Antarctic HFO ban since 2011 (MARPOL I/43)</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>Marine Insight — New Polar Code amendments 2026 (MSC.538(107)): https://www.marineinsight.com/know-more/new-polar-code-amendments-2026/</li><li>PRS — IMO requirements 2026: https://prs.pl/wp-content/uploads/Biulletin_IMO_nr_15_2025_IMO-Requirements-2026_ENG.pdf</li><li>UK P&amp;I — IMO regulatory update 2026: https://www.ukpandi.com/news-and-resources/news/article/imo-regulatory-update-2026/</li><li>IMO — Shipping in polar waters: https://www.imo.org/en/MediaCentre/HotTopics/Pages/Polar-default.aspx</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-ro-code","v":57544396,"title":"RO Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["ro-code","recognized-organization","class","flag-state","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>RO Code — Code for Recognized Organizations</b></p><p><b>What is the RO Code</b></p><ul><li>International standard for organizations (mostly classification societies) that do statutory surveys and issue certificates on behalf of a flag State</li><li>Sets minimum criteria to be recognized, how the flag delegates authority and how it oversees the RO</li><li>Mandatory through SOLAS XI-1/1, MARPOL Annex I and II, Load Lines Protocol 1988</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>Before: Guidelines for authorization of organizations acting on behalf of the Administration A.739(18) (1993) and specifications A.789(19) (1995)</li><li>RO Code adopted 21 Jun 2013 by MSC.349(92) and 17 May 2013 by MEPC.237(65)</li><li>Mandatory from 1 Jan 2015 (SOLAS, MARPOL I and II, LL Protocol amendments)</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>Flag States (Administrations) that delegate statutory work</li><li>Recognized Organizations: e.g., IACS members (ABS, BV, ClassNK, DNV, KR, LR, RINA, CCS, IRS, PRS, CRS, TL) and non-IACS ROs</li><li>Statutory work: surveys, audits and certificates under SOLAS, MARPOL, LL, Tonnage, ISM, ISPS (as an RSO), MLC (if delegated), AFS, BWM</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part 1 — General</b></p><ul><li>Definitions (Administration, RO, statutory certification and services, delegation, oversight)</li><li>Objective: uniform, high-quality statutory work on behalf of flags</li></ul><p><b>Part 2 — Requirements for recognized organizations</b></p><ul><li>Organization and management: legal entity, adequate resources, documented quality management system (ISO 9001-based and IACS QSCS-type)</li><li>Technical capability: rules, technical staff, research, global network of exclusive surveyors</li><li>Independence and impartiality: no conflict of interest; not controlled by owners/builders it surveys</li><li>Qualification and training of surveyors (records, competence for each task)</li><li>Rules and procedures published and kept up to date</li><li>Internal audits and corrective actions</li><li>Information to the flag: reports of surveys, deficiencies, withdrawal of class, detentions</li><li>Agreement with the flag: written agreement covering scope of delegation, reporting, legal basis (model agreement in Part 3)</li></ul><p><b>Part 3 — Oversight by the flag State</b></p><ul><li>Written agreement, defined scope of authority</li><li>Monitoring: audits of the RO's offices, ships surveyed (flag-state inspections), reviews of PSC detentions with RO responsibility</li><li>Corrective actions and, if needed, withdrawal of recognition</li><li>Flag keeps final responsibility for the completeness and efficiency of surveys (SOLAS I/6)</li></ul><p><b>Part 4 — Model agreement between Administration and RO</b> (non-mandatory form)</p><p><b>RO responsibility and PSC</b></p><ul><li>If a ship is detained for a deficiency the RO should have found, PSC records it as \"RO-related\" — affects the RO's performance list (Paris/Tokyo MoU RO performance tables)</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>Class (RO) issues statutory certificates only within the flag's written delegation — check the certificate states \"under authority of the Government of …\"</li><li>Flag still responsible; RO-related detentions damage the RO's rating</li><li>When changing flag or class, keep RO survey status continuous (transfer of class agreements, IACS PR 1A/1B)</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — Member State Audit and implementation (RO Code context): https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/MSAS/Pages/Default.aspx</li><li>IMO — Resolution MSC.349(92) RO Code: https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/MSCResolutions/MSC.349(92).pdf</li></ul>"},{"id":"note-imo-tdc-code","v":73467330,"title":"TDC Code","category":"Maritime › IMO › Codes","tags":["tdc","timber","deck-cargo","logs","imo","code"],"body":"<p><b>TDC Code — Code of Safe Practice for Ships Carrying Timber Deck Cargoes (2011 TDC Code)</b></p><p><b>What is the TDC Code</b></p><ul><li>IMO guidance on stowing, securing and carrying timber (logs, sawn wood, cants, pulpwood) on deck</li><li>Recommendatory (Assembly resolution), but used with SOLAS VI/5 (Cargo Securing Manual) and the Load Line Convention (timber freeboard)</li><li>Covers stability with timber deck cargo, lashing systems, crew safety</li></ul><p><b>History</b></p><ul><li>1972 first TDC Code · 1991 Code (A.715(17))</li><li>2011 TDC Code adopted 30 Nov 2011 (A.1048(27)) · replaced the 1991 Code</li><li>2011 Code changed to performance-based securing (calculations like CSS Annex 13) and added uprights for logs</li></ul><p><b>Applies to</b></p><ul><li>All ships ≥24 m carrying timber deck cargo</li><li>Ships assigned timber load lines (Load Lines Convention reg 41–45, 1966/1988) — reduced freeboard only if timber deck cargo is stowed and secured properly</li></ul><hr><p><b>Part A — Operational requirements</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 General: purpose, application, definitions (timber, timber deck cargo, timber load line, uprights, lashing)</li><li>Ch 2 Stability:<ul><li>Loading condition in the stability booklet · allow for water absorption (+10% weight of deck timber) and ice accretion</li><li>Metacentric height (GM) ≥0.10 m at all times (incl. end of voyage) · avoid excessive GM (violent rolling)</li><li>Loss of cargo or shift from list — monitor list during loading</li></ul></li><li>Ch 3 Before loading: check lashing equipment certified, uprights and sockets, deck fittings; plan per Cargo Securing Manual</li><li>Ch 4 Loading and stowage:<ul><li>Stow compactly, no gaps; deck cargo height within visibility limits (SOLAS V/22) and stability limits</li><li>Height on deck generally ≤ the limits in the manual · access protection: guardrails/lifelines (≥1 m high) across the cargo, safe walkways</li></ul></li><li>Ch 5 Securing:<ul><li>Logs: uprights (stanchions) + hog/loop lashings + wiggle wire as designed; uniform tension</li><li>Sawn wood (packaged): independent lashings over each row, spacing per manual (generally ≤3 m)</li><li>Lashing tension checked and tightened regularly during the voyage</li></ul></li><li>Ch 6 Personnel safety: walkways, lifelines, lighting, PPE, no walking on top in bad weather</li><li>Ch 7 Voyage planning and voyage: avoid heavy weather if possible; check and re-tension lashings · procedures if cargo shifts or list develops (jettisoning only as last resort, with master's decision)</li></ul><p><b>Part B — Design principles (securing arrangements)</b></p><ul><li>Ch 1 General · Ch 2 Design of timber deck cargo securing arrangements:<ul><li>Forces from ship motions and green seas · friction coefficients (wood on wood, wood on steel)</li><li>Securing system strength: MSL of lashings and uprights · uprights strong enough for transverse forces</li></ul></li><li>Ch 3 Physical properties of timber cargoes (densities, friction, water absorption)</li><li>Annexes: calculation examples, lashing types (loop, hog, wiggle wire, chains), maintenance</li></ul><p><b>Load Line link (timber freeboard)</b></p><ul><li>Timber load lines (LT, LS, LW, LWNA, LTF, LF) marked aft of the load line disc</li><li>Can be used only when timber deck cargo is stowed solid, height ≥ standard height of superstructure (generally), and secured per the rules</li></ul><p><b>Key Point</b></p><ul><li>GM must stay positive with margin at arrival (water absorption +10%, ice, consumption of fuel and water)</li><li>Lifelines/guardrails and safe walkways over the cargo before sailing</li><li>Re-tension lashings daily and after heavy weather; record it</li><li>Timber load lines only valid with properly stowed and secured deck cargo</li></ul><p><b>Sources (checked 10 Nov 2026)</b></p><ul><li>IMO — 2011 TDC Code, resolution A.1048(27): https://wwwcdn.imo.org/localresources/en/KnowledgeCentre/IndexofIMOResolutions/AssemblyDocuments/A.1048(27).pdf</li><li>Skuld — Timber deck cargoes: https://www.skuld.com/topics/cargo/timber/</li></ul>"}];
